// ── Retina · BCSC Section 12 · Part 1 · Fluorescein & ICG Angiography
// Source: BCSC Section 12 (Retina and Vitreous), Ch. 2 (Diagnostic Approach).
const BCSC_FFA_RECALL: RecallQ[] = [
  { id: 1, question: "What wavelengths do the excitation and barrier filters transmit in fluorescein angiography?", answer: "Excitation filter: blue-green light at 465–490 nm (fluorescein's peak excitation). Barrier filter: yellow-green at 520–530 nm (fluorescein's peak emission). The barrier filter blocks essentially all visible wavelengths except the light emitted by fluorescein." },
  { id: 2, question: "What percentage of fluorescein is protein-bound, and why does that matter?", answer: "About 80% is protein-bound. The intact blood–retina barrier prevents fluorescein from diffusing into retinal tissue, so normal retinal vessels do not leak. Dye leaks only where a blood–ocular barrier is absent (new vessels) or defective (inflammation, ischemia)." },
  { id: 3, question: "From which structure does fluorescein readily leak, and what limits its use for choroidal imaging?", answer: "It leaks readily from the fenestrated choriocapillaris, staining surrounding tissue. That rapid leakage, plus light absorption and scattering by RPE and choroidal pigment, limits fluorescein's usefulness for choroidal imaging — ICG is preferred there." },
  { id: 4, question: "Describe the timing of the phases of a normal fluorescein angiogram.", answer: "Choroidal (prearterial) flush ~8–12 s; retinal arterial phase 11–18 s; arteriovenous phase 1–3 s later (with laminar venous flow); complete venous filling over the next 5–10 s; recirculation/mid phase at 2–4 min; late phase at 4–5 min (gradual dye elimination)." },
  { id: 5, question: "What is laminar venous flow on FA?", answer: "Thin columns of dye seen along the walls of the larger veins during the early arteriovenous phase before the veins fill completely — a consequence of the parabolic flow profile (faster central, slower peripheral wall flow)." },
  { id: 6, question: "Why does the fovea appear dark on fluorescein angiography?", answer: "Three reasons: macular xanthophyll pigment absorbs the blue excitation light; the foveal RPE cells are taller and contain more melanin than peripheral RPE; and there are no retinal vessels in the foveal avascular zone." },
  { id: 7, question: "What are the two major categories of FA abnormality?", answer: "Hypofluorescence (reduced or absent fluorescence) and hyperfluorescence (abnormally excessive fluorescence)." },
  { id: 8, question: "What are the two patterns of hypofluorescence?", answer: "Vascular filling defects (nonperfusion or obstruction of an artery, vein, or capillary — seen as delayed or absent filling) and blocked fluorescence (obstruction of the excitation or emitted light by pigment, blood, or fibrous tissue)." },
  { id: 9, question: "How can blocked fluorescence be used to determine a lesion's depth?", answer: "By relating the level of blocked fluorescence to the retinal circulation. If a lesion blocks the choroidal fluorescence but retinal vessels are seen coursing over it, the lesion lies above the choroid and beneath the retinal vessels." },
  { id: 10, question: "What are the four patterns of hyperfluorescence?", answer: "Leakage, staining, pooling, and transmission (window) defect." },
  { id: 11, question: "Define leakage and its behavior over the course of an FA.", answer: "A gradual, marked increase in fluorescence from seepage of dye across a compromised blood–retina barrier. Both brightness and size increase through the study and the borders become indistinct (e.g., CNV, neovascularization, active inflammation)." },
  { id: 12, question: "Define staining and how to recognize it.", answer: "Fluorescein entering solid tissue or material that retains dye — scar, drusen, vessel walls, optic nerve tissue, sclera. Fluorescence increases in brightness through transit and persists in late frames, but the borders remain fixed and intact (size does not change)." },
  { id: 13, question: "Define pooling and give a classic example.", answer: "Accumulation of dye within a fluid-filled space whose margins trap it, producing distinct borders (round/oval). Classic example: pooling under a serous RPE or neurosensory detachment in central serous chorioretinopathy." },
  { id: 14, question: "Define a transmission (window) defect and its time course.", answer: "A view of normal choroidal fluorescence through an RPE pigment defect. It appears early with choroidal filling, peaks with peak choroidal filling, does NOT enlarge, and fades in late frames as choroidal dye is diluted; the dye stays in the choroid and does not enter the retina." },
  { id: 15, question: "Summarize how brightness and size change over time for the four hyperfluorescence patterns.", answer: "Leakage: brightness ↑, size ↑. Staining: brightness ↑, size unchanged (irregular shape). Pooling: brightness same/↑, size unchanged (regular round/oval). Window defect: brightness decreases late, size unchanged." },
  { id: 16, question: "What is the inner blood–retina barrier, and what happens when it is compromised?", answer: "The tight junctions of the retinal vascular endothelium. When compromised, dye leaks through vessel walls into the retinal parenchyma and cystoid spaces (e.g., cystoid macular edema)." },
  { id: 17, question: "What is the outer blood–retina barrier, and what is the effect of its breakdown?", answer: "The retinal pigment epithelium (its tight junctions). When compromised, dye traverses the RPE into the subretinal space or neurosensory retina." },
  { id: 18, question: "What is the classic FA appearance of cystoid macular edema?", answer: "Late pooling of dye in petalloid cystoid spaces radiating from the central macula (Henle fiber layer), often with late staining of the optic nerve head — as seen in postsurgical (Irvine–Gass) CME." },
  { id: 19, question: "Contrast the FA appearance of classic versus occult CNV.", answer: "Classic CNV: an early, well-defined lacy hyperfluorescent network that leaks progressively, with pooling/leakage in the late phase. Occult CNV: poorly defined late leakage and staining without a discrete early network." },
  { id: 20, question: "What temporary and common adverse effects follow fluorescein injection?", answer: "Temporary yellowing of the skin and conjunctiva (6–12 h) and yellow-green urine; nausea and vomiting in ~5% (passes within seconds); and urticaria/hives in ~5% (treated with diphenhydramine unless very mild)." },
  { id: 21, question: "What serious adverse effects can occur with FA, and how is the risk managed?", answer: "Rare hypotension, shock, laryngeal spasm, and even death (~1:222,000). A physician should be on the premises and a crash cart available. Prior urticarial reactions raise the risk; premedication with antihistamines and/or corticosteroids reduces it." },
  { id: 22, question: "How is fluorescein extravasation managed, and what are the pregnancy/lactation considerations?", answer: "Extravasation into the skin is painful and treated with ice-cold compresses for 5–10 minutes and close follow-up until edema/pain/redness resolve. No teratogenicity is proven, but FA is generally avoided in the first trimester unless necessary; fluorescein is excreted in breast milk during lactation." },
  { id: 23, question: "What properties of indocyanine green make it ideal for choroidal imaging?", answer: "ICG is a water-soluble tricarbocyanine dye that is ~98% protein-bound, so it diffuses poorly through the choriocapillaris fenestrations and remains intravascular — ideal for imaging choroidal vessels. Its near-infrared excitation/emission also penetrates RPE, pigment, and blood better than fluorescein." },
  { id: 24, question: "What are ICG's excitation and emission wavelengths, and how is it cleared?", answer: "Excitation ~790–805 nm and emission ~825–835 nm, both in the near-infrared range. ICG is metabolized in the liver and excreted into the bile." },
  { id: 25, question: "Describe the phases of a normal ICG angiogram.", answer: "Early phase: first appearance of dye in the choroidal circulation (large choroidal arteries and veins plus retinal vessels visible). Middle phase (5–15 min): choroidal veins become indistinct with diffuse homogeneous choroidal fluorescence and reduced retinal vessel fluorescence. Late phase (~15 min): little retinal or choroidal detail remains." },
];

const BCSC_FFA_MCQ: MCQ[] = [
  { id: 1, question: "The excitation filter of a fundus camera set up for fluorescein angiography transmits light at:", options: ["520–530 nm yellow-green", "465–490 nm blue-green", "790–805 nm near-infrared", "620–640 nm red"], correct: 1, explanation: "The excitation filter passes blue-green light (465–490 nm, peak excitation); the barrier filter passes yellow-green light (520–530 nm, peak emission)." },
  { id: 2, question: "The barrier filter transmits light at approximately:", options: ["465–490 nm", "520–530 nm", "825–835 nm", "400–450 nm"], correct: 1, explanation: "The barrier filter passes fluorescein's emitted yellow-green light at 520–530 nm and blocks other visible wavelengths." },
  { id: 3, question: "Approximately what fraction of circulating fluorescein is protein-bound?", options: ["20%", "50%", "80%", "98%"], correct: 2, explanation: "Fluorescein is ~80% protein-bound; the free fraction fluoresces and can leak where barriers are defective. (ICG, by contrast, is ~98% bound.)" },
  { id: 4, question: "Fluorescein does NOT normally leak from retinal vessels because of:", options: ["Its large molecular size", "The intact inner blood–retina barrier (tight junctions of retinal endothelium)", "Rapid hepatic clearance", "Binding to RPE melanin"], correct: 1, explanation: "The inner blood–retina barrier (retinal vascular endothelial tight junctions) keeps dye intravascular in healthy retina." },
  { id: 5, question: "On a normal FA, the choroid typically begins to fill at:", options: ["1–3 seconds", "8–12 seconds", "20–25 seconds", "2–4 minutes"], correct: 1, explanation: "The choroidal flush appears at ~8–12 s, about a half-second before the retinal circulation; retinal arteries fill at 11–18 s." },
  { id: 6, question: "\"Laminar flow\" on fluorescein angiography refers to:", options: ["Uniform bright filling of the arteries", "Thin columns of dye along the walls of the larger veins during early venous filling", "Leakage from the choriocapillaris", "Dye pooling under the RPE"], correct: 1, explanation: "Laminar venous flow is the early arteriovenous-phase appearance of dye along vein walls before complete venous filling." },
  { id: 7, question: "The late phase of FA (gradual dye elimination) occurs at approximately:", options: ["15–18 seconds", "1 minute", "4–5 minutes", "20 minutes"], correct: 2, explanation: "Recirculation begins at 2–4 min, and the late phase (dye elimination) is seen at ~4–5 min." },
  { id: 8, question: "The fovea appears hypofluorescent (dark) on FA for all of the following reasons EXCEPT:", options: ["Macular xanthophyll pigment absorbs excitation light", "Taller foveal RPE cells with more melanin", "Absence of vessels in the foveal avascular zone", "A thickened internal limiting membrane at the fovea"], correct: 3, explanation: "The dark fovea reflects xanthophyll pigment, denser foveal RPE melanin, and the avascular FAZ — not the ILM." },
  { id: 9, question: "Hypofluorescence on FA is caused by which two mechanisms?", options: ["Leakage and staining", "Pooling and window defect", "Vascular filling defect and blocked fluorescence", "Transmission defect and leakage"], correct: 2, explanation: "The two hypofluorescence patterns are vascular filling (perfusion) defects and blocked fluorescence." },
  { id: 10, question: "A pigmented lesion blocks fluorescence, but retinal vessels are seen coursing over it. This localizes the lesion to:", options: ["The inner retina", "Anterior to the choroid, deep to the retinal vessels", "The vitreous cavity", "The optic nerve head"], correct: 1, explanation: "Depth is judged by which vessels are obscured; retinal vessels visible over a blocked choroid place the lesion between the two layers." },
  { id: 11, question: "Which hyperfluorescence pattern increases in BOTH brightness and size over the course of the study?", options: ["Staining", "Pooling", "Leakage", "Window defect"], correct: 2, explanation: "Leakage progressively increases in both brightness and area, with indistinct borders." },
  { id: 12, question: "A lesion that brightens through transit and persists late but keeps fixed, intact borders represents:", options: ["Leakage", "Staining", "Pooling", "Filling defect"], correct: 1, explanation: "Staining is dye entering solid retained material (scar, drusen, vessel wall, sclera, optic nerve) with unchanging borders." },
  { id: 13, question: "Late accumulation of dye within a serous RPE detachment, with sharply demarcated borders, is best described as:", options: ["Staining", "Pooling", "Window defect", "Blocked fluorescence"], correct: 1, explanation: "Pooling is dye trapped in a fluid-filled space with distinct margins, classically the RPE detachment of central serous chorioretinopathy." },
  { id: 14, question: "A transmission (window) defect characteristically:", options: ["Increases in size over the study", "Appears early, does not enlarge, and fades in late frames", "Leaks dye into the neurosensory retina", "Is brightest in the late phase"], correct: 1, explanation: "A window defect follows choroidal fluorescence: early appearance, peak with choroidal filling, no enlargement, and late fading as choroidal dye dilutes." },
  { id: 15, question: "Which pattern is characterized by decreasing brightness and unchanged size over time?", options: ["Leakage", "Pooling", "Staining", "Window (transmission) defect"], correct: 3, explanation: "The window defect dims in late frames (choroidal dye dilution) and never enlarges." },
  { id: 16, question: "Geographic atrophy exposing bare choroid through absent RPE produces which FA finding?", options: ["Leakage", "A transmission (window) defect", "Blocked fluorescence", "Pooling"], correct: 1, explanation: "Loss of RPE pigment unmasks normal choroidal fluorescence — an early window defect that fades late without leaking." },
  { id: 17, question: "The outer blood–retina barrier is formed by:", options: ["Retinal vascular endothelial tight junctions", "The retinal pigment epithelium", "Müller cell foot plates", "The internal limiting membrane"], correct: 1, explanation: "The RPE tight junctions form the outer BRB; the retinal endothelium forms the inner BRB." },
  { id: 18, question: "Cystoid macular edema from inner blood–retina barrier breakdown appears on FA as:", options: ["An early window defect", "Late petalloid pooling in the central macula with optic disc staining", "Blocked fluorescence", "Early bright staining of drusen"], correct: 1, explanation: "CME shows late petalloid pooling (Henle layer) and disc staining — classically Irvine–Gass after cataract surgery." },
  { id: 19, question: "On FA, \"classic\" choroidal neovascularization is characterized by:", options: ["A poorly defined area of late staining only", "An early, well-defined lacy hyperfluorescent network with progressive leakage", "Blocked fluorescence throughout the study", "An early window defect that fades late"], correct: 1, explanation: "Classic CNV is a discrete early network that leaks; occult CNV shows late, poorly defined leakage/staining." },
  { id: 20, question: "Drusen typically appear on FA as:", options: ["Early blocked fluorescence", "Staining — hyperfluorescence that increases then persists with fixed borders", "Progressive leakage with expanding borders", "Pooling under a detachment"], correct: 1, explanation: "Drusen stain: they take up and retain dye without leaking or expanding." },
  { id: 21, question: "The most common adverse effects of fluorescein injection are:", options: ["Anaphylaxis and shock", "Nausea/vomiting and urticaria, each in ~5%", "Seizures", "Permanent skin staining"], correct: 1, explanation: "Nausea/vomiting (~5%) and hives (~5%) are most common; nausea passes in seconds and hives respond to diphenhydramine." },
  { id: 22, question: "A patient's skin and urine turn yellow-green after FA. The appropriate action is:", options: ["Emergent evaluation for hepatic failure", "Reassurance — this is expected and resolves within hours", "Administer diphenhydramine", "Start IV corticosteroids"], correct: 1, explanation: "Skin/conjunctival yellowing (6–12 h) and discolored urine are universal, expected, and benign." },
  { id: 23, question: "The estimated risk of death from fluorescein angiography is approximately:", options: ["1:2,000", "1:22,000", "1:222,000", "1:2,000,000"], correct: 2, explanation: "Fatal reactions are rare, ~1:222,000; a physician and crash cart should nonetheless be available." },
  { id: 24, question: "A patient had urticaria after a prior fluorescein study and now needs another FA. The best approach is:", options: ["Refuse FA permanently", "Premedicate with antihistamines ± corticosteroids", "Use half the usual dye dose", "No precautions are needed"], correct: 1, explanation: "Prior urticaria increases recurrence risk; premedication with antihistamines and/or steroids reduces it." },
  { id: 25, question: "Painful extravasation of fluorescein into the skin during injection is managed with:", options: ["Warm compresses", "Ice-cold compresses for 5–10 minutes with close follow-up", "Incision and drainage", "Systemic antibiotics"], correct: 1, explanation: "Cold compresses and observation until swelling and pain resolve are recommended." },
  { id: 26, question: "Regarding FA in pregnancy and lactation:", options: ["It is absolutely contraindicated throughout pregnancy", "It is proven teratogenic", "It is often avoided in the first trimester (though no teratogenicity is proven) and the dye passes into breast milk", "It is completely safe with no considerations"], correct: 2, explanation: "No teratogenicity is proven, but many clinicians avoid the first trimester; fluorescein is excreted in breast milk." },
  { id: 27, question: "Indocyanine green is superior to fluorescein for imaging the choroid mainly because:", options: ["It is not protein-bound, so it diffuses freely", "It is ~98% protein-bound and near-infrared, so it stays intravascular and penetrates the RPE", "It is excreted renally", "It fluoresces in the ultraviolet range"], correct: 1, explanation: "High protein binding limits diffusion through choriocapillaris fenestrations, and near-infrared light penetrates pigment/blood — ideal for choroidal imaging." },
  { id: 28, question: "ICG excitation and emission peaks lie in which part of the spectrum?", options: ["Ultraviolet", "Blue-green", "Near-infrared (~790–835 nm)", "Red"], correct: 2, explanation: "ICG excitation ~790–805 nm and emission ~825–835 nm are both near-infrared." },
  { id: 29, question: "Indocyanine green is cleared by:", options: ["Renal excretion", "Hepatic metabolism and biliary excretion", "Exhalation", "RPE phagocytosis"], correct: 1, explanation: "ICG is metabolized by the liver and excreted into the bile." },
  { id: 30, question: "In the middle phase of a normal ICG angiogram (5–15 minutes), you expect:", options: ["First appearance of dye in the choroidal arteries", "Indistinct choroidal veins with diffuse homogeneous choroidal fluorescence and diminished retinal vessel fluorescence", "Complete absence of any fluorescence", "Bright leakage from the retinal capillaries"], correct: 1, explanation: "The middle phase shows fading choroidal vein detail with diffuse choroidal fluorescence and reduced retinal vessel signal." },
];

// ── Cornea · Mannis · Part 4 · Dry Eye & Ocular Allergy Medications
// Source: "Medication for Dry Eye and Ocular Allergy" (TFOS DEWS III–based lecture).
const CORNEA_MEDS_RECALL: RecallQ[] = [
  { id: 1, question: "What is the TFOS DEWS III definition of dry eye?", answer: "A multifactorial, symptomatic disease characterized by loss of homeostasis of the tear film and/or the ocular surface, in which tear film instability, hyperosmolarity, ocular surface inflammation and damage, and neurosensory abnormalities play etiological roles." },
  { id: 2, question: "What are the Step 1 (baseline / mild) interventions for dry eye?", answer: "Patient education and lifestyle modification, elimination of offending medications, low-viscosity lubrication, and lid hygiene with warm compresses for MGD." },
  { id: 3, question: "What defines Step 2 (moderate) dry eye management?", answer: "Advanced lubrication (preservative-free artificial tears, osmoprotectants, or hyaluronic acid), secretagogues, anti-inflammatory therapy, and doxycycline or azithromycin for chronic blepharitis/MGD." },
  { id: 4, question: "What are Steps 3 and 4 of dry eye management?", answer: "Step 3 (severe): biological substitutes (autologous serum/PRP) and mechanical interventions (punctal occlusion, contact lens, moisture chamber spectacles). Step 4 (refractory): surgery — tarsorrhaphy or amniotic membrane transplant." },
  { id: 5, question: "What is the trade-off of viscosity-enhancing artificial tears?", answer: "Higher viscosity increases tear residence/retention time and comfort, but causes transient blurring of vision and unwanted debris on the eyelids." },
  { id: 6, question: "Which cellulose demulcent is notable for promoting epithelial healing?", answer: "Carboxymethylcellulose (CMC) — a high-molecular-weight cellulose derivative that can promote epithelial healing. Cellulose derivatives (CMC, HPMC, HMC) are the most widely used demulcents." },
  { id: 7, question: "What is the role of Dextran 70 in artificial tears?", answer: "A high-molecular-weight (70 kDa) polysaccharide that enhances demulcent quality when combined with another demulcent (e.g., 0.3% HPMC + 0.1% dextran 70)." },
  { id: 8, question: "How do cationic oil-in-water nanoemulsion tears improve retention?", answer: "The positively charged oil nanodroplets are electrostatically attracted to the negatively charged ocular surface mucin, improving spreading and retention time; they also replace the lipid layer (nanoemulsion droplet size 10–100 nm)." },
  { id: 9, question: "What is an emollient (ointment) tear, and when is it used?", answer: "An ointment of petroleum/lanolin/mineral oils with higher viscosity and longer residence than demulcents, but it causes blurry vision and eyelash drying. Used for severe dry eye, at night, and for exposure keratopathy." },
  { id: 10, question: "What are the key properties of hyaluronic acid, and its residence-time advantage?", answer: "A glycosaminoglycan (sodium hyaluronate) that is water-retaining and mucoadhesive, giving tear film stability and healing of epithelial defects. 0.2% HA has significantly longer ocular surface residence time than 0.3% HPMC or 1.4% PVA." },
  { id: 11, question: "What is normal tear osmolarity, and the rationale for hypo-osmolar drops?", answer: "Normal tear osmolarity is 296–308 mOsm/L; dry eye causes hyperosmolarity. Hypo-osmolar eye drops lower the elevated tear osmolarity back toward normal." },
  { id: 12, question: "What are osmoprotectants and their effects?", answer: "Compounds (L-carnitine, erythritol, betaine, trehalose, glycerol) that protect cells under osmotic stress by balancing osmotic pressure without disturbing cell metabolism. They decrease apoptosis and inflammatory cytokines, increase goblet cells, and reduce corneal staining." },
  { id: 13, question: "Name the two classes of eye-drop preservatives with examples and relative toxicity.", answer: "Detergents (disrupt microbial cell membranes; high ocular toxicity) — benzalkonium chloride (BAK), Polyquad, cetrimide. Oxidizing agents (disrupt microbial metabolism; gentle on human tissue) — sodium perborate (Genaqua) and stabilized oxychloro complex (SOC/Purite)." },
  { id: 14, question: "What is the usage limit for preserved eye drops, and why?", answer: "Preserved drops should be used ≤4 times/day; using more than 4×/day for a long period causes preservative sensitivity/toxicity. Preservatives do help prevent contamination and can enhance corneal penetration of some drugs." },
  { id: 15, question: "Which electrolytes are most critical in artificial tears, and what are their roles?", answer: "Potassium (K) and bicarbonate (HCO3). K maintains corneal thickness; HCO3 supports mucin adhesion, promotes recovery, and helps regulate pH." },
  { id: 16, question: "Describe autologous serum eye drops — properties, required testing, and drawbacks.", answer: "Biologically similar to human tears (nutrients, pH, vitamins, fibronectin, growth factors); used at 20–100% concentration for severe ocular surface disease; serology (HIV, hepatitis, syphilis) is required. Benefits: epithelial healing, proliferation/migration, inhibition of inflammatory cytokines, increased goblet cells/mucin. Drawbacks: must be refrigerated, blood-borne infection and bacterial contamination risk, expensive, limited availability." },
  { id: 17, question: "How are secretagogues classified?", answer: "Oral: cholinergic agonists (pilocarpine, carbachol, bethanechol) and mucolytics (bromhexine, ambroxol). Topical: P2Y2 receptor agonist (diquafosol). They also reduce ocular surface inflammation." },
  { id: 18, question: "Oral pilocarpine — indication, dose, side effects, contraindications; and topical pilocarpine?", answer: "Indication: xerostomia (radiation-induced) and Sjögren syndrome; 5 mg orally qid. Side effects: excessive sweating, N/V, headache. Contraindicated in COPD, severe kidney disease, and heart disease. Topical pilocarpine has NO secretagogue effect." },
  { id: 19, question: "What is the mechanism and onset of diquafosol?", answer: "A P2Y2 purinergic receptor agonist that stimulates both water and mucin secretion; it takes about 2–4 weeks to see an effect (dosed roughly 6 times/day)." },
  { id: 20, question: "How does rebamipide work?", answer: "It promotes production of mucin-like glycoproteins (MUC1, MUC4, MUC16). Given as 2% QID; approved in Japan." },
  { id: 21, question: "Compare the mechanisms of cyclosporine A and lifitegrast in dry eye.", answer: "Cyclosporine A inhibits IL-2–driven T-lymphocyte activation (a calcineurin inhibitor), formulated as an oil-based emulsion (0.05% bid / 0.1% OD). Lifitegrast (5% bid) blocks the LFA-1/ICAM-1 interaction to inhibit T-cell migration (ICAM-1 is overexpressed on cornea/conjunctiva in dry eye)." },
  { id: 22, question: "How is doxycycline used in MGD, and what is its dual mechanism?", answer: "Doxycycline 100 mg bid for 1–2 weeks, then taper to 50 mg daily. Antibacterial effect decreases bacterial lipolytic enzymes; anti-inflammatory effect inhibits collagenase, phospholipase A2, and MMPs. Side effects: GI upset, photosensitization, azotemia. Contraindicated in pregnancy, breastfeeding, and allergy." },
  { id: 23, question: "Classify ocular allergy by hypersensitivity type.", answer: "Type I (IgE-mediated mast cell degranulation): acute allergic conjunctivitis, seasonal (SAC) and perennial (PAC) allergic conjunctivitis. Type IV (T-cell–mediated, delayed): vernal (VKC) and atopic (AKC) keratoconjunctivitis." },
  { id: 24, question: "What are the roles of H1 and H2 receptors in the early allergic response?", answer: "H1: increases vascular permeability → itching, pain, tearing, redness, and edema. H2: vasodilation, cell proliferation, and mucous production." },
  { id: 25, question: "What is the mechanism of mast cell stabilizers, and its clinical implication?", answer: "They block calcium influx into mast cells, preventing degranulation. Onset is delayed (~2 weeks), so they are used for prevention rather than acute relief (e.g., lodoxamide, pemirolast, cromolyn)." },
  { id: 26, question: "How do corticosteroids act in the arachidonic acid pathway, and how does that differ from NSAIDs?", answer: "Steroids inhibit phospholipase A2, blocking release of arachidonic acid and thereby suppressing BOTH the cyclooxygenase (prostaglandins) and lipoxygenase (leukotrienes) pathways. NSAIDs inhibit only cyclooxygenase. Steroids have no effect on mast cell/histamine release, but are the most potent agents for severe ocular allergy (short course ~2 weeks, effective in acute and chronic disease)." },
  { id: 27, question: "Which topical steroids have LOW versus HIGH IOP-raising potential?", answer: "Low potential: fluorometholone (FML) and loteprednol (an ester/'soft' steroid). High potential: 0.1% dexamethasone raises IOP the most; 1% prednisolone acetate (Pred Forte) is a potent anti-inflammatory. Loteprednol and FML are preferred when prolonged use is needed." },
  { id: 28, question: "List the ocular and systemic side effects of corticosteroids.", answer: "Ocular: ocular hypertension/glaucoma, posterior subcapsular cataract (PSC), secondary infection, poor epithelial healing, corneal thinning, uveitis, ptosis, and ocular discomfort. Systemic: adrenal insufficiency, Cushing syndrome, peptic ulcer, osteoporosis, hypertension/diabetes, muscle weakness/atrophy, activation of infection, and delayed wound healing." },
  { id: 29, question: "Compare the immunomodulators cyclosporine and tacrolimus — class, source, mechanism, and potency.", answer: "Both are calcineurin inhibitors that block T-cell activation and cytokine production. Cyclosporine A is a metabolite of the fungus Tolypocladium inflatum and binds cyclophilin; tacrolimus (FK506) is a macrolide from Streptomyces tsukubaensis and binds FK-binding protein, with roughly 100× the inhibitory potency of cyclosporine." },
  { id: 30, question: "What is the clinical role, onset, and dosing of topical cyclosporine?", answer: "It is not used as primary therapy — it is reserved for patients not responding to steroids, steroid responders (IOP rise), or those with steroid complications. Onset is slow (1–4 weeks) and long-term use is needed. Dosing: 0.05% (Restasis) bid or 0.1% (Ikervis) OD; off-label for VKC/AKC; main side effect is burning on instillation. Caution in children and pregnancy." },
  { id: 31, question: "Describe the layers and key components of the normal tear film.", answer: "From anterior to posterior: a lipid layer (from meibomian glands), a muco-aqueous layer (aqueous with dissolved gel-forming mucins plus proteins — lysozyme, lactoferrin, IgA, transferrin, galectin-3, trefoil factor), and the glycocalyx (membrane-spanning mucins) overlying the corneal/conjunctival epithelium." },
  { id: 32, question: "How do artificial tears differ compositionally from human tears?", answer: "Human tears contain aqueous, mucin, lipid, electrolytes, immunoglobulins, antimicrobial agents, and growth factors. Artificial tears replace the aqueous/mucin/lipid components with polymers and variable electrolytes, but LACK immunoglobulins, antimicrobial agents, and growth factors — and often add preservatives." },
  { id: 33, question: "What are the properties of an ideal artificial tear?", answer: "Physical characteristics similar to natural tears, a long ocular residence time, and preservative-free formulation." },
  { id: 34, question: "What are the properties of polyvinyl alcohol (PVA) tears?", answer: "PVA is a water-soluble polymer that increases contact/residence time, is transparent and colourless, and is non-irritating (related agents include polyvinyl pyrrolidone and polyvinyl chloride)." },
  { id: 35, question: "Define the oil-based emulsion droplet sizes used in artificial tears.", answer: "Macroemulsion: droplet > 100 nm (appears cloudy because droplets scatter light). Microemulsion: < 10 nm. Nanoemulsion: 10–100 nm. These emulsions replace the lipid layer (e.g., castor oil, mineral oil)." },
  { id: 36, question: "What is carbomer, and how does its retention compare?", answer: "Carbomer is high-molecular-weight polyacrylic acid. It has a longer retention time than other demulcents but shorter than petroleum-based ointments." },
  { id: 37, question: "What is the pH of natural tears, and what causes a stinging sensation on instillation?", answer: "Natural tear pH is 6.5–7.6 (varying with CO2 saturation and meibomian lipids in the tear film). Stinging results from a mismatch between the pH of the eye drop and the pH of the tears." },
  { id: 38, question: "What are the advantages of autologous serum eye drops?", answer: "They enhance corneal epithelial wound healing, increase epithelial cell proliferation and migration, inhibit release of inflammatory cytokines, and increase the number of goblet cells and mucin expression in the conjunctiva." },
  { id: 39, question: "How is bromhexine used as a secretagogue?", answer: "Bromhexine is an oral mucolytic secretagogue dosed at 24–48 mg/day divided tid; side effects include rash and nausea/vomiting." },
  { id: 40, question: "Compare the topical secretagogues diquafosol, rebamipide, and ecabet sodium.", answer: "Diquafosol increases BOTH aqueous and mucin (≈6 times/day, effect over 3–6 months). Rebamipide increases mucin (≈4 times/day, ~2 weeks). Ecabet sodium increases mucin (≈4 times/day, ~90 days)." },
  { id: 41, question: "How are macrolides (azithromycin) used in dry eye/MGD?", answer: "They have both antibacterial and anti-inflammatory effects and are used for MGD. Oral azithromycin (somewhat controversial) is pulsed 500 mg × 3 days weekly for 3 weeks (side effects: diarrhea, N/V); topical azithromycin is 1–1.5%." },
  { id: 42, question: "What surgical/mechanical options are used for dry eye?", answer: "Punctal occlusion (temporary or permanent), tarsorrhaphy, moisture chamber spectacles, and contact lenses." },
  { id: 43, question: "List the six categories of ocular allergy medication.", answer: "Antihistamines, vasoconstrictors, mast cell stabilizers, dual-acting drugs, steroids, and immunomodulators." },
  { id: 44, question: "Describe the early-phase allergic reaction sequence and its mediators.", answer: "Mast cell degranulation releases histamine (plus prostaglandins, tryptase, heparin). Nerve stimulation → itching (within 3–5 min); vasodilation → redness (5–10 min); endothelial gaping with fluid leakage → swelling." },
  { id: 45, question: "Describe the late-phase allergic reaction.", answer: "Cytokine-driven influx of eosinophils, PMNs, basophils, and T-lymphocytes into the conjunctival mucosa, with increased ICAM/VCAM expression. Eosinophil activation causes cellular disaggregation, epithelial desquamation, and tissue damage — a T-cell–mediated reaction." },
  { id: 46, question: "What are the non-pharmacologic treatments for ocular allergy?", answer: "Identify and avoid the allergen, avoid eye rubbing, remove causative agents (contact lens, sutures), saline (NSS) irrigation, protective eyeglasses, cold compresses, and air conditioning." },
  { id: 47, question: "Match each anti-allergy drug class to its main effect.", answer: "Artificial tears — dilute allergens/mediators; antihistamines & vasoconstrictors — block mediators; mast cell stabilizers — prevent mediator release; steroids & NSAIDs — inhibit arachidonic acid breakdown; immunomodulators — anti-inflammatory." },
  { id: 48, question: "What are the properties of topical antihistamines, with examples?", answer: "Selective H1 antagonists with rapid onset but short duration (3–4 hours); often combined with a vasoconstrictor for an additive effect. Examples: levocabastine (Livostin) 0.05% QID (caution < 12 yr) and emedastine (Emadine) 0.05% QID (caution < 3 yr and with contact lens use)." },
  { id: 49, question: "What is the mechanism, side-effect profile, and cautions of topical vasoconstrictors?", answer: "Adrenergic agonists that constrict conjunctival vessels (phenylephrine, tetrahydrozoline, naphazoline). Side effects: stinging, pupil dilation, blurred vision, epithelial erosion, and rebound congestion. Caution in cardiovascular disease and angle-closure glaucoma." },
  { id: 50, question: "What are combined antihistamine/vasoconstrictor eye drops?", answer: "Fixed combinations of an antihistamine (antazoline or pheniramine) with a decongestant (naphazoline or tetrahydrozoline) — e.g., Hista-oph, Spersallerg, Naphcon-A, Visine-A. Used for allergic conjunctivitis (off-label VKC/AKC). Side effects: burning, rebound congestion, headache, tachycardia. Caution in angle closure and with MAO inhibitors." },
  { id: 51, question: "Name mast cell stabilizer eye drops and their main use.", answer: "Lodoxamide (Alomide 0.1%), pemirolast (Pemirox 0.1%), and cromolyn sodium (Crolom 4%, Vividrin 2%). Primary use is vernal keratoconjunctivitis (VKC), off-label for allergic conjunctivitis, AKC, and GPC." },
  { id: 52, question: "What are the dual-acting (multiple-action) anti-allergy agents?", answer: "Agents that block histamine (H1) AND stabilize mast cells (some also inhibit eosinophils): alcaftadine (Lastacaft), olopatadine (Pataday 0.2% OD / Patanol 0.1% bid), ketotifen (Zaditen), epinastine (Elestat), and azelastine (Optivar)." },
  { id: 53, question: "What are the main topical corticosteroid molecules and preparations?", answer: "Prednisolone acetate (Pred Forte 1%, Pred Mild 0.125%) and prednisolone phosphate (Inflamase); dexamethasone (Maxidex 0.1%); fluorometholone (FML 0.1%, FML Forte 0.25%); and loteprednol etabonate (Lotemax 0.5%, Alrex 0.2%). Acetate/alcohol suspensions penetrate the cornea better than phosphate solutions." },
  { id: 54, question: "Give examples of combined steroid + antibiotic eye drops.", answer: "Tobradex (0.1% dexamethasone + 0.3% tobramycin), Zylet (0.5% loteprednol + 0.3% tobramycin), Maxitrol (dexamethasone + neomycin + polymyxin B), and Vigadexa (dexamethasone + moxifloxacin) — used for inflammatory conditions and after surgery." },
  { id: 55, question: "Name the ophthalmic NSAIDs and their uses.", answer: "Ketorolac (Acular 0.5%), nepafenac (Nevanac 0.1%), diclofenac (Voltaren), bromfenac (Xibrom), flurbiprofen (Ocufen), and indomethacin. They inhibit cyclooxygenase and are used for post-operative pain/inflammation and cystoid macular edema." },
  { id: 56, question: "What are the goals of ocular allergy treatment?", answer: "Relieve signs and symptoms promptly and effectively with minimal side effects; prevent further exacerbation and allow symptom-free living with the least drug use; and prevent serious complications of the disease." },
];

const CORNEA_MEDS_MCQ: MCQ[] = [
  { id: 1, question: "TFOS DEWS III lists all of the following as etiological in dry eye EXCEPT:", options: ["Tear film instability", "Hyperosmolarity", "Elevated intraocular pressure", "Neurosensory abnormalities"], correct: 2, explanation: "The DEWS III definition names tear film instability, hyperosmolarity, ocular surface inflammation/damage, and neurosensory abnormalities — not IOP." },
  { id: 2, question: "Which is a Step 1 (baseline) intervention for dry eye?", options: ["Autologous serum eye drops", "Punctal occlusion", "Low-viscosity lubrication and lid hygiene", "Amniotic membrane transplant"], correct: 2, explanation: "Step 1 is education/lifestyle, stopping offending meds, low-viscosity lubrication, and lid hygiene/warm compresses. The others are later steps." },
  { id: 3, question: "The main drawback of high-viscosity artificial tears is:", options: ["Corneal toxicity", "Transient blurring and debris on the eyelids", "Raised intraocular pressure", "Pupillary dilation"], correct: 1, explanation: "Higher viscosity improves retention but transiently blurs vision and can leave debris on the lids." },
  { id: 4, question: "Which demulcent is noted for promoting corneal epithelial healing?", options: ["Polyvinyl alcohol", "Carboxymethylcellulose (CMC)", "Dextran 70", "Propylene glycol"], correct: 1, explanation: "CMC is a high-molecular-weight cellulose derivative that can promote epithelial healing." },
  { id: 5, question: "Cationic oil-in-water nanoemulsion tears improve retention because:", options: ["Negatively charged droplets bind the cornea", "Positively charged oil droplets are attracted to negatively charged surface mucin", "They increase tear osmolarity", "They lower tear pH"], correct: 1, explanation: "Electrostatic attraction between the positively charged oil nanodroplets and negatively charged ocular surface mucin improves spreading and retention." },
  { id: 6, question: "An ointment (emollient) tear is most appropriate:", options: ["As daytime first-line for mild dry eye", "At night or for exposure keratopathy", "Immediately before driving", "For daytime use in contact lens wearers"], correct: 1, explanation: "Ointments blur vision, so they are used at night, for severe dry eye, and for exposure keratopathy." },
  { id: 7, question: "Compared with 0.3% HPMC or 1.4% PVA, 0.2% hyaluronic acid has:", options: ["Shorter residence time", "Significantly longer ocular surface residence time", "Higher preservative content", "No mucoadhesive property"], correct: 1, explanation: "0.2% HA has significantly longer ocular surface residence time than 0.3% HPMC or 1.4% PVA." },
  { id: 8, question: "Normal tear film osmolarity is approximately:", options: ["150–200 mOsm/L", "296–308 mOsm/L", "400–450 mOsm/L", "500 mOsm/L"], correct: 1, explanation: "Normal tear osmolarity is 296–308 mOsm/L; dry eye produces hyperosmolarity." },
  { id: 9, question: "Osmoprotectants (e.g., trehalose, betaine, L-carnitine) act by:", options: ["Raising tear osmolarity", "Protecting cells from osmotic stress without disturbing metabolism", "Blocking histamine", "Constricting conjunctival vessels"], correct: 1, explanation: "Osmoprotectants balance osmotic pressure without disturbing cell metabolism, reducing apoptosis, inflammatory cytokines, and corneal staining." },
  { id: 10, question: "Which preservative is a detergent-type with high ocular surface toxicity?", options: ["Sodium perborate", "Stabilized oxychloro complex (Purite)", "Benzalkonium chloride (BAK)", "Genaqua"], correct: 2, explanation: "BAK is a detergent preservative with high ocular toxicity; sodium perborate and SOC/Purite are gentler oxidizing agents." },
  { id: 11, question: "Preserved eye drops should generally be limited to:", options: ["Once daily", "≤ 4 times per day", "Unlimited frequency", "Night-time use only"], correct: 1, explanation: "Preserved drops used >4×/day long-term cause preservative sensitivity/toxicity; ≤4×/day is advised, otherwise switch to preservative-free." },
  { id: 12, question: "Which electrolytes are considered most critical in artificial tears?", options: ["Na and Cl", "Ca and Mg", "K and HCO3", "Fe and Cu"], correct: 2, explanation: "K (maintains corneal thickness) and HCO3 (mucin adhesion, recovery, pH) are the most critical electrolytes in artificial tears." },
  { id: 13, question: "Before prescribing autologous serum eye drops, which is required?", options: ["IOP measurement only", "Serologic testing for HIV, hepatitis, and syphilis", "Corneal topography", "Genetic testing"], correct: 1, explanation: "Blood-derived products require serology (HIV, hepatitis, syphilis) before preparation." },
  { id: 14, question: "Which statement about topical pilocarpine is correct?", options: ["It is the most effective topical secretagogue", "It has no secretagogue effect", "It stimulates P2Y2 receptors", "It is first-line for MGD"], correct: 1, explanation: "Only oral cholinergic agonists act as secretagogues; topical pilocarpine has no secretagogue effect." },
  { id: 15, question: "Oral pilocarpine is contraindicated in:", options: ["Hypertension only", "COPD, severe kidney disease, and heart disease", "Diabetes mellitus", "Migraine"], correct: 1, explanation: "Cholinergic stimulation is dangerous in COPD, severe renal disease, and heart disease." },
  { id: 16, question: "Diquafosol relieves dry eye by:", options: ["Blocking IL-2", "Stimulating the P2Y2 receptor to increase water and mucin secretion", "Inhibiting MMPs", "Constricting conjunctival vessels"], correct: 1, explanation: "Diquafosol is a P2Y2 purinergic agonist increasing aqueous and mucin secretion (effect in ~2–4 weeks)." },
  { id: 17, question: "Rebamipide improves the tear film by:", options: ["Reducing goblet cells", "Promoting mucin-like glycoproteins (MUC1, MUC4, MUC16)", "Blocking H1 receptors", "Raising tear osmolarity"], correct: 1, explanation: "Rebamipide up-regulates mucin-like glycoproteins (MUC1/4/16), improving the mucin layer." },
  { id: 18, question: "Lifitegrast reduces ocular surface inflammation by:", options: ["Blocking the LFA-1/ICAM-1 interaction to inhibit T-cell migration", "Stabilizing mast cells", "Antagonizing H1 receptors", "Inhibiting cyclooxygenase"], correct: 0, explanation: "Lifitegrast binds LFA-1 and blocks LFA-1/ICAM-1 binding; ICAM-1 is overexpressed on the ocular surface in dry eye." },
  { id: 19, question: "Doxycycline benefits MGD partly because it:", options: ["Increases bacterial lipase activity", "Inhibits collagenase, phospholipase A2, and MMPs", "Stimulates tear secretion via P2Y2", "Constricts conjunctival vessels"], correct: 1, explanation: "Beyond its antibacterial effect (reducing lipolytic enzymes), doxycycline inhibits collagenase, phospholipase A2, and MMPs." },
  { id: 20, question: "Doxycycline for MGD is contraindicated in:", options: ["Elderly patients", "Pregnancy and breastfeeding", "Diabetics", "Contact lens wearers"], correct: 1, explanation: "Tetracyclines are contraindicated in pregnancy and breastfeeding (and drug allergy)." },
  { id: 21, question: "Seasonal and perennial allergic conjunctivitis are mediated by:", options: ["Type IV (T-cell) delayed hypersensitivity", "Type I (IgE-mediated) hypersensitivity", "Type II cytotoxic reaction", "Type III immune-complex reaction"], correct: 1, explanation: "SAC and PAC are Type I, IgE-mediated mast cell degranulation. VKC and AKC involve Type IV." },
  { id: 22, question: "Vernal and atopic keratoconjunctivitis primarily involve:", options: ["Type I IgE-mediated degranulation only", "Type IV T-cell–mediated delayed hypersensitivity", "Complement-mediated cell lysis", "Neutrophilic vasculitis"], correct: 1, explanation: "VKC and AKC are chronic, T-cell–mediated (Type IV) forms of ocular allergy." },
  { id: 23, question: "Histamine acting on H1 receptors in the eye causes:", options: ["Vasoconstriction", "Increased vascular permeability → itching, redness, tearing, edema", "Suppression of mucous production", "Pupillary constriction"], correct: 1, explanation: "H1 stimulation raises vascular permeability, producing itching, redness, tearing, and edema; H2 causes vasodilation and mucous production." },
  { id: 24, question: "A key limitation of topical antihistamine eye drops (levocabastine, emedastine) is:", options: ["Slow onset over weeks", "Short duration of action (~3–4 hours)", "Raised intraocular pressure", "They stabilize mast cells only"], correct: 1, explanation: "Topical antihistamines act fast but only last ~3–4 hours, so they are often combined with a vasoconstrictor." },
  { id: 25, question: "A common problem with topical vasoconstrictor (decongestant) eye drops is:", options: ["Rebound congestion with chronic use", "Delayed onset over weeks", "Mast cell stabilization", "Lowering of IOP"], correct: 0, explanation: "Adrenergic vasoconstrictors cause rebound congestion; cautions include cardiovascular disease and angle-closure glaucoma." },
  { id: 26, question: "Mast cell stabilizers are best used:", options: ["For immediate relief of acute itching", "For prevention, because onset is delayed ~2 weeks", "As monotherapy for anaphylaxis", "To constrict conjunctival vessels"], correct: 1, explanation: "They block calcium influx and prevent degranulation, but the delayed (~2-week) onset makes them prophylactic agents." },
  { id: 27, question: "Olopatadine and ketotifen are examples of:", options: ["Pure vasoconstrictors", "Dual-acting antihistamine / mast cell stabilizers", "Calcineurin inhibitors", "NSAIDs"], correct: 1, explanation: "These multiple-action agents block H1 and stabilize mast cells (and affect eosinophils), giving both fast and sustained relief." },
  { id: 28, question: "How do corticosteroids differ from NSAIDs in the arachidonic acid pathway?", options: ["Steroids block only cyclooxygenase", "Steroids inhibit phospholipase A2 (upstream), blocking both COX and lipoxygenase; NSAIDs block only COX", "NSAIDs block phospholipase A2", "Both block only lipoxygenase"], correct: 1, explanation: "Steroids act upstream at phospholipase A2, suppressing both prostaglandins and leukotrienes; NSAIDs inhibit only COX." },
  { id: 29, question: "Which topical steroid has the LOWEST tendency to raise intraocular pressure?", options: ["0.1% Dexamethasone", "1% Prednisolone acetate", "Loteprednol or fluorometholone", "They all raise IOP equally"], correct: 2, explanation: "Loteprednol and fluorometholone have low IOP-raising potential; 0.1% dexamethasone raises IOP the most." },
  { id: 30, question: "Topical cyclosporine and tacrolimus share which mechanism/class?", options: ["Antihistamines", "Calcineurin inhibitors that block T-cell activation and cytokine production", "Mast cell stabilizers", "Adrenergic agonists"], correct: 1, explanation: "Both are calcineurin inhibitors; tacrolimus (binds FK-binding protein) is roughly 100× more potent than cyclosporine (binds cyclophilin)." },
  { id: 31, question: "In artificial tears, Dextran 70 is best described as:", options: ["A low-molecular-weight electrolyte", "A 70-kDa high-molecular-weight polysaccharide that enhances demulcent quality when combined with another demulcent", "A detergent preservative", "A cationic oil"], correct: 1, explanation: "Dextran 70 is a 70-kDa polysaccharide used with another demulcent (e.g., 0.3% HPMC + 0.1% dextran 70)." },
  { id: 32, question: "Liquid polyol demulcents (propylene glycol, PEG, glycerin) characteristically:", options: ["Have higher viscosity than cellulose", "Mimic mucin/glycocalyx adhesion but have lower viscosity than cellulose", "Are oil-based emulsions", "Contain benzalkonium chloride"], correct: 1, explanation: "Polyols are sugar-like hydrogenated carbohydrates that mimic mucin adhesion; their viscosity is lower than cellulose derivatives." },
  { id: 33, question: "A macroemulsion artificial tear (droplet > 100 nm) appears:", options: ["Perfectly clear", "Cloudy, because the droplets scatter light", "Bright yellow", "Effervescent"], correct: 1, explanation: "Larger droplets scatter light and look cloudy; nanoemulsions (10–100 nm) and microemulsions (<10 nm) are clearer." },
  { id: 34, question: "Carbomer is chemically:", options: ["A cellulose derivative", "Polyacrylic acid (high molecular weight) with retention longer than demulcents but less than petroleum", "A polysaccharide like dextran", "An oil"], correct: 1, explanation: "Carbomer is high-MW polyacrylic acid; its retention time is between demulcents and petroleum ointments." },
  { id: 35, question: "Compared with demulcents, emollient ointments have:", options: ["Lower viscosity", "Higher viscosity, giving longer residence but blurring vision and drying the lashes", "Identical properties to saline", "An osmotic mechanism"], correct: 1, explanation: "Petroleum/lanolin/mineral-oil ointments are more viscous and longer-lasting than demulcents but blur vision — hence night-time/severe use." },
  { id: 36, question: "Natural tear film pH is approximately:", options: ["5.0–5.5", "6.5–7.6", "8.0–8.5", "4.5"], correct: 1, explanation: "Tear pH is ~6.5–7.6; a mismatch between drop pH and tear pH causes a stinging sensation." },
  { id: 37, question: "Oxidizing-type preservatives (sodium perborate, stabilized oxychloro complex) are preferred because they:", options: ["Have high ocular toxicity", "Are gentle on human tissue, breaking down into water, oxygen, and salts", "Are strong detergents", "Cannot prevent contamination"], correct: 1, explanation: "Oxidizing agents disrupt microbial metabolism but degrade to innocuous products (e.g., sodium perborate → O2 + H2O), making them gentle." },
  { id: 38, question: "Autologous serum eye drops for severe ocular surface disease are typically used at what concentration?", options: ["1–5%", "20–100%", "0.1%", "200%"], correct: 1, explanation: "Serum is diluted to 20–100% and is biologically similar to human tears (nutrients, fibronectin, growth factors)." },
  { id: 39, question: "A recognized disadvantage of autologous serum eye drops is:", options: ["They must be refrigerated and carry blood-borne infection / contamination risk", "They contain no growth factors", "They reliably raise IOP", "They contain benzalkonium chloride"], correct: 0, explanation: "Serum drops need refrigeration, risk blood-borne infection and bacterial contamination, are expensive, and have limited availability." },
  { id: 40, question: "Which oral secretagogue is a mucolytic rather than a cholinergic agonist?", options: ["Pilocarpine", "Carbachol", "Bromhexine", "Bethanechol"], correct: 2, explanation: "Bromhexine (and ambroxol) are mucolytic oral secretagogues; pilocarpine, carbachol, and bethanechol are cholinergic agonists." },
  { id: 41, question: "Typical side effects of oral pilocarpine include:", options: ["Excessive sweating, nausea/vomiting, and headache", "Constipation and dry mouth", "Hypertension and mydriasis", "Photosensitivity"], correct: 0, explanation: "As a cholinergic agonist, pilocarpine causes sweating, GI upset, and headache; it is contraindicated in COPD, severe renal disease, and heart disease." },
  { id: 42, question: "Rebamipide ophthalmic suspension is dosed and licensed as:", options: ["2% QID, approved in Japan", "5% twice daily, US-approved", "An oral tablet only", "A once-yearly injection"], correct: 0, explanation: "Rebamipide (Mucosta) 2% QID, approved in Japan, up-regulates MUC1/4/16 mucin-like glycoproteins." },
  { id: 43, question: "Cyclosporine A for dry eye is formulated as:", options: ["An aqueous solution", "An oil-based emulsion", "A petroleum ointment", "A carbomer gel"], correct: 1, explanation: "Cyclosporine is an oil-based emulsion (0.05% bid / 0.1% OD) that inhibits IL-2–driven T-cell activation." },
  { id: 44, question: "A recognized side effect of lifitegrast is:", options: ["Raised intraocular pressure", "Decreased visual acuity and irritation", "Posterior subcapsular cataract", "Mydriasis"], correct: 1, explanation: "Lifitegrast commonly causes transient decreased VA and irritation/dysgeusia — not IOP rise or cataract." },
  { id: 45, question: "Oral azithromycin for MGD is often given as:", options: ["A single 1 g dose", "A pulse of 500 mg for 3 days, repeated weekly for 3 weeks", "100 mg daily for a year", "IV therapy only"], correct: 1, explanation: "A common (if debated) MGD regimen is pulsed oral azithromycin 500 mg × 3 days weekly × 3; topical azithromycin is 1–1.5%." },
  { id: 46, question: "Which is a mechanical/surgical option to retain tears in dry eye?", options: ["Punctal occlusion", "LASIK", "Trabeculectomy", "Vitrectomy"], correct: 0, explanation: "Punctal occlusion (temporary or permanent) retains tears; tarsorrhaphy, moisture chamber spectacles, and contact lenses are other options." },
  { id: 47, question: "In the early allergic response, itching from nerve stimulation appears within about:", options: ["3–5 minutes", "1 hour", "24 hours", "1 week"], correct: 0, explanation: "Histamine-driven nerve stimulation causes itching within ~3–5 minutes; vasodilation/redness follows at ~5–10 minutes." },
  { id: 48, question: "The late-phase allergic reaction is characterized by:", options: ["Immediate mast cell degranulation only", "Influx of eosinophils, neutrophils, basophils, and T-lymphocytes with increased ICAM/VCAM expression", "Vasoconstriction and pallor", "Absence of inflammation"], correct: 1, explanation: "The late phase recruits inflammatory cells to the conjunctival mucosa with adhesion-molecule upregulation and eosinophil-mediated tissue damage." },
  { id: 49, question: "In the anti-allergy scheme, mast cell stabilizers act to:", options: ["Block histamine receptors", "Prevent mediator release", "Constrict conjunctival vessels", "Dilute allergens"], correct: 1, explanation: "Mast cell stabilizers prevent mediator (e.g., histamine) release; antihistamines/vasoconstrictors block mediators; artificial tears dilute them." },
  { id: 50, question: "Topical antihistamine levocabastine (Livostin) carries an age caution in children under:", options: ["12 years", "6 months", "18 years", "No age caution exists"], correct: 0, explanation: "Levocabastine is cautioned in children <12 years; emedastine (Emadine) is cautioned <3 years and with contact lens use." },
  { id: 51, question: "The mechanism of topical vasoconstrictor (decongestant) drops in allergy is:", options: ["H1-receptor antagonism", "Adrenergic agonism causing conjunctival vessel constriction", "Mast cell stabilization", "Calcineurin inhibition"], correct: 1, explanation: "Vasoconstrictors are adrenergic agonists; side effects include mydriasis, epithelial erosion, and rebound congestion." },
  { id: 52, question: "Combined antihistamine/vasoconstrictor eye drops are specifically cautioned in patients taking:", options: ["Monoamine oxidase inhibitors (also caution in angle-closure)", "Vitamin C", "Oral antibiotics", "Statins"], correct: 0, explanation: "The decongestant component interacts with MAO inhibitors and can precipitate angle closure." },
  { id: 53, question: "Which drug is a mast cell stabilizer commonly used in vernal keratoconjunctivitis?", options: ["Olopatadine", "Cromolyn sodium (or lodoxamide / pemirolast)", "Naphazoline", "Prednisolone acetate"], correct: 1, explanation: "Cromolyn, lodoxamide (Alomide), and pemirolast (Pemirox) are mast cell stabilizers used in VKC (olopatadine is dual-acting)." },
  { id: 54, question: "Olopatadine 0.2% (Pataday) dosing is:", options: ["Once daily (OD)", "Every hour", "Once weekly", "Six times daily"], correct: 0, explanation: "Pataday (olopatadine 0.2%) is once daily; Patanol (olopatadine 0.1%) is twice daily." },
  { id: 55, question: "Which topical steroid preparation tends to raise intraocular pressure the MOST?", options: ["Fluorometholone", "Loteprednol", "0.1% dexamethasone", "Prednisolone 0.125%"], correct: 2, explanation: "0.1% dexamethasone has the greatest IOP-raising potential; fluorometholone and loteprednol have low potential." },
  { id: 56, question: "Tobradex is a fixed combination of:", options: ["Loteprednol + tobramycin", "Dexamethasone + tobramycin", "Dexamethasone + neomycin + polymyxin B", "Prednisolone + ofloxacin"], correct: 1, explanation: "Tobradex = 0.1% dexamethasone + 0.3% tobramycin. (Zylet = loteprednol + tobramycin; Maxitrol = dexamethasone + neomycin + polymyxin B.)" },
  { id: 57, question: "Ophthalmic NSAIDs (e.g., ketorolac, nepafenac) act by:", options: ["Inhibiting phospholipase A2", "Inhibiting only cyclooxygenase", "Stabilizing mast cells", "Blocking H1 receptors"], correct: 1, explanation: "NSAIDs inhibit only COX (unlike steroids, which act upstream at phospholipase A2); used for post-op pain/inflammation and CME." },
  { id: 58, question: "Tacrolimus differs from cyclosporine in that it:", options: ["Is a corticosteroid", "Binds FK-binding protein, is ~100× more potent, and is derived from Streptomyces tsukubaensis", "Blocks histamine receptors", "Has no immunosuppressive effect"], correct: 1, explanation: "Tacrolimus (FK506) binds FK-binding protein and is roughly 100× more potent than cyclosporine, which binds cyclophilin." },
  { id: 59, question: "Topical cyclosporine is best characterized as:", options: ["First-line primary therapy for all dry eye", "Not a primary therapy — reserved for steroid non-responders/responders/complications, with slow (1–4 week) onset", "A rapid-acting decongestant", "A preservative"], correct: 1, explanation: "Cyclosporine is second-line, slow in onset (1–4 weeks), and needs long-term use; caution in children and pregnancy." },
  { id: 60, question: "Which correctly matches an anti-allergy drug class to its mechanism?", options: ["Steroid → mast cell stabilization", "Artificial tears → dilution of allergens and mediators", "Antihistamine → arachidonic acid inhibition", "Immunomodulator → vasoconstriction"], correct: 1, explanation: "Artificial tears dilute allergens/mediators; steroids/NSAIDs act on the arachidonic acid pathway; antihistamines block mediators; immunomodulators are anti-inflammatory." },
];

// ── Basic Science · BCSC Section 2 · Part 1 · Pharmacokinetics & Ocular Drug Delivery
// Source: BCSC Section 2 (Fundamentals & Principles), Ch. 15 (Pharmacologic Principles).
const BASIC_PK_RECALL: RecallQ[] = [
  { id: 1, question: "List the routes of ophthalmic drug delivery.", answer: "Topical (eyedrops), oral/systemic, intravitreal, intracameral, subconjunctival, sub-Tenon, retrobulbar, and posterior juxtascleral." },
  { id: 2, question: "Why is topical the most common route, and what is the primary intraocular pathway?", answer: "Topical eyedrops maximize anterior segment concentration while minimizing systemic toxicity. The transcorneal route is the primary pathway for a medication to reach intraocular concentrations." },
  { id: 3, question: "What is the topical bioavailability, and how much of an eyedrop is retained?", answer: "Topical bioavailability is < 5%. Of a 50-μL drop, the tear lake only rises from ~7 to ~10 μL, so at most ~20% is retained; ocular tissues ultimately absorb only 1–10% of the applied medication." },
  { id: 4, question: "Why is there no benefit to instilling two drops of the same drug at once?", answer: "The tear lake holds only ~10 μL, so a second immediate drop mostly overflows and washes out the first. Patients should wait at least 5 minutes between drops." },
  { id: 5, question: "What is the tear turnover rate, and how quickly is drug lost?", answer: "Tear turnover is ~16%/minute (faster with reflex tearing). Only ~10% of the original dose remains at 4 minutes and ~3.4% at 10 minutes after instillation." },
  { id: 6, question: "Define the residence time of a topical medication.", answer: "The amount of time a drug remains in the tear reservoir and tear film. It is affected by drug formulation, the timing of subsequent drops, tear production, and drainage." },
  { id: 7, question: "How do nasolacrimal occlusion and eyelid closure improve topical therapy?", answer: "Both block the nasolacrimal pump, increasing residence time and corneal absorption while decreasing systemic absorption and toxicity. Apply digital pressure at the medial canthus, or close the eyes for 5 minutes, after each drop." },
  { id: 8, question: "How does topical drug reach the systemic circulation, and what is the % → mg/mL trick?", answer: "Absorption across the nasal mucosa bypasses first-pass hepatic metabolism, so a drop can give a meaningful systemic dose. Concentration: add a zero to the percentage for mg/mL (1% = 10 mg/mL); there are ~20 drops per mL." },
  { id: 9, question: "How fast does the nasolacrimal system remove drug relative to ocular absorption?", answer: "The nasolacrimal system can remove topically applied medication about 100× faster than the ocular surface can absorb it." },
  { id: 10, question: "What factors determine corneal drug penetration?", answer: "Concentration/solubility in the vehicle, vehicle viscosity, lipid solubility, pH, ionic and steric form, molecular size, surfactants, reflex tearing, and drug binding." },
  { id: 11, question: "Distinguish a solution from a suspension, with examples.", answer: "A solution requires adequate aqueous solubility. A suspension disperses a poorly soluble drug in a medium — it must be shaken before use and may be more irritating. Examples of suspensions: prednisolone acetate and brinzolamide." },
  { id: 12, question: "What are the characteristics of an emulsion, with examples?", answer: "A mixture of two immiscible liquids stabilized by an emulsifier; it gives increased contact time and bioavailability, looks cloudy but needs no shaking, and is more viscous (foreign-body sensation). Examples: difluprednate and cyclosporine." },
  { id: 13, question: "State the key concentration conversions.", answer: "1% = 1 g/100 mL = 10 mg/mL. A 1:1000 dilution = 1 mg/mL = 0.1%." },
  { id: 14, question: "What is the role of viscosity agents, and the optimal viscosity?", answer: "Methylcellulose, PVA, and gellan/xanthan gum increase retention in the inferior cul-de-sac (allowing once-daily timolol). Optimal viscosity is ~12–15 cP; above 15 cP causes irritation, reflex blinking, stickiness, and blurring without more absorption." },
  { id: 15, question: "Describe the lipid-solubility barriers of the cornea.", answer: "The corneal epithelium (lipophilic) and endothelium (lipophilic) sandwich a hydrophilic stroma, so an ideal drug is amphipathic — but lipid solubility matters more than water solubility for penetration. Tight junctions and desmosomes limit paracellular passage." },
  { id: 16, question: "How does conjunctival permeability compare with the cornea?", answer: "The conjunctiva is ~20× more permeable to small water-soluble molecules than the cornea, because of its larger paracellular spaces — but rapid clearance limits its contribution to intraocular drug levels." },
  { id: 17, question: "How do pH and ionization affect penetration?", answer: "Nonionized (uncharged) molecules are more lipophilic and cross membranes more readily; raising the pH increases the uncharged fraction and thus penetration. Many ophthalmic drugs are alkaloids (weak bases), most stable at acidic pH. Tear pH is ~7.4." },
  { id: 18, question: "How does scleral charge affect drug passage?", answer: "The scleral extracellular matrix is negatively charged, which limits passage of positively charged molecules — even though the sclera itself is hydrophilic." },
  { id: 19, question: "How does molecular size influence penetration?", answer: "Small (< 450 Da) lipophilic molecules favor transcellular pathways; larger hydrophilic molecules favor paracellular pathways. Transscleral absorption depends more on molecular size than on lipophilicity." },
  { id: 20, question: "How do surfactant preservatives affect drug penetration?", answer: "Surface-active preservatives (e.g., benzalkonium chloride) disrupt corneal epithelial cell membranes, reducing the barrier and increasing penetration — 0.1% carbachol + 0.03% BAK gives the same miosis as 2% carbachol alone. Corneal abrasion similarly increases penetration." },
  { id: 21, question: "What triggers reflex tearing, and why does it matter?", answer: "Non-isotonic solutions, nonphysiologic pH, or irritants trigger reflex tearing that washes out the tear-lake drug reservoir and shortens corneal contact time." },
  { id: 22, question: "How does drug binding affect pharmacokinetics? Give an example.", answer: "Tear/ocular-surface proteins and melanin bind drugs, creating a slow-release reservoir or making drug unavailable — altering onset, peak, and duration, and sometimes causing toxicity after discontinuation. Example: chloroquine/hydroxychloroquine retinal toxicity that progresses even after the drug is stopped." },
  { id: 23, question: "What is the composition of ophthalmic ointments, and which drugs benefit?", answer: "Petrolatum + mineral oil (mineral oil lets it melt at body temperature). Water-soluble drugs sit as microcrystals with slow release. Fluorometholone, chloramphenicol, and tetracycline reach higher aqueous levels as an ointment than as drops." },
  { id: 24, question: "What barriers do periocular injections bypass, and what routes reach the posterior segment?", answer: "Subconjunctival/sub-Tenon injections bypass the conjunctival and corneal epithelial barriers. Drug can reach the posterior segment transsclerally into the uvea, via periocular vessels into the systemic circulation, or transcorneally into the anterior segment." },
  { id: 25, question: "What is the advantage of a sub-Tenon injection?", answer: "The Tenon capsule is a lipophilic barrier; a hydrophilic drug placed in the sub-Tenon space penetrates intraocular tissue faster than a topical application — useful for low-lipid-solubility drugs like penicillin, and for delivering anesthesia." },
  { id: 26, question: "What are retrobulbar/peribulbar and other local injections used for?", answer: "Retrobulbar/peribulbar injections act at the injection site (not intraocular) — mainly for anesthesia. Other local injectables include botulinum toxin (blepharospasm, hemifacial spasm, strabismus) and retrobulbar alcohol (chronic pain in blind eyes)." },
  { id: 27, question: "What are the two types of intraocular injection and their risks?", answer: "Intracameral (into the anterior chamber) and intravitreal (into the vitreous). Risks include transient ocular hypertension and inflammation/infection; preservative-free preparations and strict asepsis are essential." },
  { id: 28, question: "Which antibiotics are used intracamerally for endophthalmitis prophylaxis?", answer: "Given at the end of cataract surgery: cefuroxime (broad-spectrum cephalosporin), vancomycin (covers MRSA but risks resistance and hemorrhagic occlusive retinal vasculitis), or diluted preservative-free moxifloxacin (4th-generation fluoroquinolone). All must be preservative-free." },
  { id: 29, question: "Why must intracameral medications such as lidocaine be preservative-free?", answer: "Preservatives (or dosing errors) with intracameral medications can cause toxic anterior segment syndrome (TASS)." },
  { id: 30, question: "What are the main categories of intravitreally delivered drugs?", answer: "Anti-VEGF agents (CNV, diabetic retinopathy, DME, RVO), corticosteroids (triamcinolone; sustained-release dexamethasone and fluocinolone implants), antivirals (foscarnet, ganciclovir for CMV), and surgical adjuncts (silicone oil, intraocular gases, perfluorocarbon)." },
  { id: 31, question: "How are intravitreal drugs cleared, and how does that affect half-life?", answer: "Hydrophilic, high-molecular-weight drugs exit anteriorly via aqueous humor outflow (longer half-life); lipophilic, low-molecular-weight drugs exit posteriorly across the retina/RPE into the choroid (shorter half-life)." },
  { id: 32, question: "Describe the blood–ocular barriers to systemically administered drugs.", answer: "The retinal vascular endothelium is nonfenestrated with tight junctions (like the brain). The choroid and ciliary body have fenestrated vessels but are sequestered by the RPE tight junctions and the nonpigmented ciliary epithelium, respectively." },
  { id: 33, question: "What determines how well a systemic drug penetrates the eye?", answer: "Higher lipid solubility (chloramphenicol penetrates ~20× better than penicillin) and lower plasma-protein binding — only the unbound fraction crosses the barrier, so >90%-bound sulfonamides penetrate poorly. Bolus dosing exceeds protein binding, giving higher intraocular peaks than a slow drip." },
  { id: 34, question: "Which oral antifungals penetrate the eye well, and give a sustained-release oral example.", answer: "Fluconazole and voriconazole have high oral bioavailability and good intraocular levels (used for endogenous fungal endophthalmitis). Sustained-release acetazolamide extends IOP lowering from ~10 to ~20 hours." },
  { id: 35, question: "Why does an inflamed eye show better drug penetration?", answer: "Inflammation disrupts the blood–aqueous and blood–retina barriers (demonstrated by fluorescein leakage during angiography), increasing intraocular drug penetration compared with a healthy eye." },
  { id: 36, question: "What are prodrugs, and give ophthalmic examples.", answer: "Inactive derivatives activated by ocular enzymes (esterases/amidases), with improved lipid solubility/penetration. Prostaglandin analogues (latanoprost, travoprost, tafluprost) are hydrolyzed to active FP-receptor agonists; valacyclovir → acyclovir; famciclovir → penciclovir." },
  { id: 37, question: "What are ocular inserts, and how do soluble and insoluble types differ?", answer: "Sterile drug-impregnated devices placed in the conjunctival fornix for steady-state delivery. Soluble inserts dissolve via interaction with the tear film (no removal needed); insoluble inserts give more constant release but must be removed. The first was a pilocarpine insert (40 μg/hr, 1970s)." },
  { id: 38, question: "Describe intraocular sustained-release implants.", answer: "The first was the ganciclovir intravitreal implant for CMV retinitis (discontinued 2015). Current FDA-approved implants: fluocinolone acetonide intravitreal implants, a biodegradable 0.7-mg dexamethasone intravitreal implant, and a biodegradable 10-µg bimatoprost intracameral implant." },
  { id: 39, question: "Describe punctal-plug drug delivery.", answer: "The only FDA-approved system is a resorbable intracanalicular dexamethasone insert (0.4 mg over 30 days) for postoperative inflammation/pain. Advantages: no preservative exposure, lower dose, controlled release; limitations: low dose capacity, irritation, and spontaneous extrusion." },
  { id: 40, question: "Summarize liposomes, nanotechnology, and iontophoresis for ocular delivery.", answer: "Liposomes are phospholipid-bilayer microspheres (water-soluble drug in the aqueous core, hydrophobic drug in the bilayer; limited by short shelf life and low loading). Nanotechnology uses nanoparticles/micelles/emulsions (e.g., 0.09% cyclosporine nanomicelle for dry eye). Iontophoresis uses an electrical current to drive charged molecules into tissue for high local concentration (limited by discomfort/tissue damage)." },
  { id: 41, question: "Distinguish pharmacogenetics from pharmacogenomics.", answer: "Pharmacogenetics studies how variation in single genes affects drug efficacy/toxicity; pharmacogenomics considers many genes. It divides into variations affecting drug metabolism (pharmacokinetics) and drug targets (pharmacodynamics). Ophthalmic examples (AMD, glaucoma — e.g., PTGFR SNPs and latanoprost response) exist but are conflicting." },
  { id: 42, question: "How does aging alter drug pharmacokinetics?", answer: "Older patients have less lean body mass, less body water and albumin, and more adipose tissue, plus declining renal function and variable hepatic changes. These extend the half-life of most systemic drugs and potentiate effects, so drug effects and toxicity can increase even when the dose is reduced." },
];

const BASIC_PK_MCQ: MCQ[] = [
  { id: 1, question: "The primary pathway by which a topically applied medication reaches intraocular concentrations is the:", options: ["Transconjunctival route", "Transcorneal route", "Transscleral route", "Systemic circulation"], correct: 1, explanation: "The transcorneal route is the main pathway for topical drugs to achieve intraocular levels." },
  { id: 2, question: "At most, approximately what percentage of a topically instilled eyedrop is retained by the eye?", options: ["~20%", "~50%", "~80%", "~100%"], correct: 0, explanation: "The tear lake only rises from ~7 to ~10 μL, so at most ~20% of a 50-μL drop is retained." },
  { id: 3, question: "The tear lake of a blinking, upright eye holds about:", options: ["3 μL", "10 μL", "30 μL", "50 μL"], correct: 1, explanation: "The tear lake holds ~10 μL, which is why a second simultaneous drop overflows." },
  { id: 4, question: "Tear turnover in the undisturbed eye is approximately:", options: ["1%/min", "16%/min", "50%/min", "90%/min"], correct: 1, explanation: "Tear turnover is ~16%/min (faster with reflex tearing), rapidly clearing instilled drug." },
  { id: 5, question: "Which maneuver increases ocular drug residence time while decreasing systemic absorption?", options: ["Instilling two drops at once", "Nasolacrimal occlusion or 5-minute eyelid closure", "Blinking rapidly after instillation", "Using a less viscous vehicle"], correct: 1, explanation: "Both block the nasolacrimal pump, raising ocular absorption and lowering systemic absorption/toxicity." },
  { id: 6, question: "A 1% ophthalmic solution contains how much drug per mL?", options: ["1 mg/mL", "10 mg/mL", "100 mg/mL", "0.1 mg/mL"], correct: 1, explanation: "1% = 1 g/100 mL = 10 mg/mL (add a zero to the percentage)." },
  { id: 7, question: "A 1:1000 solution is equivalent to:", options: ["1%", "0.1%", "0.01%", "10%"], correct: 1, explanation: "1:1000 = 1 g/1000 mL = 1 mg/mL = 0.1%." },
  { id: 8, question: "Ocular tissues absorb approximately what fraction of a topically applied medication?", options: ["1–10%", "20–30%", "~50%", "~80%"], correct: 0, explanation: "Only about 1–10% of a topical drop is actually absorbed by ocular tissues." },
  { id: 9, question: "Topical drug absorbed via the nasal mucosa is clinically significant because it:", options: ["Undergoes extensive first-pass metabolism", "Bypasses first-pass hepatic metabolism, reaching meaningful blood levels", "Is immediately excreted", "Cannot enter the bloodstream"], correct: 1, explanation: "Nasal mucosal absorption avoids first-pass liver metabolism, so a drop or two can deliver a systemic dose." },
  { id: 10, question: "Prednisolone acetate and brinzolamide are examples of which formulation?", options: ["Solution", "Suspension", "Emulsion", "Ointment"], correct: 1, explanation: "Both are suspensions of poorly soluble drug — they must be shaken before use." },
  { id: 11, question: "Which ophthalmic formulation must be shaken before instillation?", options: ["Solution", "Suspension", "Emulsion", "Gel-forming drop"], correct: 1, explanation: "A suspension must be shaken to redistribute the drug; an emulsion does not require shaking." },
  { id: 12, question: "Difluprednate and cyclosporine are formulated as:", options: ["Suspensions", "Emulsions", "Solutions", "Ointments"], correct: 1, explanation: "Both are topical emulsions (two immiscible liquids with an emulsifier)." },
  { id: 13, question: "An advantage of an emulsion over a solution is:", options: ["It never blurs vision", "Increased contact time and bioavailability", "It requires shaking", "Lower viscosity"], correct: 1, explanation: "Emulsions increase contact time and bioavailability, though they are more viscous (FB sensation)." },
  { id: 14, question: "The optimal viscosity for an ophthalmic vehicle is about:", options: ["1–2 cP", "12–15 cP", "30–40 cP", "> 50 cP"], correct: 1, explanation: "Delivery improves in the 1–15 cP range, optimally 12–15 cP." },
  { id: 15, question: "Viscosity above 15 cP tends to:", options: ["Proportionally increase aqueous drug levels", "Cause irritation, reflex blinking, and blurring without added benefit", "Improve comfort", "Reduce drainage"], correct: 1, explanation: "Above 15 cP there is no proportional gain, and irritation, reflex blinking, stickiness, and blurring occur." },
  { id: 16, question: "The corneal stroma is best characterized as:", options: ["Lipophilic", "Hydrophilic", "Positively charged", "Impermeable"], correct: 1, explanation: "The stroma is hydrophilic, sandwiched between lipophilic epithelium and endothelium." },
  { id: 17, question: "To cross the cornea, an ideal drug should be:", options: ["Purely lipophilic", "Purely hydrophilic", "Amphipathic, with lipid solubility being more important", "Large and charged"], correct: 2, explanation: "The layered cornea favors amphipathic drugs, but lipid solubility is the more important determinant of penetration." },
  { id: 18, question: "Compared with the cornea, the conjunctiva is roughly how much more permeable to small water-soluble molecules?", options: ["Equal", "2×", "20×", "100×"], correct: 2, explanation: "The conjunctiva is ~20× more permeable to small water-soluble molecules due to larger paracellular spaces." },
  { id: 19, question: "Raising the pH of a weak-base drug solution generally:", options: ["Increases the ionized fraction and decreases penetration", "Increases the uncharged, lipid-soluble fraction and increases penetration", "Has no effect on penetration", "Precipitates the drug"], correct: 1, explanation: "A higher pH shifts a weak base toward the uncharged, more lipid-soluble form, improving penetration." },
  { id: 20, question: "The negatively charged scleral extracellular matrix preferentially limits passage of:", options: ["Negatively charged molecules", "Positively charged molecules", "Neutral molecules", "Lipophilic molecules"], correct: 1, explanation: "The negatively charged scleral matrix repels/retards positively charged molecules." },
  { id: 21, question: "For transcellular corneal penetration, molecules are best if they are:", options: ["Large and hydrophilic", "Small (< 450 Da) and lipophilic", "Highly charged", "Heavily protein-bound"], correct: 1, explanation: "Small (<450 Da) lipophilic molecules cross transcellularly; larger hydrophilic ones use paracellular routes." },
  { id: 22, question: "Transscleral absorption is limited more by:", options: ["Lipophilicity", "Molecular size", "pH", "Charge alone"], correct: 1, explanation: "Although the sclera is hydrophilic, transscleral passage depends more on molecular size than lipophilicity." },
  { id: 23, question: "Adding benzalkonium chloride to a drop increases penetration because it:", options: ["Raises the solution pH", "Disrupts corneal epithelial cell membranes, reducing the barrier", "Increases viscosity", "Binds ocular melanin"], correct: 1, explanation: "As a surfactant, BAK disrupts epithelial membranes and lowers the barrier (0.1% carbachol + 0.03% BAK ≈ 2% carbachol)." },
  { id: 24, question: "Retinal toxicity that progresses even after the drug is discontinued is characteristic of:", options: ["Timolol", "Chloroquine / hydroxychloroquine", "Latanoprost", "Acetazolamide"], correct: 1, explanation: "Melanin binding creates a reservoir, so aminoquinoline retinal toxicity can progress after stopping the drug." },
  { id: 25, question: "Ophthalmic ointments are typically composed of:", options: ["Water and glycerin", "Petrolatum and mineral oil", "Hyaluronic acid", "Balanced salt solution"], correct: 1, explanation: "Petrolatum + mineral oil (which melts at body temperature and is a lipid solvent)." },
  { id: 26, question: "Which drugs achieve higher aqueous levels as an ointment than as drops?", options: ["Timolol and pilocarpine", "Fluorometholone, chloramphenicol, and tetracycline", "Latanoprost", "Acetazolamide"], correct: 1, explanation: "These lipophilic-plus-somewhat-water-soluble drugs escape the ointment and reach higher aqueous levels." },
  { id: 27, question: "Sub-Tenon injection is especially useful for drugs that are:", options: ["Highly lipid-soluble", "Low in lipid solubility (e.g., penicillin)", "Gases", "Preserved"], correct: 1, explanation: "Placing a poorly lipid-soluble drug past the Tenon barrier lets it penetrate faster than topical application." },
  { id: 28, question: "Toxic anterior segment syndrome (TASS) after an intracameral medication is most associated with:", options: ["Preservatives or dosing errors", "Preservative-free lidocaine", "Balanced salt solution", "Trypan blue"], correct: 0, explanation: "Preservatives and/or dosing errors with intracameral agents cause TASS — use preservative-free preparations." },
  { id: 29, question: "The most common form of intraocular drug delivery is:", options: ["Intracameral injection", "Intravitreal injection", "Subconjunctival injection", "Retrobulbar injection"], correct: 1, explanation: "Intravitreal injection is the most common intraocular delivery route (anti-VEGF, steroids, etc.)." },
  { id: 30, question: "Intracameral cefuroxime given at the end of cataract surgery is used to:", options: ["Dilate the pupil", "Prevent endophthalmitis", "Stain the anterior capsule", "Lower intraocular pressure"], correct: 1, explanation: "Intracameral antibiotics (cefuroxime, moxifloxacin, vancomycin) reduce postoperative endophthalmitis." },
  { id: 31, question: "A rare but serious complication of intracameral vancomycin is:", options: ["TASS", "Hemorrhagic occlusive retinal vasculitis", "Cataract", "Angle-closure glaucoma"], correct: 1, explanation: "Vancomycin is linked to hemorrhagic occlusive retinal vasculitis (HORV), and its indiscriminate use risks resistance." },
  { id: 32, question: "Which intracameral agent is used to constrict the pupil during intraocular surgery?", options: ["Epinephrine", "Acetylcholine or carbachol", "Phenylephrine", "Tropicamide"], correct: 1, explanation: "Acetylcholine and carbachol are intracameral miotics; epinephrine/phenylephrine are used for dilation." },
  { id: 33, question: "Hydrophilic, high-molecular-weight intravitreal drugs are cleared mainly via:", options: ["The posterior (retina/RPE/choroid) route", "The anterior (aqueous outflow) route, giving a longer half-life", "Nasolacrimal drainage", "Corneal evaporation"], correct: 1, explanation: "Large hydrophilic molecules exit anteriorly with aqueous outflow, giving a longer half-life than posterior clearance." },
  { id: 34, question: "The retinal vascular endothelium resembles that of the brain because it is:", options: ["Fenestrated", "Nonfenestrated with tight junctions", "Absent", "Highly permeable"], correct: 1, explanation: "Retinal vessels are nonfenestrated with tight junctions, forming the inner blood–retina barrier." },
  { id: 35, question: "The fenestrated choroidal vessels are sequestered from the retina by:", options: ["The sclera", "The RPE tight junctions", "Bruch membrane alone", "The vitreous"], correct: 1, explanation: "The RPE tight junctions form the outer blood–retina barrier, sequestering the fenestrated choroid." },
  { id: 36, question: "Which fraction of a systemically administered drug can cross the blood–ocular barrier?", options: ["Protein-bound", "Unbound (free)", "Ionized", "Metabolized"], correct: 1, explanation: "Only the unbound (free) drug crosses; heavy protein binding limits penetration." },
  { id: 37, question: "Chloramphenicol penetrates the eye ~20× better than penicillin mainly because it is:", options: ["More protein-bound", "More lipid-soluble", "Smaller", "More charged"], correct: 1, explanation: "Higher lipid solubility improves blood–ocular barrier penetration." },
  { id: 38, question: "Sulfonamides penetrate the eye poorly despite being lipid-soluble because:", options: ["They are too small", "More than 90% is bound to plasma proteins", "They are highly ionized", "They are metabolized within the eye"], correct: 1, explanation: "Only free drug crosses; >90% protein binding leaves little available to penetrate." },
  { id: 39, question: "Bolus IV administration achieves higher intraocular levels than a slow drip because it:", options: ["Avoids renal clearance", "Exceeds the plasma protein–binding capacity, raising free drug", "Is more lipid-soluble", "Bypasses the liver"], correct: 1, explanation: "A bolus saturates protein binding, transiently increasing free drug and intraocular peaks." },
  { id: 40, question: "Which oral antifungals achieve good intraocular concentrations for endogenous fungal endophthalmitis?", options: ["Amphotericin and nystatin", "Fluconazole and voriconazole", "Griseofulvin", "Terbinafine"], correct: 1, explanation: "Fluconazole and voriconazole have high oral bioavailability and good intraocular penetration." },
  { id: 41, question: "Drug penetration into an inflamed eye is typically:", options: ["Decreased", "Increased, due to blood–ocular barrier disruption", "Unchanged", "Completely blocked"], correct: 1, explanation: "Inflammation breaks down the blood–aqueous and blood–retina barriers, increasing penetration." },
  { id: 42, question: "Latanoprost, travoprost, and tafluprost are examples of:", options: ["Suspensions", "Prodrugs requiring hydrolysis to active FP-receptor agonists", "Preservatives", "Antibiotics"], correct: 1, explanation: "These prostaglandin-analogue prodrugs are hydrolyzed to their active acid forms in the eye." },
  { id: 43, question: "Valacyclovir is a prodrug of:", options: ["Ganciclovir", "Acyclovir", "Penciclovir", "Foscarnet"], correct: 1, explanation: "Oral valacyclovir is rapidly converted to acyclovir; famciclovir is a prodrug of penciclovir." },
  { id: 44, question: "A soluble ocular insert differs from an insoluble one in that it:", options: ["Must be removed after use", "Dissolves in the tear film and needs no removal", "Delivers no drug", "Is nonbiodegradable"], correct: 1, explanation: "Soluble inserts dissolve (no removal); insoluble inserts give steadier release but must be removed." },
  { id: 45, question: "The first sustained-release intraocular implant was developed for:", options: ["Diabetic macular edema", "Cytomegalovirus retinitis (the ganciclovir implant)", "Glaucoma", "Age-related macular degeneration"], correct: 1, explanation: "The ganciclovir intravitreal implant for CMV retinitis was the first (discontinued 2015)." },
  { id: 46, question: "The only FDA-approved punctal-plug drug delivery system releases:", options: ["Timolol", "Dexamethasone (0.4 mg over 30 days)", "Latanoprost", "Cyclosporine"], correct: 1, explanation: "A resorbable intracanalicular dexamethasone insert (0.4 mg/30 days) is approved for postoperative inflammation/pain." },
  { id: 47, question: "In a liposome, a water-soluble drug is carried in the:", options: ["Lipid bilayer", "Aqueous interior core", "Outer surface only", "It cannot carry water-soluble drugs"], correct: 1, explanation: "Water-soluble drug dissolves in the aqueous core; a hydrophobic drug intercalates into the lipid bilayer." },
  { id: 48, question: "Iontophoresis enhances ocular drug delivery by:", options: ["Increasing vehicle viscosity", "Using an electrical current to drive charged molecules into tissue", "Blocking the puncta", "Raising the solution pH"], correct: 1, explanation: "Iontophoresis moves charged molecules with an electrical current, giving high local concentration with little systemic absorption." },
  { id: 49, question: "Pharmacogenetics is best defined as the study of how:", options: ["Many genes together affect drug response", "Variation in single genes affects drug efficacy or toxicity", "Drugs are metabolized in the liver only", "Drugs are excreted"], correct: 1, explanation: "Pharmacogenetics focuses on single genes; pharmacogenomics considers many genes." },
  { id: 50, question: "Compared with younger patients, older patients typically have:", options: ["More lean body mass and body water", "Less lean mass, less body water/albumin, more adipose, and declining renal function — extending drug half-life", "Faster drug clearance", "No pharmacokinetic differences"], correct: 1, explanation: "These age-related changes extend the half-life of most systemic drugs and potentiate effects, even at reduced doses." },
];

// ── Basic Science · BCSC Section 10 · Part 1 · Medical Management of Glaucoma
// Source: BCSC Section 10 (Glaucoma), Ch. 12 (Medical Management of Glaucoma & OHT).
const BASIC_GLAUCOMA_RECALL: RecallQ[] = [
  { id: 1, question: "What is 'target pressure' in glaucoma management?", answer: "The IOP below which the clinician estimates that the rate of progression will be slowed enough to minimize further vision loss over the patient's lifetime. It is individualized for each eye based on the IOP at which damage occurred and its severity." },
  { id: 2, question: "What are the guideline target IOPs by disease severity?", answer: "Ocular hypertension: < 25 mm Hg and 20% below baseline. Mild damage: < 21 and 25% below. Moderate damage: < 18 and 30% below. Severe damage: < 15 and 30% below." },
  { id: 3, question: "Which factors argue for a lower vs a higher target pressure?", answer: "Lower the target for: new optic disc hemorrhage, thinner cornea, rapid progression or severe loss in the fellow eye, family history of glaucoma blindness, and early age at onset. Raise the target for: decreased life expectancy/comorbidities, injury at very high IOP, stable/slow damage, and high central corneal thickness." },
  { id: 4, question: "List the classes of ocular hypotensive medications.", answer: "Prostaglandin analogues (including a nitric-oxide donor), adrenergic drugs (β-antagonists and α-agonists), carbonic anhydrase inhibitors (topical and systemic), parasympathomimetics/miotics (direct-acting cholinergic and indirect-acting anticholinesterase), Rho kinase inhibitors, and hyperosmotic agents." },
  { id: 5, question: "What is the mechanism of prostaglandin analogues?", answer: "They are prodrugs hydrolyzed by corneal esterase, lowering IOP mainly by increasing uveoscleral outflow (and some conventional outflow). They bind PG/prostamide receptors → activate matrix metalloproteinases → remodel the ciliary body, trabecular meshwork, and sclera (increasing space between ciliary muscle fascicles)." },
  { id: 6, question: "Name the five prostaglandin analogues and the unique one.", answer: "Latanoprost, travoprost, bimatoprost, tafluprost, and latanoprostene bunod (~30% IOP reduction, once daily — less effective twice daily). Latanoprostene bunod adds a nitric-oxide donor that increases conventional outflow, giving ~32% diurnal reduction and ~1 mm Hg advantage over latanoprost." },
  { id: 7, question: "What is the onset/washout profile of PG analogues?", answer: "Peak effect at 10–14 hours; maximum IOP lowering may take up to 6 weeks; washout is 4–6 weeks. Switch agents after a 4–6 week trial if the response is inadequate." },
  { id: 8, question: "Describe PG-analogue iris and skin pigmentation.", answer: "Permanent darkening of the iris and periocular skin from an increased number of melanosomes within melanocytes (not melanocyte proliferation). Risk depends on baseline iris color — up to ~79% green-brown and ~85% hazel irides vs ~8% blue (~33% overall at 5 years with latanoprost). There is no associated melanoma risk." },
  { id: 9, question: "What other adverse effects do PG analogues cause?", answer: "Conjunctival hyperemia (more with bimatoprost/travoprost), hypertrichosis, trichiasis, and distichiasis (reversible); prostaglandin-associated periorbitopathy (deep upper-lid sulcus, ptosis, enophthalmos, inferior scleral show — from periorbital fat atrophy); ~1% nongranulomatous anterior uveitis; possible cystoid macular edema; and herpetic keratitis reactivation. Systemic: flulike symptoms, joint/muscle pain, headache." },
  { id: 10, question: "Describe the sustained-release bimatoprost implant.", answer: "A 10-µg intracameral, biodegradable (PLGA) implant, FDA-approved in 2020 for one-time use. Its most concerning adverse effect is corneal endothelial cell loss; most patients (69%) needed no additional IOP therapy through 20 months." },
  { id: 11, question: "What is the mechanism of β-blockers in glaucoma?", answer: "They inhibit cAMP production in the ciliary epithelium, reducing aqueous humor secretion (~20–50%) and lowering IOP ~20–30%. The effect begins within 1 hour and can persist up to 4 weeks after stopping; systemic absorption can lower IOP in the untreated fellow eye." },
  { id: 12, question: "Why are β-blockers ineffective at night?", answer: "Aqueous production is already reduced during sleep, so β-blockers add little effect and do not lower nocturnal IOP." },
  { id: 13, question: "Name the five topical β-blockers and their selectivity.", answer: "Betaxolol — the only β1-selective agent (less effective at lowering IOP but safer in asthma and less depressive). Nonselective: carteolol (intrinsic sympathomimetic activity), levobunolol, metipranolol, and timolol." },
  { id: 14, question: "What are the systemic risks of β-blockers and the key questions before prescribing?", answer: "Bronchospasm, bradycardia, heart block, hypotension, reduced exercise tolerance, CNS depression, and masked hypoglycemia in diabetics. Ask about asthma (β2 bronchospasm) and check heart rate/history of heart block. Abrupt withdrawal can exacerbate hyperthyroidism." },
  { id: 15, question: "How can systemic absorption of a topical β-blocker be reduced?", answer: "Use a gel vehicle (lower systemic absorption than solution) and perform nasolacrimal occlusion after instillation." },
  { id: 16, question: "What is the mechanism of α2-adrenergic agonists, and how does brimonidine differ from apraclonidine?", answer: "α2 agonists reduce aqueous production (α2 receptor → inhibitory G protein → ↓ adenylate cyclase → ↓ cAMP). Brimonidine — but not apraclonidine — also increases uveoscleral outflow." },
  { id: 17, question: "Contrast the clinical roles of brimonidine and apraclonidine.", answer: "Brimonidine tartrate is the main long-term α2 agonist (highly α2-selective). Apraclonidine (a clonidine derivative) is used mostly perioperatively to blunt IOP spikes after laser or cataract surgery, because tachyphylaxis and hypersensitivity blepharoconjunctivitis limit long-term use." },
  { id: 18, question: "What are the adverse effects of α2-agonists?", answer: "Ocular: allergic follicular conjunctivitis and contact blepharodermatitis — a delayed-type, dose-dependent hypersensitivity (less with brimonidine than apraclonidine); >20% long-term intolerance; rare granulomatous anterior uveitis. Systemic: dry mouth and lethargy (clonidine-like CNS effects). Advise nasolacrimal occlusion." },
  { id: 19, question: "Why is brimonidine contraindicated in young children, and what drug interactions matter?", answer: "In infants/young children it risks CNS depression, apnea, bradycardia, and hypotension (low volume of distribution and increased CNS penetration). It also interacts dangerously with MAO inhibitors and tricyclic antidepressants." },
  { id: 20, question: "What is the mechanism of carbonic anhydrase inhibitors?", answer: "They decrease aqueous production by inhibiting ciliary epithelial carbonic anhydrase (>90% of the enzyme must be inhibited). Systemic CAIs additionally cause renal metabolic acidosis that reduces ciliary Na⁺/K⁺-ATPase activity." },
  { id: 21, question: "What are the topical CAIs and their formulation quirks?", answer: "Dorzolamide (2%, a low-pH solution → prominent stinging) and brinzolamide (1% suspension → white tear-film deposits); dosed 2–3×/day, ~15–20% IOP reduction. Both risk corneal decompensation in eyes with compromised endothelium." },
  { id: 22, question: "What are the systemic CAIs, their uses, and dosing?", answer: "Acetazolamide (250 mg QID, or 500 mg sustained-release BID; renally excreted — reduce dose in renal insufficiency) and methazolamide (25–50 mg 2–3×/day; longer-acting, less protein-bound, liver-metabolized, but less effective). Used for severely elevated IOP or as a temporizing measure before surgery (oral onset < 1 h; IV within 15 min)." },
  { id: 23, question: "What are the systemic CAI adverse effects and key contraindication?", answer: "Paresthesias, anorexia, weight loss, malaise/depression, renal stones (calcium oxalate/phosphate), hypokalemia (especially with thiazides — monitor K⁺), sickle cell crisis risk, and rare fatal blood dyscrasias (aplastic anemia)/SJS/hepatic necrosis. Contraindicated in hepatic cirrhosis (risk of hepatic encephalopathy from raised ammonia)." },
  { id: 24, question: "How do sulfa allergy and CAIs relate?", answer: "Only ~10% of patients allergic to sulfonamide antibiotics react to sulfonamide non-antibiotics such as CAIs. This reflects a general predisposition to allergy rather than true cross-reactivity." },
  { id: 25, question: "What is the mechanism of miotics (parasympathomimetics)?", answer: "Contraction of the longitudinal ciliary muscle (inserting into the scleral spur, trabecular meshwork, and inner wall of Schlemm canal) stretches the trabecular meshwork and widens Schlemm canal, improving conventional outflow (pilocarpine lowers IOP ~15–25%). Indirect agents inhibit acetylcholinesterase, prolonging endogenous acetylcholine." },
  { id: 26, question: "What are the special uses of pilocarpine?", answer: "Blunting the IOP spike of pigmentary glaucoma during vigorous activity, aphakic glaucoma, and plateau iris syndrome (persistently occludable angle despite a patent iridotomy). It is rarely used long-term (poor adherence from adverse effects and 3–4×/day dosing) and is more effective in lighter irides." },
  { id: 27, question: "What are the adverse effects of miotics?", answer: "Induced myopia and brow ache (ciliary spasm), miosis impairing dim-light vision, retinal detachment (do a peripheral retinal exam first), cataract (especially indirect agents), pseudopemphigoid, epiphora, blood–aqueous barrier disruption (avoid in uveitic glaucoma), and paradoxical angle closure in phacomorphic narrow angles." },
  { id: 28, question: "What is special about echothiophate?", answer: "An indirect-acting anticholinesterase, rarely used but effective in aphakic glaucoma; it is cataractogenic and causes iris pigment epithelial cysts. Depolarizing muscle relaxants (succinylcholine) must be avoided for 6 weeks after stopping it." },
  { id: 29, question: "What is the mechanism of Rho kinase inhibitors?", answer: "Inhibiting ROCK1/ROCK2 relaxes the actin cytoskeleton of trabecular-meshwork and Schlemm-canal outflow cells, increasing conventional outflow; it also lowers episcleral venous pressure." },
  { id: 30, question: "Describe netarsudil.", answer: "0.02% once daily, preferably at night (because of hyperemia); a mixed ROCK1/2 inhibitor and norepinephrine-transporter inhibitor (lowering episcleral venous pressure). It gives ~20% IOP reduction and is additive with latanoprost. Ripasudil was the first Rho kinase inhibitor (approved in Japan)." },
  { id: 31, question: "What are the adverse effects of Rho kinase inhibitors?", answer: "Conjunctival hyperemia (>50–60%, worst in the first hours → nighttime dosing), subconjunctival hemorrhage, cornea verticillata (like amiodarone — from lysosomal phospholipid accumulation; reversible ~1 year after stopping; no visual effect), and reticular corneal edema." },
  { id: 32, question: "What are the benefits and composition of fixed-combination glaucoma drops?", answer: "Benefits: improved convenience and less preservative exposure. Timolol is combined with a CAI (dorzolamide or brinzolamide), an α2 agonist (brimonidine), or a PG analogue; other combinations include brimonidine/brinzolamide and latanoprost/netarsudil. Efficacy is similar to the components given separately." },
  { id: 33, question: "What are the hyperosmotic agents and their mechanism?", answer: "Oral glycerol (50%, 1–1.5 g/kg) and IV mannitol (20%, 0.5–2 g/kg), used for acute severe IOP elevation. They raise blood osmolality, creating a gradient that draws water from the vitreous; higher/faster doses lower IOP more, but the effect is transient with possible rebound." },
  { id: 34, question: "What are the adverse effects and cautions of hyperosmotic agents?", answer: "Headache, confusion, backache, acute congestive heart failure and MI (increased preload — IV worse than oral), subdural/subarachnoid hemorrhage, and urinary retention. Glycerol can cause hyperglycemia/ketoacidosis in diabetics (it is metabolized to glucose/ketones); mannitol is not metabolized. Both are contraindicated in renal failure/dialysis." },
  { id: 35, question: "Which agents increase conventional vs uveoscleral outflow vs reduce production?", answer: "Increase uveoscleral outflow: PG analogues (primary), brimonidine (secondary). Increase conventional (trabecular) outflow: miotics, Rho kinase inhibitors, and latanoprostene bunod's nitric-oxide component. Decrease aqueous production: β-blockers, α2 agonists, and carbonic anhydrase inhibitors." },
  { id: 36, question: "What is the first-line medical therapy for open-angle glaucoma/OHT?", answer: "Prostaglandin analogues, β-blockers, or laser trabeculoplasty. PG analogues are the most common first-line agents (superior efficacy, once-daily dosing, favorable safety). If contraindicated or ineffective, netarsudil, brimonidine, or a topical CAI are reasonable options." },
  { id: 37, question: "How do preservatives affect glaucoma therapy, and what are the options?", answer: "Benzalkonium chloride (BAK), the commonest preservative in glaucoma drops, is toxic to the ocular surface with frequent/prolonged use. Strategies: limit dosing frequency, use combination agents, or use preservative-free/alternatively preserved formulations (e.g., Purite, SofZia, unit-dose vials)." },
  { id: 38, question: "How should eyedrops be administered for best effect?", answer: "Instill one drop into the inferior fornix, keep the eyes closed for 1–3 minutes, and perform nasolacrimal occlusion (increases corneal penetration, limits systemic absorption and unpleasant taste). Wait at least 5 minutes between different drops. About 25% of glaucoma patients have someone else administer their drops." },
  { id: 39, question: "How are glaucoma medications handled in pregnancy and breastfeeding?", answer: "Minimize medications, use the lowest effective dose, advise nasolacrimal occlusion, and avoid antifibrotics. Brimonidine is the preferred agent (former Category B) but is discontinued before delivery (infant apnea) and avoided while breastfeeding. β-blockers are concentrated in breast milk (relatively contraindicated). Systemic CAIs are teratogenic (avoid); avoid topical CAIs especially in the first trimester. Topical PG analogues are likely low-risk despite being embryocidal/abortifacient at high systemic doses." },
  { id: 40, question: "What is the main barrier to glaucoma medication adherence, and how can it be improved?", answer: "Forgetfulness is the most-cited barrier. Improve adherence by using the fewest bottles and least-frequent dosing, prescribing generics (cheaper), pairing dosing with a daily routine, using reminders/apps and dosing charts, and providing eyedrop aids." },
  { id: 41, question: "What is the α2-agonist IOP-lowering profile over the day?", answer: "Brimonidine's peak IOP reduction is ~26% at 2 hours but only ~14–15% at trough (12 hours), and it does not lower nocturnal IOP." },
  { id: 42, question: "What are the nonselective adrenergic agonists?", answer: "Epinephrine and dipivefrin (a prodrug of epinephrine). They reduce aqueous production and increase both uveoscleral and conventional outflow, but are now rarely used because of local adverse effects and are no longer available in the US." },
  { id: 43, question: "What is apraclonidine's perioperative role and its α1 effects?", answer: "It blunts acute IOP spikes after laser peripheral iridotomy, trabeculoplasty, capsulotomy, and cataract surgery. Its greater α1 affinity (vs brimonidine) causes conjunctival blanching, eyelid retraction, and mydriasis — whereas brimonidine commonly causes miosis." },
  { id: 44, question: "Compare methazolamide with acetazolamide.", answer: "Methazolamide has a longer duration of action, is less protein-bound, is metabolized by the liver, and causes less acidosis (so possibly less nephrolithiasis) — but it is less effective. Acetazolamide is not metabolized and is renally excreted, requiring dose reduction in renal insufficiency." },
  { id: 45, question: "When are hyperosmotic and systemic CAIs used in acute IOP elevation?", answer: "In eyes with severe IOP elevation (e.g., acute angle closure), hyperosmotic agents and systemic CAIs are used to rapidly lower IOP before definitive treatment. Lowering IOP also relieves ischemia of the pupillary constrictor so pilocarpine can work and the pupil can constrict before laser peripheral iridotomy." },
];

const BASIC_GLAUCOMA_MCQ: MCQ[] = [
  { id: 1, question: "Prostaglandin analogues lower IOP primarily by:", options: ["Decreasing aqueous humor production", "Increasing uveoscleral outflow", "Increasing conventional outflow only", "Osmotic dehydration of the vitreous"], correct: 1, explanation: "PG analogues act mainly by increasing uveoscleral outflow (with some conventional outflow effect)." },
  { id: 2, question: "Prostaglandin analogues are prodrugs activated by:", options: ["Hepatic metabolism", "Corneal esterase hydrolysis", "Renal excretion", "Gastric acid"], correct: 1, explanation: "They penetrate the cornea and are hydrolyzed by corneal esterase to the active form." },
  { id: 3, question: "Which prostaglandin analogue contains a nitric-oxide–donating moiety?", options: ["Latanoprost", "Travoprost", "Latanoprostene bunod", "Bimatoprost"], correct: 2, explanation: "Latanoprostene bunod adds a nitric-oxide donor that increases conventional outflow." },
  { id: 4, question: "The recommended dosing of prostaglandin analogues is:", options: ["Once daily (less effective twice daily)", "Twice daily", "Four times daily", "Weekly"], correct: 0, explanation: "PG analogues are dosed once daily and are paradoxically less effective when given twice daily." },
  { id: 5, question: "Iris pigmentation from prostaglandin analogues results from:", options: ["Melanocyte proliferation", "Increased melanosomes within melanocytes (permanent)", "Iris stromal atrophy", "Chronic inflammation"], correct: 1, explanation: "The permanent darkening reflects more melanosomes per melanocyte, not melanocyte proliferation, and carries no melanoma risk." },
  { id: 6, question: "Iris darkening from latanoprost is most likely in eyes with:", options: ["Blue irides", "Green-brown or hazel irides", "Albinotic irides", "Aniridia"], correct: 1, explanation: "Mixed-color (green-brown ~79%, hazel ~85%) irides are affected far more than blue (~8%)." },
  { id: 7, question: "Prostaglandin-associated periorbitopathy includes all of the following EXCEPT:", options: ["Deepening of the upper eyelid sulcus", "Enophthalmos", "Proptosis with eyelid retraction", "Inferior scleral show"], correct: 2, explanation: "Periorbital fat atrophy causes a deep sulcus, ptosis, enophthalmos, and inferior scleral show — not proptosis/retraction." },
  { id: 8, question: "Conjunctival hyperemia from PG analogues is most common with:", options: ["Latanoprost and tafluprost", "Bimatoprost and travoprost", "Latanoprostene bunod", "Tafluprost only"], correct: 1, explanation: "Hyperemia (vasodilation) is most frequent with bimatoprost and travoprost." },
  { id: 9, question: "The main safety concern of the intracameral bimatoprost sustained-release implant is:", options: ["Cataract", "Corneal endothelial cell loss", "Angle-closure glaucoma", "Retinal detachment"], correct: 1, explanation: "The most concerning trial finding was a decrease in corneal endothelial cell density." },
  { id: 10, question: "β-blockers lower IOP by:", options: ["Increasing uveoscleral outflow", "Decreasing aqueous humor production", "Increasing conventional outflow", "Osmotic effect"], correct: 1, explanation: "They inhibit ciliary-epithelium cAMP, reducing aqueous secretion." },
  { id: 11, question: "Why do β-blockers fail to lower IOP during sleep?", options: ["They wash out overnight", "Nocturnal aqueous production is already reduced", "They only increase outflow", "They are metabolized at night"], correct: 1, explanation: "With little aqueous being produced at night, aqueous-suppressants add little effect." },
  { id: 12, question: "The only β1-selective topical β-blocker is:", options: ["Timolol", "Levobunolol", "Betaxolol", "Carteolol"], correct: 2, explanation: "Betaxolol is β1-selective — safer in asthma but less effective at lowering IOP." },
  { id: 13, question: "Compared with nonselective β-blockers, betaxolol is:", options: ["More effective at lowering IOP", "Less effective at lowering IOP but safer in asthma", "Contraindicated in all patients", "A prodrug"], correct: 1, explanation: "β1-selectivity spares bronchial β2 receptors (safer in asthma) at the cost of some IOP efficacy." },
  { id: 14, question: "Before prescribing a topical β-blocker, it is most important to ask about:", options: ["Asthma and heart rate/heart block", "Penicillin allergy", "Renal stones", "Iris color"], correct: 0, explanation: "β-blockers risk bronchospasm and bradycardia/heart block, so asthma and cardiac history are key." },
  { id: 15, question: "Which β-blocker has intrinsic sympathomimetic activity?", options: ["Timolol", "Betaxolol", "Carteolol", "Metipranolol"], correct: 2, explanation: "Carteolol has intrinsic sympathomimetic activity and may spare nocturnal pulse/BP." },
  { id: 16, question: "Systemic absorption of a topical β-blocker is reduced by:", options: ["A gel vehicle and nasolacrimal occlusion", "A higher concentration", "Twice-daily dosing", "Adding benzalkonium chloride"], correct: 0, explanation: "Gel formulations and nasolacrimal occlusion lower plasma drug levels." },
  { id: 17, question: "In addition to reducing aqueous production, brimonidine (unlike apraclonidine) also:", options: ["Increases conventional outflow", "Increases uveoscleral outflow", "Dehydrates the vitreous", "Causes miosis to open the angle"], correct: 1, explanation: "Brimonidine additionally increases uveoscleral outflow; apraclonidine does not." },
  { id: 18, question: "Apraclonidine is used mainly for:", options: ["Long-term monotherapy", "Perioperative blunting of IOP spikes after laser/cataract surgery", "Acute angle-closure attacks", "Pediatric glaucoma"], correct: 1, explanation: "Tachyphylaxis and hypersensitivity limit apraclonidine to short-term/perioperative use." },
  { id: 19, question: "Brimonidine is contraindicated in:", options: ["The elderly", "Infants and young children (apnea, CNS depression)", "Diabetics", "Asthmatics"], correct: 1, explanation: "Its clonidine-like CNS effects risk apnea, bradycardia, and hypotension in young children." },
  { id: 20, question: "The ocular allergy from α2-agonists is a:", options: ["Type I immediate hypersensitivity", "Delayed-type (Type IV), dose-dependent hypersensitivity", "Autoimmune vasculitis", "Purely toxic reaction"], correct: 1, explanation: "It is a delayed-type, dose-dependent hypersensitivity (follicular conjunctivitis, contact blepharodermatitis)." },
  { id: 21, question: "α2-agonists interact dangerously with:", options: ["Statins", "MAO inhibitors and tricyclic antidepressants", "Oral antibiotics", "NSAIDs"], correct: 1, explanation: "MAOIs and TCAs impair metabolism of apraclonidine/brimonidine, risking toxicity." },
  { id: 22, question: "Dipivefrin is:", options: ["A carbonic anhydrase inhibitor", "A prodrug of epinephrine", "A β-blocker", "A prostaglandin analogue"], correct: 1, explanation: "Dipivefrin is a prodrug of epinephrine (a nonselective adrenergic agonist), now rarely used." },
  { id: 23, question: "Carbonic anhydrase inhibitors lower IOP by:", options: ["Increasing uveoscleral outflow", "Decreasing aqueous humor production", "Increasing conventional outflow", "Osmotic dehydration"], correct: 1, explanation: "CAIs suppress ciliary-epithelium carbonic anhydrase, reducing aqueous formation." },
  { id: 24, question: "What percentage of ciliary carbonic anhydrase must be inhibited to lower IOP?", options: ["25%", "50%", ">90%", "100%"], correct: 2, explanation: "More than 90% of the enzyme must be inhibited to reduce aqueous production." },
  { id: 25, question: "Which topical CAI is a low-pH solution notorious for stinging?", options: ["Brinzolamide", "Dorzolamide", "Acetazolamide", "Methazolamide"], correct: 1, explanation: "Dorzolamide is a low-pH solution that stings; brinzolamide is a suspension." },
  { id: 26, question: "Brinzolamide (a suspension) characteristically causes:", options: ["Low-pH stinging", "White deposits in the tear film", "Iris darkening", "Miosis"], correct: 1, explanation: "As a suspension, brinzolamide can leave white tear-film deposits." },
  { id: 27, question: "Systemic carbonic anhydrase inhibitors are contraindicated in:", options: ["Hypertension", "Hepatic cirrhosis (risk of hepatic encephalopathy)", "Migraine", "Asthma"], correct: 1, explanation: "Reduced ammonia excretion can precipitate hepatic encephalopathy in cirrhosis." },
  { id: 28, question: "A serious electrolyte risk of systemic CAIs, especially with thiazides, is:", options: ["Hyperkalemia", "Hypokalemia", "Hypernatremia", "Hypercalcemia"], correct: 1, explanation: "CAIs cause potassium loss; concurrent thiazides worsen hypokalemia — monitor K⁺." },
  { id: 29, question: "Regarding sulfa allergy and CAIs:", options: ["All sulfa-allergic patients react to CAIs", "Only ~10% of sulfonamide-antibiotic-allergic patients react (predisposition, not true cross-reactivity)", "CAIs contain penicillin", "Cross-reactivity is essentially 100%"], correct: 1, explanation: "Sulfonamide non-antibiotics like CAIs cross-react in only ~10%, reflecting allergic predisposition." },
  { id: 30, question: "Compared with acetazolamide, methazolamide is:", options: ["More effective and shorter-acting", "Longer-acting, less protein-bound, liver-metabolized, but less effective", "Excreted unchanged by the kidney", "Not a carbonic anhydrase inhibitor"], correct: 1, explanation: "Methazolamide is longer-acting and liver-metabolized but less potent than acetazolamide." },
  { id: 31, question: "Acetazolamide requires dose reduction in:", options: ["Hepatic disease", "Renal insufficiency (it is renally excreted, not metabolized)", "Asthma", "Diabetes"], correct: 1, explanation: "Acetazolamide is renally excreted unchanged, so renal insufficiency requires a lower dose." },
  { id: 32, question: "Miotics improve aqueous outflow by:", options: ["Increasing uveoscleral outflow", "Ciliary muscle contraction stretching the trabecular meshwork (conventional outflow)", "Reducing aqueous production", "Osmotic dehydration"], correct: 1, explanation: "Longitudinal ciliary muscle contraction stretches the trabecular meshwork and widens Schlemm canal." },
  { id: 33, question: "Pilocarpine is useful in all of the following EXCEPT:", options: ["Pigmentary glaucoma exercise-induced IOP spikes", "Aphakic glaucoma", "Plateau iris syndrome", "Uveitic glaucoma"], correct: 3, explanation: "Miotics disrupt the blood–aqueous barrier and should be avoided in uveitic glaucoma." },
  { id: 34, question: "Which complication warrants a peripheral retinal exam before starting miotics?", options: ["Cystoid macular edema", "Retinal detachment", "Retinal vein occlusion", "Age-related macular degeneration"], correct: 1, explanation: "Miotics are associated with retinal detachment, so a peripheral exam is advised first." },
  { id: 35, question: "After stopping echothiophate, which agent must be avoided for ~6 weeks?", options: ["Aspirin", "Succinylcholine (a depolarizing muscle relaxant)", "Warfarin", "Penicillin"], correct: 1, explanation: "Anticholinesterase effects prolong succinylcholine action; avoid it for 6 weeks after stopping." },
  { id: 36, question: "Miotics can precipitate paradoxical angle closure in eyes with:", options: ["Wide open angles", "Phacomorphic narrow angles (forward lens movement)", "Aphakia", "Pseudophakia"], correct: 1, explanation: "Ciliary contraction moves the lens–iris interface forward, worsening pupillary block in phacomorphic narrow angles." },
  { id: 37, question: "Rho kinase inhibitors lower IOP mainly by:", options: ["Reducing aqueous production", "Relaxing the trabecular-meshwork cytoskeleton to increase conventional outflow", "Increasing uveoscleral outflow", "Osmotic dehydration"], correct: 1, explanation: "ROCK inhibition relaxes outflow-cell cytoskeleton, increasing conventional outflow and lowering episcleral venous pressure." },
  { id: 38, question: "Netarsudil is dosed once daily, preferably at night, because of:", options: ["Better nighttime absorption", "Conjunctival hyperemia that peaks in the first hours", "Cataract risk", "Systemic hypotension"], correct: 1, explanation: "Hyperemia is most intense soon after instillation, so bedtime dosing improves tolerability." },
  { id: 39, question: "A corneal finding after prolonged netarsudil use, resembling amiodarone toxicity, is:", options: ["Band keratopathy", "Cornea verticillata", "Keratic precipitates", "Corneal arcus"], correct: 1, explanation: "Cornea verticillata (whorl keratopathy) from lysosomal phospholipid accumulation; it is reversible and visually insignificant." },
  { id: 40, question: "Besides ROCK inhibition, netarsudil also inhibits:", options: ["Carbonic anhydrase", "The norepinephrine transporter (lowering episcleral venous pressure)", "Acetylcholinesterase", "Adenylate cyclase"], correct: 1, explanation: "Its NET-inhibitor activity reduces episcleral venous pressure." },
  { id: 41, question: "A key benefit of fixed-combination glaucoma drops is:", options: ["Greater efficacy than the separate components", "Improved convenience and less preservative exposure", "No adverse effects", "Once-weekly dosing"], correct: 1, explanation: "Combinations improve convenience and reduce preservative load; efficacy is similar to the components separately." },
  { id: 42, question: "Which is NOT a commonly used fixed-combination partner with timolol?", options: ["Dorzolamide", "Brimonidine", "Latanoprost", "Pilocarpine"], correct: 3, explanation: "Timolol is combined with CAIs, α2-agonists, or PG analogues — not pilocarpine." },
  { id: 43, question: "Hyperosmotic agents lower IOP by:", options: ["Decreasing aqueous production", "Creating an osmotic gradient that draws water from the vitreous", "Increasing conventional outflow", "Blocking carbonic anhydrase"], correct: 1, explanation: "Raising blood osmolality pulls water out of the vitreous, transiently lowering IOP." },
  { id: 44, question: "Which hyperosmotic can precipitate ketoacidosis in diabetics because it is metabolized to glucose?", options: ["Mannitol", "Glycerol", "Acetazolamide", "Timolol"], correct: 1, explanation: "Glycerol is metabolized to glucose/ketones (hyperglycemia/ketoacidosis); mannitol is not metabolized." },
  { id: 45, question: "Hyperosmotic agents are contraindicated in:", options: ["Renal failure / dialysis", "Asthma", "Migraine", "Diabetes insipidus"], correct: 0, explanation: "They are contraindicated in renal failure and used cautiously in heart failure (fluid overload)." },
  { id: 46, question: "IV mannitol is more likely than oral glycerol to precipitate:", options: ["Ketoacidosis", "Congestive heart failure (from increased preload)", "Renal stones", "Conjunctival hyperemia"], correct: 1, explanation: "Rapid IV volume expansion raises cardiac preload, risking CHF/MI." },
  { id: 47, question: "The most common first-line agent for open-angle glaucoma is:", options: ["A β-blocker", "A prostaglandin analogue", "Pilocarpine", "Oral acetazolamide"], correct: 1, explanation: "PG analogues are first-line for their superior efficacy, once-daily dosing, and safety." },
  { id: 48, question: "The most common preservative in glaucoma drops, toxic to the ocular surface, is:", options: ["Purite (sodium chlorite)", "Benzalkonium chloride (BAK)", "SofZia", "Sodium perborate"], correct: 1, explanation: "BAK is the commonest preservative and a major cause of drop-related ocular surface disease." },
  { id: 49, question: "Proper eyedrop technique to boost penetration and cut systemic absorption is:", options: ["Blink rapidly after instilling", "Eyes closed 1–3 minutes with nasolacrimal occlusion", "Instill two drops at once", "Aim only at the central cornea"], correct: 1, explanation: "Closed eyes plus nasolacrimal occlusion increase corneal uptake and reduce systemic absorption/taste." },
  { id: 50, question: "The preferred glaucoma agent during pregnancy (discontinued before delivery) is:", options: ["Timolol", "Brimonidine", "Acetazolamide", "Latanoprost"], correct: 1, explanation: "Brimonidine (former Category B) is preferred but stopped before delivery due to infant apnea risk, and avoided while breastfeeding." },
  { id: 51, question: "Systemic carbonic anhydrase inhibitors during pregnancy are:", options: ["Preferred first-line", "Teratogenic and should be avoided", "Completely safe", "Only used topically"], correct: 1, explanation: "Systemic CAIs are teratogenic (forelimb deformities reported) and should be avoided in pregnancy." },
  { id: 52, question: "The approximate IOP reduction achieved by prostaglandin analogues is:", options: ["~5%", "~30%", "~60%", "~80%"], correct: 1, explanation: "PG analogues reduce IOP by roughly 30%, the greatest of the single-agent classes." },
  { id: 53, question: "β-blockers reduce aqueous production by inhibiting:", options: ["Carbonic anhydrase", "cAMP production in the ciliary epithelium", "Na⁺/K⁺-ATPase directly", "The α2 receptor"], correct: 1, explanation: "β-blockade lowers ciliary-epithelium cAMP, reducing aqueous secretion." },
  { id: 54, question: "Brimonidine's IOP-lowering at trough (12 hours post-dose) is:", options: ["Greater than at peak", "Lower (~14–15%), and it does not lower nocturnal IOP", "Zero", "Unchanged from peak"], correct: 1, explanation: "Its effect wanes to ~14–15% at trough and it lacks nocturnal efficacy." },
  { id: 55, question: "The most common patient-reported barrier to glaucoma medication adherence is:", options: ["Cost only", "Forgetfulness", "Stinging on instillation", "Iris color change"], correct: 1, explanation: "Forgetfulness is the most cited barrier; routines, reminders, and simpler regimens help." },
];

// ── Basic Science · BCSC Section 2 · Part 3 · Ocular & Corneal Anatomy
// Source: BCSC Section 2 (Fundamentals & Principles), Ch. 2 (The Eye).
const BASIC_ANATOMY_RECALL: RecallQ[] = [
  { id: 1, question: "What are the key dimensions and shape of the globe?", answer: "Corneal radius of curvature ~8 mm (less than the sclera's ~12 mm); anteroposterior diameter ~23–25 mm; transverse diameter ~24 mm. The globe is an oblate spheroid (equatorial radius greater than polar radius)." },
  { id: 2, question: "What are the three compartments of the eye and their contents/volumes?", answer: "Anterior chamber (between iris and cornea; aqueous; depth ~3.11 mm, ~220 µL), posterior chamber (behind the iris, contains the lens; aqueous; ~60 µL), and the vitreous cavity (>2/3 of eye volume; ~5–6 mL of vitreous gel). Total eye volume ~6.5–7 mL." },
  { id: 3, question: "What are the three concentric coats of the globe?", answer: "Outer protective corneoscleral layer (clear cornea + opaque sclera, collagen; the cornea provides most refractive power); middle vascular uvea (iris, ciliary body, choroid — nutritive, produces aqueous, supplies the outer retina); and inner neural retina (photoreceptors and neural elements)." },
  { id: 4, question: "What are the three layers of the tear film and their sources?", answer: "Outer lipid layer (meibomian glands — limits evaporation), middle mucoaqueous layer (lacrimal/accessory lacrimal glands + conjunctival goblet cells — gel-like, growth factors, antimicrobial, provides a medium for oxygen to the avascular cornea), and the inner glycocalyx (goblet + epithelial cells — membrane-associated mucins MUC1/4/16; lets the tear film glide)." },
  { id: 5, question: "What are the functions of the tear film?", answer: "Lubrication of the cornea/conjunctiva, exchange of solutes including oxygen, antimicrobial defense, and removal of debris. The air–tear film interface is also a major refractive element of the eye." },
  { id: 6, question: "List the five layers of the cornea from anterior to posterior.", answer: "Epithelium, Bowman layer, stroma, Descemet membrane, and endothelium." },
  { id: 7, question: "Describe corneal optics and nutrition.", answer: "Refractive index 1.376; radius of curvature ~8 mm; power ~43.25 D of the eye's total ~58.60 D (the cornea supplies most refractive power) and it covers ~1/6 of the globe surface. Oxygen comes from air/eyelid vasculature via the tear film; nutrients and macromolecules come from the aqueous humor." },
  { id: 8, question: "What are corneal dimensions and the central-vs-peripheral thickness profile?", answer: "~11–12 mm horizontally and ~10–11 mm vertically. The central cornea is thinner (500–600 µm) than the periphery (~1000 µm) because the posterior surface is more curved than the anterior. The cornea is prolate (steeper centrally, flatter peripherally)." },
  { id: 9, question: "Define prolate, oblate, and hyperprolate corneal profiles.", answer: "Prolate = the typical cornea (steeper centrally, flatter peripherally). Oblate = flatter centrally (e.g., after myopic ablation). Hyperprolate = e.g., after hyperopic ablation." },
  { id: 10, question: "Give the approximate thickness of each of the five corneal layers.", answer: "Epithelium 40–50 µm, Bowman layer 8–15 µm, stroma 470–500 µm, Descemet membrane 10–12 µm, endothelium 4–6 µm." },
  { id: 11, question: "Describe the corneal epithelium — type, origin, and cell layers.", answer: "A lipophilic, nonkeratinized, stratified squamous epithelium derived from surface ectoderm; 4–6 cell layers; ~40–50 µm thick. From deep to superficial: basal cells, 2–3 layers of polygonal wing cells, and 1–2 layers of surface cells." },
  { id: 12, question: "What is the clinical significance of corneal epithelial hemidesmosomes?", answer: "Basal cells (~12 µm wide, ~6000 cells/mm²) attach to the basal lamina by hemidesmosomes. Trauma that disrupts this layer can cause recurrent corneal erosion from improper re-formation of the hemidesmosomes." },
  { id: 13, question: "Describe the corneal surface epithelial cells.", answer: "1–2 layers of very thin cells joined by tight junctions that act as a diffusion barrier; microvilli make the apical membrane irregular, but the tear film renders the surface optically smooth. Cells migrate from the basal layer toward the tears (where they are shed) and centripetally from limbal stem cells." },
  { id: 14, question: "What is Bowman layer, and how does it heal?", answer: "A modified anterior stroma (~8–15 µm) of randomly dispersed collagen fibrils. Unlike Descemet membrane, it does NOT regenerate after injury — it is replaced by scar tissue." },
  { id: 15, question: "Describe the corneal stroma's composition and thickness.", answer: "~470–500 µm (~90% of corneal thickness), composed of collagen-producing keratocytes, ground substance, and collagen lamellae — obliquely oriented in the anterior third (with interlacing) and perpendicular in the posterior two-thirds." },
  { id: 16, question: "What establishes corneal transparency, and what causes stromal clouding?", answer: "Uniform collagen fibril size and separation across the entire cornea establishes transparency; edema that separates the fibrils causes stromal clouding." },
  { id: 17, question: "Name the stromal collagen types and their roles.", answer: "Predominantly type I (with some type V). Type III (stromal wound healing), type VI (intertwined between fibrils, binding lamellae together), and type VII (anchoring fibrils of the epithelium)." },
  { id: 18, question: "What generates the stroma's swelling pressure?", answer: "Proteoglycans with negatively charged glycosaminoglycans (e.g., keratan sulfate) repel each other and draw in sodium and, secondarily, water — producing the stromal swelling pressure." },
  { id: 19, question: "Describe corneal keratocytes.", answer: "About 2.4 million keratocytes occupy ~5% of stromal volume (denser anteriorly, ~1058/mm², than posteriorly, ~771/mm²). They synthesize collagen and proteoglycans, resemble fibrocytes, and their flat profile minimizes disturbance of light transmission." },
  { id: 20, question: "What is the Dua layer?", answer: "A thin (~10–20 µm), acellular layer at the deepest stroma, highly adherent to Descemet membrane; an important landmark during deep anterior lamellar keratoplasty (DALK)." },
  { id: 21, question: "Describe Descemet membrane and how it changes with age.", answer: "The PAS-positive basal lamina of the endothelium — a true basement membrane rich in type IV collagen. It thickens with age (3–4 µm at birth → 10–12 µm in adults): an anterior banded zone formed in utero, and a posterior nonbanded zone laid down by the endothelium throughout life (~0.1 µm/year)." },
  { id: 22, question: "Distinguish Hassall-Henle warts from corneal guttae.", answer: "Hassall-Henle warts are peripheral excrescences of Descemet membrane, common with aging. Corneal guttae are central excrescences, characteristic of Fuchs endothelial dystrophy (also seen with increasing age)." },
  { id: 23, question: "Describe the corneal endothelium — structure, origin, and density.", answer: "A single layer of hexagonal cells (~4–6 µm) of neural crest (neuroectodermal) origin; ~500,000 cells in a young adult at ~3000/mm² centrally (up to ~8000/mm² peripherally). Mitosis is limited in humans and cell numbers decrease with age." },
  { id: 24, question: "What is the function of the corneal endothelium, and what happens with cell loss?", answer: "Active ion transport pumps water out of the stroma, maintaining stromal deturgescence and transparency. Cell loss (surgery, inflammation, Fuchs dystrophy) causes decompensation, stromal edema, and vision loss; because mitosis is limited, remaining cells spread and enlarge (polymegathism and pleomorphism)." },
  { id: 25, question: "What is the source and density of corneal innervation?", answer: "The cornea is the most densely innervated tissue in the body. Innervation comes from the long ciliary nerves (branches of the nasociliary nerve → ophthalmic division of the trigeminal nerve, CN V1)." },
  { id: 26, question: "Name the four layers of corneal nerves and describe their course.", answer: "Midcorneal stromal nerves, subepithelial plexus, subbasal plexus, and intraepithelial nerve terminals. Nerves enter the anterior stroma radially and lose their myelin sheath within ~1 mm of the limbus, then arborize toward the epithelium." },
  { id: 27, question: "What are the functions of corneal nerves?", answer: "They provide pain, touch, and temperature sensation and support the epithelium and wound healing via neuropeptides and growth factors. CN V1 nociception drives reflex tearing (via the lacrimal system) and blinking (via the orbicularis oculi)." },
  { id: 28, question: "Why is the limbus clinically important (three reasons)?", answer: "Its relationship to the anterior chamber angle, its use as a surgical landmark, and its supply of corneal epithelial stem cells. It is also the site of passage of the collector channels linking Schlemm canal to aqueous veins." },
  { id: 29, question: "Where do corneal epithelial stem cells reside, and what does their loss cause?", answer: "In the limbal palisades of Vogt. Diffuse limbal stem cell damage (e.g., chemical burns, trachoma) causes chronic epithelial surface disease." },
  { id: 30, question: "What are the landmarks of the corneoscleral junction?", answer: "It begins centrally in a plane connecting the end of Bowman layer and Schwalbe line (the termination of Descemet membrane); internally, its posterior limit is the anterior tip of the scleral spur." },
  { id: 31, question: "What is the surgical limbus and why is it blue-gray?", answer: "The blue/gray zone used as an external landmark for cataract and glaucoma incisions. Its blue-gray color is due to scattering of light through the oblique cornea–sclera interface (over 1–2 mm); its posterior border overlies the trabecular meshwork." },
  { id: 32, question: "Describe scleral coverage, coverings, and openings.", answer: "The sclera covers the posterior five-sixths of the globe, with an anterior opening for the cornea and a posterior opening for the optic nerve. Rectus tendons insert into superficial scleral collagen; the Tenon capsule and bulbar conjunctiva overlie it and fuse near the limbus." },
  { id: 33, question: "Describe the regional variation in scleral thickness.", answer: "Thinnest (~0.3 mm) just behind the rectus muscle insertions; thickest (~1.0 mm) at the posterior pole around the optic nerve; ~0.4–0.5 mm at the equator and ~0.6 mm anterior to the muscle insertions — important for suture placement in strabismus and retinal detachment surgery." },
  { id: 34, question: "What are the common sites of scleral rupture after blunt trauma?", answer: "The superonasal quadrant near the limbus; a circumferential arc parallel to the corneal limbus opposite the site of impact; and behind the rectus muscle insertions." },
  { id: 35, question: "Describe the episcleral vessel plexuses and their disease correlates.", answer: "The superficial episcleral plexus (radial, beneath the Tenon capsule) is inflamed in episcleritis; the deep episcleral plexus (resting on the scleral surface) is involved in scleritis. The sclera itself is essentially avascular." },
  { id: 36, question: "What are the scleral emissaria, and which one transmits vortex veins / choroidal melanoma?", answer: "Anterior emissaria (anterior ciliary arteries), middle emissaria (vortex veins), and posterior emissaria (lamina cribrosa; short/long posterior ciliary vessels and nerves). Extraocular extension of choroidal melanoma occurs via the middle emissaria." },
  { id: 37, question: "What are Axenfeld loops?", answer: "Branches of the ciliary nerves that loop out through the sclera posterior to the nasal and temporal limbus. They are sometimes pigmented and can be mistaken for uveal tissue or malignant melanoma." },
  { id: 38, question: "Why is the sclera opaque while the cornea is clear?", answer: "The sclera has greater variation in collagen fibril separation and diameter and more fibril interweaving (its fibers also taper and are discontinuous). The cornea's uniform, evenly spaced fibrils plus avascularity keep it transparent." },
  { id: 39, question: "What comprises the blood–ocular barrier?", answer: "Intercellular junctions of adjacent cells forming the blood–aqueous barrier and the blood–retina barrier (inner and outer). It prevents extravasation of intravascular contents into the eye." },
  { id: 40, question: "How does the avascular cornea obtain oxygen and nutrients?", answer: "Oxygen comes from the air (and eyelid vasculature) dissolved in the tear film; nutrients and macromolecules come from the aqueous humor." },
  { id: 41, question: "What is the current model of tear film structure?", answer: "A 2-phase model — an outer lipid layer overlying a mucoaqueous phase, with the glycocalyx resting on the epithelial microvilli — replacing the older 3-layer 'sandwich' model. Total thickness is ~3.4 µm." },
  { id: 42, question: "What are the key volume, composition, and physical values of the tear film?", answer: "Volume ~7.4 µL (unanesthetized) / ~2.6 µL (anesthetized), decreasing with age. Water 98.2%, solid 1.8%. Osmolarity 296–308 mOsm/L, pH 6.5–7.6, turnover 12–16%/min (up to ~300% when stimulated)." },
  { id: 43, question: "Why is there never a need for more than one eyedrop at a time?", answer: "The eye holds only ~25–30 µL of fluid before overflow, and an average eyedrop is ~45 µL — so a second simultaneous drop is simply wasted." },
  { id: 44, question: "What are the functions of the tear film lipid layer?", answer: "It impedes tear evaporation, contributes to the tear film's optical properties, maintains a hydrophobic barrier that prevents tear overflow (by lowering surface tension), and prevents tears from damaging the eyelid margin." },
  { id: 45, question: "What secretes the lipid layer, and how is it innervated?", answer: "It is ~43 nm thick and secreted mainly by the meibomian (tarsal) glands (~30–40 in the upper lid, 20–30 in the lower), which are innervated by cholinesterase-positive parasympathetic (VIP) nerves and abundant NPY nerves. The glands of Zeis also contribute lipid." },
  { id: 46, question: "How does the meibum melting point relate to meibomian gland dysfunction?", answer: "Normal meibum melts at ~32–40°C; in MGD the melting point rises and secretions stagnate/inspissate. Warm compresses can increase tear film lipid layer thickness by more than 80% within 5 minutes." },
  { id: 47, question: "What are the functions of the mucoaqueous layer?", answer: "It transmits oxygen to the avascular epithelium, maintains constant electrolyte composition, provides antibacterial/antiviral defense, smooths corneal surface irregularities, converts the epithelium from hydrophobic to hydrophilic (for even tear spread), reduces surface tension (stabilizing the film), and lubricates the lids." },
  { id: 48, question: "What are the sources of the aqueous tear component?", answer: "The main lacrimal gland (orbital and palpebral lobes, divided by the lateral horn of the levator aponeurosis) and the accessory glands — glands of Krause (2/3 of accessory glands, in the lateral upper fornix) and glands of Wolfring (along the proximal tarsal margin)." },
  { id: 49, question: "Describe the tear electrolytes and their roles.", answer: "Na⁺ parallels serum, while K⁺ is 5–7× serum. Na⁺/K⁺/Cl⁻ regulate osmotic flow of fluid from cornea to tears (supporting corneal clarity); HCO3⁻ buffers pH; and Fe²⁺/Cu²⁺/Mg²⁺/Ca²⁺/PO4³⁻ act as enzyme cofactors." },
  { id: 50, question: "How does tear glucose behave in diabetes?", answer: "Fasting tear glucose is 3.6–4.1 mg/mL in people with or without diabetes; after a 100-mg oral glucose load, tear glucose exceeds 11 mg/mL in 96% of diabetic individuals tested." },
  { id: 51, question: "What immunoglobulins and antimicrobial proteins are in tears?", answer: "The predominant immunoglobulin is secretory IgA (IgM, IgD, and IgE are also present). Antimicrobial constituents include lysozyme, lactoferrin, group II phospholipase A2, lipocalins, defensins, and interferon (which inhibits viral replication and may limit herpetic keratitis)." },
  { id: 52, question: "What roles do MMP-9 and ICAM-1 play in dry eye?", answer: "Tear MMP-9 (elevated in Sjögren syndrome, graft-vs-host disease, and after LASIK) cleaves basement membrane and tight-junction proteins, parallels corneal staining, and marks late-stage dry eye. ICAM-1 upregulation drives lymphocyte migration into lacrimal/conjunctival tissue; lifitegrast blocks lymphocyte–ICAM adhesion." },
  { id: 53, question: "What are the two main types of mucin, and their sources?", answer: "Secreted mucins (gel-forming and soluble) released into the tears mainly by conjunctival goblet cells, and membrane-spanning mucins embedded in the epithelial cell membrane (expressed by conjunctival/corneal stratified squamous cells). Goblet cells make only ~2–3 µL/day of mucin vs ~2–3 mL/day of aqueous." },
  { id: 54, question: "How does mucous discharge differ among ocular surface diseases?", answer: "Stringy, thin, translucent mucus is characteristic of dry eye; globular and crusting mucus occurs in infection; and thick, tenacious, stretchy strands occur in vernal conjunctivitis." },
  { id: 55, question: "How is reflex tear secretion controlled?", answer: "It is neurally mediated: sensory stimulation (mechanical/thermal/chemical), psychogenic factors, or bright light activate a reflex via CN V1 afferents that drives parasympathetic and sympathetic efferents to the lacrimal glands. All lacrimal glands function as one unit with the ocular surface and brain." },
  { id: 56, question: "Describe the vicious circle of dry eye disease.", answer: "Ocular surface inflammation (from blepharitis, drugs, conjunctivitis, contact lenses, surgery, or autoimmune disease) releases cytokines that block neural signals for tear secretion, downregulating lacrimal gland function and worsening the dry eye. Tear turnover is lower in symptomatic (~5%) than asymptomatic (~12%) dry eye." },
  { id: 57, question: "How do hormones influence tear secretion?", answer: "Peptide hormones (α-MSH and ACTH) stimulate protein secretion from the main lacrimal gland; androgens (steroid hormones) stimulate sIgA secretion from the lacrimal gland and lipid secretion from the meibomian glands." },
  { id: 58, question: "How does blinking maintain the tear film?", answer: "A complete blink compresses the fornices, clears debris and insoluble mucin, and expresses meibomian secretions; the lower lid moves nasally to push tears toward the puncta. On opening, the upper lid draws the mucoaqueous phase up by capillary action and the lipid spreads instantly. Incomplete/infrequent blinking (e.g., Parkinson disease, Bell palsy) causes dry eye." },
  { id: 59, question: "What finding is diagnostic of dry eye, and what tests are used?", answer: "Elevated tear osmolarity is diagnostic. Tests include tear breakup time, fluorescein/lissamine green/rose bengal staining, osmolarity testing, the Schirmer test, tear meniscus evaluation, and MMP-9 testing." },
  { id: 60, question: "Which agents are FDA-approved for the inflammatory component of dry eye, and how do they work?", answer: "Topical cyclosporine A emulsion (a fungus-derived peptide that stimulates aqueous tear production, especially in autoimmune dry eye) and lifitegrast (an LFA-1 antagonist that blocks ICAM-1/LFA-1 binding). Because T cells live ~90 days, these agents take time to reach full effect." },
  { id: 61, question: "How is corneal avascularity maintained?", answer: "By soluble VEGF receptor 1 (sFlt-1), which binds and blocks the angiogenic VEGF-A present in the cornea. Suppressing sFlt-1 frees VEGF-A and causes corneal vascularization. Avascularity preserves optical clarity and contributes to corneal immune privilege." },
  { id: 62, question: "How does the cornea obtain oxygen and glucose metabolically?", answer: "Oxygen comes from the tear film (air + eyelid vasculature) and aqueous humor. Glucose (the main substrate for epithelium, keratocytes, and endothelium) reaches the stroma from the aqueous by carrier-mediated transport through the endothelium, and the epithelium by diffusion through the stroma and from the tear film; the tear film/limbal vessels supply only ~10%." },
  { id: 63, question: "What are the three glucose metabolic pathways in the cornea, and a keratocyte peculiarity?", answer: "The hexose monophosphate (HMP) shunt, the TCA cycle, and glycolysis. The HMP shunt handles 35–65% of glucose in the epithelium/endothelium but very little in keratocytes, which lack 6-phosphogluconate dehydrogenase. NADPH from the HMP shunt protects the cornea from UV-generated oxidative stress." },
  { id: 64, question: "What are the consequences of corneal lactic acid accumulation?", answer: "Anaerobic conditions (e.g., tight, low-oxygen-permeability contact lenses) increase lactic acid, causing edema (an osmotic load) or stromal acidosis that alters endothelial morphology and function — a cause of contact lens overwear complications." },
  { id: 65, question: "What is the role of aldehyde dehydrogenase and transketolase in the cornea?", answer: "Together they make up 40–50% of the soluble proteins of the corneal stroma. Like the enzyme crystallins of the lens, they contribute to the cornea's optical properties and protect corneal cells from free radicals by absorbing UV-B radiation." },
  { id: 66, question: "What are the key biomechanical principles of the cornea?", answer: "The paracentral/peripheral cornea is stiffer than the center; elastic strength is greatest anteriorly and decreases posteriorly (so LASIK flap creation weakens the cornea and can contribute to ectasia). Corneal stiffness increases with age from natural collagen crosslinking — so the need for CXL in ectatic disorders falls with age." },
  { id: 67, question: "What is the corneal epithelial glycocalyx, and the epithelial migration rate?", answer: "Apical microvilli/microplicae are coated with glycocalyx (mucin glycoproteins) that promotes tear film stability and surface wettability. During healing, epithelial cells migrate ~2 mm/day — a rate adversely affected by preservatives in topical eyedrops." },
  { id: 68, question: "Where do corneal epithelial stem cells reside, and what are transient amplifying cells?", answer: "Stem cells reside mainly in the limbal palisades of Vogt (with recent evidence of some central corneal stem cells). Central basal cells proliferate actively; limbal basal cells are slow-cycling stem cells plus their progeny, the transient amplifying (TA) cells (regulated by growth factors, cytokines, and ECM). Amniotic membrane can upregulate TA cells to enhance healing." },
  { id: 69, question: "What determines drug penetration across the cornea?", answer: "Hydrophilic molecules penetrate the epithelium poorly (small polar molecules <500 Da may pass tight junctions; damaged/inflamed epithelium is more permeable). To cross the lipophilic epithelium a molecule should be uncharged, but to penetrate the hydrophilic stroma it should be charged — so an ideal drug can dissociate at physiologic pH and temperature within the stroma." },
  { id: 70, question: "Describe Bowman layer's composition and clinical relevance.", answer: "Randomly packed type I and V collagen fibers (~30 nm) in a proteoglycan/glycoprotein matrix; secreted in embryogenesis by anterior keratocytes and epithelium; acellular and non-regenerating. It shields keratocytes from epithelial growth factors (e.g., TGF-β); removing it in PRK/LASEK can cause corneal haze, whereas LASIK transects but retains it (central haze is rare)." },
  { id: 71, question: "Describe the corneal stromal architecture that produces transparency.", answer: "~90% of corneal thickness; ~200 lamellae (1.5–2.5 µm) of collagen fibrils. Fibrils are uniform (~30 nm), run parallel from limbus to limbus within a lamella, and are regularly spaced (~55–60 nm center-to-center) — obliquely oriented anteriorly and perpendicular posteriorly. This uniform diameter and regular spacing produce transparency; the stroma is less compact posteriorly (allowing deeper intrastromal ring segments)." },
  { id: 72, question: "What are the collagen and proteoglycan components of the corneal stroma?", answer: "Type I collagen predominates (~70% of stromal dry weight; types V, VI, VII, XII, XIV also present; type III in wound healing). Proteoglycans (~10% of dry weight) carry hygroscopic glycosaminoglycans — keratan sulfate, chondroitin sulfate, and dermatan sulfate — that attract water and generate the stroma's swelling pressure." },
  { id: 73, question: "Which MMPs are present in the healthy vs the injured cornea?", answer: "Only the MMP-2 proenzyme is found in the healthy cornea; after injury MMP-1, -3, and -9 are synthesized. Proteinase inhibitors (from the cornea, tears, aqueous humor, and limbal vessels) restrict damage during inflammation, ulceration, and wound healing." },
  { id: 74, question: "Describe Descemet membrane's composition and change with age (physiology).", answer: "A specialized basement membrane (10–12 µm) secreted by the endothelium, with an anterior banded layer and a posterior nonbanded layer laid down throughout life — making it 3–4× thicker in adulthood than at birth. Type IV collagen is the most abundant." },
  { id: 75, question: "How does corneal endothelial cell density change with age?", answer: "~4000 cells/mm² in the first decade, ~2600/mm² at age 40, with physiologic loss of ~0.6%/year; the minimum density for adequate function is ~400–700 cells/mm². Endothelial cells do not replicate — neighboring cells enlarge to cover losses." },
  { id: 76, question: "Describe corneal endothelial cell structure and junctions.", answer: "A monolayer of hexagonal cells (~20 µm diameter) with complex interdigitations and tight junctions (a barrier to aqueous humor), but no desmosomes; ~20–30 microvilli per cell project into the aqueous humor." },
  { id: 77, question: "How does the endothelium keep the cornea clear?", answer: "It acts as both a permeability barrier and a pump: temperature-dependent Na⁺/K⁺-ATPase pumps water out of the stroma (generating negative hydrostatic pressure that also holds LASIK flaps in place), maintaining stromal hydration at ~78% for transparency. It obtains its oxygen from the aqueous humor. GAG-driven swelling pressure opposes this pump; barrier breakdown (trauma, acute hydrops) causes edema and loss of clarity." },
  { id: 78, question: "What is a retrocorneal fibrous membrane (RCFM)?", answer: "After severe corneal injury, infiltrating polymorphonuclear leukocytes can transform endothelial cells into fibroblastic cells that lay down a fibrous membrane (predominantly type I collagen) between Descemet membrane and the endothelium, significantly reducing visual acuity." },
];

const BASIC_ANATOMY_MCQ: MCQ[] = [
  { id: 1, question: "The radius of curvature of the cornea is approximately:", options: ["8 mm", "12 mm", "24 mm", "4 mm"], correct: 0, explanation: "The cornea's radius (~8 mm) is smaller (steeper) than the sclera's (~12 mm)." },
  { id: 2, question: "The average volume of the anterior chamber is about:", options: ["60 µL", "220 µL", "5 mL", "1 mL"], correct: 1, explanation: "The anterior chamber holds ~220 µL; the posterior chamber ~60 µL." },
  { id: 3, question: "Which compartment makes up more than two-thirds of the eye's volume?", options: ["Anterior chamber", "Posterior chamber", "Vitreous cavity", "Suprachoroidal space"], correct: 2, explanation: "The vitreous cavity (~5–6 mL) is the largest compartment." },
  { id: 4, question: "The middle vascular coat (uvea) consists of:", options: ["Cornea, sclera, limbus", "Iris, ciliary body, choroid", "Retina, RPE, choroid", "Lens, zonules, vitreous"], correct: 1, explanation: "The uvea = iris + ciliary body + choroid; it is nutritive and produces aqueous humor." },
  { id: 5, question: "The outer lipid layer of the tear film is secreted by the:", options: ["Lacrimal gland", "Conjunctival goblet cells", "Meibomian glands", "Corneal epithelium"], correct: 2, explanation: "Meibomian glands secrete the lipid layer, which limits tear evaporation." },
  { id: 6, question: "Membrane-associated mucins (MUC1, 4, 16) are found in the tear film's:", options: ["Lipid layer", "Mucoaqueous layer", "Glycocalyx", "Corneal stroma"], correct: 2, explanation: "The glycocalyx contains membrane-associated mucins that let the tear film glide over the epithelium." },
  { id: 7, question: "Most of the eye's total refractive power is provided by the:", options: ["Lens", "Cornea (~43 D of ~59 D)", "Vitreous", "Aqueous humor"], correct: 1, explanation: "The cornea provides ~43.25 D of the eye's ~58.60 D total power." },
  { id: 8, question: "The central cornea is thinner than the periphery because:", options: ["The epithelium is thicker peripherally", "The posterior corneal surface is more curved than the anterior", "Tear film pools centrally", "Of limbal stem cells"], correct: 1, explanation: "Greater posterior curvature makes the center (500–600 µm) thinner than the periphery (~1000 µm)." },
  { id: 9, question: "Which corneal layer does NOT regenerate and heals by scarring?", options: ["Epithelium", "Bowman layer", "Descemet membrane", "Endothelium"], correct: 1, explanation: "Bowman layer is replaced by scar tissue after injury; Descemet, by contrast, regenerates." },
  { id: 10, question: "Basal corneal epithelial cells attach to the basal lamina by:", options: ["Tight junctions", "Desmosomes", "Hemidesmosomes", "Gap junctions"], correct: 2, explanation: "Hemidesmosomes anchor basal cells to the basal lamina/Bowman layer." },
  { id: 11, question: "Disruption of corneal epithelial hemidesmosomes can cause:", options: ["Fuchs dystrophy", "Recurrent corneal erosion", "Keratoconus", "Band keratopathy"], correct: 1, explanation: "Improper hemidesmosome re-formation after trauma leads to recurrent corneal erosion." },
  { id: 12, question: "Corneal surface epithelial cells act as a diffusion barrier because of their:", options: ["Hemidesmosomes", "Tight junctions", "Microvilli", "Desmosomes"], correct: 1, explanation: "Tight junctions between surface cells form the epithelial diffusion barrier." },
  { id: 13, question: "The corneal epithelium is derived from:", options: ["Neural crest", "Surface ectoderm", "Mesoderm", "Neuroectoderm"], correct: 1, explanation: "The corneal epithelium arises from surface ectoderm (the endothelium, from neural crest)." },
  { id: 14, question: "The stroma constitutes approximately what fraction of corneal thickness?", options: ["10%", "50%", "90%", "25%"], correct: 2, explanation: "The stroma (~470–500 µm) is ~90% of the cornea's thickness." },
  { id: 15, question: "Corneal stromal transparency is primarily due to:", options: ["Avascularity alone", "Uniform collagen fibril size and separation", "High water content", "A thick epithelium"], correct: 1, explanation: "Uniform fibril diameter and spacing (with avascularity) produces transparency; edema disrupts it." },
  { id: 16, question: "The predominant collagen type in the corneal stroma is:", options: ["Type IV", "Type I", "Type VII", "Type II"], correct: 1, explanation: "Type I predominates (with some type V); type IV is in Descemet membrane." },
  { id: 17, question: "Which collagen forms the anchoring fibrils of the corneal epithelium?", options: ["Type I", "Type III", "Type VII", "Type VI"], correct: 2, explanation: "Type VII forms epithelial anchoring fibrils; type III is in wound healing and type VI binds lamellae." },
  { id: 18, question: "Descemet membrane is especially rich in which collagen?", options: ["Type I", "Type IV", "Type V", "Type VII"], correct: 1, explanation: "As a true basement membrane, Descemet is rich in type IV collagen." },
  { id: 19, question: "The Dua layer is an important surgical landmark during:", options: ["LASIK", "Deep anterior lamellar keratoplasty (DALK)", "Trabeculectomy", "Cataract surgery"], correct: 1, explanation: "The pre-Descemet (Dua) layer is a landmark in DALK." },
  { id: 20, question: "With age, Descemet membrane:", options: ["Thins", "Thickens (3–4 µm at birth to 10–12 µm in adults)", "Stays constant", "Disappears"], correct: 1, explanation: "The endothelium adds a posterior nonbanded zone throughout life, thickening Descemet." },
  { id: 21, question: "Peripheral excrescences of Descemet membrane common with aging are called:", options: ["Corneal guttae", "Hassall-Henle warts", "Kayser-Fleischer rings", "Vogt striae"], correct: 1, explanation: "Peripheral excrescences are Hassall-Henle warts; central ones are guttae." },
  { id: 22, question: "Central corneal guttae are characteristic of:", options: ["Keratoconus", "Fuchs endothelial dystrophy", "Recurrent erosion", "Band keratopathy"], correct: 1, explanation: "Central Descemet excrescences (guttae) are the hallmark of Fuchs endothelial dystrophy." },
  { id: 23, question: "The corneal endothelium is derived from:", options: ["Surface ectoderm", "Neural crest (neuroectoderm)", "Mesoderm", "Endoderm"], correct: 1, explanation: "The endothelium is of neural crest (neuroectodermal) origin." },
  { id: 24, question: "The primary function of the corneal endothelium is:", options: ["Refraction", "Active ion transport that pumps water out of the stroma (deturgescence)", "Tear production", "Sensation"], correct: 1, explanation: "Endothelial ion pumps keep the stroma dehydrated (deturgescent) and clear." },
  { id: 25, question: "Central corneal endothelial cell density in a young adult is approximately:", options: ["500/mm²", "3000/mm²", "8000/mm²", "12,000/mm²"], correct: 1, explanation: "Central density is ~3000/mm² (up to ~8000/mm² peripherally), falling with age." },
  { id: 26, question: "After endothelial cell loss, the remaining cells characteristically show:", options: ["Mitosis and normalization", "Polymegathism and pleomorphism", "Keratinization", "Scarring"], correct: 1, explanation: "With limited mitosis, cells spread/enlarge (polymegathism) and vary in shape (pleomorphism)." },
  { id: 27, question: "The cornea is innervated by branches of:", options: ["The facial nerve (CN VII)", "The long ciliary nerves from the nasociliary nerve (CN V1)", "The optic nerve (CN II)", "The maxillary nerve (CN V2)"], correct: 1, explanation: "Corneal innervation is from the long ciliary (nasociliary) nerves, i.e., ophthalmic division of CN V." },
  { id: 28, question: "Corneal nerves lose their myelin sheath:", options: ["At the corneal center", "Within ~1 mm of the limbus", "In Descemet membrane", "In the endothelium"], correct: 1, explanation: "Nerves demyelinate within ~1 mm of the limbus to preserve transparency." },
  { id: 29, question: "Which tissue has the highest density of nerve terminals of any epithelium in the body?", options: ["Skin", "Corneal epithelium", "Conjunctiva", "Nasal mucosa"], correct: 1, explanation: "The corneal epithelium is the most densely innervated epithelial tissue." },
  { id: 30, question: "Corneal epithelial stem cells reside in the:", options: ["Central cornea", "Limbal palisades of Vogt", "Conjunctival fornix", "Endothelium"], correct: 1, explanation: "Limbal stem cells reside in the palisades of Vogt; their loss causes surface disease." },
  { id: 31, question: "Diffuse limbal stem cell damage (e.g., chemical burns) leads to:", options: ["Fuchs dystrophy", "Chronic epithelial surface disease", "Cataract", "Glaucoma"], correct: 1, explanation: "Loss of the limbal stem cell source produces chronic corneal epithelial surface disease." },
  { id: 32, question: "Schwalbe line represents the termination of:", options: ["Bowman layer", "Descemet membrane", "The trabecular meshwork", "The scleral spur"], correct: 1, explanation: "Schwalbe line is the peripheral termination of Descemet membrane at the angle." },
  { id: 33, question: "The blue-gray appearance of the surgical limbus is due to:", options: ["Pigment", "Light scatter through the oblique cornea–sclera interface", "Blood vessels", "Melanin deposition"], correct: 1, explanation: "Light scattering across the gradual (1–2 mm) corneoscleral interface gives the blue-gray zone." },
  { id: 34, question: "The sclera covers what portion of the globe surface?", options: ["One-sixth", "One-half", "The posterior five-sixths", "The entire globe"], correct: 2, explanation: "The sclera covers the posterior five-sixths; the cornea covers ~one-sixth." },
  { id: 35, question: "The sclera is thinnest:", options: ["At the posterior pole", "Just behind the rectus muscle insertions (~0.3 mm)", "At the equator", "At the limbus"], correct: 1, explanation: "The sclera is thinnest (~0.3 mm) just behind the rectus insertions — a perforation risk in muscle surgery." },
  { id: 36, question: "The sclera is thickest:", options: ["At the equator", "At the posterior pole around the optic nerve (~1.0 mm)", "Behind the rectus insertions", "At the limbus"], correct: 1, explanation: "It is thickest (~1.0 mm) at the posterior pole around the optic nerve head." },
  { id: 37, question: "In episcleritis, the inflamed vascular layer is the:", options: ["Deep episcleral plexus", "Superficial episcleral plexus", "Intrascleral plexus", "Choroid"], correct: 1, explanation: "Episcleritis involves the superficial episcleral plexus; scleritis involves the deep plexus." },
  { id: 38, question: "Choroidal melanoma extends extraocularly most commonly via the:", options: ["Anterior emissaria", "Middle emissaria (vortex veins)", "Posterior emissaria", "Lamina cribrosa"], correct: 1, explanation: "Extraocular extension of choroidal melanoma occurs via the middle emissaria (vortex vein channels)." },
  { id: 39, question: "Axenfeld loops are:", options: ["Scleral vessels", "Pigmented ciliary nerve loops that can mimic melanoma", "Trabecular structures", "Limbal stem cell niches"], correct: 1, explanation: "Axenfeld loops are ciliary nerve loops through the sclera, sometimes pigmented and mistaken for melanoma." },
  { id: 40, question: "Vortex veins exit the globe through the:", options: ["Anterior emissaria", "Middle emissaria", "Posterior emissaria", "Optic canal"], correct: 1, explanation: "Vortex (vorticose) veins exit via the middle emissaria." },
  { id: 41, question: "The sclera's opacity (vs corneal clarity) is due mainly to:", options: ["Higher water content", "Greater variation in collagen fibril diameter/separation and more interweaving", "Melanin content", "Rich vascularity"], correct: 1, explanation: "Irregular, interwoven, tapering scleral collagen scatters light, unlike the cornea's uniform fibrils." },
  { id: 42, question: "The blood–ocular barrier comprises the:", options: ["Cornea and lens", "Blood–aqueous and blood–retina barriers", "Tear film and conjunctiva", "Sclera and episclera"], correct: 1, explanation: "Intercellular junctions form the blood–aqueous and (inner/outer) blood–retina barriers." },
  { id: 43, question: "The cornea derives its nutrients and macromolecules from the:", options: ["Retinal vessels", "Aqueous humor", "Scleral vessels", "Choroid"], correct: 1, explanation: "The avascular cornea gets nutrients/macromolecules from aqueous humor and oxygen from the tear film." },
  { id: 44, question: "The typical (normal) corneal profile is:", options: ["Oblate", "Prolate (steeper centrally)", "Perfectly spherical", "Hyperprolate"], correct: 1, explanation: "The normal cornea is prolate — steeper centrally, flattening peripherally." },
  { id: 45, question: "After myopic ablation, the cornea typically becomes:", options: ["Prolate", "Oblate (flatter centrally)", "Hyperprolate", "Spherical"], correct: 1, explanation: "Myopic ablation flattens the center, producing an oblate profile." },
  { id: 46, question: "The average anterior chamber depth in an emmetropic eye is approximately:", options: ["1.5 mm", "3.11 mm", "5 mm", "0.5 mm"], correct: 1, explanation: "Average anterior chamber depth is ~3.11 mm." },
  { id: 47, question: "The corneal stroma's swelling pressure is generated by:", options: ["Endothelial pumping", "Negatively charged proteoglycan glycosaminoglycans drawing in sodium and water", "Collagen contraction", "Tear film osmolarity"], correct: 1, explanation: "Negatively charged GAGs (keratan sulfate) draw in Na⁺ and water, creating swelling pressure that the endothelium counteracts." },
  { id: 48, question: "Wing cells of the corneal epithelium are located:", options: ["Deepest, on the basal lamina", "Between the basal and surface cells", "At the very surface", "In the stroma"], correct: 1, explanation: "Polygonal wing cells lie in 2–3 layers between the basal and surface cells." },
  { id: 49, question: "The Tenon capsule and bulbar conjunctiva fuse:", options: ["At the posterior pole", "Near the limbus", "At the equator", "At the optic nerve"], correct: 1, explanation: "The Tenon capsule and bulbar conjunctiva fuse near the limbus." },
  { id: 50, question: "The refractive index of the cornea is approximately:", options: ["1.000", "1.376", "1.500", "1.336"], correct: 1, explanation: "The corneal refractive index is ~1.376." },
  { id: 51, question: "The current model of the tear film is:", options: ["A 3-layer sandwich (lipid, aqueous, mucin)", "A 2-phase model (lipid over mucoaqueous)", "A single homogeneous layer", "Aqueous over lipid"], correct: 1, explanation: "Evidence supports a 2-phase model — a lipid layer over a mucoaqueous phase — not the older 3-layer model." },
  { id: 52, question: "The approximate total thickness of the tear film is:", options: ["0.5 µm", "3.4 µm", "40 µm", "100 µm"], correct: 1, explanation: "OCT/reflectometry put the tear film at ~3.4 µm thick." },
  { id: 53, question: "Normal tear film osmolarity is:", options: ["250–270 mOsm/L", "296–308 mOsm/L", "350–400 mOsm/L", "500 mOsm/L"], correct: 1, explanation: "Normal tear osmolarity is 296–308 mOsm/L." },
  { id: 54, question: "Elevated tear film osmolarity is diagnostic of:", options: ["Glaucoma", "Dry eye disease", "Keratoconus", "Cataract"], correct: 1, explanation: "Hyperosmolarity of the tears is diagnostic of dry eye syndrome." },
  { id: 55, question: "Why is there no benefit to instilling two eyedrops at once?", options: ["Drops are too small", "The eye holds only ~25–30 µL and a drop is ~45 µL", "The cornea is impermeable", "Reflex tearing halts absorption"], correct: 1, explanation: "One ~45-µL drop already exceeds the eye's ~25–30 µL capacity, so a second drop overflows." },
  { id: 56, question: "The tear film lipid layer is secreted mainly by the:", options: ["Lacrimal gland", "Goblet cells", "Meibomian (tarsal) glands", "Glands of Krause"], correct: 2, explanation: "Meibomian glands secrete the lipid layer; the glands of Zeis contribute some lipid." },
  { id: 57, question: "In meibomian gland dysfunction, the meibum melting point:", options: ["Decreases", "Increases, so secretions stagnate", "Is unchanged", "Falls to room temperature"], correct: 1, explanation: "MGD raises the melting point (~35°C vs ~32°C normal), causing inspissation." },
  { id: 58, question: "The tear potassium concentration compared with serum is:", options: ["Equal", "5–7 times higher", "Half", "Ten times lower"], correct: 1, explanation: "Tear K⁺ is 5–7× serum, whereas Na⁺ parallels serum." },
  { id: 59, question: "Which electrolyte primarily buffers tear pH?", options: ["Sodium", "Potassium", "Bicarbonate", "Calcium"], correct: 2, explanation: "Bicarbonate (HCO3⁻) regulates tear pH (~6.5–7.6)." },
  { id: 60, question: "The predominant immunoglobulin in tears is:", options: ["IgG", "IgM", "Secretory IgA", "IgE"], correct: 2, explanation: "Secretory IgA predominates; IgM, IgD, and IgE are also present." },
  { id: 61, question: "Secreted (gel-forming) mucins are produced mainly by:", options: ["Corneal endothelium", "Conjunctival goblet cells", "Meibomian glands", "The lacrimal gland"], correct: 1, explanation: "Conjunctival goblet cells release secreted mucins; membrane-spanning mucins come from epithelial cells." },
  { id: 62, question: "Which mucous discharge is characteristic of vernal conjunctivitis?", options: ["Stringy, thin, translucent", "Globular and crusting", "Thick, tenacious, stretchy strands", "Purely watery"], correct: 2, explanation: "Thick, tenacious, stretchy mucus is typical of vernal conjunctivitis; stringy/thin = dry eye; globular = infection." },
  { id: 63, question: "Reflex tear secretion is triggered by all of the following EXCEPT:", options: ["Corneal sensory stimulation", "Bright light", "Psychogenic factors", "Sleep"], correct: 3, explanation: "Reflex tearing responds to irritation, bright light, and emotion; secretion falls (not rises) during sleep." },
  { id: 64, question: "Tear turnover rate in symptomatic dry eye compared with asymptomatic dry eye is:", options: ["Higher", "Lower (~5% vs ~12%)", "Identical", "Zero"], correct: 1, explanation: "Symptomatic dry eye has a lower turnover (~5%) than asymptomatic (~12%)." },
  { id: 65, question: "Androgens stimulate secretion of:", options: ["Aqueous only", "sIgA from the lacrimal gland and lipid from meibomian glands", "Mucin only", "Bicarbonate"], correct: 1, explanation: "Androgens drive lacrimal sIgA and meibomian lipid secretion; α-MSH/ACTH drive lacrimal protein secretion." },
  { id: 66, question: "Incomplete or infrequent blinking (e.g., Parkinson disease, Bell palsy) causes:", options: ["Glaucoma", "Dry eye from ineffective tear distribution", "Cataract", "Uveitis"], correct: 1, explanation: "Poor blink dynamics leave the tear film unevenly distributed, producing dry eye." },
  { id: 67, question: "Which two topical agents are FDA-approved for the inflammatory component of dry eye?", options: ["Timolol and latanoprost", "Cyclosporine A and lifitegrast", "Ganciclovir and foscarnet", "Atropine and pilocarpine"], correct: 1, explanation: "Cyclosporine A emulsion and lifitegrast target dry-eye inflammation." },
  { id: 68, question: "Lifitegrast reduces dry-eye inflammation by:", options: ["Blocking histamine", "Antagonizing LFA-1 to block ICAM-1/LFA-1 binding", "Inhibiting carbonic anhydrase", "Stabilizing mast cells"], correct: 1, explanation: "Lifitegrast is an LFA-1 antagonist that blocks lymphocyte adhesion to ICAM-1." },
  { id: 69, question: "Corneal avascularity is maintained by:", options: ["VEGF-A", "Soluble VEGF receptor 1 (sFlt-1)", "MMP-9", "Bicarbonate"], correct: 1, explanation: "sFlt-1 binds and neutralizes angiogenic VEGF-A; its suppression causes corneal vascularization." },
  { id: 70, question: "The primary metabolic substrate of the cornea is:", options: ["Fatty acids", "Glucose", "Ketone bodies", "Lactate"], correct: 1, explanation: "Glucose is the main substrate for the epithelium, keratocytes, and endothelium." },
  { id: 71, question: "The corneal stroma receives most of its glucose from the:", options: ["Tear film", "Limbal vessels", "Aqueous humor (via carrier-mediated transport through the endothelium)", "Air"], correct: 2, explanation: "The endothelium carrier-transports glucose from aqueous humor to the stroma; tears/limbal vessels supply only ~10%." },
  { id: 72, question: "Which enzyme do keratocytes lack, limiting their HMP shunt activity?", options: ["Na⁺/K⁺-ATPase", "6-phosphogluconate dehydrogenase", "Carbonic anhydrase", "Aldehyde dehydrogenase"], correct: 1, explanation: "Keratocytes lack 6-phosphogluconate dehydrogenase, so they run little HMP shunt (unlike epithelium/endothelium)." },
  { id: 73, question: "NADPH generated by the HMP shunt primarily functions to:", options: ["Produce ATP", "Protect the cornea from oxidative (UV) stress", "Pump water out of the stroma", "Synthesize collagen"], correct: 1, explanation: "NADPH is a reducing agent that defends the constantly UV-exposed cornea against oxidative stress." },
  { id: 74, question: "Corneal lactic acid accumulation (e.g., from tight contact lenses) causes:", options: ["Improved clarity", "Stromal edema and acidosis", "Corneal vascularization", "Increased endothelial density"], correct: 1, explanation: "Anaerobic lactic acid buildup causes osmotic edema and stromal acidosis — a contact-lens overwear complication." },
  { id: 75, question: "Aldehyde dehydrogenase and transketolase make up what fraction of soluble corneal stromal proteins?", options: ["5%", "20%", "40–50%", "90%"], correct: 2, explanation: "Together they are 40–50% of soluble stromal proteins and (like lens crystallins) aid optical properties and UV-B protection." },
  { id: 76, question: "Regarding corneal biomechanics, which is TRUE?", options: ["The center is stiffer than the periphery", "Elastic strength is greatest anteriorly and decreases posteriorly", "Stiffness decreases with age", "The posterior stroma is strongest"], correct: 1, explanation: "Anterior stroma is strongest; anterior-lamella disruption (LASIK flap) weakens the cornea and can lead to ectasia." },
  { id: 77, question: "Why is corneal haze more common after PRK/LASEK than after LASIK?", options: ["LASIK removes more tissue", "PRK/LASEK removes Bowman layer, exposing keratocytes to epithelial growth factors", "LASIK uses more laser energy", "PRK damages the endothelium"], correct: 1, explanation: "Removing Bowman layer exposes keratocytes to TGF-β and other growth factors, causing haze; LASIK retains Bowman." },
  { id: 78, question: "The normal rate of corneal epithelial cell migration during healing is:", options: ["0.2 mm/day", "2 mm/day", "10 mm/day", "0.02 mm/day"], correct: 1, explanation: "Epithelial migration is ~2 mm/day and is slowed by eyedrop preservatives." },
  { id: 79, question: "Corneal epithelial stem cells reside mainly in the:", options: ["Central endothelium", "Limbal palisades of Vogt", "Descemet membrane", "Conjunctival fornix"], correct: 1, explanation: "Stem cells reside in the limbal palisades of Vogt (with some evidence of central corneal stem cells)." },
  { id: 80, question: "To cross the lipophilic corneal epithelium, an organic drug molecule should be:", options: ["Charged", "Uncharged", "Very large", "Highly protein-bound"], correct: 1, explanation: "It should be uncharged to cross the lipophilic epithelium but charged to penetrate the hydrophilic stroma — so it must dissociate at physiologic pH." },
  { id: 81, question: "Hydrophilic molecules may pass through corneal epithelial tight junctions if their mass is below:", options: ["100 Da", "500 Da", "5000 Da", "50,000 Da"], correct: 1, explanation: "Small polar molecules under ~500 Da can pass paracellularly through epithelial tight junctions." },
  { id: 82, question: "Bowman layer is composed of randomly packed collagen types:", options: ["I and V", "IV only", "II and IX", "III only"], correct: 0, explanation: "Bowman layer contains randomly packed type I and V collagen (~30 nm) in a proteoglycan/glycoprotein matrix." },
  { id: 83, question: "When damaged, Bowman layer:", options: ["Regenerates fully", "Does not regenerate (it is acellular)", "Is replaced by endothelium", "Thickens with scar", "None"], correct: 1, explanation: "Bowman layer is acellular and does not regenerate; loss (e.g., in PRK) predisposes to haze." },
  { id: 84, question: "The corneal stroma comprises approximately what fraction of corneal thickness?", options: ["10%", "50%", "90%", "25%"], correct: 2, explanation: "The stroma is ~90% of corneal thickness (~200 lamellae)." },
  { id: 85, question: "The average corneal stromal collagen fibril diameter is approximately:", options: ["5 nm", "30 nm", "300 nm", "1000 nm"], correct: 1, explanation: "Uniform ~30-nm fibrils with regular ~55–60 nm spacing produce transparency." },
  { id: 86, question: "The major collagen of the corneal stroma (~70% of dry weight) is:", options: ["Type IV", "Type I", "Type VII", "Type III"], correct: 1, explanation: "Type I predominates (~70% of stromal dry weight); type IV is in Descemet, type III in wound healing." },
  { id: 87, question: "The glycosaminoglycans of the corneal stroma include all of the following EXCEPT:", options: ["Keratan sulfate", "Chondroitin sulfate", "Dermatan sulfate", "Hyaluronic acid"], correct: 3, explanation: "Corneal stromal GAGs are keratan, chondroitin, and dermatan sulfate — not hyaluronic acid." },
  { id: 88, question: "Which MMP is present in the healthy, uninjured cornea?", options: ["MMP-1", "The MMP-2 proenzyme", "MMP-9", "MMP-3"], correct: 1, explanation: "Only MMP-2 proenzyme is found in healthy cornea; MMP-1, -3, and -9 appear after injury." },
  { id: 89, question: "The most abundant collagen in Descemet membrane is:", options: ["Type I", "Type IV", "Type V", "Type VII"], correct: 1, explanation: "As a basement membrane, Descemet is richest in type IV collagen." },
  { id: 90, question: "The minimum corneal endothelial cell density required for adequate function is approximately:", options: ["100 cells/mm²", "400–700 cells/mm²", "2000 cells/mm²", "3000 cells/mm²"], correct: 1, explanation: "Below ~400–700 cells/mm² the endothelial pump can no longer maintain deturgescence." },
  { id: 91, question: "The corneal endothelium maintains stromal hydration at approximately:", options: ["50%", "78%", "90%", "98%"], correct: 1, explanation: "The Na⁺/K⁺-ATPase pump keeps the stroma at ~78% hydration for clarity." },
  { id: 92, question: "Corneal endothelial cells are joined by ___ but never have ___:", options: ["Desmosomes; tight junctions", "Tight junctions; desmosomes", "Gap junctions; hemidesmosomes", "Hemidesmosomes; tight junctions"], correct: 1, explanation: "Endothelial cells form tight junctions (a barrier to aqueous) but never desmosomes." },
];

const BCSC_CSC_RECALL: RecallQ[] = [
  { id: 1, question: "What is central serous chorioretinopathy (CSC), and at what anatomic level does the leak occur?", answer: "CSC (also called central serous retinopathy, CSR) is an idiopathic serous detachment of the neurosensory retina caused by leakage at the level of the retinal pigment epithelium (RPE). The leak is secondary to hyperpermeability of the choriocapillaris, as demonstrated on indocyanine green angiography (ICGA)." },
  { id: 2, question: "Trace the historical understanding of CSC through von Graefe, Maumenee, and Gass.", answer: "Von Graefe first described it in 1866 as 'recurrent central retinitis.' Maumenee first performed fluorescein angioscopy and localized the leak to the RPE — not the retinal vessels, which had been the presumed source. Gass then described the fluorescein angiography (FA) findings, suggested laser photocoagulation, and proposed that the disease was due to choriocapillaris hyperpermeability — a hypothesis confirmed decades later by ICGA." },
  { id: 3, question: "What are the classic demographics of CSC?", answer: "It occurs primarily in persons aged 35–55 years, with a male-to-female ratio of 3:1. There are currently no reliable statistics suggesting any association with race." },
  { id: 4, question: "What symptoms do patients with CSC describe?", answer: "Sudden onset of blurred or dim vision, micropsia, metamorphopsia, paracentral scotomata, decreased color vision, and prolonged afterimages." },
  { id: 5, question: "What is the typical visual acuity in CSC, and how can it often be improved?", answer: "Visual acuity ranges from 20/20 to 20/200, but in most patients it is better than 20/30. Decreased acuity can often be improved with a small hyperopic correction (the serous elevation shortens the effective axial length)." },
  { id: 6, question: "Describe the fundus and FA appearance of acute CSC.", answer: "Acute CSC shows a round or oval retinal elevation in the macula that often involves the fovea. FA reveals leaks from the RPE that may appear early as a focal dot (the 'dot' form) or as a tree-/mushroom-shaped ascent of dye in the subretinal space (the 'smokestack' form). Acute CSC usually has one or a few leaks." },
  { id: 7, question: "What is the grayish-white, feathered-edge subretinal material sometimes seen with vigorous leaks, and what is it believed to be?", answer: "It is subretinal fibrin. Vigorous leaks can deposit this grayish-white, feathered-edge material, which is generally believed to be fibrin." },
  { id: 8, question: "How does chronic CSC differ from acute CSC on examination and FA?", answer: "Chronic CSC shows granular RPE pigmentation; FA reveals many small, sometimes inconspicuous leaks; and there is widespread shallow detachment with areas of photoreceptor atrophy (rather than the single/few discrete leaks and focal elevation of the acute form)." },
  { id: 9, question: "Clinical pearl: what do RPE changes on OCT or FAF suggest?", answer: "RPE changes on optical coherence tomography (OCT) or fundus autofluorescence (FAF) may indicate previous episodes of CSC and resolved subretinal fluid." },
  { id: 10, question: "What personality and psychological factors are associated with CSC?", answer: "CSC is associated with stress and with a tense, driven ('type A') personality." },
  { id: 11, question: "List the systemic associations of CSC.", answer: "Endogenous hypercortisolism (Cushing syndrome), hypertension, sleep apnea, use of psychopharmacologic medications, and pregnancy." },
  { id: 12, question: "How does corticosteroid use relate to CSC, and what is the curious exception?", answer: "Use of systemic corticosteroids by any route — intramuscular, topical, inhalational, epidural, or intra-articular — is associated with CSC. Curiously, use of intraocular corticosteroids does not appear to be associated with the condition." },
  { id: 13, question: "Which patients are particularly vulnerable to severe, chronic CSC?", answer: "Organ transplant recipients and patients with autoimmune disease requiring long-standing, high-dose steroids are particularly vulnerable to the more severe and chronic variants." },
  { id: 14, question: "What does fundus autofluorescence (FAF) show in CSC?", answer: "FAF shows accumulation of shed photoreceptor outer segments in the subretinal space and distributed RPE defects. In chronic CSC, descending tracts created by gravitating fluid are seen on both FA and FAF." },
  { id: 15, question: "What are the white dots seen under the retina in some CSC eyes theorized to be?", answer: "They are theorized to be macrophages containing fluorophores derived from phagocytized photoreceptor outer segments — outer segments that could not be phagocytized by the RPE because the subretinal fluid physically separated the retina from the RPE." },
  { id: 16, question: "What does enhanced depth imaging OCT (EDI-OCT) demonstrate in CSC?", answer: "EDI-OCT shows thickening of the choroid, and in areas where thickening is most prominent, posterior loculation of fluid in the deep choroid." },
  { id: 17, question: "What is the role of ICGA in CSC today, and what has largely replaced it?", answer: "ICGA can reveal choroidal vascular hyperpermeability, but it has largely been supplanted by OCT — even for detecting possible coexisting CNV. OCT angiography is particularly adept at detecting secondary CNV." },
  { id: 18, question: "How often is choroidal neovascularization (CNV) present in CSC, and in whom?", answer: "CNV may be present in up to 20% of cases in individuals older than 50 years. OCT angiography is well suited to detect this secondary CNV." },
  { id: 19, question: "What entities are in the differential diagnosis of CSC, and why can they be hard to distinguish?", answer: "Type 1 CNV and polypoidal choroidal vasculopathy (PCV, a variant of type 1 CNV). FA findings overlap significantly — both show fluorescein leakage and poor visualization of structures beneath the RPE. Complicating matters, type 1 CNV and PCV appear to be associated with CSC and may be its sequelae." },
  { id: 20, question: "How does OCT help distinguish CNV associated with CSC?", answer: "If CNV is present, OCT demonstrates an irregular, wavy, shallow elevation of the RPE by a layer of material with heterogeneous reflectivity. The neovascularization is generally easy to detect with OCT angiography." },
  { id: 21, question: "What is the pachychoroid phenotype, and how does it relate to CSC?", answer: "Pachychoroid ('pachy' = thick) is a thick, poorly tessellated choroid phenotype commonly seen in CSC. Its recognition was facilitated by widespread use of EDI-OCT, which allows quantitative evaluation of the choroid." },
  { id: 22, question: "Describe the spectrum of pachychoroid disease.", answer: "It ranges from pigment epithelial changes over a thick, poorly tessellated choroid (pachychoroid pigment epitheliopathy), to pachyvessels that on cross-sectional OCT appear to compress and obliterate the overlying choriocapillaris, to polypoidal choroidal vasculopathy (PCV)." },
  { id: 23, question: "Why has the definition of pachychoroid shifted toward pathogenesis, and what are pachyvessels now understood to be?", answer: "Increased choroidal thickness is not universally present, so a more pathogenesis-based definition is used. Pachyvessels are actually dilated anastomotic venous channels running across the choroidal watershed zones and traversing the macula. This points to choroidal venous insufficiency as a potential unifying underlying pathogenesis." },
  { id: 24, question: "What is the natural history and prognosis of CSC?", answer: "CSC is generally self-limited and resolves spontaneously, with the majority of patients attaining excellent visual results. However, it can be destructive in some chronic cases, causing visually significant scotomata." },
  { id: 25, question: "What is the role of verteporfin photodynamic therapy (PDT) in CSC?", answer: "PDT — at full fluence or, more commonly, reduced fluence — decreases or eliminates subretinal fluid, decreases choroidal thickness, and reduces choroidal vascular hyperpermeability. It is guided by FA or ICGA, using a spot large enough to cover the main leakage point(s) with a surrounding safety margin of 1000 µm." },
  { id: 26, question: "What is the risk profile of PDT, and can it be repeated?", answer: "Full-fluence PDT (in AMD studies) carries a ~4% risk of vision decrease. CSC may recur after successful PDT, but therapy can be repeated safely — especially if lower fluence is used and the lesion is extrafoveal." },
  { id: 27, question: "Why is laser photocoagulation no longer preferred for CSC?", answer: "Secondary CNV occurred in the immediate postoperative period in up to 2% of eyes treated with photocoagulation. Moreover, unlike PDT, photocoagulation has no effect on choroidal thickness." },
  { id: 28, question: "What is the evidence for mineralocorticoid receptor antagonists (eplerenone/spironolactone) in CSC?", answer: "Anecdotal reports suggested some benefit, but the randomized, double-blind, placebo-controlled VICI trial of eplerenone in chronic CSC (25 mg/day for 1 week, increasing to 50 mg/day for up to 12 months) demonstrated no benefit." },
];

const BCSC_CSC_MCQ: MCQ[] = [
  { id: 1, question: "The serous detachment in central serous chorioretinopathy results from leakage at the level of the:", options: ["Retinal vascular endothelium (inner blood–retina barrier)", "Retinal pigment epithelium (RPE)", "Internal limiting membrane", "Bruch membrane only"], correct: 1, explanation: "CSC is an idiopathic serous detachment from leakage at the level of the RPE, secondary to choriocapillaris hyperpermeability." },
  { id: 2, question: "The underlying vascular abnormality in CSC, confirmed by ICGA, is:", options: ["Retinal capillary nonperfusion", "Choriocapillaris (choroidal) hyperpermeability", "Retinal arteriolar occlusion", "Optic nerve head neovascularization"], correct: 1, explanation: "CSC leakage is secondary to hyperpermeability of the choriocapillaris, as seen on ICGA — a hypothesis originally proposed by Gass." },
  { id: 3, question: "Who first localized the CSC leak to the RPE (rather than retinal vessels) using fluorescein angioscopy?", options: ["Von Graefe", "Maumenee", "Gass", "Spaide"], correct: 1, explanation: "Maumenee first performed fluorescein angioscopy and found the leak at the level of the RPE, not the retinal vessels." },
  { id: 4, question: "Von Graefe's original 1866 term for CSC was:", options: ["Idiopathic macular telangiectasia", "Recurrent central retinitis", "Central serous retinopathy", "Pachychoroid pigment epitheliopathy"], correct: 1, explanation: "Von Graefe described the condition in 1866 as 'recurrent central retinitis.'" },
  { id: 5, question: "Gass contributed all of the following to the understanding of CSC EXCEPT:", options: ["Describing the FA findings", "Suggesting laser photocoagulation as treatment", "Proposing choriocapillaris hyperpermeability as the cause", "Performing the first ICGA confirming the choroidal source"], correct: 3, explanation: "Gass described FA, suggested laser, and proposed choriocapillaris hyperpermeability; ICGA confirmation came decades later, not from Gass." },
  { id: 6, question: "The classic demographic for CSC is:", options: ["Women aged 20–30, ratio 3:1 female", "Men aged 35–55, ratio 3:1 male", "Children under 10, no sex predilection", "Adults over 70, ratio 3:1 male"], correct: 1, explanation: "CSC occurs primarily in persons aged 35–55, with a male-to-female ratio of 3:1." },
  { id: 7, question: "Regarding a racial association with CSC, current data indicate:", options: ["A strong predilection for persons of African descent", "A strong predilection for persons of Asian descent", "No reliable statistics suggesting any race association", "Exclusive occurrence in persons of European descent"], correct: 2, explanation: "At present there are no reliable statistics suggesting any association with race." },
  { id: 8, question: "Which symptom is characteristic of CSC?", options: ["Macropsia", "Micropsia", "Nyctalopia", "Sudden painless total vision loss"], correct: 1, explanation: "CSC symptoms include micropsia, metamorphopsia, paracentral scotomata, decreased color vision, dim/blurred vision, and prolonged afterimages." },
  { id: 9, question: "In most patients with CSC, visual acuity is:", options: ["Worse than 20/200", "Between 20/100 and 20/200", "Better than 20/30", "Exactly 20/20 in all cases"], correct: 2, explanation: "Acuity ranges from 20/20 to 20/200, but in most patients it is better than 20/30." },
  { id: 10, question: "Decreased visual acuity in CSC can often be improved with:", options: ["A small myopic correction", "A small hyperopic correction", "Prism correction", "Cycloplegia"], correct: 1, explanation: "The serous elevation of the retina induces relative hyperopia, so a small hyperopic correction often improves acuity." },
  { id: 11, question: "On FA, a 'smokestack' pattern in acute CSC represents:", options: ["A window defect from RPE atrophy", "A tree-/mushroom-shaped ascent of dye in the subretinal space", "Late staining of a fibrous scar", "Blocked fluorescence from subretinal blood"], correct: 1, explanation: "The smokestack is a vertical, tree-shaped movement of dye up into the subretinal space; the dot form is a focal early leak." },
  { id: 12, question: "Acute CSC typically presents on FA with:", options: ["Dozens of pinpoint leaks throughout the posterior pole", "One or a few discrete leaks", "No leakage at all", "A lacy early neovascular network"], correct: 1, explanation: "Acute CSC generally presents with one or a few leaks; chronic CSC shows many small, sometimes inconspicuous leaks." },
  { id: 13, question: "A grayish-white, feathered-edge subretinal material seen with vigorous leaks in CSC is believed to be:", options: ["Hard exudate (lipid)", "Fibrin", "Subretinal blood", "Drusenoid material"], correct: 1, explanation: "Vigorous leaks can deposit grayish-white, feathered-edge subretinal material generally believed to be fibrin." },
  { id: 14, question: "Which finding is characteristic of CHRONIC rather than acute CSC?", options: ["A single focal RPE leak with a smokestack", "Granular RPE pigmentation with widespread shallow detachment and photoreceptor atrophy", "A round foveal elevation with a single dot leak", "Complete absence of RPE changes"], correct: 1, explanation: "Chronic CSC shows granular pigmentation, many small leaks, widespread shallow detachment, and photoreceptor atrophy." },
  { id: 15, question: "RPE changes on OCT or FAF in a patient with current CSC most usefully indicate:", options: ["Coexisting glaucoma", "Previous episodes of CSC and resolved subretinal fluid", "Active choroidal melanoma", "Diabetic macular ischemia"], correct: 1, explanation: "The clinical pearl: RPE changes on OCT or FAF may suggest previous CSC episodes and resolved subretinal fluid." },
  { id: 16, question: "CSC is classically associated with which personality description?", options: ["Passive and withdrawn", "Tense, driven personality under stress", "Impulsive and disinhibited", "No personality association exists"], correct: 1, explanation: "CSC is associated with stress and a tense, driven personality." },
  { id: 17, question: "Which is NOT among the recognized systemic associations of CSC?", options: ["Cushing syndrome (endogenous hypercortisolism)", "Obstructive sleep apnea", "Pregnancy", "Hypothyroidism"], correct: 3, explanation: "Recognized associations include Cushing syndrome, hypertension, sleep apnea, psychopharmacologic medication use, and pregnancy — not hypothyroidism." },
  { id: 18, question: "Regarding corticosteroids and CSC, which statement is correct?", options: ["Only oral steroids are associated; other routes are safe", "Systemic steroids by many routes are associated, but intraocular steroids curiously are not", "Intraocular steroids are the strongest risk factor", "No form of corticosteroid is associated with CSC"], correct: 1, explanation: "Systemic steroids (IM, topical, inhalational, epidural, intra-articular) are associated; curiously, intraocular steroids are not." },
  { id: 19, question: "Which patients are most vulnerable to severe, chronic CSC?", options: ["Young myopes with no medications", "Organ transplant recipients and autoimmune patients on long-standing high-dose steroids", "Patients with treated ocular hypertension", "Emmetropic children"], correct: 1, explanation: "Organ transplant recipients and autoimmune patients requiring long-standing high-dose steroids are particularly vulnerable to severe, chronic variants." },
  { id: 20, question: "On fundus autofluorescence, the subretinal accumulation in CSC represents:", options: ["Lipofuscin within intact RPE only", "Shed photoreceptor outer segments plus distributed RPE defects", "Subretinal hemorrhage", "Calcified drusen"], correct: 1, explanation: "FAF shows accumulation of shed photoreceptor outer segments in the subretinal space and distributed RPE defects." },
  { id: 21, question: "The white dots seen under the retina in some CSC eyes are theorized to be:", options: ["Cotton-wool spots", "Macrophages containing fluorophores from phagocytized outer segments", "Hard drusen", "Fibrin clumps"], correct: 1, explanation: "The white dots are theorized to be macrophages containing fluorophores derived from phagocytized photoreceptor outer segments." },
  { id: 22, question: "Why do outer-segment–derived fluorophores accumulate subretinally in CSC?", options: ["The RPE overproduces lipofuscin", "Subretinal fluid physically separates the retina from the RPE, so the RPE cannot phagocytize shed outer segments", "Photoreceptors migrate into the choroid", "The choriocapillaris phagocytizes them"], correct: 1, explanation: "The fluid separates retina from RPE, preventing RPE phagocytosis of shed outer segments, which then accumulate." },
  { id: 23, question: "'Descending tracts' seen on FA and FAF in chronic CSC are produced by:", options: ["Radial peripapillary capillary dropout", "Gravitational tracking of subretinal fluid", "Retinal arteriolar sheathing", "Vitreomacular traction bands"], correct: 1, explanation: "Chronic CSC eyes can display descending tracts created by gravitating subretinal fluid on both FA and FAF." },
  { id: 24, question: "Enhanced depth imaging OCT (EDI-OCT) in CSC characteristically shows:", options: ["A thin, highly tessellated choroid", "Choroidal thickening with posterior loculation of fluid in the deep choroid", "Choroidal calcification", "Complete choriocapillaris atrophy"], correct: 1, explanation: "EDI-OCT shows choroidal thickening and, where thickening is greatest, posterior loculation of fluid in the deep choroid." },
  { id: 25, question: "For most imaging tasks in CSC today, ICGA has been largely supplanted by:", options: ["Fluorescein angiography", "Optical coherence tomography (including OCT angiography)", "B-scan ultrasonography", "Fundus photography alone"], correct: 1, explanation: "Although ICGA reveals choroidal hyperpermeability, OCT (including OCTA) has largely supplanted it, even for detecting coexisting CNV." },
  { id: 26, question: "Coexisting CNV may be present in up to what percentage of CSC cases in patients older than 50?", options: ["1%", "5%", "20%", "60%"], correct: 2, explanation: "CNV may be present in up to 20% of cases in individuals older than 50 years." },
  { id: 27, question: "Which imaging modality is described as especially adept at detecting secondary CNV in CSC?", options: ["Fundus autofluorescence", "OCT angiography", "Fluorescein angiography", "Color fundus photography"], correct: 1, explanation: "OCT angiography seems particularly adept at detecting secondary CNV developing in CSC patients." },
  { id: 28, question: "The differential diagnosis of CSC prominently includes:", options: ["Type 1 CNV and polypoidal choroidal vasculopathy (PCV)", "Optic neuritis and papilledema", "Retinal artery occlusion and cotton-wool spots", "Retinoblastoma and Coats disease"], correct: 0, explanation: "Type 1 CNV and PCV (a variant of type 1 CNV) are key differentials, with overlapping FA leakage and poor sub-RPE visualization." },
  { id: 29, question: "PCV is best characterized as:", options: ["A form of retinal vasculitis", "A variant of type 1 CNV", "A hereditary photoreceptor dystrophy", "A choroidal tumor"], correct: 1, explanation: "Polypoidal choroidal vasculopathy is described as a variant of type 1 CNV and is part of the pachychoroid spectrum." },
  { id: 30, question: "On OCT, CNV associated with CSC typically appears as:", options: ["A dome-shaped solid choroidal mass", "An irregular, wavy, shallow RPE elevation with heterogeneous reflectivity", "A full-thickness macular hole", "Cystoid spaces in the inner retina only"], correct: 1, explanation: "CNV in CSC shows an irregular, wavy, shallow RPE elevation by material of heterogeneous reflectivity." },
  { id: 31, question: "Type 1 CNV and PCV complicate CSC management partly because they:", options: ["Never occur together with CSC", "Appear to be associated with CSC and may be its sequelae", "Are always congenital", "Respond only to laser photocoagulation"], correct: 1, explanation: "Type 1 CNV and PCV appear to be associated with CSC and may be its sequelae, complicating treatment decisions." },
  { id: 32, question: "The pachychoroid phenotype refers to a choroid that is:", options: ["Abnormally thin and highly tessellated", "Thick (or with dilated pachyvessels) and poorly tessellated", "Completely avascular", "Calcified"], correct: 1, explanation: "Pachychoroid ('pachy' = thick) describes a thick, poorly tessellated choroid phenotype commonly seen in CSC." },
  { id: 33, question: "Recognition of the pachychoroid phenotype was facilitated by:", options: ["Widespread use of enhanced depth imaging OCT (EDI-OCT)", "Fluorescein angiography", "Electroretinography", "Visual field testing"], correct: 0, explanation: "Widespread EDI-OCT use enabled quantitative evaluation of the choroid and recognition of the thick (pachy) phenotype." },
  { id: 34, question: "Order the pachychoroid spectrum from earliest to most advanced.", options: ["PCV → pachyvessels → pachychoroid pigment epitheliopathy", "Pachychoroid pigment epitheliopathy → pachyvessels → PCV", "Pachyvessels → PCV → pachychoroid pigment epitheliopathy", "PCV → pachychoroid pigment epitheliopathy → pachyvessels"], correct: 1, explanation: "The spectrum ranges from pachychoroid pigment epitheliopathy, to pachyvessels, to PCV." },
  { id: 35, question: "On cross-sectional OCT, pachyvessels appear to:", options: ["Compress and obliterate the overlying choriocapillaris", "Replace the RPE", "Fill the vitreous cavity", "Form within the inner retina"], correct: 0, explanation: "Pachyvessels appear to compress and obliterate the overlying choriocapillaris on cross-sectional OCT." },
  { id: 36, question: "The more pathogenesis-based view now holds that pachyvessels are:", options: ["Neovascular tufts from the retinal circulation", "Dilated anastomotic venous channels crossing the choroidal watershed zones", "Dilated choroidal arteries", "Lymphatic channels"], correct: 1, explanation: "Pachyvessels are now understood as dilated anastomotic venous channels crossing the choroidal watershed zones and traversing the macula." },
  { id: 37, question: "The proposed unifying underlying pathogenesis of the pachychoroid spectrum is:", options: ["Choroidal venous insufficiency", "Retinal arterial hypertension", "Autoimmune choroiditis", "RPE senescence alone"], correct: 0, explanation: "Dilated anastomotic venous channels across watershed zones point to choroidal venous insufficiency as a unifying pathogenesis." },
  { id: 38, question: "The natural history of most CSC is:", options: ["Rapidly progressive blindness", "Generally self-limited with spontaneous resolution and excellent visual results", "Uniformly requiring urgent surgery", "Progression to retinal detachment in all cases"], correct: 1, explanation: "CSC is generally self-limited and resolves spontaneously, with most patients attaining excellent visual results." },
  { id: 39, question: "Chronic CSC can be destructive by causing:", options: ["Angle-closure glaucoma", "Visually significant scotomata", "Corneal decompensation", "Lens dislocation"], correct: 1, explanation: "In some chronic cases CSC is destructive, causing visually significant scotomata." },
  { id: 40, question: "Verteporfin PDT in CSC does all of the following EXCEPT:", options: ["Decrease or eliminate subretinal fluid", "Decrease choroidal thickness", "Reduce choroidal vascular hyperpermeability", "Reliably eradicate any coexisting type 1 CNV"], correct: 3, explanation: "PDT decreases subretinal fluid, choroidal thickness, and choroidal hyperpermeability; eradicating CNV is not its described effect in CSC." },
  { id: 41, question: "PDT for CSC is more commonly performed at:", options: ["Full fluence only", "Reduced fluence", "No light activation", "Double fluence"], correct: 1, explanation: "PDT is used at full fluence or, more commonly, reduced fluence for CSC." },
  { id: 42, question: "When treating CSC with PDT, the recommended surrounding safety margin around the leakage point is:", options: ["100 µm", "500 µm", "1000 µm", "2000 µm"], correct: 2, explanation: "A spot large enough to cover the main leak(s) with a surrounding safety margin of 1000 µm is used." },
  { id: 43, question: "PDT for CSC is guided by:", options: ["OCT alone", "Fluorescein angiography or ICGA", "Visual fields", "Electro-oculography"], correct: 1, explanation: "PDT treatment is guided by either FA or ICGA to localize the main leakage point(s)." },
  { id: 44, question: "Full-fluence PDT (from AMD studies) carries approximately what risk of vision decrease?", options: ["0.1%", "4%", "20%", "50%"], correct: 1, explanation: "Full-fluence PDT is associated with roughly a 4% risk of vision decrease in AMD studies." },
  { id: 45, question: "Repeat PDT for recurrent CSC is safest when:", options: ["Full fluence is used and the lesion is subfoveal", "Lower fluence is used and the lesion is extrafoveal", "It is combined with laser photocoagulation", "It is performed monthly indefinitely"], correct: 1, explanation: "CSC may recur after PDT; retreatment can be done safely, especially with lower fluence and an extrafoveal lesion." },
  { id: 46, question: "Laser photocoagulation is no longer preferred for CSC largely because:", options: ["It causes cataract in most patients", "Secondary CNV occurred in the immediate postoperative period in up to 2% of treated eyes", "It requires general anesthesia", "It has no effect on subretinal fluid"], correct: 1, explanation: "Up to 2% of laser-treated eyes developed secondary CNV in the immediate postoperative period; laser also does not affect choroidal thickness." },
  { id: 47, question: "Compared with PDT, laser photocoagulation for CSC differs in that it:", options: ["Also decreases choroidal thickness", "Has no effect on choroidal thickness", "Reduces choroidal hyperpermeability more effectively", "Carries no risk of CNV"], correct: 1, explanation: "Unlike PDT, photocoagulation has no effect on choroidal thickness (and carries a CNV risk)." },
  { id: 48, question: "The VICI randomized controlled trial of eplerenone for chronic CSC demonstrated:", options: ["A large treatment benefit", "No benefit from the medication", "Increased risk of stroke", "Superiority over PDT"], correct: 1, explanation: "The VICI trial (eplerenone 25 mg/day increasing to 50 mg/day for up to 12 months) demonstrated no benefit in chronic CSC." },
];

const RETINA_HTN_RECALL: RecallQ[] = [
  { id: 1, question: "Which vascular structures does hypertension affect, and why are they significant?", answer: "Hypertension affects the arterioles and capillaries — the anatomical loci of both autoregulation and nonperfusion. This is why hypertensive damage manifests as autoregulatory failure and areas of retinal nonperfusion." },
  { id: 2, question: "What are focal intraretinal periarteriolar transudates (FIPTs), and when do they appear?", answer: "FIPTs are lesions produced by an acute hypertensive episode at the precapillary level. On fundus exam they appear as small, deep, tan lesions; on fluorescein angiography they show punctate hyperfluorescence." },
  { id: 3, question: "What are cotton-wool spots (soft exudates), and what do they indicate?", answer: "Cotton-wool spots are infarctions of the nerve fiber layer (NFL) and indicate ischemia of the retinal NFL. On fundus exam they are large, superficial, white lesions; on angiography they correspond to areas of nonperfusion." },
  { id: 4, question: "What does uncontrolled systemic hypertension lead to at the retinal level?", answer: "Nonperfusion at various retinal levels, along with neuronal loss and associated scotomata." },
  { id: 5, question: "List the more chronic hypertensive retinal lesions.", answer: "Microaneurysms, intraretinal microvascular abnormalities (IRMAs), blot hemorrhages, lipid exudates (hard exudates), venous beading, and neovascularization." },
  { id: 6, question: "Why is it difficult to classify which retinal vascular changes are caused strictly by hypertension?", answer: "The relationship between hypertensive and arteriosclerotic vascular changes is complex, varying with the duration and severity of hypertension, the presence of diabetic retinopathy, the severity of dyslipidemia, the patient's age, and smoking history." },
  { id: 7, question: "What is the predictive value of focal arteriolar narrowing and arteriovenous (AV) nicking for hypertension severity?", answer: "These often-cited features have little predictive value for the severity of hypertension." },
  { id: 8, question: "What does the Modified Scheie Classification of Hypertensive Retinopathy mostly describe?", answer: "It is a well-known historical classification of mostly arteriosclerotic retinopathy (not purely hypertensive change)." },
  { id: 9, question: "What are Modified Scheie Grades 0 and 1?", answer: "Grade 0: No changes. Grade 1: Barely detectable arterial narrowing." },
  { id: 10, question: "What is Modified Scheie Grade 2?", answer: "Obvious arterial narrowing with focal irregularities." },
  { id: 11, question: "What is Modified Scheie Grade 3?", answer: "Grade 2 changes plus retinal hemorrhages and/or exudates." },
  { id: 12, question: "What is Modified Scheie Grade 4?", answer: "Grade 3 changes plus optic nerve head swelling." },
  { id: 13, question: "What vascular occlusive and aneurysmal complications can hypertension cause?", answer: "Branch retinal artery occlusion (BRAO), branch retinal vein occlusion (BRVO), central retinal vein occlusion (CRVO), and retinal arterial macroaneurysms." },
  { id: 14, question: "What is the effect of coexisting hypertension and diabetes on the retina?", answer: "The coexistence of hypertension and diabetes results in more severe retinopathy." },
  { id: 15, question: "Contrast the fundus appearance of cotton-wool spots versus FIPTs.", answer: "Cotton-wool spots are large, superficial, and white. FIPTs are small, deep, and tan." },
  { id: 16, question: "On fluorescein angiography, what corresponds to cotton-wool spots versus FIPTs?", answer: "Cotton-wool spots correspond to areas of nonperfusion; FIPTs correspond to punctate hyperfluorescence." },
  { id: 17, question: "Describe the fundus findings of an acute (malignant) hypertensive episode.", answer: "Optic nerve head edema, dilated capillaries on the nerve head, flame-shaped NFL hemorrhages, cotton-wool spots, macular exudates, and subretinal fluid — elements of both retinopathy and optic neuropathy." },
  { id: 18, question: "What does OCT show in an acute hypertensive episode?", answer: "Fluid in the nerve fiber layer, the retina, and the subretinal space." },
  { id: 19, question: "What happens to acute hypertensive retinopathy after antihypertensive therapy is started?", answer: "Resolution of optic nerve head edema and reduction in retinal exudation and hemorrhage over the following months." },
  { id: 20, question: "In whom does hypertensive choroidopathy typically occur?", answer: "In young patients who have experienced an episode of acute, severe hypertension." },
  { id: 21, question: "What systemic conditions are associated with hypertensive choroidopathy?", answer: "Preeclampsia, eclampsia, pheochromocytoma, and renal hypertension." },
  { id: 22, question: "What choroidal pathology occurs in hypertensive choroidopathy?", answer: "Lobular nonperfusion of the choriocapillaris." },
];

const RETINA_HTN_MCQ: MCQ[] = [
  { id: 1, question: "Hypertension primarily affects which vascular structures — the anatomical loci of autoregulation and nonperfusion?", options: ["Large retinal veins", "Arterioles and capillaries", "Choroidal melanocytes", "The central retinal artery trunk"], correct: 1, explanation: "Hypertension affects arterioles and capillaries, the loci of both autoregulation and nonperfusion." },
  { id: 2, question: "Focal intraretinal periarteriolar transudates (FIPTs) form at which level?", options: ["Postcapillary venules", "The precapillary level", "The choriocapillaris", "The subretinal space"], correct: 1, explanation: "An acute hypertensive episode can produce FIPTs at the precapillary level." },
  { id: 3, question: "Cotton-wool spots represent:", options: ["Lipid deposits in the outer plexiform layer", "Infarctions (ischemia) of the nerve fiber layer", "Microaneurysm leakage", "Drusen"], correct: 1, explanation: "Cotton-wool spots are NFL infarctions indicating ischemia of the retinal nerve fiber layer." },
  { id: 4, question: "Cotton-wool spots are also known as:", options: ["Hard exudates", "Soft exudates", "Roth spots", "Hollenhorst plaques"], correct: 1, explanation: "Cotton-wool spots are also called soft exudates." },
  { id: 5, question: "Lipid exudates are also called:", options: ["Soft exudates", "Hard exudates", "Cotton-wool spots", "FIPTs"], correct: 1, explanation: "Lipid exudates are also called hard exudates." },
  { id: 6, question: "All of the following are CHRONIC hypertensive retinal lesions EXCEPT:", options: ["Microaneurysms", "IRMAs", "Focal intraretinal periarteriolar transudates (FIPTs)", "Venous beading"], correct: 2, explanation: "FIPTs are a feature of an ACUTE hypertensive episode; microaneurysms, IRMAs, and venous beading are chronic lesions." },
  { id: 7, question: "Uncontrolled systemic hypertension leads to all of the following EXCEPT:", options: ["Retinal nonperfusion", "Neuronal loss", "Scotomata", "Enhanced retinal autoregulation"], correct: 3, explanation: "Uncontrolled hypertension causes nonperfusion, neuronal loss, and scotomata — it impairs, not enhances, autoregulation." },
  { id: 8, question: "The often-cited features of focal arteriolar narrowing and arteriovenous nicking:", options: ["Are highly predictive of hypertension severity", "Have little predictive value for hypertension severity", "Are pathognomonic for malignant hypertension", "Indicate diabetic retinopathy"], correct: 1, explanation: "These features have been shown to have little predictive value for the severity of hypertension." },
  { id: 9, question: "The Modified Scheie Classification mostly describes:", options: ["Diabetic retinopathy", "Mostly arteriosclerotic retinopathy", "Venous occlusive disease", "Choroidal neovascularization"], correct: 1, explanation: "The Modified Scheie Classification is a historical classification of mostly arteriosclerotic retinopathy." },
  { id: 10, question: "Modified Scheie Grade 1 is:", options: ["No changes", "Barely detectable arterial narrowing", "Obvious narrowing with focal irregularities", "Optic nerve head swelling"], correct: 1, explanation: "Grade 1 is barely detectable arterial narrowing." },
  { id: 11, question: "Modified Scheie Grade 2 is:", options: ["Barely detectable narrowing", "Obvious arterial narrowing with focal irregularities", "Hemorrhages and exudates", "Disc swelling"], correct: 1, explanation: "Grade 2 is obvious arterial narrowing with focal irregularities." },
  { id: 12, question: "Modified Scheie Grade 3 adds what to Grade 2?", options: ["Optic nerve head swelling", "Retinal hemorrhages and/or exudates", "Arteriolar narrowing", "Neovascularization"], correct: 1, explanation: "Grade 3 is Grade 2 plus retinal hemorrhages and/or exudates." },
  { id: 13, question: "Modified Scheie Grade 4 is distinguished by:", options: ["Retinal hemorrhages", "Exudates", "Optic nerve head swelling", "Focal arteriolar narrowing"], correct: 2, explanation: "Grade 4 is Grade 3 plus optic nerve head swelling." },
  { id: 14, question: "In the Modified Scheie Classification, which grade FIRST includes retinal hemorrhages and/or exudates?", options: ["Grade 1", "Grade 2", "Grade 3", "Grade 4"], correct: 2, explanation: "Hemorrhages and/or exudates first appear at Grade 3." },
  { id: 15, question: "Hypertension may be complicated by all of the following EXCEPT:", options: ["Branch retinal artery occlusion", "Central retinal vein occlusion", "Retinal arterial macroaneurysm", "Central serous chorioretinopathy"], correct: 3, explanation: "Recognized complications are BRAO, BRVO, CRVO, and retinal arterial macroaneurysms — not CSC." },
  { id: 16, question: "The coexistence of hypertension and diabetes results in:", options: ["Milder retinopathy", "More severe retinopathy", "No change in retinopathy", "Protection against nonperfusion"], correct: 1, explanation: "Coexisting hypertension and diabetes results in more severe retinopathy." },
  { id: 17, question: "On fundus examination, FIPTs appear as:", options: ["Large, superficial, white lesions", "Small, deep, tan lesions", "Refractile crystals", "Flame-shaped hemorrhages"], correct: 1, explanation: "FIPTs are small, deep, tan lesions, in contrast to the large, superficial, white cotton-wool spots." },
  { id: 18, question: "On fundus examination, cotton-wool spots appear as:", options: ["Small, deep, tan lesions", "Large, superficial, white lesions", "Dot-blot hemorrhages", "Hard yellow deposits"], correct: 1, explanation: "Cotton-wool spots are large, superficial, and white." },
  { id: 19, question: "On fluorescein angiography, cotton-wool spots correspond to:", options: ["Punctate hyperfluorescence", "Areas of nonperfusion", "Late leakage", "Blocked fluorescence from lipid"], correct: 1, explanation: "Cotton-wool spots correspond to areas of nonperfusion on angiography." },
  { id: 20, question: "On fluorescein angiography, FIPTs correspond to:", options: ["Areas of nonperfusion", "Punctate hyperfluorescence", "Dark hypofluorescent spots", "Staining of vessel walls"], correct: 1, explanation: "FIPTs show punctate hyperfluorescence on angiography." },
  { id: 21, question: "The fundus of an acute (malignant) hypertensive episode shows all of the following EXCEPT:", options: ["Optic nerve head edema", "Flame-shaped NFL hemorrhages", "Macular exudates and subretinal fluid", "Peripheral pigmentary degeneration"], correct: 3, explanation: "Acute hypertensive episodes show disc edema, flame hemorrhages, cotton-wool spots, macular exudates, and subretinal fluid — not peripheral pigmentary degeneration." },
  { id: 22, question: "OCT in an acute hypertensive episode characteristically shows fluid in:", options: ["Only the subretinal space", "The nerve fiber layer, retina, and subretinal space", "Only the choroid", "The vitreous cavity"], correct: 1, explanation: "OCT shows fluid in the NFL, retina, and subretinal space." },
  { id: 23, question: "After systemic antihypertensive medications are started, acute hypertensive retinopathy typically shows:", options: ["Progression to tractional detachment", "Resolution of disc edema and reduced exudation/hemorrhage", "No change for years", "Immediate neovascularization"], correct: 1, explanation: "Treatment leads to resolution of optic nerve head edema and reduction in retinal exudation and hemorrhage." },
  { id: 24, question: "Hypertensive choroidopathy typically occurs in:", options: ["Elderly patients with chronic mild hypertension", "Young patients after acute, severe hypertension", "Diabetic patients exclusively", "Highly myopic patients"], correct: 1, explanation: "Hypertensive choroidopathy typically occurs in young patients following acute, severe hypertension." },
  { id: 25, question: "Hypertensive choroidopathy is associated with all of the following EXCEPT:", options: ["Preeclampsia/eclampsia", "Pheochromocytoma", "Renal hypertension", "Primary open-angle glaucoma"], correct: 3, explanation: "Associations include preeclampsia/eclampsia, pheochromocytoma, and renal hypertension — not open-angle glaucoma." },
  { id: 26, question: "The choroidal abnormality in hypertensive choroidopathy is:", options: ["Lobular nonperfusion of the choriocapillaris", "Choroidal neovascularization", "Choroidal effusion from scleritis", "Choroidal melanoma"], correct: 0, explanation: "Hypertensive choroidopathy features lobular nonperfusion of the choriocapillaris." },
  { id: 27, question: "The abbreviation FIPT stands for:", options: ["Focal intraretinal periarteriolar transudates", "Focal inner-plexiform transient spots", "Fluorescein-imaged perivascular tracts", "Foveal intraretinal pigment transudates"], correct: 0, explanation: "FIPT = focal intraretinal periarteriolar transudate." },
  { id: 28, question: "Cotton-wool spots indicate ischemia of which retinal layer?", options: ["Outer plexiform layer", "Nerve fiber layer", "Photoreceptor layer", "Retinal pigment epithelium"], correct: 1, explanation: "Cotton-wool spots are infarctions indicating ischemia of the nerve fiber layer." },
  { id: 29, question: "Which of the following is an ACUTE rather than chronic sign of hypertensive retinopathy?", options: ["Lipid (hard) exudates", "Venous beading", "Focal intraretinal periarteriolar transudates", "Neovascularization"], correct: 2, explanation: "FIPTs are produced by an acute hypertensive episode; the others are chronic lesions." },
  { id: 30, question: "Neuronal loss from uncontrolled hypertension produces:", options: ["Metamorphopsia", "Scotomata", "Nyctalopia", "Isolated color desaturation"], correct: 1, explanation: "Uncontrolled hypertension causes neuronal loss and associated scotomata." },
  { id: 31, question: "Factors that complicate attributing retinal vascular changes to hypertension include all of the following EXCEPT:", options: ["Coexisting diabetic retinopathy", "Severity of dyslipidemia", "Smoking history", "Refractive error"], correct: 3, explanation: "Confounders include diabetic retinopathy, dyslipidemia, age, and smoking — not refractive error." },
  { id: 32, question: "Which vascular occlusion is a recognized complication of hypertension?", options: ["Branch retinal vein occlusion", "Ophthalmic artery aneurysm", "Cavernous sinus thrombosis", "Carotid–cavernous fistula"], correct: 0, explanation: "Hypertension may be complicated by BRAO, BRVO, and CRVO." },
];

const RETINA_RAO_RECALL: RecallQ[] = [
  { id: 1, question: "What is the classic clinical presentation of central retinal artery occlusion (CRAO)?", answer: "Sudden, complete, and painless loss of vision in one eye." },
  { id: 2, question: "What visual acuity do most patients present with in CRAO?", answer: "Three-fourths of patients present with visual acuity in the range of counting fingers." },
  { id: 3, question: "Why does the retina become opaque, particularly in the posterior pole, in CRAO?", answer: "The retina becomes opaque and edematous especially in the posterior pole, where the nerve fiber and ganglion cell layers are thickest — so the inner-retinal infarction is most visible there." },
  { id: 4, question: "What produces the cherry-red spot in CRAO?", answer: "The orange reflex from the intact choroidal vasculature beneath the foveola stands out against the surrounding opaque, edematous neural retina, producing a cherry-red spot." },
  { id: 5, question: "What are the OCT findings in CRAO, even before the cherry-red spot appears?", answer: "OCT shows a normal macular profile with diffuse hyperreflectivity and loss of internal layer definition — hyperreflectivity of the inner two-thirds of the retina in areas of clinical opacification." },
  { id: 6, question: "How can a cilioretinal artery affect the clinical picture of CRAO?", answer: "A cilioretinal artery may preserve some degree of macular vision. On SD-OCT, all retinal layers remain identifiable in the area perfused by the cilioretinal artery." },
  { id: 7, question: "What happens to subfoveal fluid on OCT in CRAO?", answer: "Subfoveal fluid is variably present in these cases." },
  { id: 8, question: "What is the long-term course of the artery and retina after CRAO, and why is vision loss usually permanent?", answer: "With time the central retinal artery reopens or recanalizes and the retinal edema clears, but the effect on visual acuity is usually permanent because the inner retina has been infarcted." },
  { id: 9, question: "What are the visual outcome statistics after CRAO?", answer: "In one study, 66% of eyes had final visual acuity worse than 20/400, and 18% had 20/40 or better." },
  { id: 10, question: "Which eyes are most likely to recover vision to 20/40 or better after CRAO?", answer: "Most eyes in which visual acuity recovers to 20/40 or better have a patent cilioretinal artery." },
  { id: 11, question: "What usually causes vaso-occlusive vision loss to the level of no light perception?", answer: "Choroidal vascular insufficiency from partial or complete ophthalmic artery occlusion, or occlusion of the parent ciliary arteries in conjunction with occlusion of the central retinal artery." },
  { id: 12, question: "According to nonhuman primate studies, when does irreversible retinal damage begin in complete CRAO?", answer: "Irreversible damage to the sensory retina begins after about 90 minutes of complete CRAO. Nonetheless, clinical return of vision can occasionally occur even after the obstruction has persisted for many hours." },
  { id: 13, question: "What are the two main mechanisms causing CRAO?", answer: "Emboli originating anywhere between the heart and the ophthalmic artery, or atherosclerosis-related thrombosis occurring at the level of the lamina cribrosa." },
  { id: 14, question: "What can emboli within the carotid distribution cause besides CRAO?", answer: "Transient ischemic attacks (TIAs), amaurosis fugax, or both." },
  { id: 15, question: "What are Hollenhorst plaques — their appearance, location, and significance?", answer: "Bright cholesterol emboli, typically located at retinal artery bifurcations. They suggest a carotid atheromatous origin and may be an indication for endarterectomy if accompanied by relevant symptoms and findings." },
  { id: 16, question: "What proportion of CRAO cases are due to giant cell arteritis (GCA), and when should it be suspected?", answer: "GCA accounts for approximately 1%–2% of CRAO cases. When an embolus is not readily visible, a thorough evaluation for GCA should be considered. GCA risk increases with advancing age, starting at 55 years." },
  { id: 17, question: "Which laboratory markers are typically elevated in GCA?", answer: "The erythrocyte sedimentation rate (ESR), C-reactive protein, and fibrinogen (all markers of inflammation) are usually elevated. A complete blood count may show an elevated platelet count (suggestive of GCA) and aids interpretation of the ESR." },
  { id: 18, question: "How is suspected GCA managed in the setting of CRAO?", answer: "Institute high-dose systemic corticosteroids promptly, because the second eye can become involved within hours to days. Perform a temporal artery biopsy within 14 days to confirm the diagnosis and determine the need for prolonged corticosteroid treatment." },
  { id: 19, question: "What is the most important step in initial management of CRAO?", answer: "Identification of the underlying systemic etiologic factors and an urgent referral to an emergency department for a stroke workup." },
  { id: 20, question: "How did stroke organizations reclassify retinal artery occlusion in 2011 and 2013?", answer: "The National Stroke Association and the American Heart Association included \"retinal cell death\" in their consensus statement defining central nervous system infarction (stroke)." },
  { id: 21, question: "What is the leading cause of death in patients with retinal arterial obstruction, and what is the acute cardiac risk?", answer: "Cardiovascular disease is the leading cause of death, with an elevated risk of myocardial infarction within the first 7 days after onset of the obstruction." },
  { id: 22, question: "What fraction of CRAO patients may have undiagnosed risk factors?", answer: "As many as 78% of patients may have undiagnosed risk factors." },
  { id: 23, question: "When and where should neuroimaging be performed after a CRAO?", answer: "Patients should preferably undergo neuroimaging within 24 hours of symptom onset and be evaluated at an emergency department associated with a stroke center." },
  { id: 24, question: "What is the role and limitation of carotid ultrasonography, and what is preferred for definitive imaging?", answer: "Carotid ultrasonography is a useful first-line screening test but depends on an experienced ultrasonographer and images only the extracranial carotid. CT angiography or MR angiography is recommended for a more definitive study." },
  { id: 25, question: "What brain imaging is recommended, and what does it reveal in acute RAO?", answer: "Brain MRI with diffusion-weighted imaging is ideal. Multiple small (often silent) cerebral infarctions are seen in up to 31% of patients with acute RAO, and their presence is associated with a higher chance of identifying an embolic cause." },
  { id: 26, question: "What unproven acute therapies have been described for symptomatic RAO?", answer: "Digital ocular massage, anterior chamber paracentesis, vasodilation, breathing into a paper bag, carbogen therapy (95% oxygen / 5% carbon dioxide), topical IOP-lowering medications, hyperbaric oxygen, and transvitreal Nd:YAG embolysis — but there are no level I data to support any specific therapy." },
  { id: 27, question: "What are the American Heart Association's tPA recommendations for RAO?", answer: "Intravenous tPA for patients without systemic contraindication evaluated within 4.5 hours of visual symptom onset; or intra-arterial tPA via superselective catheterization of the ipsilateral ophthalmic artery for patients who are not IV candidates and are evaluated within 6 hours of onset." },
  { id: 28, question: "How often does neovascularization of the iris (NVI) develop after CRAO, and how is it treated?", answer: "NVI develops in approximately 18% of eyes within 1–12 weeks (mean ~4 weeks) after acute CRAO. Treatment — full-scatter PRP (regression in ~two-thirds of cases) and/or anti-VEGF therapy — is highly effective in avoiding neovascular glaucoma." },
  { id: 29, question: "Describe the cilioretinal artery: its origin, prevalence, and the most common setting for its occlusion.", answer: "The cilioretinal artery arises from the short posterior ciliary vessels (not the central retinal artery) and is present in approximately 18%–32% of eyes, usually supplying part of the macular circulation. Its occlusion most commonly occurs with a CRVO, where increased hydrostatic pressure reduces cilioretinal flow to stagnation. When it occurs in isolation, GCA should be considered." },
  { id: 30, question: "What are the clinical features of ophthalmic artery occlusion?", answer: "Ophthalmic artery occlusion is very rare and typically produces vision loss to the level of light perception or no light perception, because simultaneous nonperfusion of the choroid and retina causes ischemia of all retinal layers (both inner and outer retina)." },
];

const RETINA_RAO_MCQ: MCQ[] = [
  { id: 1, question: "The classic presentation of central retinal artery occlusion (CRAO) is:", options: ["Gradual, painful vision loss over weeks", "Sudden, complete, painless monocular vision loss", "Bilateral simultaneous vision loss", "Transient flashes with floaters"], correct: 1, explanation: "CRAO characteristically causes sudden, complete, and painless loss of vision in one eye." },
  { id: 2, question: "Approximately what fraction of CRAO patients present with counting-fingers vision?", options: ["One-quarter", "One-half", "Three-fourths", "Nearly all"], correct: 2, explanation: "Three-fourths of patients present with visual acuity in the counting-fingers range." },
  { id: 3, question: "Retinal opacification in CRAO is most prominent in the posterior pole because there:", options: ["The choroid is thickest", "The nerve fiber and ganglion cell layers are thickest", "The RPE is most pigmented", "The vitreous is most adherent"], correct: 1, explanation: "The posterior pole shows the most opacification because the NFL and ganglion cell layers (inner retina) are thickest there." },
  { id: 4, question: "The cherry-red spot in CRAO is produced by:", options: ["Subretinal hemorrhage at the fovea", "The intact choroidal circulation beneath the foveola seen against the surrounding opaque retina", "Lipofuscin accumulation in the foveal RPE", "Neovascularization at the fovea"], correct: 1, explanation: "The intact choroidal vasculature beneath the thin foveola shows through as an orange reflex, contrasting with the opaque neural retina." },
  { id: 5, question: "The earliest OCT finding in CRAO, even before the cherry-red spot, is:", options: ["Full-thickness macular hole", "Diffuse inner-retinal hyperreflectivity with loss of layer definition", "Subretinal drusenoid deposits", "Choroidal thickening"], correct: 1, explanation: "OCT shows diffuse hyperreflectivity of the inner retina with loss of internal layer definition." },
  { id: 6, question: "Which vessel may preserve some central vision in CRAO?", options: ["Cilioretinal artery", "Central retinal vein", "Long posterior ciliary artery", "Vortex vein"], correct: 0, explanation: "A cilioretinal artery may preserve some macular vision; eyes recovering to 20/40 or better usually have a patent cilioretinal artery." },
  { id: 7, question: "On SD-OCT in CRAO, hyperreflectivity characteristically involves:", options: ["The outer third of the retina", "The inner two-thirds of the retina", "The choriocapillaris", "The full-thickness sclera"], correct: 1, explanation: "SD-OCT shows hyperreflectivity of the inner two-thirds of the retina in areas of clinical opacification." },
  { id: 8, question: "Subfoveal fluid on OCT in CRAO is:", options: ["Always absent", "Variably present", "Diagnostic of ophthalmic artery occlusion", "Pathognomonic of GCA"], correct: 1, explanation: "Subfoveal fluid is variably present in CRAO." },
  { id: 9, question: "Despite recanalization of the central retinal artery, vision loss in CRAO is usually permanent because:", options: ["The choroid remains nonperfused", "The inner retina has been infarcted", "The lens becomes cataractous", "The optic nerve is avulsed"], correct: 1, explanation: "Even after the artery reopens and edema clears, the inner retina has already infarcted, so the visual deficit is usually permanent." },
  { id: 10, question: "In one study of CRAO, what proportion of eyes had final VA worse than 20/400?", options: ["18%", "33%", "50%", "66%"], correct: 3, explanation: "66% of eyes had final VA worse than 20/400; 18% had 20/40 or better." },
  { id: 11, question: "In that same study, what proportion of CRAO eyes achieved 20/40 or better?", options: ["18%", "40%", "66%", "80%"], correct: 0, explanation: "18% of eyes achieved 20/40 or better, most of which had a patent cilioretinal artery." },
  { id: 12, question: "Eyes that recover to 20/40 or better after CRAO most often have:", options: ["A patent cilioretinal artery", "A complete ophthalmic artery occlusion", "Coexisting CRVO", "Neovascular glaucoma"], correct: 0, explanation: "A patent cilioretinal artery is the feature most associated with recovery to 20/40 or better." },
  { id: 13, question: "Vaso-occlusive vision loss to no light perception in the setting of CRAO usually indicates:", options: ["Isolated inner-retinal ischemia", "Choroidal insufficiency from ophthalmic or parent ciliary artery occlusion", "A patent cilioretinal artery", "Vitreous hemorrhage"], correct: 1, explanation: "NLP vision usually reflects choroidal insufficiency from ophthalmic artery or parent ciliary artery occlusion combined with CRAO." },
  { id: 14, question: "In nonhuman primate studies, irreversible sensory retinal damage begins after how long of complete CRAO?", options: ["10 minutes", "30 minutes", "90 minutes", "6 hours"], correct: 2, explanation: "Irreversible damage begins after ~90 minutes of complete CRAO, though clinical recovery can rarely occur even after many hours." },
  { id: 15, question: "CRAO is most often caused by:", options: ["Emboli or thrombosis at the lamina cribrosa", "Congenital vascular malformation", "Venous stasis", "Vitreous traction on a vessel"], correct: 0, explanation: "CRAO is most often caused by emboli (heart to ophthalmic artery) or atherosclerotic thrombosis at the lamina cribrosa." },
  { id: 16, question: "Atherosclerosis-related thrombotic CRAO occurs at the level of the:", options: ["Macula", "Lamina cribrosa", "Vortex ampulla", "Ciliary body"], correct: 1, explanation: "Thrombotic CRAO occurs at the level of the lamina cribrosa." },
  { id: 17, question: "Emboli within the carotid distribution can cause:", options: ["Transient ischemic attacks and/or amaurosis fugax", "Central serous chorioretinopathy", "Posterior vitreous detachment", "Angle-closure glaucoma"], correct: 0, explanation: "Carotid emboli can cause TIAs, amaurosis fugax, or both." },
  { id: 18, question: "Hollenhorst plaques are:", options: ["Calcific emboli from cardiac valves", "Bright cholesterol emboli at retinal artery bifurcations", "Platelet-fibrin emboli", "Talc particles"], correct: 1, explanation: "Hollenhorst plaques are bright cholesterol emboli typically found at retinal artery bifurcations." },
  { id: 19, question: "The presence of Hollenhorst plaques suggests:", options: ["A cardiac septal defect", "A carotid atheromatous source", "Giant cell arteritis", "A hypercoagulable state"], correct: 1, explanation: "They suggest a carotid atheromatous origin and may indicate endarterectomy with relevant symptoms/findings." },
  { id: 20, question: "Giant cell arteritis accounts for approximately what percentage of CRAO cases?", options: ["1%–2%", "10%–15%", "25%", "50%"], correct: 0, explanation: "GCA accounts for approximately 1%–2% of CRAO cases." },
  { id: 21, question: "When should GCA be strongly considered as the cause of a CRAO?", options: ["When a Hollenhorst plaque is clearly visible", "When no embolus is readily visible", "In any patient under 40", "When the cilioretinal artery is patent"], correct: 1, explanation: "When an embolus is not readily visible, a thorough evaluation for GCA should be considered." },
  { id: 22, question: "The risk of GCA increases with advancing age, starting at approximately:", options: ["40 years", "50 years", "55 years", "70 years"], correct: 2, explanation: "GCA risk increases with age, starting at about 55 years." },
  { id: 23, question: "Which laboratory value is NOT a typical marker elevated in GCA?", options: ["Erythrocyte sedimentation rate (ESR)", "C-reactive protein", "Fibrinogen", "Serum potassium"], correct: 3, explanation: "ESR, CRP, and fibrinogen (inflammatory markers) are usually elevated in GCA; serum potassium is unrelated." },
  { id: 24, question: "On a complete blood count, which finding is suggestive of GCA?", options: ["Elevated platelet count", "Leukopenia", "Microcytic anemia", "Eosinophilia"], correct: 0, explanation: "An elevated platelet count is suggestive of GCA, and the CBC also aids interpretation of the ESR." },
  { id: 25, question: "If GCA is suspected in a patient with CRAO, the immediate step is to:", options: ["Schedule elective temporal artery biopsy in 6 weeks", "Institute high-dose systemic corticosteroids promptly", "Begin intravitreal anti-VEGF", "Observe and repeat imaging in 1 month"], correct: 1, explanation: "High-dose systemic corticosteroids should be started promptly because the fellow eye can be involved within hours to days." },
  { id: 26, question: "A temporal artery biopsy for suspected GCA should be performed within:", options: ["24 hours", "14 days", "6 weeks", "3 months"], correct: 1, explanation: "Temporal artery biopsy should be performed within 14 days to confirm the diagnosis and guide prolonged treatment." },
  { id: 27, question: "The most important step in the initial management of CRAO is:", options: ["Immediate intravitreal steroid injection", "Urgent referral to an emergency department for a stroke workup", "Prescribing oral aspirin and reassurance", "Scheduling outpatient carotid ultrasound in 2 weeks"], correct: 1, explanation: "The key step is identifying systemic etiology and urgent ED referral for a stroke workup." },
  { id: 28, question: "In 2011 and 2013, stroke organizations added which term to the consensus definition of CNS infarction?", options: ["Amaurosis fugax", "Retinal cell death", "Cotton-wool spot", "Optic disc edema"], correct: 1, explanation: "The National Stroke Association and AHA included \"retinal cell death\" in the consensus definition of CNS infarction (stroke)." },
  { id: 29, question: "The leading cause of death in patients with retinal arterial obstruction is:", options: ["Stroke", "Cardiovascular disease", "Malignancy", "Infection"], correct: 1, explanation: "Cardiovascular disease is the leading cause of death, with elevated MI risk in the first 7 days." },
  { id: 30, question: "The elevated risk of myocardial infarction after retinal arterial obstruction is greatest within:", options: ["The first 7 days", "The first 3 months", "The first year", "The first 5 years"], correct: 0, explanation: "MI risk is especially elevated within the first 7 days after onset." },
  { id: 31, question: "Undiagnosed vascular risk factors may be present in up to what fraction of CRAO patients?", options: ["18%", "31%", "50%", "78%"], correct: 3, explanation: "As many as 78% of patients may have undiagnosed risk factors." },
  { id: 32, question: "Neuroimaging after CRAO should preferably be performed within:", options: ["24 hours of symptom onset", "1 week", "1 month", "3 months"], correct: 0, explanation: "Neuroimaging should ideally occur within 24 hours of symptom onset at a stroke-center–associated ED." },
  { id: 33, question: "The recommended first-line screening test for the carotid arteries is:", options: ["Carotid ultrasonography", "Catheter angiography", "Plain radiography", "Transesophageal echocardiography"], correct: 0, explanation: "Carotid ultrasonography is a useful first-line screen, though it images only the extracranial carotid." },
  { id: 34, question: "A key limitation of carotid ultrasonography is that it:", options: ["Cannot detect cholesterol emboli", "Images only the extracranial portion of the carotid tree", "Requires iodinated contrast", "Exposes the patient to high radiation"], correct: 1, explanation: "Ultrasound images only the extracranial carotid, so CTA or MRA is preferred for definitive assessment." },
  { id: 35, question: "For a more definitive vascular imaging study after CRAO, the recommended modalities are:", options: ["CT angiography or MR angiography", "Plain CT of the head", "Fundus autofluorescence", "B-scan ultrasonography"], correct: 0, explanation: "CTA or MRA is recommended for definitive imaging of the carotid/cerebral vasculature." },
  { id: 36, question: "Silent (asymptomatic) cerebral infarctions are seen on diffusion-weighted brain MRI in up to what percentage of acute RAO patients?", options: ["5%", "18%", "31%", "66%"], correct: 2, explanation: "Up to 31% of acute RAO patients show multiple small cerebral infarctions on DWI." },
  { id: 37, question: "The presence of silent cerebral infarctions in acute RAO is associated with:", options: ["A lower chance of finding an embolic cause", "A higher chance of identifying an embolic cause", "A benign prognosis requiring no workup", "Isolated GCA"], correct: 1, explanation: "Silent infarctions are associated with a higher chance of identifying an embolic cause for the retinal ischemia." },
  { id: 38, question: "Regarding proven treatment for symptomatic RAO, which statement is correct?", options: ["Ocular massage has level I evidence", "Hyperbaric oxygen is standard of care", "There are no level I data supporting any specific therapy", "Nd:YAG embolysis is first-line"], correct: 2, explanation: "Many measures have been tried, but there are no level I data supporting any specific therapy." },
  { id: 39, question: "The AHA recommends intravenous tPA for eligible RAO patients evaluated within:", options: ["90 minutes", "4.5 hours", "12 hours", "24 hours"], correct: 1, explanation: "IV tPA is recommended for patients without contraindication evaluated within 4.5 hours of visual symptom onset." },
  { id: 40, question: "Intra-arterial tPA via superselective catheterization of the ipsilateral ophthalmic artery is recommended (for non-IV candidates) within:", options: ["4.5 hours", "6 hours", "12 hours", "48 hours"], correct: 1, explanation: "Intra-arterial tPA is an option within 6 hours of symptom onset for patients who are not IV candidates." },
  { id: 41, question: "Neovascularization of the iris develops in approximately what percentage of eyes after acute CRAO?", options: ["2%", "18%", "45%", "66%"], correct: 1, explanation: "NVI develops in ~18% of eyes within 1–12 weeks (mean ~4 weeks) after acute CRAO." },
  { id: 42, question: "Full-scatter panretinal photocoagulation causes regression of anterior segment neovascularization after CRAO in approximately:", options: ["One-tenth of cases", "One-third of cases", "Two-thirds of cases", "All cases"], correct: 2, explanation: "Full-scatter PRP results in regression of anterior segment neovascularization in about two-thirds of cases; anti-VEGF is also effective." },
  { id: 43, question: "The cilioretinal artery arises from the ___ and is present in approximately ___ of eyes.", options: ["Central retinal artery; 50%", "Short posterior ciliary vessels; 18%–32%", "Long posterior ciliary arteries; 5%", "Ophthalmic artery directly; 70%"], correct: 1, explanation: "The cilioretinal artery arises from the short posterior ciliary vessels and is present in ~18%–32% of eyes." },
  { id: 44, question: "Cilioretinal artery occlusion most commonly occurs in association with ___, and when it occurs in isolation, ___ should be considered.", options: ["CRAO; diabetes", "CRVO; giant cell arteritis", "Retinal detachment; hypertension", "AMD; sarcoidosis"], correct: 1, explanation: "It most often occurs with a CRVO (raised hydrostatic pressure causing stagnation); isolated occlusion should prompt evaluation for GCA." },
];

const UVEITIS_TOXO_RECALL: RecallQ[] = [
  { id: 1, question: "What is the clinical significance of ocular toxoplasmosis among the uveitides?", answer: "It is the most common form of infectious posterior uveitis in both adults and children." },
  { id: 2, question: "What organism causes ocular toxoplasmosis, and how is it classified?", answer: "Toxoplasma gondii — a single-cell, obligate, intracellular, apicomplexan parasite with a worldwide distribution." },
  { id: 3, question: "Who are the definitive and intermediate hosts of T. gondii?", answer: "Felines (cats) are the definitive hosts; humans and a variety of other animals serve as intermediate hosts." },
  { id: 4, question: "What are the three major forms of T. gondii, including their sizes and contents?", answer: "The oocyst (soil form, 10–12 μm, contains sporozoites); the tachyzoite (infectious/proliferative form, 4–8 μm); and the tissue cyst (latent form, 10–200 μm, containing as many as 3000 bradyzoites)." },
  { id: 5, question: "Describe the behavior of tachyzoites and what happens in an immunocompetent host.", answer: "Tachyzoites are the proliferative form found in the circulatory system and can invade nearly all host tissues. In an immunocompetent host, tachyzoite proliferation eventually stops, but some organisms persist as dormant bradyzoites within intercellular tissue cysts." },
  { id: 6, question: "What are the CDC seropositivity estimates for T. gondii in the US versus southern Brazil, and the rates of ocular disease?", answer: "The CDC estimates 11% of the US population aged ≥6 years is infected, of whom ~2% may develop ocular toxoplasmosis. In southern Brazil, ~80% are infected and up to 18% may develop eye disease — differences attributed partly to greater genotypic heterogeneity of parasites in Brazil." },
  { id: 7, question: "List the principal modes of T. gondii transmission to humans.", answer: "Ingestion of undercooked infected meat containing tissue cysts; ingestion of contaminated water, fruit, or vegetables with oocysts; contact with cat feces, litter, or soil containing oocysts; primary maternal infection during pregnancy (transplacental); and blood transfusion or organ transplantation." },
  { id: 8, question: "What was the NHANES seroprevalence of T. gondii among US women of childbearing age?", answer: "The 2009–2010 NHANES showed an age-adjusted seroprevalence of 9.1% among women of childbearing age (15–44 years) — meaning most such women remain at risk for primary infection." },
  { id: 9, question: "What fraction of primary maternal infections result in congenital infection, and when is transmission highest?", answer: "Overall, 40% of primary maternal infections result in congenital infection, with transplacental transmission highest during the third trimester." },
  { id: 10, question: "How does the risk of severe fetal disease relate to gestational age at infection?", answer: "The risk of severe fetal disease is inversely proportional to gestational age. Infection early in pregnancy may cause spontaneous abortion, stillbirth, or severe congenital disease, whereas later infection may produce latent disease in an asymptomatic, healthy-appearing infant." },
  { id: 11, question: "Does chronic or recurrent maternal infection confer a major risk of congenital toxoplasmosis?", answer: "Probably not — maternal immunity generally protects against fetal transmission. However, congenital toxoplasmosis can occur if an immune pregnant mother is reinfected with a new, more virulent strain." },
  { id: 12, question: "What is Sabin's tetrad, and how often does it occur?", answer: "The classic tetrad of congenital toxoplasmosis: retinochoroiditis, hydrocephalus or microcephaly, intracranial calcifications, and cognitive impairment. It occurs in fewer than 10% of infected children." },
  { id: 13, question: "What is the most common abnormality in congenital toxoplasmosis, and its laterality/location?", answer: "Retinochoroidal lesions, found in up to 80% of cases. They are bilateral in about 85% of affected individuals and tend to occur in the posterior pole and macula; involvement may be subclinical and chronic." },
  { id: 14, question: "What is the natural course of retinochoroiditis in children with congenital toxoplasmosis?", answer: "As many as 85% develop retinochoroiditis after a mean of 3.7 years, and 25% of these become blind in one or both eyes. Most experts recommend antiparasitic therapy in the first year of life regardless of ocular/systemic signs." },
  { id: 15, question: "Is postnatal ocular toxoplasmosis usually reactivation of congenital disease?", answer: "Not necessarily. Although once considered reactivation of congenital disease, ocular toxoplasmosis in children and adults is now recognized as newly acquired infection in a substantial proportion of cases — in one study, up to two-thirds of cases were acquired postnatally." },
  { id: 16, question: "What are the typical presenting symptoms and anterior segment findings of acquired ocular toxoplasmosis?", answer: "Unilateral blurred or hazy vision and floaters. A mild-to-moderate granulomatous anterior uveitis is often observed, and up to 20% of patients have acutely elevated IOP at presentation." },
  { id: 17, question: "Describe the classic fundus appearance of ocular toxoplasmosis, including the 'headlight in the fog' sign.", answer: "A focal, white retinochoroiditis often adjacent to a pigmented retinochoroidal scar, with moderate overlying vitreous inflammation — the active white lesion seen through the hazy vitritis produces a 'headlight in the fog' appearance. Without a pre-existing scar, recently acquired disease presents as a focal retinochoroiditis." },
  { id: 18, question: "Why can a toxoplasmic lesion near the optic nerve be misdiagnosed, and what vascular findings can accompany active lesions?", answer: "Lesions adjacent to or involving the optic nerve may be mistaken for optic neuritis. Retinal vessels near an active lesion may show perivasculitis with diffuse venous sheathing and segmental arterial plaques (previously called Kyrieleis arteriolitis); vascular occlusions may also occur." },
  { id: 19, question: "List the ocular complications of toxoplasmic retinochoroiditis.", answer: "Cataract, persistent vitreous opacities, macular edema, retinal detachment, epiretinal membranes, optic atrophy, and choroidal neovascularization (CNV)." },
  { id: 20, question: "How does ocular toxoplasmosis present in immunocompromised or older patients?", answer: "With atypical findings — large, multiple, and/or bilateral lesions, with or without associated retinochoroidal scars. This severe picture can also occur when corticosteroids are given without concomitant antiparasitic therapy, and it may simulate herpetic acute retinal necrosis (ARN)." },
  { id: 21, question: "Name the atypical presentations of ocular toxoplasmosis.", answer: "Neuroretinitis, unilateral pigmentary retinopathy simulating retinitis pigmentosa, intraocular inflammation without retinochoroiditis, and punctate outer retinal toxoplasmosis (PORT)." },
  { id: 22, question: "What characterizes punctate outer retinal toxoplasmosis (PORT)?", answer: "Small, multifocal lesions at the level of the outer retina, with exudation into the subretinal space and scant overlying vitreal inflammation." },
  { id: 23, question: "How is toxoplasmic retinochoroiditis usually diagnosed?", answer: "In most cases it is diagnosed clinically on the basis of the characteristic fundus lesion. Positive serology for anti–T. gondii IgG or IgM confirms exposure to the parasite." },
  { id: 24, question: "Contrast the behavior of anti–T. gondii IgG and IgM antibodies.", answer: "IgG appears after the first 2 weeks of infection, typically remains detectable for life at variable levels, and crosses the placenta. IgM rises early in the acute phase, usually remains detectable for less than 1 year, and does not cross the placenta." },
  { id: 25, question: "How are IgG and IgM interpreted in newborns versus adults?", answer: "In the appropriate clinical context, IgG supports the diagnosis of toxoplasmic retinochoroiditis. IgM confirms congenital infection in newborns (since it doesn't cross the placenta) but indicates newly acquired disease in adults." },
  { id: 26, question: "What test is used when the diagnosis of ocular toxoplasmosis is uncertain?", answer: "PCR testing of aqueous humor and vitreous fluid." },
  { id: 27, question: "What is the natural history of untreated toxoplasmic retinochoroiditis in an immunocompetent patient?", answer: "The disease may be self-limiting: without treatment the lesion borders become sharper and less edematous over a 6- to 8-week period, and RPE hyperplasia develops gradually over months. In immunocompromised patients the disease is often more severe and progressive." },
  { id: 28, question: "Why do most anti-toxoplasmic antibiotics fail to eradicate the organism, and what is the implication?", answer: "Most antibiotics have efficacy against the active tachyzoite only, not the tissue-encysted bradyzoite. Because cysts persist in normal-appearing retina, the disease is recurrent, with new lesions arising at the margins of old scars." },
  { id: 29, question: "Which patient populations should be treated for ocular toxoplasmosis?", answer: "Immunocompromised patients (HIV/AIDS, neoplastic disease, or receiving immunomodulatory therapy), patients with congenital toxoplasmosis, and pregnant women with recently acquired disease." },
  { id: 30, question: "List the relative indications for treating ocular toxoplasmosis.", answer: "Lesions threatening the optic nerve, fovea, or major vasculature; decreased vision; lesions with moderate-to-severe vitreous inflammation; lesions greater than 1 disc diameter; persistence of disease for more than 1 month; and the presence of multiple active lesions." },
  { id: 31, question: "What is the 'classic triple therapy' for toxoplasmosis, and what monitoring is required?", answer: "Pyrimethamine (loading 50–100 mg, then 25–50 mg/day for 4–8 weeks) plus sulfadiazine (1 g four times daily) plus folinic acid (5–10 mg/day). Because pyrimethamine causes myelosuppression (leukopenia, thrombocytopenia), monitor with a complete blood count every 2 weeks." },
  { id: 32, question: "Why is trimethoprim-sulfamethoxazole commonly used, and at what dose?", answer: "Because of its accessibility, simplicity of administration, and low cost. Many ophthalmologists use 160 mg/800 mg twice daily. Intravitreal clindamycin (1 mg/0.1 mL) with or without periocular dexamethasone is an off-label alternative for patients who cannot tolerate systemic therapy." },
  { id: 33, question: "What are the rules for corticosteroid use in ocular toxoplasmosis?", answer: "In immunocompetent patients, systemic corticosteroids (~0.25–0.75 mg/kg, usually ≤60 mg/day) may be started only after 48 hours of antimicrobial therapy and continued 3–5 weeks with antimicrobial coverage throughout. Systemic steroids without antimicrobial coverage, and long-acting periocular/intravitreal steroids (e.g., triamcinolone), are contraindicated because they can potentiate a severe retinitis progressing to panophthalmitis, blindness, or loss of the eye. Topical steroids can be used liberally for anterior segment inflammation." },
  { id: 34, question: "How is ocular toxoplasmosis managed in pregnancy and in HIV/AIDS?", answer: "In pregnancy, spiramycin (400 mg three times daily) reduces tachyzoite transmission to the fetus without major teratogenic risk (substitutions: azithromycin, clindamycin, atovaquone; sulfonamides are safe in the first two trimesters). In HIV/AIDS, extended systemic treatment is needed given frequent cerebral involvement (56% of cases) and recurrence when medication is stopped; atovaquone acts synergistically with pyrimethamine and sulfadiazine." },
];

const UVEITIS_TOXO_MCQ: MCQ[] = [
  { id: 1, question: "Ocular toxoplasmosis is the most common form of:", options: ["Anterior uveitis in adults", "Infectious posterior uveitis in adults and children", "Panuveitis in the elderly", "Intermediate uveitis in children"], correct: 1, explanation: "Ocular toxoplasmosis is the most common infectious posterior uveitis in both adults and children." },
  { id: 2, question: "Toxoplasma gondii is classified as a(n):", options: ["Spirochete", "Obligate intracellular apicomplexan parasite", "Double-stranded DNA virus", "Dimorphic fungus"], correct: 1, explanation: "T. gondii is a single-cell, obligate, intracellular, apicomplexan parasite with worldwide distribution." },
  { id: 3, question: "The definitive host of Toxoplasma gondii is the:", options: ["Human", "Dog", "Feline (cat)", "Rodent"], correct: 2, explanation: "Felines are the definitive hosts; humans and other animals are intermediate hosts." },
  { id: 4, question: "Which form of T. gondii contains sporozoites and is the soil form?", options: ["Tachyzoite", "Bradyzoite", "Oocyst", "Tissue cyst"], correct: 2, explanation: "The oocyst (soil form, 10–12 μm) contains sporozoites." },
  { id: 5, question: "The infectious, proliferative form of T. gondii found in the circulation is the:", options: ["Oocyst", "Tachyzoite", "Tissue cyst", "Bradyzoite"], correct: 1, explanation: "The tachyzoite (4–8 μm) is the proliferative form in the circulatory system that invades host tissues." },
  { id: 6, question: "The latent tissue cyst of T. gondii may contain as many as:", options: ["30 bradyzoites", "300 bradyzoites", "3000 bradyzoites", "30,000 bradyzoites"], correct: 2, explanation: "The tissue cyst (10–200 μm) can contain as many as 3000 bradyzoites." },
  { id: 7, question: "In an immunocompetent host, T. gondii persists in a dormant state as:", options: ["Sporozoites in oocysts", "Bradyzoites within tissue cysts", "Free tachyzoites in blood", "Merozoites in the liver"], correct: 1, explanation: "Tachyzoite proliferation stops, but organisms persist as dormant bradyzoites within intercellular tissue cysts." },
  { id: 8, question: "The CDC estimates that what percentage of the US population aged ≥6 years is infected with T. gondii?", options: ["2%", "11%", "40%", "80%"], correct: 1, explanation: "The CDC estimates 11% of the US population aged ≥6 years is infected; ~2% of those may develop ocular disease." },
  { id: 9, question: "Approximately what percentage of infected individuals in southern Brazil may develop ocular toxoplasmosis?", options: ["2%", "9%", "18%", "40%"], correct: 2, explanation: "In southern Brazil ~80% are infected and up to 18% may develop eye disease, in part due to greater parasite genotypic heterogeneity." },
  { id: 10, question: "Which is NOT a recognized mode of T. gondii transmission?", options: ["Ingestion of undercooked meat with tissue cysts", "Contact with cat feces/soil containing oocysts", "Mosquito bite", "Blood transfusion or organ transplantation"], correct: 2, explanation: "Transmission is via ingestion of cysts/oocysts, transplacental spread, or transfusion/transplantation — not mosquito vectors." },
  { id: 11, question: "The NHANES age-adjusted seroprevalence of T. gondii among US women of childbearing age was approximately:", options: ["0.2%", "9.1%", "25%", "40%"], correct: 1, explanation: "The 2009–2010 NHANES showed a 9.1% seroprevalence among women aged 15–44 years." },
  { id: 12, question: "What percentage of primary maternal T. gondii infections result in congenital infection?", options: ["10%", "40%", "56%", "85%"], correct: 1, explanation: "Overall, 40% of primary maternal infections result in congenital infection." },
  { id: 13, question: "Transplacental transmission of T. gondii is highest during which trimester?", options: ["First trimester", "Second trimester", "Third trimester", "It is equal across trimesters"], correct: 2, explanation: "Transplacental transmission is highest during the third trimester." },
  { id: 14, question: "The risk of severe fetal disease from maternal toxoplasmosis is:", options: ["Directly proportional to gestational age", "Inversely proportional to gestational age", "Independent of gestational age", "Highest in the third trimester"], correct: 1, explanation: "Severe fetal disease risk is inversely proportional to gestational age — early infection causes the most severe disease." },
  { id: 15, question: "Congenital toxoplasmosis can occur in an immune pregnant mother when she is:", options: ["Exposed to sunlight", "Reinfected with a new, more virulent strain", "Given routine prenatal vitamins", "Vaccinated against toxoplasmosis"], correct: 1, explanation: "Maternal immunity usually protects the fetus, but reinfection with a new, more virulent strain can cause congenital disease." },
  { id: 16, question: "Sabin's tetrad of congenital toxoplasmosis includes all of the following EXCEPT:", options: ["Retinochoroiditis", "Hydrocephalus or microcephaly", "Intracranial calcifications", "Sensorineural deafness"], correct: 3, explanation: "Sabin's tetrad is retinochoroiditis, hydrocephalus/microcephaly, intracranial calcifications, and cognitive impairment." },
  { id: 17, question: "Sabin's tetrad occurs in what proportion of children with congenital toxoplasmosis?", options: ["Fewer than 10%", "About 40%", "About 80%", "Nearly all"], correct: 0, explanation: "The classic full tetrad occurs in fewer than 10% of infected children." },
  { id: 18, question: "The most common abnormality in congenital toxoplasmosis is:", options: ["Cataract", "Retinochoroidal lesions", "Optic nerve hypoplasia", "Corneal scarring"], correct: 1, explanation: "Retinochoroidal lesions are the most common abnormality, found in up to 80% of cases." },
  { id: 19, question: "Congenital toxoplasmic retinochoroidal lesions are bilateral in approximately:", options: ["25% of cases", "50% of cases", "85% of cases", "100% of cases"], correct: 2, explanation: "Lesions are bilateral in about 85% of affected individuals, favoring the posterior pole and macula." },
  { id: 20, question: "In one study, what proportion of ocular toxoplasmosis cases were attributable to acquired postnatal infection?", options: ["One-tenth", "One-third", "Up to two-thirds", "Essentially none"], correct: 2, explanation: "Up to two-thirds of ocular toxoplasmosis cases were acquired postnatally rather than being reactivation of congenital disease." },
  { id: 21, question: "What proportion of patients with acquired ocular toxoplasmosis have acutely elevated IOP at presentation?", options: ["Up to 20%", "About 50%", "About 75%", "Virtually all"], correct: 0, explanation: "Up to 20% have acutely elevated IOP, often with a mild-to-moderate granulomatous anterior uveitis." },
  { id: 22, question: "The 'headlight in the fog' appearance in ocular toxoplasmosis refers to:", options: ["A cherry-red spot at the fovea", "A focal white retinochoroiditis seen through overlying vitritis", "Subretinal cholesterol deposits", "A macular star of hard exudates"], correct: 1, explanation: "The active white retinochoroiditis lesion viewed through dense overlying vitreous inflammation looks like a headlight in fog." },
  { id: 23, question: "The active lesion of reactivated ocular toxoplasmosis is classically located:", options: ["In the far peripheral retina only", "Adjacent to a pigmented retinochoroidal scar", "Within the anterior chamber angle", "On the corneal endothelium"], correct: 1, explanation: "Reactivation classically appears as focal white retinochoroiditis adjacent to an old pigmented retinochoroidal scar (a 'satellite' lesion)." },
  { id: 24, question: "A toxoplasmic lesion adjacent to the optic nerve may be mistaken for:", options: ["Central retinal vein occlusion", "Optic neuritis", "Macular hole", "Retinoblastoma"], correct: 1, explanation: "Juxtapapillary lesions may be mistaken for optic neuritis." },
  { id: 25, question: "Segmental arterial plaques (previously called Kyrieleis arteriolitis) near an active toxoplasmic lesion represent:", options: ["Cholesterol emboli", "A form of perivascular inflammation", "Calcified drusen", "Neovascularization"], correct: 1, explanation: "Vessels near an active lesion can show perivasculitis with venous sheathing and segmental arterial plaques (Kyrieleis arteriolitis)." },
  { id: 26, question: "Which is NOT a recognized complication of toxoplasmic retinochoroiditis?", options: ["Macular edema", "Choroidal neovascularization", "Epiretinal membrane", "Angle-closure from a plateau iris"], correct: 3, explanation: "Complications include cataract, vitreous opacities, macular edema, retinal detachment, ERM, optic atrophy, and CNV — not plateau-iris angle closure." },
  { id: 27, question: "Atypical, severe ocular toxoplasmosis with large/multiple/bilateral lesions is characteristic of:", options: ["Young immunocompetent patients", "Immunocompromised or older patients", "Patients on antiparasitic therapy", "Patients with a solitary peripheral scar"], correct: 1, explanation: "Immunocompromised and older patients, or those given steroids without antiparasitic cover, develop large, multiple, and/or bilateral lesions." },
  { id: 28, question: "Ocular toxoplasmosis can clinically simulate which herpetic entity?", options: ["Acute retinal necrosis (ARN)", "Geographic herpes simplex keratitis", "Herpes zoster ophthalmicus dermatitis", "Epidemic keratoconjunctivitis"], correct: 0, explanation: "Severe toxoplasmic retinitis may simulate herpetic acute retinal necrosis (ARN)." },
  { id: 29, question: "Punctate outer retinal toxoplasmosis (PORT) is characterized by:", options: ["Large full-thickness necrotizing lesions with dense vitritis", "Small multifocal outer-retinal lesions with subretinal exudation and scant vitritis", "Diffuse arteriolar occlusion", "Isolated optic disc edema"], correct: 1, explanation: "PORT features small, multifocal outer-retinal lesions with subretinal exudation and little overlying vitreal inflammation." },
  { id: 30, question: "Ocular toxoplasmosis is most often diagnosed by:", options: ["Serologic titers alone", "The characteristic clinical fundus appearance", "Routine PCR of blood", "Temporal artery biopsy"], correct: 1, explanation: "Diagnosis is usually clinical, based on the characteristic fundus lesion; serology confirms exposure." },
  { id: 31, question: "Anti–T. gondii IgG antibodies:", options: ["Do not cross the placenta and disappear within weeks", "Appear after ~2 weeks, persist for life, and cross the placenta", "Are detectable only during acute infection", "Are pathognomonic of congenital disease in adults"], correct: 1, explanation: "IgG appears after ~2 weeks, typically persists for life at variable levels, and crosses the placenta." },
  { id: 32, question: "Anti–T. gondii IgM antibodies:", options: ["Persist for life and cross the placenta", "Rise early in acute infection, last <1 year, and do not cross the placenta", "Are only produced by newborns", "Confirm remote infection"], correct: 1, explanation: "IgM rises early in the acute phase, usually persists <1 year, and does not cross the placenta." },
  { id: 33, question: "A positive IgM titer in a NEWBORN indicates:", options: ["Maternal antibody transfer only", "Congenital infection", "Prior vaccination", "A false-positive result"], correct: 1, explanation: "Because IgM does not cross the placenta, its presence in a newborn confirms congenital infection." },
  { id: 34, question: "A positive IgM titer in an ADULT indicates:", options: ["Remote childhood infection", "Newly acquired disease", "Immunity from prior exposure", "Congenital toxoplasmosis"], correct: 1, explanation: "In adults, IgM positivity indicates newly acquired disease." },
  { id: 35, question: "When the diagnosis of ocular toxoplasmosis is uncertain, the confirmatory test is:", options: ["Serum ACE level", "PCR of aqueous humor and vitreous fluid", "Fluorescein angiography", "Chest CT"], correct: 1, explanation: "PCR of aqueous and vitreous fluid can be performed in cases of diagnostic uncertainty." },
  { id: 36, question: "In an untreated immunocompetent patient, toxoplasmic lesion borders typically sharpen and become less edematous over:", options: ["24–48 hours", "6–8 weeks", "6–8 months", "Several years"], correct: 1, explanation: "Untreated lesions in immunocompetent patients typically sharpen over a 6- to 8-week period, with RPE hyperplasia over months." },
  { id: 37, question: "Most anti-toxoplasmic antibiotics are effective against:", options: ["The encysted bradyzoite only", "The active tachyzoite only", "Both tachyzoites and bradyzoites equally", "The oocyst only"], correct: 1, explanation: "Most antibiotics act against the active tachyzoite, not the tissue-encysted bradyzoite — hence recurrences." },
  { id: 38, question: "Which patient group is a clear indication for treating ocular toxoplasmosis?", options: ["Immunocompromised patients", "Any patient with a small quiescent peripheral scar", "Asymptomatic patients with old bilateral scars", "Patients with 20/20 vision and no active lesion"], correct: 0, explanation: "Treatment is indicated for immunocompromised patients, those with congenital disease, and pregnant women with recently acquired disease." },
  { id: 39, question: "Which is a recognized RELATIVE indication for treating ocular toxoplasmosis?", options: ["A lesion less than 1 disc diameter in the far periphery", "A lesion threatening the fovea or optic nerve", "An old inactive pigmented scar", "Absence of any vitritis"], correct: 1, explanation: "Relative indications include lesions threatening the optic nerve/fovea/major vasculature, decreased vision, moderate-severe vitritis, lesions >1 disc diameter, persistence >1 month, or multiple active lesions." },
  { id: 40, question: "The 'classic triple therapy' for toxoplasmosis consists of:", options: ["Pyrimethamine, sulfadiazine, and folinic acid", "Acyclovir, valganciclovir, and prednisone", "Isoniazid, rifampin, and ethambutol", "Clindamycin, doxycycline, and azithromycin"], correct: 0, explanation: "Classic triple therapy is pyrimethamine + sulfadiazine + folinic acid." },
  { id: 41, question: "Folinic acid is added to pyrimethamine-based therapy in order to:", options: ["Enhance antiparasitic potency", "Reduce pyrimethamine-induced bone marrow suppression", "Prevent Stevens-Johnson syndrome", "Improve intraocular penetration"], correct: 1, explanation: "Folinic acid mitigates pyrimethamine's myelosuppression (leukopenia, thrombocytopenia); a CBC is monitored every 2 weeks." },
  { id: 42, question: "Trimethoprim-sulfamethoxazole is popular for ocular toxoplasmosis mainly because of:", options: ["Its lack of any adverse effects", "Its accessibility, simplicity, and low cost", "Its activity against bradyzoite cysts", "Its intravitreal-only administration"], correct: 1, explanation: "TMP-SMX (160/800 mg twice daily) is favored for accessibility, simplicity, and low cost." },
  { id: 43, question: "An off-label intravitreal option for patients who cannot tolerate systemic therapy is:", options: ["Intravitreal amphotericin B", "Intravitreal clindamycin (± periocular dexamethasone)", "Intravitreal ganciclovir", "Intravitreal bevacizumab"], correct: 1, explanation: "Intravitreal clindamycin (1 mg/0.1 mL), with or without periocular dexamethasone, is an off-label alternative." },
  { id: 44, question: "In immunocompetent patients, systemic corticosteroids for ocular toxoplasmosis should be started:", options: ["Before any antimicrobial therapy", "Only after 48 hours of antimicrobial therapy", "Only as monotherapy", "Never under any circumstance"], correct: 1, explanation: "Systemic steroids may be added only after 48 hours of antimicrobial therapy, with antimicrobial coverage continued throughout." },
  { id: 45, question: "Which corticosteroid use is CONTRAINDICATED in ocular toxoplasmosis?", options: ["Topical steroids for anterior segment inflammation", "Systemic steroids without antimicrobial coverage", "Systemic steroids after 48 h of antimicrobials", "A tapering oral course with antibiotic cover"], correct: 1, explanation: "Systemic steroids without antimicrobial coverage — and long-acting periocular/intravitreal depot steroids — are contraindicated because they can potentiate severe retinitis." },
  { id: 46, question: "The danger of long-acting periocular/intravitreal steroids (e.g., triamcinolone) in toxoplasmosis is:", options: ["Cataract only", "Potentiating a severe retinitis that may progress to panophthalmitis or loss of the eye", "Reduced IOP", "Corneal melting"], correct: 1, explanation: "Depot steroids can potentiate fulminant retinitis progressing to panophthalmitis, blindness, or loss of the eye." },
  { id: 47, question: "Which agent is used in pregnancy to reduce tachyzoite transmission to the fetus?", options: ["Pyrimethamine", "Spiramycin", "Doxycycline", "Ganciclovir"], correct: 1, explanation: "Spiramycin (400 mg three times daily) reduces fetal transmission without major teratogenic risk; it is obtained via the FDA in the US." },
  { id: 48, question: "Sulfonamides are considered safe in pregnancy during:", options: ["The first two trimesters", "The third trimester only", "The entire pregnancy without restriction", "No part of pregnancy"], correct: 0, explanation: "Sulfonamides may be used safely in the first two trimesters of pregnancy." },
  { id: 49, question: "Extended systemic therapy is required in HIV/AIDS-associated ocular toxoplasmosis largely because of:", options: ["A benign self-limited course", "Frequent cerebral involvement (~56%) and recurrence when medication is stopped", "Lack of any effective drug", "Resistance to all sulfonamides"], correct: 1, explanation: "HIV/AIDS patients need extended treatment given frequent cerebral involvement (56%) and recurrence on stopping medication; atovaquone acts synergistically with pyrimethamine and sulfadiazine." },
  { id: 50, question: "In patients with recurrent toxoplasmic retinochoroiditis, which regimen reduced reactivation over ~20 months?", options: ["Daily high-dose prednisone", "Long-term intermittent trimethoprim-sulfamethoxazole (160/800 mg three times weekly)", "Monthly intravitreal triamcinolone", "A single course of azithromycin"], correct: 1, explanation: "Long-term intermittent TMP-SMX (160/800 mg three times weekly) decreased the risk of reactivation over a 20-month period." },
];

const BASIC_AQUEOUS_RECALL: RecallQ[] = [
  { id: 1, question: "What secretes aqueous humor, and from what substrate?", answer: "Aqueous humor is secreted by the nonpigmented ciliary epithelium (NPE) from a substrate of blood plasma." },
  { id: 2, question: "How does aqueous humor differ from plasma, and what is the functional benefit of its high ascorbate content?", answer: "Aqueous has a low protein content and a high concentration of ascorbate. The antioxidant properties of ascorbate help protect intraocular structures by blocking ultraviolet (UV) light." },
  { id: 3, question: "What three structures' tight junctions compose the blood–aqueous barrier?", answer: "The tight junctions of (1) the nonpigmented ciliary epithelium, (2) the iris vasculature, and (3) the inner wall endothelium of the Schlemm canal." },
  { id: 4, question: "What is plasmoid aqueous, and when does it occur?", answer: "When the blood–aqueous barrier is disrupted, blood and ocular fluids mix, producing a protein-rich plasmoid aqueous — as occurs in anterior uveitis." },
  { id: 5, question: "What are the iris and ciliary body anatomically, and what is the iris's function?", answer: "They are the anterior parts of the uvea (uveal tract), continuous with the choroid posteriorly. The iris is a highly pigmented, movable diaphragm between the anterior and posterior chambers that regulates the amount of light reaching the retina." },
  { id: 6, question: "List the main functions of the ciliary body.", answer: "It produces aqueous humor, regulates its composition, and contributes to uveoscleral outflow — thereby influencing the ionic environment and metabolism of the cornea, lens, and trabecular meshwork. It is also a major pharmacologic target in glaucoma." },
  { id: 7, question: "How does the ciliary body defend against oxidative stress?", answer: "It secretes protective molecules into the aqueous and has the highest concentration of redox (reduction–oxidation) enzymes in the anterior segment. It also contains cytochrome P450 enzymes (far fewer than the liver) that detoxify by converting hydrophobic to hydrophilic compounds via hydroxylation." },
  { id: 8, question: "What is the physiologic role of the aqueous humor?", answer: "It is a transparent fluid filling the anterior and posterior chambers, serving as the major nutrient source for the avascular lens and cornea and as a medium for removing waste products." },
  { id: 9, question: "Why is the aqueous optically clear, and what is its refractive index?", answer: "The blood–aqueous barrier restricts plasma proteins from entering the aqueous, so it is essentially protein-free, giving it a refractive index of 1.336 and allowing optical clarity for transmission of light." },
  { id: 10, question: "Describe the two-layer structure of the ciliary epithelium and how they are oriented.", answer: "It is a bilayer of polarized cells: the nonpigmented epithelium (NPE) faces the aqueous, and the pigmented epithelium (PE) faces the ciliary stroma. The two layers are joined at their apical membranes, so their basal membranes face the aqueous and the stroma, respectively." },
  { id: 11, question: "Contrast the barrier properties of the NPE and PE layers.", answer: "The NPE has tight junctions near its apical membrane that form part of the blood–aqueous barrier and prevent paracellular transport from stroma into the posterior chamber. The PE is a 'leaky' epithelium that allows solutes to move between its cells." },
  { id: 12, question: "What channels does the NPE express to move water, and at what rate is aqueous secreted?", answer: "The NPE expresses aquaporin channels that facilitate water transport. Aqueous is secreted at 2–3 µL/min, a rate that fluctuates with circadian rhythm and other factors, causing IOP variation over 24 hours." },
  { id: 13, question: "What is the clinical relevance of the circadian fluctuation of IOP?", answer: "It is the basis for measuring IOP at different times of day in glaucoma patients to assess for fluctuation; the highest or lowest IOP may occur outside normal clinic hours." },
  { id: 14, question: "What are the active and passive mechanisms by which aqueous enters the posterior chamber?", answer: "Active: energy-dependent secretion of certain ions and substrates. Passive: diffusion and ultrafiltration." },
  { id: 15, question: "Which two key proteins drive active aqueous secretion, and how?", answer: "Na+,K+-ATPase and carbonic anhydrase (CA) in the NPE. Active secretion of sodium (with accompanying anions) creates high osmolarity on the basolateral/aqueous side of the NPE, which promotes the diffusion of water." },
  { id: 16, question: "Where is carbonic anhydrase found in the ciliary epithelium, and how do CA inhibitors work?", answer: "In humans CA is present in both the PE and NPE. CA inhibitors reduce the rate of entry of sodium and bicarbonate into the aqueous, thereby reducing aqueous flow." },
  { id: 17, question: "Define cotransport, symport, and antiport.", answer: "Cotransport is the coupled transport of two substances across a membrane, one moving down its gradient to drive the other against its gradient. Symporters move molecules in the same direction; antiporters move them in opposite directions." },
  { id: 18, question: "What are the three steps of unidirectional net aqueous secretion from stroma to posterior chamber?", answer: "(1) Uptake of solute and water at the stromal surface by PE cells; (2) transfer of solute and water from PE to NPE cells; (3) transfer of solute and water by NPE cells into the posterior chamber." },
  { id: 19, question: "Define ultrafiltration in aqueous formation and its relationship to IOP.", answer: "Ultrafiltration is the nonenzymatic component that depends on IOP, blood pressure, and blood osmotic pressure in the ciliary body. Ultrafiltration decreases with increasing IOP." },
  { id: 20, question: "State the Goldmann equation and define each term.", answer: "IOP = (F − U)/C + EVP, where F = rate of aqueous production, U = uveoscleral (pressure-insensitive) drainage, C = trabecular (pressure-sensitive) outflow facility, and EVP = episcleral venous pressure." },
  { id: 21, question: "Name conditions and agents that can disrupt the blood–aqueous barrier.", answer: "Ocular trauma (mechanical, chemical, or physical), infection or inflammation, ischemia, and certain pharmacologic agents (e.g., prostaglandin analogues, cholinesterase inhibitors)." },
  { id: 22, question: "What happens to aqueous protein content and mediators when the blood–aqueous barrier breaks down?", answer: "Inflammatory mediators, immunoglobulins, fibrin, and proteases rise, and the growth-factor balance is disrupted. Aqueous protein content may increase 10–100 times normal, especially high-molecular-weight polypeptides." },
  { id: 23, question: "What are the clinical sequelae of blood–aqueous barrier breakdown?", answer: "Fibrinous exudate (± macrophage reaction and cyclitic membranes), peripheral and posterior synechiae, and an abnormal neovascular response. Chronic disruption drives hyperplastic responses of the lens epithelium, corneal endothelium, trabecular meshwork, and iris, and complicated cataract." },
  { id: 24, question: "Is aqueous humor an ultrafiltrate of plasma? Explain.", answer: "No. Aqueous is produced by energy-dependent (active) processes in the ciliary epithelium, allowing precise control over the composition of the fluid — it is not a simple ultrafiltrate of plasma." },
  { id: 25, question: "Compare the Na+, Cl−, HCO3−, ascorbate, and glucose levels of aqueous versus plasma (per Table 9-1).", answer: "Aqueous vs plasma (mmol/kg H₂O): Na⁺ 163 vs 146 (higher); Cl⁻ 134 vs 109 (higher); HCO₃⁻ 20 vs 28 (lower); ascorbate 1.06 vs 0.04 (much higher); glucose 3 vs 6 (lower)." },
  { id: 26, question: "How do the inorganic ion concentrations of aqueous compare with plasma?", answer: "Sodium, potassium, and magnesium are similar to plasma; calcium is only about half the plasma level. The two major anions are chloride and bicarbonate. Iron, copper, and zinc are present at essentially plasma levels (~1 mg/mL). Phosphate is too low to have significant buffering capacity." },
  { id: 27, question: "What is the most abundant organic anion in aqueous, and why is its level high?", answer: "Lactate — always higher than in plasma, resulting from the glycolytic metabolism upon which the avascular lens depends." },
  { id: 28, question: "How much higher is aqueous ascorbate than plasma ascorbate, and what does it do?", answer: "Ascorbate (vitamin C) is 10–50 times higher in aqueous than in plasma. Its antioxidant properties protect intraocular structures by blocking UV light." },
  { id: 29, question: "What is the aqueous glucose level relative to plasma, and how does glucose cross the ciliary epithelium?", answer: "Aqueous glucose is roughly 50%–70% of plasma. It enters the posterior chamber faster than its size/lipid solubility would predict, indicating transport by facilitated diffusion. In diabetes, aqueous (and thus lens) glucose rises, with short-term refractive and long-term cataract implications." },
  { id: 30, question: "What is the role of glutathione in the aqueous humor?", answer: "Glutathione (a tripeptide with a reactive sulfhydryl group) stabilizes the aqueous redox state by reconverting oxidized ascorbate to its functional form and removing excess hydrogen peroxide. It is also a substrate for glutathione S-transferases, which detoxify electrophilic compounds and are highly concentrated in the ciliary epithelium." },
  { id: 31, question: "How much protein is in normal aqueous versus plasma, and which proteins predominate?", answer: "Normal aqueous contains ~0.02 g protein/100 mL versus ~7 g/100 mL in plasma. Albumin and transferrin are most abundant and together may account for ~50% of the total protein." },
  { id: 32, question: "Which proteins are synthesized within the ciliary body and secreted into the aqueous (per cDNA studies)?", answer: "Complement component C4 (classical pathway), α2-macroglobulin (proteinase inhibition/carrier), apolipoprotein D (binds cholesterol, cholesteryl esters, arachidonic acid), and selenoprotein P (antioxidant), among others." },
  { id: 33, question: "Which proteinase inhibitors in aqueous are best studied, and why does their balance matter?", answer: "α2-macroglobulin and α1-antitrypsin. An imbalance between proteinases and their inhibitors could alter aqueous composition and may cause disease such as glaucoma." },
  { id: 34, question: "What fibrinolytic components are present in the aqueous?", answer: "Both plasminogen and plasminogen activator are found in human and monkey aqueous, but only traces of plasmin; these could help regulate outflow resistance." },
  { id: 35, question: "Why does the ciliary epithelium have neuroendocrine properties, and what might they regulate?", answer: "The ciliary epithelia are derived from neuroectoderm and are functionally similar to neuroendocrine glands. They contain neuroendocrine peptides (neurotensin, angiotensin, endothelins, natriuretic peptides) and processing enzymes that may help determine aqueous composition, the circadian rhythm of secretion/IOP, ciliary blood flow, and ocular immune privilege." },
  { id: 36, question: "Name the growth-modulatory factors identified in aqueous humor and a key function.", answer: "TGF-β1 and -β2, acidic and basic FGF (aFGF/bFGF), IGF-I and IGF-binding proteins (IGFBPs), VEGFs, and transferrin. Together they normally suppress mitosis of the corneal endothelium and trabecular meshwork in vivo." },
  { id: 37, question: "How are aqueous growth-factor levels altered in disease?", answer: "IGFBPs are elevated fivefold in diabetes without retinopathy; IGF-I is elevated in diabetic retinopathy; VEGF is elevated in retinal vascular disease and acute nonarteritic ischemic optic neuropathy; interleukin-2 is reduced." },
  { id: 38, question: "How may VEGF-A regulate IOP?", answer: "VEGF-A may lower IOP by upregulating endothelial nitric oxide synthase (eNOS) and elevating nitric oxide (NO), which increases aqueous outflow facility. VEGF-A blockade may therefore raise IOP by decreasing NO production. VEGF-A is elevated with retinal vascular disease and iris neovascularization and increased by hypoxia; it falls after intravitreal anti-VEGF injection." },
  { id: 39, question: "Where does aqueous oxygen come from, and which structures depend on it?", answer: "From the blood supply to the ciliary body and iris (atmospheric O₂ flux across the cornea is negligible). The corneal endothelium critically depends on aqueous oxygen for its active fluid-transport pump; the lens and trabecular meshwork endothelium also rely on aqueous oxygen. Aqueous O₂ partial pressure is lower than in arterial blood." },
  { id: 40, question: "What is the CO₂ content and pH of aqueous, and why does vitrectomy raise cataract/glaucoma risk?", answer: "CO₂ is 40–60 mm Hg (~3% of total bicarbonate) and the pH is ~7.5–7.6; CO₂ is lost by diffusion across the cornea. Aqueous oxygen concentration increases after vitreous degeneration or vitrectomy, and the elevated oxygen induces oxidative damage in the lens and trabecular meshwork, increasing the risk of cataract and open-angle glaucoma." },
];

const BASIC_AQUEOUS_MCQ: MCQ[] = [
  { id: 1, question: "Aqueous humor is secreted by the:", options: ["Pigmented ciliary epithelium (PE)", "Nonpigmented ciliary epithelium (NPE)", "Trabecular meshwork endothelium", "Corneal endothelium"], correct: 1, explanation: "Aqueous is secreted by the nonpigmented ciliary epithelium (NPE) from a substrate of blood plasma." },
  { id: 2, question: "Compared with plasma, aqueous humor characteristically has:", options: ["High protein and low ascorbate", "Low protein and high ascorbate", "High protein and high ascorbate", "Low protein and low ascorbate"], correct: 1, explanation: "Aqueous has low protein content and a high ascorbate concentration; ascorbate's antioxidant action blocks UV light." },
  { id: 3, question: "The blood–aqueous barrier is composed of the tight junctions of all of the following EXCEPT:", options: ["Nonpigmented ciliary epithelium", "Iris vasculature", "Inner wall endothelium of Schlemm canal", "Corneal epithelium"], correct: 3, explanation: "The barrier comprises tight junctions of the NPE, iris vasculature, and Schlemm canal inner wall endothelium — not the corneal epithelium." },
  { id: 4, question: "Disruption of the blood–aqueous barrier producing a protein-rich 'plasmoid aqueous' is characteristic of:", options: ["Primary open-angle glaucoma", "Anterior uveitis", "Cataract", "Dry eye disease"], correct: 1, explanation: "Barrier breakdown lets blood and ocular fluids mix, forming plasmoid aqueous, as in anterior uveitis." },
  { id: 5, question: "The iris and ciliary body constitute the:", options: ["Posterior uvea", "Anterior uvea (uveal tract)", "Neural retina", "Outer fibrous tunic"], correct: 1, explanation: "The iris and ciliary body are the anterior parts of the uvea, continuous with the choroid posteriorly." },
  { id: 6, question: "Which of the following is NOT a function of the ciliary body?", options: ["Producing aqueous humor", "Regulating aqueous composition", "Contributing to uveoscleral outflow", "Focusing light onto the fovea"], correct: 3, explanation: "The ciliary body produces and regulates aqueous and contributes to uveoscleral outflow; image focusing onto the fovea is not its role." },
  { id: 7, question: "The anterior segment structure with the highest concentration of redox enzymes is the:", options: ["Cornea", "Lens", "Ciliary body", "Trabecular meshwork"], correct: 2, explanation: "The ciliary body has the highest concentration of redox enzymes in the anterior segment and defends against oxidative stress." },
  { id: 8, question: "Cytochrome P450 enzymes in the ciliary body detoxify compounds by:", options: ["Converting hydrophilic to hydrophobic compounds", "Converting hydrophobic to hydrophilic compounds via hydroxylation", "Cleaving disulfide bonds", "Phosphorylating substrates"], correct: 1, explanation: "Ciliary body P450 enzymes convert hydrophobic to hydrophilic compounds via hydroxylation for detoxification." },
  { id: 9, question: "The aqueous humor is the major nutrient source for which avascular structures?", options: ["Lens and cornea", "Retina and optic nerve", "Sclera and choroid", "Conjunctiva and lacrimal gland"], correct: 0, explanation: "Aqueous nourishes the avascular lens and cornea and removes their waste products." },
  { id: 10, question: "The refractive index of the essentially protein-free aqueous humor is approximately:", options: ["1.000", "1.336", "1.376", "1.406"], correct: 1, explanation: "Being nearly protein-free gives aqueous a refractive index of 1.336 and optical clarity." },
  { id: 11, question: "In the ciliary epithelium bilayer, the layer that faces the aqueous humor is the:", options: ["Pigmented epithelium (PE)", "Nonpigmented epithelium (NPE)", "Trabecular endothelium", "Bruch membrane"], correct: 1, explanation: "The NPE faces the aqueous; the PE faces the ciliary stroma, and the two are joined at their apical membranes." },
  { id: 12, question: "Which ciliary epithelial layer is considered a 'leaky' epithelium?", options: ["Nonpigmented epithelium (NPE)", "Pigmented epithelium (PE)", "Both are equally tight", "Neither is leaky"], correct: 1, explanation: "The PE is a leaky epithelium allowing solute movement between cells; the NPE's tight junctions form the barrier." },
  { id: 13, question: "Water transport across the NPE is facilitated by:", options: ["Voltage-gated sodium channels", "Aquaporin channels", "Gap junctions", "Clathrin-coated pits"], correct: 1, explanation: "The NPE expresses aquaporin channels that facilitate water transport into the posterior chamber." },
  { id: 14, question: "The normal rate of aqueous humor secretion is approximately:", options: ["0.2–0.3 µL/min", "2–3 µL/min", "20–30 µL/min", "200–300 µL/min"], correct: 1, explanation: "Aqueous is secreted at 2–3 µL/min, fluctuating with circadian rhythm." },
  { id: 15, question: "IOP measurements are taken at different times of day in glaucoma patients because:", options: ["The cornea thickens overnight", "IOP fluctuates with circadian rhythm, and peaks/troughs may fall outside clinic hours", "Aqueous stops forming at night", "Tonometers drift during the day"], correct: 1, explanation: "Aqueous secretion and IOP vary with circadian rhythm, so extremes can occur outside normal clinic hours." },
  { id: 16, question: "Which are the passive mechanisms of aqueous entry into the posterior chamber?", options: ["Na+,K+-ATPase and carbonic anhydrase activity", "Diffusion and ultrafiltration", "Endocytosis and exocytosis", "Symport and antiport"], correct: 1, explanation: "Passive mechanisms are diffusion and ultrafiltration; active secretion is energy-dependent." },
  { id: 17, question: "Active aqueous secretion is driven primarily by which enzyme creating an osmotic gradient?", options: ["Carbonic anhydrase alone", "Na+,K+-ATPase", "Lactate dehydrogenase", "Glutathione S-transferase"], correct: 1, explanation: "Na+,K+-ATPase actively secretes sodium, raising osmolarity on the aqueous side and drawing water in; CA supplies H+/HCO3−." },
  { id: 18, question: "Carbonic anhydrase inhibitors lower IOP by:", options: ["Increasing episcleral venous pressure", "Reducing the entry of sodium and bicarbonate into the aqueous, decreasing aqueous flow", "Dilating the trabecular meshwork", "Blocking aquaporin channels directly"], correct: 1, explanation: "CA inhibitors reduce sodium and bicarbonate entry into aqueous, reducing aqueous production." },
  { id: 19, question: "A membrane transporter that moves two molecules in OPPOSITE directions is a(n):", options: ["Symporter", "Antiporter", "Uniporter", "Aquaporin"], correct: 1, explanation: "Antiporters cotransport molecules in opposite directions; symporters move them in the same direction." },
  { id: 20, question: "In the 3-step model of net aqueous secretion, the first step is:", options: ["Transfer of solute/water from PE to NPE", "Uptake of solute and water at the stromal surface by PE cells", "Extrusion into the posterior chamber by NPE", "Reabsorption into the stroma"], correct: 1, explanation: "Step 1: PE cells take up solute and water at the stromal surface; then PE→NPE; then NPE→posterior chamber." },
  { id: 21, question: "Ultrafiltration in aqueous formation:", options: ["Increases with increasing IOP", "Decreases with increasing IOP", "Is an energy-dependent process", "Is independent of blood pressure"], correct: 1, explanation: "Ultrafiltration is nonenzymatic, depends on IOP/BP/blood osmotic pressure, and decreases as IOP rises." },
  { id: 22, question: "In the Goldmann equation IOP = (F − U)/C + EVP, the term 'F' represents:", options: ["Outflow facility", "Rate of aqueous humor production", "Uveoscleral drainage", "Episcleral venous pressure"], correct: 1, explanation: "F is the rate of aqueous production; U is uveoscleral drainage, C is trabecular outflow facility, EVP is episcleral venous pressure." },
  { id: 23, question: "In the Goldmann equation, 'C' (trabecular outflow facility) represents the:", options: ["Pressure-insensitive pathway", "Pressure-sensitive outflow pathway", "Episcleral venous pressure", "Rate of aqueous production"], correct: 1, explanation: "The trabecular pathway (C) is pressure-sensitive; the uveoscleral pathway (U) is pressure-insensitive." },
  { id: 24, question: "The uveoscleral outflow term (U) in the Goldmann equation is described as:", options: ["Pressure-sensitive", "Pressure-insensitive", "Dependent only on EVP", "Equal to aqueous production"], correct: 1, explanation: "Uveoscleral outflow is the pressure-insensitive pathway; trabecular outflow is pressure-sensitive." },
  { id: 25, question: "Which agent class is noted to disrupt the blood–aqueous barrier?", options: ["Prostaglandin analogues and cholinesterase inhibitors", "Topical antihistamines", "Artificial tears", "Beta-blocker eyedrops"], correct: 0, explanation: "Trauma, inflammation, ischemia, and drugs such as prostaglandin analogues and cholinesterase inhibitors can disrupt the barrier." },
  { id: 26, question: "With breakdown of the blood–aqueous barrier, aqueous protein content may rise by approximately:", options: ["10%–20%", "2–3 times", "10–100 times normal", "1000 times normal"], correct: 2, explanation: "Protein content may increase 10–100 times normal, especially high-molecular-weight polypeptides." },
  { id: 27, question: "A clinical sequela of chronic blood–aqueous barrier breakdown is:", options: ["Corneal dystrophy of Fuchs from birth", "Synechiae, cyclitic membranes, and complicated cataract", "Congenital glaucoma", "Optic disc drusen"], correct: 1, explanation: "Sequelae include fibrinous exudate, cyclitic membranes, peripheral/posterior synechiae, neovascularization, and complicated cataract." },
  { id: 28, question: "Aqueous humor is best described as:", options: ["A simple ultrafiltrate of plasma", "The product of energy-dependent secretion by the ciliary epithelium", "A transudate driven only by hydrostatic pressure", "Identical in composition to vitreous"], correct: 1, explanation: "Aqueous is not an ultrafiltrate; it is produced by active, energy-dependent processes allowing precise compositional control." },
  { id: 29, question: "According to Table 9-1, compared with plasma the aqueous humor has a HIGHER concentration of:", options: ["Bicarbonate", "Glucose", "Sodium and chloride", "Protein"], correct: 2, explanation: "Aqueous has higher Na⁺ (163 vs 146) and Cl⁻ (134 vs 109), but lower HCO₃⁻, glucose, and protein than plasma." },
  { id: 30, question: "Compared with plasma, the aqueous bicarbonate (HCO₃⁻) concentration is:", options: ["Higher", "Lower", "Identical", "Absent"], correct: 1, explanation: "Aqueous HCO₃⁻ (~20) is lower than plasma (~28) per Table 9-1." },
  { id: 31, question: "The calcium concentration in aqueous humor compared with plasma is:", options: ["Twice as high", "About half", "Identical", "Ten times higher"], correct: 1, explanation: "Sodium, potassium, and magnesium are similar to plasma, but calcium in aqueous is only about half the plasma level." },
  { id: 32, question: "The two major anions in the aqueous humor are:", options: ["Chloride and bicarbonate", "Phosphate and sulfate", "Lactate and ascorbate", "Nitrate and acetate"], correct: 0, explanation: "Chloride and bicarbonate are the two major anions; phosphate is too low to buffer significantly." },
  { id: 33, question: "The most abundant organic anion in aqueous humor is:", options: ["Ascorbate", "Lactate", "Pyruvate", "Citrate"], correct: 1, explanation: "Lactate is the most abundant organic anion, reflecting the glycolytic metabolism of the avascular lens." },
  { id: 34, question: "The high lactate level in aqueous humor primarily reflects:", options: ["Aerobic metabolism of the cornea", "Glycolytic metabolism of the avascular lens", "Active transport by the trabecular meshwork", "Bacterial fermentation"], correct: 1, explanation: "Lactate is high because the avascular lens depends on glycolytic (anaerobic) metabolism." },
  { id: 35, question: "Ascorbate (vitamin C) concentration in aqueous is approximately how much higher than in plasma?", options: ["The same", "2–3 times", "10–50 times", "1000 times"], correct: 2, explanation: "Aqueous ascorbate is 10–50 times higher than plasma; it acts as an antioxidant blocking UV light." },
  { id: 36, question: "The aqueous glucose concentration is approximately what fraction of plasma glucose?", options: ["10%–20%", "50%–70%", "100%", "150%"], correct: 1, explanation: "Aqueous glucose is roughly 50%–70% of plasma; it crosses the ciliary epithelium by facilitated diffusion." },
  { id: 37, question: "The rapid entry of glucose into the posterior chamber (faster than predicted by size/lipid solubility) indicates transport by:", options: ["Simple diffusion", "Facilitated diffusion", "Primary active transport", "Pinocytosis"], correct: 1, explanation: "Glucose enters faster than expected, indicating facilitated diffusion across the ciliary epithelium." },
  { id: 38, question: "In diabetes, increased aqueous glucose raises lens glucose, leading to:", options: ["Short-term refractive change and long-term cataract", "Immediate retinal detachment", "Corneal neovascularization", "Acute angle closure"], correct: 0, explanation: "Elevated lens glucose in diabetes has short-term refractive and long-term cataract implications." },
  { id: 39, question: "Inositol, important for phospholipid synthesis, is present in aqueous at a concentration approximately:", options: ["Half that of plasma", "Equal to plasma", "10 times that of plasma", "100 times that of plasma"], correct: 2, explanation: "Inositol is ~10 times higher in aqueous than plasma and supports anterior-segment phospholipid synthesis." },
  { id: 40, question: "Glutathione stabilizes the redox state of the aqueous by all of the following EXCEPT:", options: ["Reconverting oxidized ascorbate to its functional form", "Removing excess hydrogen peroxide", "Serving as a substrate for glutathione S-transferases", "Directly absorbing UV light"], correct: 3, explanation: "Glutathione regenerates ascorbate, removes H₂O₂, and is a GST substrate; UV blocking is a property of ascorbate, not glutathione." },
  { id: 41, question: "Although blood contains abundant glutathione, plasma glutathione is low because:", options: ["It is rapidly excreted by the kidney", "Virtually all glutathione resides within erythrocytes", "It binds tightly to albumin", "It is destroyed by plasma esterases"], correct: 1, explanation: "Nearly all blood glutathione is inside erythrocytes, so plasma levels are low (≤5 µmol/L)." },
  { id: 42, question: "Normal aqueous humor protein content is approximately:", options: ["0.02 g/100 mL", "0.7 g/100 mL", "2 g/100 mL", "7 g/100 mL"], correct: 0, explanation: "Aqueous contains ~0.02 g protein/100 mL versus ~7 g/100 mL in plasma." },
  { id: 43, question: "The most abundant plasma proteins in the aqueous humor, together ~50% of total protein, are:", options: ["Albumin and transferrin", "IgG and IgM", "Fibrinogen and complement", "Ceruloplasmin and haptoglobin"], correct: 0, explanation: "Albumin and transferrin are the most abundant and together may account for ~50% of aqueous protein." },
  { id: 44, question: "Which protein synthesized by the ciliary body has antioxidant properties?", options: ["Complement C4", "α2-macroglobulin", "Apolipoprotein D", "Selenoprotein P"], correct: 3, explanation: "Selenoprotein P, isolated from ciliary body cDNA, has antioxidant properties." },
  { id: 45, question: "Apolipoprotein D, secreted into the aqueous, functions to:", options: ["Activate the complement cascade", "Bind and transport hydrophobic substances such as cholesterol and arachidonic acid", "Inhibit proteinases", "Generate nitric oxide"], correct: 1, explanation: "Apolipoprotein D binds and transports hydrophobic substances including cholesterol, cholesteryl esters, and arachidonic acid." },
  { id: 46, question: "The two most extensively studied proteinase inhibitors in aqueous humor are:", options: ["α2-macroglobulin and α1-antitrypsin", "Cathepsin D and cathepsin O", "Plasmin and plasminogen", "TGF-β1 and TGF-β2"], correct: 0, explanation: "α2-macroglobulin and α1-antitrypsin are the best-studied inhibitors; proteinase/inhibitor imbalance may contribute to glaucoma." },
  { id: 47, question: "Regarding the fibrinolytic system in aqueous, which is TRUE?", options: ["Plasmin is abundant; plasminogen is absent", "Plasminogen and plasminogen activator are present, but only traces of plasmin", "Only plasmin is present", "None of these components exist in aqueous"], correct: 1, explanation: "Plasminogen and plasminogen activator are found in aqueous, but only traces of plasmin; they may regulate outflow resistance." },
  { id: 48, question: "The ciliary epithelium has neuroendocrine properties because it is derived from:", options: ["Surface ectoderm", "Neuroectoderm (neural crest/neuroepithelium)", "Mesoderm", "Endoderm"], correct: 1, explanation: "The ciliary epithelia are neuroectoderm-derived and functionally resemble neuroendocrine glands." },
  { id: 49, question: "Which set of markers reflects the neuroendocrine nature of the ciliary body?", options: ["Neurotensin, angiotensin, endothelins, natriuretic peptides", "Rhodopsin and opsins", "Collagen I and IV", "Keratin and vimentin"], correct: 0, explanation: "Neurotensin, angiotensin, endothelins, and natriuretic peptides are neuroendocrine markers identified in the ciliary body." },
  { id: 50, question: "Growth-modulatory factors in aqueous (TGF-β, FGF, IGF, VEGF) are thought to normally:", options: ["Stimulate rapid corneal endothelial mitosis", "Suppress mitosis of the corneal endothelium and trabecular meshwork", "Trigger retinal detachment", "Dissolve the trabecular meshwork"], correct: 1, explanation: "Their coordinated action normally suppresses in vivo mitosis of the corneal endothelium and trabecular meshwork." },
  { id: 51, question: "Which aqueous growth factor/protein is elevated fivefold in patients with diabetes WITHOUT retinopathy?", options: ["IGF-I", "IGF-binding proteins (IGFBPs)", "VEGF", "Interleukin-2"], correct: 1, explanation: "IGFBPs are elevated fivefold in diabetes without retinopathy; IGF-I rises with diabetic retinopathy." },
  { id: 52, question: "VEGF-A may lower IOP by:", options: ["Constricting episcleral veins", "Upregulating eNOS and nitric oxide, increasing outflow facility", "Blocking aquaporins", "Stimulating Na+,K+-ATPase"], correct: 1, explanation: "VEGF-A upregulates eNOS and NO, increasing aqueous outflow facility; VEGF-A blockade may raise IOP by lowering NO." },
  { id: 53, question: "Aqueous VEGF-A is particularly elevated in eyes with:", options: ["Secondary iris neovascularization", "Simple myopia", "Dry eye", "Corneal arcus"], correct: 0, explanation: "VEGF-A is elevated in retinal vascular disease and especially in eyes with secondary iris neovascularization; it falls after intravitreal anti-VEGF." },
  { id: 54, question: "The oxygen in the aqueous humor is derived mainly from:", options: ["Atmospheric diffusion across the cornea", "The blood supply to the ciliary body and iris", "The retinal circulation", "The tear film"], correct: 1, explanation: "Aqueous oxygen comes from the ciliary body and iris blood supply; atmospheric flux across the cornea is negligible." },
  { id: 55, question: "The corneal endothelium depends on aqueous oxygen chiefly to power:", options: ["Melanin synthesis", "Its active fluid-transport pump that maintains corneal transparency", "Photoreceptor renewal", "Mucin secretion"], correct: 1, explanation: "The corneal endothelium relies on aqueous oxygen for the active fluid-transport (pump) that keeps the cornea clear." },
  { id: 56, question: "After vitrectomy, the increased aqueous/vitreous cavity oxygen concentration raises the risk of:", options: ["Cataract and open-angle glaucoma", "Retinitis pigmentosa", "Keratoconus", "Amblyopia"], correct: 0, explanation: "Elevated oxygen after vitreous degeneration/vitrectomy induces oxidative damage in the lens and trabecular meshwork, increasing cataract and open-angle glaucoma risk." },
];

// Anterior chamber / uveal ANATOMY (BCSC Section 2, Chapter 2). Appended to
// the Aqueous Humor part; ids continue from the physiology arrays so the
// concatenated Part 4 pool keeps unique ids for React keys and answer tracking.
const BASIC_AC_ANAT_RECALL: RecallQ[] = [
  { id: 41, question: "What forms the anterior and posterior borders of the anterior chamber?", answer: "The anterior chamber is bordered anteriorly by the cornea and posteriorly by the iris diaphragm and the pupil." },
  { id: 42, question: "List the structures of the anterior chamber angle.", answer: "Schwalbe line; the Schlemm canal and trabecular meshwork; the scleral spur; the anterior border of the ciliary body (where its longitudinal fibers insert into the scleral spur); and the peripheral iris." },
  { id: 43, question: "What is the average anterior chamber depth, and how does it vary?", answer: "It averages 3.11 mm. It is deeper in aphakia, pseudophakia, and myopia, and shallower in hyperopia. The AC is deepest centrally and narrowest slightly central to the angle recess." },
  { id: 44, question: "What are the two main aqueous outflow pathways?", answer: "(1) The trabecular pathway — through the trabecular meshwork into the Schlemm canal and episcleral veins; and (2) the uveoscleral pathway — through the root of the iris and ciliary body face into the suprachoroidal space." },
  { id: 45, question: "How much of aqueous outflow is uveoscleral in young people, and how does it change with age?", answer: "The uveoscleral pathway accounts for up to 50% of aqueous outflow in young people and is thought to decrease with increasing age." },
  { id: 46, question: "Where does aqueous ultimately drain, and what determines IOP?", answer: "In both main pathways aqueous joins the venous circulation of the eye, which drains into the cavernous sinus. IOP is determined by the rate of aqueous production, the resistance of the trabecular meshwork, and the pressure of the veins receiving the aqueous — so changes in intrathoracic or cavernous sinus pressure can elevate IOP." },
  { id: 47, question: "Describe the third (minor) aqueous outflow pathway and its significance.", answer: "A third pathway in which aqueous traverses the vitreous, crosses the retina, and enters the choroid. It has minimal effect on IOP but is important in maintaining retinal adhesion." },
  { id: 48, question: "What does the internal scleral sulcus accommodate, and what are its anterior and posterior landmarks?", answer: "It accommodates the Schlemm canal externally and the trabecular meshwork internally. Its anterior margin is Schwalbe line (the peripheral limit of Descemet membrane) and its posterior landmark is the scleral spur." },
  { id: 49, question: "What inserts into the scleral spur, and what is the effect of its contraction?", answer: "The longitudinal fibers of the ciliary muscle insert into the scleral spur; their contraction opens up the trabecular spaces, increasing aqueous outflow." },
  { id: 50, question: "What are scleral spur cells, and what stimulates increased outflow through them?", answer: "Myofibroblast-like, contractile cells disposed circumferentially within the scleral spur; they resemble mechanoreceptors, receive sensory innervation, and connect to the trabecular meshwork by elastic tissue. Stimulation with vasoactive intestinal polypeptide (VIP) or calcitonin gene–related peptide (CGRP) increases aqueous outflow." },
  { id: 51, question: "From which tissues is the trabecular meshwork derived, and what cells line it?", answer: "The outflow apparatus is derived from the cornea, sclera, iris, and ciliary body. The TM is a circular spongework of connective tissue lined by trabeculocytes, which have contractile and phagocytic properties. In cross section it is roughly triangular — apex at Schwalbe line, base at the scleral spur and ciliary body." },
  { id: 52, question: "What are the three layers of the trabecular meshwork?", answer: "The uveal, corneoscleral, and juxtacanalicular layers (the juxtacanalicular being directly adjacent to the Schlemm canal)." },
  { id: 53, question: "How are the uveal and corneoscleral meshworks divided?", answer: "By an imaginary line drawn from Schwalbe line to the scleral spur: the uveal meshwork lies internal to this line (facing the anterior chamber) and the corneoscleral meshwork lies external to it." },
  { id: 54, question: "What are the age-related changes of the trabecular meshwork?", answer: "Increased pigmentation, decreased number of trabecular cells, thickening of the basement membrane, two- to threefold thickening of trabecular sheets, loss of endothelial cellularity, increased connective tissue, debris accumulation, and glycosaminoglycan accumulation — all increasing outflow resistance (changes that are exaggerated in open-angle glaucoma)." },
  { id: 55, question: "What distinguishes the uveal meshwork?", answer: "It faces the anterior chamber, is composed of cordlike trabeculae, has fewer elastic fibers than the corneoscleral meshwork, and its trabeculocytes contain pigment granules; its apertures are less circular and larger than those of the corneoscleral meshwork." },
  { id: 56, question: "What distinguishes the corneoscleral meshwork?", answer: "It consists of thin, flat, perforated connective tissue sheets in a laminar pattern. Each beam is covered by a monolayer of thin trabecular cells with pinocytotic vesicles; the basal lamina forms the outer cortex and the inner core is collagen and elastic fibers." },
  { id: 57, question: "Why is the juxtacanalicular meshwork the site of greatest outflow resistance?", answer: "It invests the Schlemm canal and forms its inner wall; the endothelial meshwork here has spaces up to ~10 μm through which aqueous percolates. It contributes the most to outflow resistance because the pathway is narrow and tortuous and because of resistance from extracellular proteoglycans and glycoproteins — it is the highest point of resistance to aqueous outflow." },
  { id: 58, question: "Describe the structure of the Schlemm canal.", answer: "A circular tube resembling a lymphatic vessel, formed by a continuous monolayer of nonfenestrated endothelium and a thin connective tissue wall. Its basement membrane is poorly defined, the lateral endothelial walls are joined by tight junctions, and the surfaces contain micropinocytotic vesicles." },
  { id: 59, question: "What are the giant vacuoles of the Schlemm canal, and what is their function?", answer: "Larger vesicles along the internal (inner) canal wall, lined by a single membrane. Their size and number increase with a rise in IOP, and they are thought to contribute to the pressure-dependent outflow of aqueous (forming transcellular channels)." },
  { id: 60, question: "How does one type of MIGS use the Schlemm canal?", answer: "A microstent is implanted in the Schlemm canal to bypass the trabecular meshwork — the point of greatest resistance — thereby increasing aqueous outflow." },
  { id: 61, question: "Describe the collector channels, aqueous veins, and Sondermann channels.", answer: "Approximately 25–30 collector channels arise from the Schlemm canal and drain into the deep and midscleral venous plexuses; up to 8 drain directly into the episcleral venous plexus as aqueous veins (visible in the conjunctiva by biomicroscopy). Sondermann channels are small, tortuous, blind diverticula extending from the canal into the trabecular meshwork." },
  { id: 62, question: "What are the three parts of the uvea and their locations?", answer: "The iris (anterior uvea); the ciliary body (anterior pars plicata and middle pars plana); and the choroid (posterior uvea). The uvea is the main vascular layer of the eye." },
  { id: 63, question: "At what three sites is the uvea firmly attached to the sclera, and what does this explain?", answer: "The scleral spur, the exit points of the vortex veins, and the optic nerve. These attachments account for the characteristic anterior dome-shaped choroidal detachment." },
  { id: 64, question: "How does the SUN Working Group classify uveitis anatomically?", answer: "By the primary anatomical site of inflammation: anterior uveitis (anterior chamber), intermediate uveitis (vitreous), posterior uveitis (retina or choroid), and panuveitis (anterior chamber, vitreous, and retina or choroid)." },
  { id: 65, question: "From which embryonic tissues is the iris derived, and what determines its color?", answer: "Mesodermal tissue (blood vessels, connective tissue) and neuroectodermal tissue (smooth muscle, epithelial cells, melanocytes). Iris color is genetic and determined by the relative density of melanocytes and other pigmented cells." },
  { id: 66, question: "Describe the iris topography, the collarette, and iris crypts.", answer: "Topographic features include the pupillary margin, pupillary (pupil) zone, collarette, crypts, and iris root. The collarette (anterior remnant of the hyaloidal system) divides the surface into a central pupil zone (containing the sphincter) and a peripheral ciliary zone (containing the dilator). Crypts are thinned areas of iris; peripheral crypts are a good target for laser iridotomy." },
  { id: 67, question: "Why are the iris root and pupillary margin especially vulnerable to blunt trauma?", answer: "Because of their relative thinness: blunt trauma to the iris root causes iridodialysis, and trauma at the pupillary margin causes sphincter tears and/or traumatic mydriasis." },
  { id: 68, question: "What composes the iris stroma, and how does pigmentation relate to iris color?", answer: "Pigmented cells (melanocytes), nonpigmented cells, collagen fibers, and a hyaluronic acid–containing matrix; aqueous flows freely within the loose stroma. Overall stromal structure is similar in all iris colors — blue irides have a lightly pigmented stroma, brown irides a densely pigmented one." },
  { id: 69, question: "Where is the major arterial circle of the iris actually located, and what is the minor circle?", answer: "The major arterial circle is located at the apex of the ciliary body, not in the iris. Iris vessels run a radial course from it; anastomoses near the thick collarette form the (often incomplete) minor vascular circle. Iris capillaries are large, nonfenestrated, and lack an internal elastic lamina." },
  { id: 70, question: "Describe the origin and innervation of the iris dilator muscle.", answer: "It develops from the anterior pigmented epithelium and is derived from neuroectoderm, lying anterior and parallel to the posterior pigmented epithelium. It has dual innervation: it contracts to sympathetic α1-adrenergic stimulation, while cholinergic parasympathetic input may be inhibitory." },
  { id: 71, question: "Describe the origin and innervation of the iris sphincter muscle.", answer: "Derived from neuroectoderm, it is a circular band of smooth muscle near the pupillary margin in the deep stroma. Its primary innervation is parasympathetic from the Edinger-Westphal nucleus traveling with CN III (muscarinic), with reciprocal sympathetic input that relaxes it in darkness." },
  { id: 72, question: "What is the iris posterior pigmented epithelium (IPE), and what is ectropion uveae?", answer: "The IPE is densely pigmented, continuous with the nonpigmented ciliary epithelium and thus the neurosensory retina; its basal surface borders the posterior chamber, and it curves around the pupil as the pigment ruff. Ectropion uveae — an outfolding of IPE over the pupil — is a misnomer, because the IPE is derived from neuroectoderm (not neural crest) and is not truly part of the uvea." },
  { id: 73, question: "Describe the shape, attachments, and principal functions of the ciliary body.", answer: "It is triangular in cross section and bridges the anterior and posterior segments; its apex points posteriorly toward the ora serrata and its base gives rise to the iris. Its only scleral attachment is at its base, via longitudinal muscle fibers inserting into the scleral spur. Its two principal functions are aqueous humor formation and lens accommodation (plus a role in trabecular/uveoscleral outflow)." },
  { id: 74, question: "Contrast the pars plana and pars plicata of the ciliary body.", answer: "The ciliary body is 6–7 mm wide. The pars plana is a relatively avascular, smooth, pigmented zone ~4 mm wide from the ora serrata to the ciliary processes — the safest posterior surgical approach to the vitreous is through it, 3–4 mm from the limbus. The pars plicata is richly vascularized with ~70–80 radial ciliary processes; zonular fibers attach mainly in the valleys of the processes and along the pars plana." },
  { id: 75, question: "Describe the ciliary muscle's three fiber groups, innervation, and effect on outflow.", answer: "Longitudinal (outer, most of the muscle, attach to the scleral spur), radial (midportion), and circular (innermost) fibers, functioning as a unit. Innervation is mainly parasympathetic via CN III short ciliary nerves (~97% to the ciliary muscle for accommodation, ~3% to the iris sphincter); sympathetic fibers relax it. Cholinergic drugs contract the ciliary muscle, opening trabecular spaces and increasing aqueous flow." },
  { id: 76, question: "Describe the choroid's layers and the supraciliary space.", answer: "The choroid (posterior uvea) averages ~0.25 mm and has three vascular layers: the choriocapillaris (innermost), the Sattler layer (middle, small vessels), and the Haller layer (outer, large vessels); it is perfused by the long and short posterior ciliary arteries and perforating anterior ciliary arteries and drains through the vortex veins. The supraciliary space is a potential space below the sclera and above the choroid and ciliary body that can expand (e.g., in uveal effusion syndrome) and is a possible drug-delivery site." },
];

const BASIC_AC_ANAT_MCQ: MCQ[] = [
  { id: 57, question: "The anterior chamber is bordered posteriorly by the:", options: ["Cornea", "Iris diaphragm and pupil", "Lens capsule", "Vitreous face"], correct: 1, explanation: "The AC is bordered anteriorly by the cornea and posteriorly by the iris diaphragm and pupil." },
  { id: 58, question: "Which is NOT a structure of the anterior chamber angle?", options: ["Schwalbe line", "Scleral spur", "Trabecular meshwork", "Ora serrata"], correct: 3, explanation: "The angle contains Schwalbe line, Schlemm canal/TM, scleral spur, the anterior ciliary body border, and peripheral iris — not the ora serrata." },
  { id: 59, question: "The average depth of the anterior chamber is approximately:", options: ["1.5 mm", "3.11 mm", "5.5 mm", "7 mm"], correct: 1, explanation: "AC depth averages 3.11 mm." },
  { id: 60, question: "The anterior chamber tends to be SHALLOWER in eyes with:", options: ["Myopia", "Aphakia", "Pseudophakia", "Hyperopia"], correct: 3, explanation: "The AC is deeper in aphakia, pseudophakia, and myopia, and shallower in hyperopia." },
  { id: 61, question: "The trabecular outflow pathway carries aqueous into the:", options: ["Suprachoroidal space", "Schlemm canal and episcleral veins", "Vitreous cavity", "Subretinal space"], correct: 1, explanation: "The trabecular pathway drains through the TM into the Schlemm canal and episcleral veins; the uveoscleral pathway goes to the suprachoroidal space." },
  { id: 62, question: "In young people, the uveoscleral pathway may account for up to what fraction of aqueous outflow?", options: ["5%", "20%", "50%", "90%"], correct: 2, explanation: "Uveoscleral outflow accounts for up to 50% in young people and decreases with age." },
  { id: 63, question: "IOP is determined by all of the following EXCEPT:", options: ["Rate of aqueous production", "Resistance of the trabecular meshwork", "Pressure of the veins receiving aqueous", "Corneal epithelial thickness"], correct: 3, explanation: "IOP depends on aqueous production, TM resistance, and receiving venous pressure — not corneal epithelial thickness." },
  { id: 64, question: "The venous drainage of aqueous ultimately reaches the:", options: ["Cavernous sinus", "Superior sagittal sinus", "Internal jugular vein directly", "Pterygoid plexus only"], correct: 0, explanation: "Aqueous joins the eye's venous circulation, which drains into the cavernous sinus; raised cavernous sinus pressure can elevate IOP." },
  { id: 65, question: "The minor 'third' outflow pathway (aqueous across the retina into the choroid) is chiefly important for:", options: ["Lowering IOP substantially", "Maintaining retinal adhesion", "Corneal nutrition", "Lens accommodation"], correct: 1, explanation: "This transretinal pathway has minimal IOP effect but helps maintain retinal adhesion." },
  { id: 66, question: "Schwalbe line represents the:", options: ["Posterior limit of the trabecular meshwork", "Peripheral termination of Descemet membrane", "Insertion of the ciliary muscle", "Anterior edge of the ciliary body"], correct: 1, explanation: "Schwalbe line is the peripheral limit of Descemet membrane and the anterior margin of the internal scleral sulcus." },
  { id: 67, question: "The scleral spur is the insertion site of the ___ fibers of the ciliary muscle.", options: ["Circular", "Radial", "Longitudinal (meridional)", "Oblique only"], correct: 2, explanation: "The longitudinal (meridional) ciliary muscle fibers insert into the scleral spur; their contraction opens the trabecular spaces." },
  { id: 68, question: "Stimulation of scleral spur cells with VIP or CGRP produces:", options: ["Decreased aqueous outflow", "Increased aqueous outflow", "Pupillary constriction", "Accommodation"], correct: 1, explanation: "VIP or CGRP stimulation of the contractile scleral spur cells increases aqueous outflow." },
  { id: 69, question: "The trabecular outflow apparatus is derived from tissue of all of the following EXCEPT the:", options: ["Cornea and sclera", "Iris", "Ciliary body", "Retina"], correct: 3, explanation: "The outflow apparatus derives from cornea, sclera, iris, and ciliary body — not the retina." },
  { id: 70, question: "In cross section the trabecular meshwork is roughly triangular, with its apex at the:", options: ["Scleral spur", "Schwalbe line", "Ciliary body band", "Iris root"], correct: 1, explanation: "The TM apex is at Schwalbe line; its base is formed by the scleral spur and ciliary body." },
  { id: 71, question: "Trabeculocytes lining the meshwork have which two notable properties?", options: ["Contractile and phagocytic", "Ciliated and secretory", "Photoreceptive and pigmented", "Neuroendocrine and fenestrated"], correct: 0, explanation: "Trabeculocytes have contractile properties (influencing outflow resistance) and phagocytic properties." },
  { id: 72, question: "The three layers of the trabecular meshwork, from inner to outer, are:", options: ["Juxtacanalicular, corneoscleral, uveal", "Uveal, corneoscleral, juxtacanalicular", "Corneoscleral, uveal, juxtacanalicular", "Uveal, juxtacanalicular, corneoscleral"], correct: 1, explanation: "From the anterior chamber outward: uveal, corneoscleral, then juxtacanalicular (adjacent to Schlemm canal)." },
  { id: 73, question: "The uveal and corneoscleral meshworks are separated by an imaginary line drawn from:", options: ["Schwalbe line to the scleral spur", "The ciliary body to the iris root", "The cornea to the lens", "Schlemm canal to the episcleral vein"], correct: 0, explanation: "A line from Schwalbe line to the scleral spur divides the internal uveal from the external corneoscleral meshwork." },
  { id: 74, question: "Which is an age-related change of the trabecular meshwork that increases outflow resistance?", options: ["Decreased pigmentation", "Thinning of trabecular sheets", "Accumulation of glycosaminoglycans and debris with loss of trabecular cells", "Increased endothelial cellularity"], correct: 2, explanation: "Aging brings GAG/debris accumulation, decreased trabecular cells, and thickened sheets — raising resistance, exaggerated in open-angle glaucoma." },
  { id: 75, question: "Compared with the corneoscleral meshwork, the uveal meshwork has:", options: ["More elastic fibers and smaller apertures", "Fewer elastic fibers and larger, less circular apertures", "No trabeculocytes", "A laminar sheet arrangement"], correct: 1, explanation: "The uveal meshwork (cordlike, facing the AC) has fewer elastic fibers and larger, less circular apertures than the corneoscleral layer." },
  { id: 76, question: "The corneoscleral meshwork is arranged as:", options: ["Cordlike trabeculae", "Thin, flat, perforated connective-tissue sheets in a laminar pattern", "A single endothelial monolayer", "A spongy avascular stroma"], correct: 1, explanation: "The corneoscleral meshwork consists of thin, flat, perforated sheets arranged in a laminar pattern." },
  { id: 77, question: "The highest point of resistance to aqueous outflow is the:", options: ["Uveal meshwork", "Corneoscleral meshwork", "Endothelial (juxtacanalicular) meshwork adjacent to the Schlemm canal", "Collector channels"], correct: 2, explanation: "The endothelial meshwork within the juxtacanalicular TM is the highest point of resistance to aqueous outflow." },
  { id: 78, question: "The Schlemm canal most closely resembles a:", options: ["Muscular artery", "Lymphatic vessel", "Fenestrated capillary", "Venous sinus with valves"], correct: 1, explanation: "The Schlemm canal is a circular tube resembling a lymphatic vessel, lined by a continuous nonfenestrated endothelial monolayer." },
  { id: 79, question: "The endothelium of the Schlemm canal is:", options: ["Fenestrated with loose junctions", "Nonfenestrated with tight junctions on the lateral walls", "Multilayered squamous", "Ciliated columnar"], correct: 1, explanation: "It is a continuous, nonfenestrated endothelial monolayer whose lateral walls are joined by tight junctions." },
  { id: 80, question: "The giant vacuoles along the inner wall of the Schlemm canal:", options: ["Decrease in number as IOP rises", "Increase in size and number as IOP rises and aid pressure-dependent outflow", "Are lined by a double membrane", "Secrete aqueous humor"], correct: 1, explanation: "Giant vacuoles increase in size/number with rising IOP and contribute to pressure-dependent (transcellular) outflow." },
  { id: 81, question: "In the described MIGS procedure, a microstent is placed in the Schlemm canal to:", options: ["Increase aqueous production", "Bypass the trabecular meshwork and increase outflow", "Seal the uveoscleral pathway", "Reduce episcleral venous pressure directly"], correct: 1, explanation: "The microstent bypasses the TM (the point of greatest resistance) to increase aqueous outflow." },
  { id: 82, question: "Approximately how many collector channels arise from the Schlemm canal?", options: ["2–3", "25–30", "70–80", "200"], correct: 1, explanation: "About 25–30 collector channels arise from the Schlemm canal and drain into scleral venous plexuses." },
  { id: 83, question: "The small, tortuous, blind diverticula extending from the Schlemm canal into the trabecular meshwork are called:", options: ["Aqueous veins", "Sondermann channels", "Collector channels", "Sattler channels"], correct: 1, explanation: "Sondermann channels are blind diverticula from the canal into the TM; aqueous veins connect to episcleral veins." },
  { id: 84, question: "The uvea is the eye's main ___ layer.", options: ["Fibrous (protective)", "Vascular", "Neural", "Epithelial"], correct: 1, explanation: "The uvea (iris, ciliary body, choroid) is the main vascular layer of the eye." },
  { id: 85, question: "The uvea is firmly attached to the sclera at all of the following EXCEPT the:", options: ["Scleral spur", "Vortex vein exit points", "Optic nerve", "Equator diffusely"], correct: 3, explanation: "The uvea is firmly attached only at the scleral spur, vortex vein exits, and optic nerve — explaining the anterior dome-shaped choroidal detachment." },
  { id: 86, question: "In the SUN classification, inflammation whose primary site is the vitreous is termed:", options: ["Anterior uveitis", "Intermediate uveitis", "Posterior uveitis", "Panuveitis"], correct: 1, explanation: "Intermediate uveitis has the vitreous as its primary site; anterior = AC, posterior = retina/choroid." },
  { id: 87, question: "The iris is embryologically derived from:", options: ["Surface ectoderm only", "Mesodermal and neuroectodermal tissue", "Endoderm", "Notochord"], correct: 1, explanation: "The iris comprises mesodermal tissue (vessels, connective tissue) and neuroectodermal tissue (muscle, epithelium, melanocytes)." },
  { id: 88, question: "Iris color is chiefly determined by:", options: ["The thickness of the cornea", "The density of melanocytes/pigmentation in the stroma and anterior border layer", "The refractive index of aqueous", "The number of iris crypts"], correct: 1, explanation: "Iris color reflects the density of pigmentation; blue irides are lightly pigmented, brown irides densely pigmented, though overall structure is similar." },
  { id: 89, question: "The collarette of the iris divides its surface into:", options: ["Anterior and posterior layers", "A central pupil zone and a peripheral ciliary zone", "Dilator and sphincter halves", "Stroma and epithelium"], correct: 1, explanation: "The collarette separates the central pupil zone (sphincter) from the peripheral ciliary zone (dilator) and is the anterior remnant of the hyaloidal system." },
  { id: 90, question: "Peripheral iris crypts are clinically useful as a target for:", options: ["Trabeculectomy", "Laser iridotomy", "Scleral buckling", "Cyclophotocoagulation"], correct: 1, explanation: "Crypts are thinned areas of iris; peripheral crypts are a good target for laser iridotomy." },
  { id: 91, question: "Blunt trauma to the thin IRIS ROOT characteristically causes:", options: ["Sphincter tears", "Iridodialysis", "Traumatic mydriasis", "Cataract"], correct: 1, explanation: "The thin iris root is prone to iridodialysis; the pupillary margin is prone to sphincter tears and traumatic mydriasis." },
  { id: 92, question: "Aqueous humor within the iris stroma:", options: ["Cannot penetrate the stroma", "Flows freely within the loose stroma along the anterior border", "Is actively secreted by the stroma", "Is blocked by tight junctions at the anterior border"], correct: 1, explanation: "Aqueous flows freely within the loose iris stroma along the crypt-bearing anterior border." },
  { id: 93, question: "The major arterial circle of the iris is actually located in the:", options: ["Pupillary margin", "Collarette", "Apex of the ciliary body", "Anterior chamber angle recess of the cornea"], correct: 2, explanation: "Despite its name, the major arterial circle lies at the apex of the ciliary body, not in the iris." },
  { id: 94, question: "The iris dilator muscle develops from the ___ and contracts in response to ___ stimulation.", options: ["Posterior pigmented epithelium; muscarinic", "Anterior pigmented epithelium; sympathetic α1-adrenergic", "Iris stroma; β-adrenergic", "Ciliary epithelium; parasympathetic"], correct: 1, explanation: "The dilator arises from the anterior pigmented epithelium (neuroectoderm) and contracts to sympathetic α1-adrenergic stimulation." },
  { id: 95, question: "The iris sphincter muscle receives its primary innervation from:", options: ["Sympathetic fibers via the superior cervical ganglion", "Parasympathetic fibers from the Edinger-Westphal nucleus via CN III", "The trigeminal nerve", "The abducens nerve"], correct: 1, explanation: "The sphincter is innervated mainly by parasympathetic fibers from the Edinger-Westphal nucleus traveling with CN III (muscarinic)." },
  { id: 96, question: "The term 'ectropion uveae' is considered a misnomer because the iris pigment epithelium is derived from:", options: ["Neural crest", "Neuroectoderm and is not truly part of the uvea", "Mesoderm", "Surface ectoderm"], correct: 1, explanation: "The IPE is neuroectoderm-derived (not neural crest), so it is not truly uveal — making 'ectropion uveae' a misnomer." },
  { id: 97, question: "The only attachment of the ciliary body to the sclera is at its base, via the:", options: ["Circular muscle fibers", "Longitudinal muscle fibers inserting into the scleral spur", "Zonular fibers", "Bruch membrane"], correct: 1, explanation: "The ciliary body attaches to the sclera only at its base, through longitudinal muscle fibers inserting into the scleral spur." },
  { id: 98, question: "The safest posterior surgical approach to the vitreous cavity is through the pars plana, located approximately how far from the limbus?", options: ["0–1 mm", "3–4 mm", "7–8 mm", "10–12 mm"], correct: 1, explanation: "The pars plana approach is 3–4 mm from the corneal limbus, avoiding the vascular pars plicata and the retina." },
  { id: 99, question: "The pars plicata contains approximately how many radial ciliary processes?", options: ["10–20", "30–40", "70–80", "150"], correct: 2, explanation: "The pars plicata is richly vascularized with ~70–80 radial folds (ciliary processes) where zonular fibers attach." },
  { id: 100, question: "The blood–aqueous barrier at the ciliary epithelium is maintained by tight junctions (zonulae occludentes) of the:", options: ["Pigmented epithelium, basal border", "Nonpigmented epithelium, near its apical border", "Ciliary stroma capillaries", "Trabecular meshwork"], correct: 1, explanation: "Tight junctions near the apical border of the nonpigmented ciliary epithelium maintain the blood–aqueous barrier." },
  { id: 101, question: "Of the parasympathetic ciliary fibers traveling in the short ciliary nerves, approximately what percentage supply the ciliary muscle for accommodation?", options: ["3%", "50%", "75%", "97%"], correct: 3, explanation: "About 97% of these fibers go to the ciliary muscle (accommodation) and ~3% to the iris sphincter." },
  { id: 102, question: "From innermost to outermost, the three vascular layers of the choroid are:", options: ["Haller, Sattler, choriocapillaris", "Choriocapillaris, Sattler, Haller", "Sattler, choriocapillaris, Haller", "Choriocapillaris, Haller, Sattler"], correct: 1, explanation: "From inner to outer: the choriocapillaris, the Sattler layer (small vessels), and the Haller layer (large vessels)." },
];

const BASIC_ANTIGLAUCOMA_RECALL: RecallQ[] = [
  { id: 1, question: "What is 'target IOP,' and what informs it?", answer: "Target IOP is an individualized, dynamic estimate — not a universally safe pressure. It is informed by structural and functional progression, treatment burden, and the patient's lifetime risk of visual disability, and is adjusted over time." },
  { id: 2, question: "Name key landmark glaucoma trials that inform target-pressure decisions.", answer: "OHTS (Ocular Hypertension Treatment Study), EMGT (Early Manifest Glaucoma Trial), UKGTS (UK Glaucoma Treatment Study), AGIS (Advanced Glaucoma Intervention Study), CNTGS (Collaborative Normal-Tension Glaucoma Study), and the European Glaucoma Society (EGS) guidelines." },
  { id: 3, question: "What preservatives are used in glaucoma eyedrops, and why does benzalkonium chloride (BAK) matter?", answer: "BAK is the common preservative; alternatives include SofZia (borate, sorbitol, propylene glycol, zinc) and Purite (stabilized sodium chlorite). BAK enhances corneal penetration — a higher BAK concentration allows a lower bimatoprost concentration while maintaining IOP-lowering efficacy — but it also contributes to ocular surface toxicity." },
  { id: 4, question: "List the cholinergic (miotic) agents by direct vs indirect action.", answer: "Direct agonists: pilocarpine HCl 1%/2%, pilocarpine gel, and carbachol (carbachol 0.01%, Miostat, is an intracameral solution for surgical miosis). Indirect agonist (cholinesterase inhibitor): echothiophate iodide." },
  { id: 5, question: "How do miotics lower IOP?", answer: "They contract the ciliary muscle, which pulls on the scleral spur and opens the trabecular meshwork spaces, increasing trabecular (conventional) outflow. They also contract the iris sphincter, producing miosis." },
  { id: 6, question: "What are the non-selective adrenergic agonists used in glaucoma?", answer: "Dipivefrin 0.1% (a lipophilic prodrug converted to epinephrine in the eye, giving better corneal penetration) and epinephrine — non-selective α and β agonists." },
  { id: 7, question: "List the α2-adrenergic agonists and their selectivity.", answer: "Selective α2 agonist: apraclonidine 1%/0.5%/0.25% (Iopidine). Highly selective α2 agonist: brimonidine tartrate 0.2% (Alphagan), and Alphagan-P 0.1%/0.15%." },
  { id: 8, question: "How do α2-adrenergic agonists lower IOP?", answer: "They decrease aqueous humor production and increase uveoscleral outflow." },
  { id: 9, question: "Why is apraclonidine used mainly short-term?", answer: "It is used to prevent IOP spikes after anterior segment laser procedures; long-term use is limited by tachyphylaxis and a high rate of allergic conjunctivitis." },
  { id: 10, question: "List the β-adrenergic antagonists used in glaucoma by selectivity.", answer: "Non-selective β-blockers: timolol 0.5%, carteolol 1%/2%, levobunolol 0.25%/0.5% (Betagan), metipranolol. Selective (β1/cardioselective) blocker: betaxolol 0.25%/0.5% (Betoptic)." },
  { id: 11, question: "How do β-blockers lower IOP, and how does betaxolol differ from timolol?", answer: "β-blockers decrease aqueous production. Betaxolol is β1-selective (cardioselective), making it safer in patients with pulmonary disease, but it is generally less effective at lowering IOP than the non-selective timolol." },
  { id: 12, question: "What are the topical carbonic anhydrase inhibitors, and their mechanism?", answer: "Dorzolamide 2% (Trusopt) and brinzolamide 1% (Azopt). They inhibit carbonic anhydrase in the ciliary body, decreasing aqueous humor production." },
  { id: 13, question: "List the prostaglandin analogues with concentrations/brands.", answer: "Latanoprost 0.005% (Xalatan), travoprost 0.004% (Travatan Z) and 0.003% (IZBA), bimatoprost 0.03%/0.01% (Lumigan), and tafluprost 0.0015% (Taflotan)." },
  { id: 14, question: "How do prostaglandin analogues lower IOP?", answer: "Primarily by increasing uveoscleral outflow (with some increase in trabecular outflow). They are typically the most effective single agents and are dosed once daily." },
  { id: 15, question: "What are the ocular side effects of prostaglandin analogues?", answer: "Increased iris and periorbital (eyelid) pigmentation, gradual eyelash changes (longer/thicker/darker), conjunctival hyperemia (~6%), eye irritation/pain, and caution is needed in cystoid macular edema and intraocular inflammation." },
  { id: 16, question: "What is prostaglandin-associated periorbitopathy (PAP)?", answer: "A constellation of periocular changes from prostaglandin analogues, including deepening of the upper eyelid sulcus (DUES), orbital fat atrophy, upper eyelid ptosis, and enophthalmos — often partially reversible on stopping the drug." },
  { id: 17, question: "What is latanoprostene bunod, and how does it work?", answer: "Latanoprostene bunod 0.024% (Vyzulta) — the first nitric oxide–containing glaucoma medication. It is metabolized to latanoprost acid (a prostaglandin analogue acting via the uveoscleral pathway) and butanediol mononitrate, which releases nitric oxide to relax the trabecular meshwork and improve trabecular outflow." },
  { id: 18, question: "What is omidenepag isopropyl, and how does it differ from prostaglandin analogues?", answer: "Omidenepag isopropyl 0.002% (Eybelis) is a selective prostaglandin EP2 receptor agonist with a non-prostaglandin structure. It increases both trabecular and uveoscleral outflow. It is contraindicated in aphakic/pseudophakic (IOL) eyes and with concomitant tafluprost use (risk of cystoid macular edema/inflammation)." },
  { id: 19, question: "What are the Rho-kinase (ROCK) inhibitors, and their targets?", answer: "Netarsudil (Rhopressa) and ripasudil (Glanatec). They inhibit ROCK1 and ROCK2 (Rho-associated protein kinases)." },
  { id: 20, question: "Describe the Rho-kinase pathway and how ROCK inhibitors lower IOP.", answer: "Rho kinase increases phosphorylation and activation of myosin light chain (MLC), which interacts with actin to alter the cytoskeleton, increasing cell stiffness and smooth-muscle contraction. ROCK inhibitors relax the cytoskeleton of outflow cells in the trabecular meshwork and Schlemm canal, increasing trabecular outflow facility." },
  { id: 21, question: "Describe netarsudil's dosing and its three IOP-lowering actions.", answer: "Netarsudil (Rhopressa) is dosed once daily at night. It increases trabecular outflow, decreases aqueous production, and decreases episcleral venous pressure. It is noninferior to latanoprost with roughly a 20% IOP reduction." },
  { id: 22, question: "What are the characteristic side effects of netarsudil?", answer: "Conjunctival hyperemia (50–60%, from conjunctival vascular smooth-muscle relaxation), conjunctival hemorrhages (10–20%), pruritus/blepharitis/punctate keratitis (~10%), cornea verticillata (15–25%, from lysosomal accumulation of phospholipids in corneal epithelial cells), and reticular ('honeycomb') corneal edema." },
  { id: 23, question: "How does ripasudil differ from netarsudil in dosing and side effects?", answer: "Ripasudil (Glanatec) is dosed twice daily. Common side effects include conjunctival hyperemia (~69%), conjunctivitis/allergic conjunctivitis (~10.7%), and blepharitis (~10.3%)." },
  { id: 24, question: "Describe the sustained-release bimatoprost implant.", answer: "Durysta — an intracameral, biodegradable sustained-release bimatoprost implant (10 µg, one-time use), FDA-approved in 2020 for ocular hypertension and POAG, with a duration of about 16 weeks. Phase III RCTs compared it with a β-blocker and with SLT." },
  { id: 25, question: "Why is repeat dosing of the bimatoprost implant limited?", answer: "Because of corneal endothelial cell loss: corneal endothelial cell density decreased by about 10.2% (10 µg) and 21.8% (15 µg) at 20-month follow-up, so it is currently approved as a one-time (single) implant per eye." },
  { id: 26, question: "Name the common timolol-based fixed-combination glaucoma drops.", answer: "Xalacom (latanoprost + timolol), Duotrav (travoprost + timolol), Ganfort (bimatoprost + timolol), Tapcom/Taptiqom (tafluprost + timolol), Cosopt (dorzolamide + timolol), Azarga (brinzolamide + timolol), and Combigan (brimonidine + timolol)." },
  { id: 27, question: "Name fixed-combination drops that do NOT contain timolol.", answer: "Simbrinza (brinzolamide + brimonidine) and Rocklatan (netarsudil + latanoprost)." },
  { id: 28, question: "Describe acetazolamide (systemic CAI) dosing and pharmacokinetics.", answer: "Acetazolamide 10–30 mg/kg/day divided every 6–8 hours (maximum 1000 mg/day), with onset ~1 hour and peak at 2–4 hours; a sustained-release formulation is available." },
  { id: 29, question: "How does methazolamide compare with acetazolamide?", answer: "Methazolamide has a longer duration and fewer systemic side effects, but is less effective; it is metabolized by the liver (versus renal excretion of acetazolamide), making it somewhat safer in renal impairment." },
  { id: 30, question: "What are the important systemic CAI cautions/contraindications?", answer: "Sickle cell crisis (acetazolamide can precipitate sickling), and increased ammonia levels causing hepatic encephalopathy in liver disease. Also renal failure, electrolyte disorders (hypokalemia), kidney stones, severe sulfonamide allergy, and frailty/dehydration." },
  { id: 31, question: "List the hyperosmotic agents with representative dosing.", answer: "Mannitol (IV) 0.25–2 g/kg of a 15–25% solution (usual adult dose 50–200 g/24 h); glycerol (oral); and isosorbide (oral) 1–1.5 mL/kg of a 100% solution." },
  { id: 32, question: "How do hyperosmotic agents work, and what are their cautions?", answer: "They raise plasma osmolarity to draw fluid (water) out of the vitreous, rapidly lowering IOP for acute pressure elevations. Cautions include CHF/MI and renal failure (volume overload), dehydration, and — for glycerol specifically — diabetes (it is metabolized and can cause hyperglycemia)." },
  { id: 33, question: "What are the contraindications/cautions for topical β-blockers?", answer: "Asthma/COPD, bradycardia, heart block, congestive heart failure, and concurrent systemic β-blocker use — because of bronchospasm and additive cardiac conduction/blood-pressure effects." },
  { id: 34, question: "What are the contraindications/cautions for α2-agonists (brimonidine)?", answer: "Infants and young children (CNS depression, apnea — brimonidine is contraindicated in children under ~2 years), severe fatigue or depression, and hypotension/syncope risk; also caution with MAO inhibitors." },
  { id: 35, question: "What are the contraindications/cautions for miotics?", answer: "Uveitis, high myopia or retinal detachment risk (miosis and ciliary contraction cause retinal traction), poor night vision, and significant cataract symptoms (miosis reduces vision)." },
  { id: 36, question: "What are the contraindications/cautions for prostaglandin analogues and latanoprostene bunod?", answer: "Active uveitis, cystoid macular edema risk, recent intraocular surgery, and a history of herpetic keratitis (risk of reactivation), plus the cosmetic periocular/iris changes." },
  { id: 37, question: "Which glaucoma medication is preferred in pregnancy, and what is the key caveat?", answer: "Brimonidine (former FDA category B) is the preferred agent during pregnancy, but it is linked to apnea and CNS depression in newborns/infants and should be discontinued before delivery; it should be avoided during breastfeeding." },
  { id: 38, question: "What are the pregnancy and breastfeeding concerns with β-blockers?", answer: "Former category C: reports of fetal/newborn growth retardation, arrhythmia, bradycardia, and lethargy. They are present/concentrated in breast milk and are relatively contraindicated during breastfeeding." },
  { id: 39, question: "How are carbonic anhydrase inhibitors handled in pregnancy and lactation?", answer: "Systemic CAIs (category C) are teratogenic in rodents (human case reports of forelimb deformities) and should be avoided in pregnancy, though acetazolamide is generally considered acceptable during breastfeeding. Topical CAIs are preferably avoided in pregnancy (especially the first trimester) but are generally acceptable — even preferred — during breastfeeding because systemic/milk exposure is low." },
  { id: 40, question: "How are prostaglandin analogues and Rho-kinase inhibitors handled in pregnancy?", answer: "Prostaglandin analogues (category C) have animal embryocidal/teratogenic effects at high doses, but topical use is unlikely to place the fetus at significant risk and they are generally acceptable during breastfeeding (very low infant exposure). Rho-kinase inhibitors show no clear animal teratogenicity, but caution is warranted in pregnancy and breastfeeding." },
  { id: 41, question: "Which glaucoma drug classes lower IOP by DECREASING aqueous production?", answer: "β-blockers, α2-agonists, and carbonic anhydrase inhibitors (topical and systemic). Netarsudil also modestly decreases aqueous production (and episcleral venous pressure)." },
  { id: 42, question: "Which classes lower IOP by INCREASING outflow, and by which route?", answer: "Increased uveoscleral outflow: prostaglandin analogues (and α2-agonists partially). Increased trabecular (conventional) outflow: miotics, ROCK inhibitors, and the EP2 agonist omidenepag; latanoprostene bunod adds NO-mediated trabecular relaxation. Netarsudil also decreases episcleral venous pressure." },
  { id: 43, question: "What is echothiophate iodide, and what perioperative caution does it carry?", answer: "An indirect cholinergic agonist — an irreversible cholinesterase (organophosphate) inhibitor with long duration. It can cause cataract and prolongs the action of succinylcholine (it inhibits plasma pseudocholinesterase), so it must be flagged before general anesthesia." },
  { id: 44, question: "What is cornea verticillata from netarsudil, and what other corneal effect can occur?", answer: "Cornea verticillata is a whorl-like pattern of corneal epithelial deposits caused by lysosomal accumulation of phospholipids in corneal epithelial cells (similar to amiodarone). Netarsudil can also cause a reticular ('honeycomb') corneal epithelial edema." },
  { id: 45, question: "Why does netarsudil cause such prominent conjunctival hyperemia?", answer: "Its Rho-kinase inhibition relaxes conjunctival vascular smooth muscle, producing vasodilation — hence hyperemia in 50–60% of patients (the most common side effect of the class)." },
  { id: 46, question: "How does carbachol compare with pilocarpine?", answer: "Carbachol is a direct cholinergic agonist that also has some indirect (cholinesterase-inhibiting) activity, giving it a longer duration than pilocarpine. Carbachol 0.01% (Miostat) is used intracamerally for surgical miosis." },
];

const BASIC_ANTIGLAUCOMA_MCQ: MCQ[] = [
  { id: 1, question: "'Target IOP' is best described as:", options: ["A single universally safe pressure of 21 mm Hg", "An individualized, dynamic estimate adjusted over time", "The pressure at which all optic nerves are protected", "A fixed value set at diagnosis and never changed"], correct: 1, explanation: "Target IOP is an individualized, dynamic estimate based on progression, treatment burden, and lifetime risk of disability — not a universal number." },
  { id: 2, question: "Which is a landmark glaucoma clinical trial?", options: ["ETDRS", "OHTS (Ocular Hypertension Treatment Study)", "DRCR.net", "CATT"], correct: 1, explanation: "OHTS, EMGT, UKGTS, AGIS, and CNTGS are landmark glaucoma trials; the others listed are retinal trials." },
  { id: 3, question: "A higher benzalkonium chloride (BAK) concentration in a bimatoprost formulation:", options: ["Reduces corneal penetration", "Enhances ocular penetration, allowing a lower drug concentration", "Eliminates all ocular surface toxicity", "Has no effect on drug delivery"], correct: 1, explanation: "Higher BAK enhances corneal penetration, so a lower bimatoprost concentration maintains efficacy — at the cost of more surface toxicity." },
  { id: 4, question: "Which preservative system is preservative-alternative (BAK-free)?", options: ["SofZia (borate, sorbitol, propylene glycol, zinc)", "Benzalkonium chloride", "Thimerosal", "Chlorhexidine"], correct: 0, explanation: "SofZia and Purite (sodium chlorite) are BAK-alternative preservative systems used to reduce surface toxicity." },
  { id: 5, question: "Which is an INDIRECT-acting cholinergic (miotic) agent?", options: ["Pilocarpine", "Carbachol drops", "Echothiophate iodide", "Acetylcholine"], correct: 2, explanation: "Echothiophate is an indirect agonist (cholinesterase inhibitor); pilocarpine and carbachol act directly." },
  { id: 6, question: "Miotics lower IOP primarily by:", options: ["Decreasing aqueous production", "Contracting the ciliary muscle to open the trabecular meshwork (increasing trabecular outflow)", "Increasing uveoscleral outflow", "Lowering episcleral venous pressure"], correct: 1, explanation: "Ciliary muscle contraction pulls on the scleral spur, opening the trabecular meshwork and increasing conventional outflow." },
  { id: 7, question: "Carbachol 0.01% (Miostat) is used as an:", options: ["Oral tablet for chronic glaucoma", "Intracameral solution for surgical miosis", "Intravitreal injection", "Periocular depot"], correct: 1, explanation: "Carbachol 0.01% (Miostat) is an intracameral solution used for miosis during surgery." },
  { id: 8, question: "Dipivefrin is best described as:", options: ["A selective α2-agonist", "A lipophilic prodrug of epinephrine", "A β1-selective blocker", "A carbonic anhydrase inhibitor"], correct: 1, explanation: "Dipivefrin 0.1% is a lipophilic prodrug converted to epinephrine in the eye, with better corneal penetration." },
  { id: 9, question: "Which is a HIGHLY selective α2-adrenergic agonist?", options: ["Apraclonidine", "Brimonidine", "Epinephrine", "Dipivefrin"], correct: 1, explanation: "Brimonidine is highly α2-selective; apraclonidine is selective but less so; epinephrine/dipivefrin are non-selective." },
  { id: 10, question: "α2-adrenergic agonists lower IOP by:", options: ["Increasing aqueous production only", "Decreasing aqueous production and increasing uveoscleral outflow", "Lowering episcleral venous pressure only", "Blocking carbonic anhydrase"], correct: 1, explanation: "α2-agonists both decrease aqueous production and enhance uveoscleral outflow." },
  { id: 11, question: "Apraclonidine is used mainly for:", options: ["Lifelong monotherapy", "Preventing post-laser IOP spikes (short-term use)", "Neovascular glaucoma prophylaxis", "Congenital glaucoma"], correct: 1, explanation: "Apraclonidine is used short-term to blunt post-laser IOP spikes; long-term use is limited by tachyphylaxis and allergy." },
  { id: 12, question: "Which topical β-blocker is β1-selective (cardioselective)?", options: ["Timolol", "Carteolol", "Levobunolol", "Betaxolol"], correct: 3, explanation: "Betaxolol (Betoptic) is β1-selective; timolol, carteolol, levobunolol, and metipranolol are non-selective." },
  { id: 13, question: "Compared with timolol, betaxolol is:", options: ["More effective at lowering IOP but worse for the lungs", "Less effective at lowering IOP but safer in pulmonary disease", "Equally effective and equally risky in asthma", "A prostaglandin analogue"], correct: 1, explanation: "Cardioselective betaxolol is safer in pulmonary disease but generally lowers IOP less than non-selective timolol." },
  { id: 14, question: "Topical β-blockers lower IOP by:", options: ["Increasing uveoscleral outflow", "Decreasing aqueous humor production", "Opening the trabecular meshwork", "Raising plasma osmolarity"], correct: 1, explanation: "β-blockers decrease aqueous humor production by the ciliary epithelium." },
  { id: 15, question: "The topical carbonic anhydrase inhibitors are:", options: ["Dorzolamide and brinzolamide", "Timolol and betaxolol", "Latanoprost and travoprost", "Netarsudil and ripasudil"], correct: 0, explanation: "Dorzolamide 2% (Trusopt) and brinzolamide 1% (Azopt) are the topical CAIs." },
  { id: 16, question: "Carbonic anhydrase inhibitors lower IOP by:", options: ["Increasing trabecular outflow", "Decreasing aqueous production", "Increasing uveoscleral outflow", "Relaxing conjunctival vessels"], correct: 1, explanation: "CAIs inhibit carbonic anhydrase in the ciliary body, reducing bicarbonate/sodium entry and thus aqueous production." },
  { id: 17, question: "Which is the brand-name concentration for latanoprost?", options: ["0.03%", "0.004%", "0.005%", "0.0015%"], correct: 2, explanation: "Latanoprost is 0.005% (Xalatan); travoprost 0.004%, bimatoprost 0.03%/0.01%, tafluprost 0.0015%." },
  { id: 18, question: "Prostaglandin analogues lower IOP primarily by:", options: ["Decreasing aqueous production", "Increasing uveoscleral outflow", "Lowering episcleral venous pressure", "Blocking β-receptors"], correct: 1, explanation: "Prostaglandin analogues mainly increase uveoscleral outflow (with some trabecular effect) and are usually the most effective single agents." },
  { id: 19, question: "Which is a recognized side effect of prostaglandin analogues?", options: ["Decreased iris pigmentation", "Increased iris and periorbital pigmentation with eyelash growth", "Shortening of the eyelashes", "Pupillary dilation"], correct: 1, explanation: "Prostaglandins cause increased iris/periorbital pigmentation, longer/thicker/darker lashes, and conjunctival hyperemia." },
  { id: 20, question: "Prostaglandin-associated periorbitopathy (PAP) characteristically includes:", options: ["Proptosis and lid retraction", "Deepening of the upper eyelid sulcus (DUES) and orbital fat atrophy", "Corneal neovascularization", "Cataract"], correct: 1, explanation: "PAP features deepening of the upper eyelid sulcus, orbital fat atrophy, ptosis, and enophthalmos." },
  { id: 21, question: "Prostaglandin analogues should be used with caution in patients with:", options: ["Dry eye only", "Cystoid macular edema, active uveitis, or herpetic keratitis history", "Refractive error", "Presbyopia"], correct: 1, explanation: "Cautions include CME, active intraocular inflammation/uveitis, and a history of herpetic keratitis." },
  { id: 22, question: "Latanoprostene bunod (Vyzulta) is notable as:", options: ["The first β-blocker prodrug", "The first nitric oxide–containing glaucoma medication", "A selective EP2 agonist", "A systemic CAI"], correct: 1, explanation: "Latanoprostene bunod 0.024% is the first NO-donating glaucoma drug — latanoprost acid plus butanediol mononitrate." },
  { id: 23, question: "The nitric oxide released by latanoprostene bunod lowers IOP by:", options: ["Contracting the ciliary muscle", "Relaxing the trabecular meshwork to increase trabecular outflow", "Decreasing aqueous production", "Increasing episcleral venous pressure"], correct: 1, explanation: "The butanediol mononitrate moiety releases NO, relaxing the trabecular meshwork and improving trabecular outflow (latanoprost acid adds uveoscleral outflow)." },
  { id: 24, question: "Omidenepag isopropyl (Eybelis) is a:", options: ["Non-selective β-blocker", "Selective prostaglandin EP2 receptor agonist with a non-prostaglandin structure", "Rho-kinase inhibitor", "Hyperosmotic agent"], correct: 1, explanation: "Omidenepag isopropyl 0.002% is a selective EP2 receptor agonist (non-prostaglandin structure) that increases both trabecular and uveoscleral outflow." },
  { id: 25, question: "Omidenepag isopropyl is contraindicated in:", options: ["Phakic eyes", "Aphakic/pseudophakic (IOL) eyes and with concomitant tafluprost", "Patients over 60", "Ocular hypertension"], correct: 1, explanation: "It is contraindicated in aphakic/pseudophakic eyes and with concomitant tafluprost due to CME/inflammation risk." },
  { id: 26, question: "The Rho-kinase (ROCK) inhibitors used in glaucoma are:", options: ["Netarsudil and ripasudil", "Dorzolamide and brinzolamide", "Timolol and betaxolol", "Mannitol and glycerol"], correct: 0, explanation: "Netarsudil (Rhopressa) and ripasudil (Glanatec) are the ROCK inhibitors; they target ROCK1 and ROCK2." },
  { id: 27, question: "Rho kinase normally increases cell stiffness/contraction by:", options: ["Dephosphorylating myosin light chain", "Phosphorylating and activating myosin light chain (MLC), altering the actin cytoskeleton", "Blocking carbonic anhydrase", "Releasing nitric oxide"], correct: 1, explanation: "Rho kinase increases MLC phosphorylation/activation and interacts with actin, raising cell stiffness and smooth-muscle contraction." },
  { id: 28, question: "ROCK inhibitors increase aqueous outflow by:", options: ["Contracting the ciliary muscle", "Relaxing the cytoskeleton of trabecular meshwork/Schlemm canal outflow cells", "Increasing uveoscleral outflow only", "Decreasing aqueous production only"], correct: 1, explanation: "ROCK inhibitors relax the cytoskeleton of TM and Schlemm canal cells, increasing trabecular outflow facility." },
  { id: 29, question: "Netarsudil (Rhopressa) is typically dosed:", options: ["Four times daily", "Once daily at night", "Every hour during an acute attack", "Weekly"], correct: 1, explanation: "Netarsudil is dosed once daily in the evening." },
  { id: 30, question: "Which is NOT one of netarsudil's IOP-lowering actions?", options: ["Increasing trabecular outflow", "Decreasing aqueous production", "Decreasing episcleral venous pressure", "Increasing aqueous production"], correct: 3, explanation: "Netarsudil increases trabecular outflow and decreases both aqueous production and episcleral venous pressure." },
  { id: 31, question: "The most common side effect of netarsudil is:", options: ["Cataract", "Conjunctival hyperemia (50–60%)", "Acute angle closure", "Retinal detachment"], correct: 1, explanation: "Conjunctival hyperemia occurs in 50–60%, from relaxation of conjunctival vascular smooth muscle." },
  { id: 32, question: "Cornea verticillata caused by netarsudil results from:", options: ["Corneal neovascularization", "Lysosomal accumulation of phospholipids in corneal epithelial cells", "Immune deposits", "Calcium deposition"], correct: 1, explanation: "Netarsudil causes whorl-like verticillata (15–25%) from lysosomal phospholipid accumulation, similar to amiodarone." },
  { id: 33, question: "A distinctive corneal side effect of netarsudil, besides verticillata, is:", options: ["Band keratopathy", "Reticular ('honeycomb') corneal edema", "Keratoconus", "Corneal arcus"], correct: 1, explanation: "Netarsudil can cause a reticular (honeycomb) corneal epithelial edema." },
  { id: 34, question: "Compared with netarsudil, ripasudil (Glanatec) is dosed:", options: ["Once daily", "Twice daily", "Once weekly", "Only intracamerally"], correct: 1, explanation: "Ripasudil is dosed twice daily; conjunctival hyperemia (~69%) is its most common effect." },
  { id: 35, question: "The sustained-release bimatoprost implant (Durysta) is administered:", options: ["As an oral tablet", "Intracamerally as a one-time biodegradable implant", "Subconjunctivally weekly", "Intravitreally"], correct: 1, explanation: "Durysta is an intracameral, biodegradable, one-time 10-µg implant (FDA approved 2020) lasting ~16 weeks." },
  { id: 36, question: "Repeat dosing of the bimatoprost implant is limited chiefly by:", options: ["Cataract formation", "Corneal endothelial cell loss", "Retinal toxicity", "Optic neuropathy"], correct: 1, explanation: "Corneal endothelial cell density fell ~10.2% (10 µg) to 21.8% (15 µg) at 20 months, limiting it to a single implant per eye." },
  { id: 37, question: "Which fixed combination pairs a prostaglandin with timolol?", options: ["Simbrinza", "Xalacom (latanoprost + timolol)", "Combigan", "Cosopt"], correct: 1, explanation: "Xalacom = latanoprost + timolol; Duotrav, Ganfort, and Tapcom are other PG+timolol combos." },
  { id: 38, question: "Cosopt is a fixed combination of:", options: ["Brimonidine + timolol", "Dorzolamide + timolol", "Brinzolamide + brimonidine", "Netarsudil + latanoprost"], correct: 1, explanation: "Cosopt = dorzolamide (CAI) + timolol; Azarga = brinzolamide + timolol." },
  { id: 39, question: "Which fixed combination contains NO timolol?", options: ["Ganfort", "Duotrav", "Simbrinza (brinzolamide + brimonidine)", "Combigan"], correct: 2, explanation: "Simbrinza (brinzolamide + brimonidine) and Rocklatan (netarsudil + latanoprost) are the non-timolol combos." },
  { id: 40, question: "Combigan is a fixed combination of:", options: ["Brimonidine + timolol", "Latanoprost + timolol", "Brinzolamide + timolol", "Netarsudil + latanoprost"], correct: 0, explanation: "Combigan = brimonidine (α2-agonist) + timolol." },
  { id: 41, question: "Rocklatan is a fixed combination of:", options: ["Netarsudil + latanoprost", "Brinzolamide + brimonidine", "Dorzolamide + timolol", "Travoprost + timolol"], correct: 0, explanation: "Rocklatan = netarsudil (ROCK inhibitor) + latanoprost (prostaglandin)." },
  { id: 42, question: "The usual maximum daily dose of acetazolamide is:", options: ["250 mg/day", "1000 mg/day", "4000 mg/day", "100 mg/day"], correct: 1, explanation: "Acetazolamide is dosed 10–30 mg/kg/day divided q6–8h, up to a maximum of 1000 mg/day (onset ~1 h, peak 2–4 h)." },
  { id: 43, question: "Compared with acetazolamide, methazolamide is:", options: ["More effective but more toxic to kidneys", "Longer-acting with fewer systemic side effects but less effective, metabolized by the liver", "A topical agent", "Contraindicated in all liver disease"], correct: 1, explanation: "Methazolamide is longer-acting, has fewer systemic effects, is less effective, and is hepatically metabolized (safer in renal impairment)." },
  { id: 44, question: "Systemic carbonic anhydrase inhibitors should be avoided in patients with:", options: ["Sickle cell disease and hepatic encephalopathy risk", "Mild refractive error", "Well-controlled hypertension only", "Presbyopia"], correct: 0, explanation: "Systemic CAIs can precipitate sickle cell crisis and raise ammonia (hepatic encephalopathy); also avoid in renal failure, sulfa allergy, and electrolyte disorders." },
  { id: 45, question: "Which is a hyperosmotic agent used for acute IOP elevation?", options: ["Mannitol", "Timolol", "Latanoprost", "Netarsudil"], correct: 0, explanation: "Hyperosmotics include IV mannitol, oral glycerol, and oral isosorbide." },
  { id: 46, question: "Intravenous mannitol lowers IOP by:", options: ["Decreasing aqueous production", "Raising plasma osmolarity to draw water out of the vitreous", "Increasing trabecular outflow", "Blocking β-receptors"], correct: 1, explanation: "Hyperosmotics raise plasma osmolarity, osmotically drawing water from the vitreous to rapidly reduce IOP." },
  { id: 47, question: "Glycerol (oral hyperosmotic) requires particular caution in patients with:", options: ["Diabetes (it is metabolized and can cause hyperglycemia)", "Myopia", "Dry eye", "Presbyopia"], correct: 0, explanation: "Glycerol is metabolized and can raise blood glucose, so it must be used cautiously in diabetes; hyperosmotics also risk volume overload in CHF/renal failure." },
  { id: 48, question: "Topical β-blockers are contraindicated/cautioned in:", options: ["Asthma/COPD, bradycardia, and heart block", "Myopia", "Dry eye", "Cataract"], correct: 0, explanation: "β-blockers risk bronchospasm and cardiac conduction/blood-pressure effects in reactive airway disease, bradycardia, heart block, and CHF." },
  { id: 49, question: "Brimonidine is specifically contraindicated in:", options: ["The elderly", "Infants and young children (risk of CNS depression and apnea)", "Phakic adults", "Patients with high myopia"], correct: 1, explanation: "α2-agonists cause CNS depression/apnea in infants and young children, so brimonidine is contraindicated in young children." },
  { id: 50, question: "Miotics should be used with caution in patients with:", options: ["High myopia / retinal detachment risk", "Hyperopia", "Dry eye only", "Corneal arcus"], correct: 0, explanation: "Miosis and ciliary contraction cause retinal traction (RD risk in high myopes), poor night vision, and reduced vision with cataract." },
  { id: 51, question: "The preferred topical glaucoma agent during pregnancy is often considered to be:", options: ["Latanoprost", "Brimonidine (with discontinuation before delivery)", "Timolol", "Acetazolamide"], correct: 1, explanation: "Brimonidine (former category B) is often preferred in pregnancy but is discontinued before delivery due to neonatal apnea risk and avoided in breastfeeding." },
  { id: 52, question: "Why should brimonidine be avoided during breastfeeding and stopped before delivery?", options: ["It causes maternal hypertension", "Risk of apnea and CNS depression in the newborn/infant", "It reduces milk supply", "It causes fetal cataract"], correct: 1, explanation: "Brimonidine crosses into the CNS and can cause apnea/CNS depression in newborns and infants." },
  { id: 53, question: "Systemic carbonic anhydrase inhibitors in pregnancy are:", options: ["First-line and safe", "Category C, teratogenic in rodents (forelimb deformities) and generally avoided", "Only used topically", "Absolutely safe in the first trimester"], correct: 1, explanation: "Systemic CAIs are category C with rodent teratogenicity and human forelimb-deformity case reports; avoid in pregnancy (acetazolamide acceptable while breastfeeding)." },
  { id: 54, question: "During breastfeeding, which agents are generally considered acceptable/preferred due to low infant exposure?", options: ["Systemic β-blockers", "Topical CAIs and topical prostaglandin analogues", "Oral acetazolamide at high dose", "Brimonidine"], correct: 1, explanation: "Topical CAIs and topical prostaglandin analogues have low systemic/milk exposure and are generally acceptable in breastfeeding; brimonidine is avoided." },
  { id: 55, question: "Which classes lower IOP by DECREASING aqueous production?", options: ["Prostaglandin analogues and miotics", "β-blockers, α2-agonists, and carbonic anhydrase inhibitors", "ROCK inhibitors and EP2 agonists", "Hyperosmotics"], correct: 1, explanation: "Aqueous suppressants are β-blockers, α2-agonists, and CAIs (netarsudil also modestly suppresses production)." },
  { id: 56, question: "Which drug uniquely lowers episcleral venous pressure as one of its mechanisms?", options: ["Timolol", "Latanoprost", "Netarsudil", "Pilocarpine"], correct: 2, explanation: "Netarsudil decreases episcleral venous pressure (in addition to increasing trabecular outflow and reducing aqueous production)." },
  { id: 57, question: "Echothiophate iodide is significant during general anesthesia because it:", options: ["Prolongs the action of succinylcholine", "Reverses all neuromuscular blockade", "Causes malignant hyperthermia", "Shortens intubation time"], correct: 0, explanation: "Echothiophate inhibits pseudocholinesterase, prolonging succinylcholine's effect — an important preoperative flag; it can also cause cataract." },
  { id: 58, question: "Which class INCREASES uveoscleral outflow as its primary mechanism?", options: ["β-blockers", "Prostaglandin analogues", "Carbonic anhydrase inhibitors", "Hyperosmotics"], correct: 1, explanation: "Prostaglandin analogues primarily increase uveoscleral outflow (α2-agonists do so secondarily)." },
  { id: 59, question: "Which agent increases BOTH trabecular and uveoscleral outflow and has a non-prostaglandin structure?", options: ["Latanoprost", "Omidenepag isopropyl (EP2 agonist)", "Brinzolamide", "Timolol"], correct: 1, explanation: "The EP2 agonist omidenepag isopropyl increases both outflow pathways and is structurally non-prostaglandin." },
  { id: 60, question: "IZBA is a formulation of which prostaglandin?", options: ["Latanoprost 0.005%", "Travoprost 0.003%", "Bimatoprost 0.01%", "Tafluprost 0.0015%"], correct: 1, explanation: "IZBA is travoprost 0.003% (a lower-concentration/BAK-free travoprost)." },
  { id: 61, question: "Netarsudil's reported IOP-lowering efficacy versus latanoprost is:", options: ["Markedly superior", "Noninferior, with about 20% IOP reduction", "Markedly inferior", "No IOP effect"], correct: 1, explanation: "Netarsudil showed noninferiority to latanoprost with roughly a 20% IOP reduction." },
  { id: 62, question: "Ripasudil's most common side effect is:", options: ["Cataract", "Conjunctival hyperemia (~69%)", "Acute angle closure", "Optic atrophy"], correct: 1, explanation: "Ripasudil most commonly causes conjunctival hyperemia (~69%), with conjunctivitis and blepharitis also frequent." },
  { id: 63, question: "Which combination correctly matches a fixed-combo brand with its components?", options: ["Ganfort = brimonidine + timolol", "Duotrav = travoprost + timolol", "Simbrinza = dorzolamide + timolol", "Azarga = netarsudil + latanoprost"], correct: 1, explanation: "Duotrav = travoprost + timolol. (Ganfort = bimatoprost+timolol; Simbrinza = brinzolamide+brimonidine; Azarga = brinzolamide+timolol.)" },
  { id: 64, question: "Betaxolol is preferred over timolol in a glaucoma patient who also has:", options: ["Reactive airway disease (asthma/COPD)", "Diabetes", "Cataract", "Dry eye"], correct: 0, explanation: "Cardioselective betaxolol is safer than non-selective timolol in reactive airway disease, though it lowers IOP somewhat less." },
  { id: 65, question: "A patient with severe sulfonamide allergy should avoid which glaucoma medication?", options: ["Timolol", "Dorzolamide (a topical carbonic anhydrase inhibitor)", "Latanoprost", "Pilocarpine"], correct: 1, explanation: "CAIs (topical dorzolamide/brinzolamide and systemic acetazolamide) are sulfonamide derivatives and are cautioned in severe sulfa allergy." },
  { id: 66, question: "The mechanism by which miotics can precipitate retinal detachment in high myopes is:", options: ["Direct retinal toxicity", "Ciliary muscle contraction causing anterior vitreoretinal traction", "Raising episcleral venous pressure", "Inducing choroidal effusion"], correct: 1, explanation: "Miotic-induced ciliary contraction produces vitreoretinal traction, a risk for retinal detachment in predisposed (high-myopic) eyes." },
];

const BASIC_EMBRYO_RECALL: RecallQ[] = [
  { id: 1, question: "Trace early embryonic development from the embryoblast to the trilaminar germ disc.", answer: "The embryoblast (inner cell mass) forms a bilaminar germ disc at week 2 — epiblast and hypoblast. At week 3, gastrulation converts it into a trilaminar germ disc with the three primary germ layers: ectoderm, mesoderm, and endoderm." },
  { id: 2, question: "During gastrulation, what does the invaginating, replacing, and remaining epiblast become?", answer: "Epiblast cells invaginate through the primitive streak: invaginating epiblast → mesoderm; cells replacing the hypoblast → endoderm; the remaining (non-invaginating) epiblast → ectoderm." },
  { id: 3, question: "What are neural crest cells, and why are they important to the eye?", answer: "Transient migratory stem cells with both ectodermal and mesodermal characteristics, recognized during neural tube formation (neurulation). They contribute to most connective/stromal ocular structures (\"everything else\") and many head and neck structures." },
  { id: 4, question: "From which embryonic tissues does the eye develop, and which germ layer contributes nothing?", answer: "Neuroectoderm, neural crest cells, surface ectoderm, and mesoderm. Endoderm does NOT contribute to the eye." },
  { id: 5, question: "Which ocular structures arise from neuroectoderm?", answer: "The retina and optic nerve (derivatives of the optic cup and optic stalk), the iris and ciliary epithelia and the iris sphincter/dilator muscles, and part of the vitreous." },
  { id: 6, question: "Which structures arise from surface ectoderm (mnemonic LEVL)?", answer: "Lens, Epithelium (of cornea and conjunctiva), Vitreous (contribution), and the Lacrimal apparatus — plus eyelid skin. Neural crest cells form \"everything else\" not otherwise assigned." },
  { id: 7, question: "Which structures arise from mesoderm (mnemonic MESV)?", answer: "Muscles (all extraocular and intraocular muscles EXCEPT the pupillary muscles), Endothelium of ocular blood vessels, Sclera (temporal portion), and part of the Vitreous. Neural crest supplies most other stromal tissues." },
  { id: 8, question: "What are the roles of Pax6 and Nrl in eye development?", answer: "Pax6 is the master control gene (\"master switch\") for eye development. Nrl is a key regulator of rod photoreceptor development in the retina." },
  { id: 9, question: "How does the eye primordium first form from the neural tube?", answer: "Neural tube formation begins in week 3. In week 4 the primary brain vesicles form; the prosencephalon (future diencephalon) gives rise to the eye. An optic pit/groove (neuroectoderm) evaginates to form the optic vesicle by day 25." },
  { id: 10, question: "How does the lens placode form, and when?", answer: "The optic vesicle induces the adjacent surface ectoderm to thicken into the lens placode by day 28." },
  { id: 11, question: "Describe formation of the optic cup and its two layers.", answer: "The lens placode invaginates to form the lens vesicle (day 32–33); reciprocal induction back toward the optic vesicle forms the two-layered optic cup. The outer layer becomes the retinal pigment epithelium (pigmented retina) and the inner layer becomes the neural (neurosensory) retina. The constricted connection to the forebrain is the optic stalk." },
  { id: 12, question: "What is the embryonic (choroidal) fissure, and what passes through it?", answer: "A groove formed by invagination extending along the ventral optic cup and stalk. It permits entry of mesoderm and neural crest cells and incorporates the hyaloid vascular system into the eye. Its two lips fuse to complete the globe." },
  { id: 13, question: "Where and how does the embryonic fissure close?", answer: "Closure begins in the mid-region of the cup near the equator and extends anteriorly (toward the rim) and posteriorly (toward the optic stalk), enclosing the hyaloid vessels. The fissure lies in the inferonasal quadrant of the globe, and closure occurs around the 5th week." },
  { id: 14, question: "What is the optic (intraretinal) ventricle, and what becomes of it?", answer: "The space between the two layers of the optic cup. As the layers approximate, it narrows and fuses by the 7th week, becoming the potential subretinal space that persists throughout life." },
  { id: 15, question: "Why is the neural retina prone to detachment from the RPE?", answer: "The RPE and neural retina are held together only by hydrogen bonds, an osmotic gradient, and electrostatic forces — there is no covalent linkage, so the two layers never fuse firmly, leaving the subretinal space as a lifelong potential space." },
  { id: 16, question: "Contrast anophthalmia and microphthalmia, including timing and causes.", answer: "Anophthalmia is total absence of the eyeball: primary from failure of optic vesicle formation in week 4, secondary from a forebrain developmental defect. Microphthalmia is a small, underdeveloped eye from arrested development after the optic vesicle has formed (reduced axial length). Both are multifactorial (e.g., SOX2), with environmental causes including rubella, toxoplasmosis, HSV, X-rays, and vitamin A deficiency." },
  { id: 17, question: "What causes a coloboma, and where is it typically found?", answer: "Failure of the embryonic fissure to close (around week 5). The defect can occur anywhere from the iris to the optic nerve depending on the extent of failed closure, and it is most commonly found in the inferonasal quadrant." },
  { id: 18, question: "Describe formation of the lens from the lens vesicle.", answer: "The anterior single-layer cuboidal epithelium persists throughout life as the lens epithelium. The posterior cells elongate and obliterate the vesicle cavity as primary lens fibers, forming the compact embryonic nucleus. The basal lamina becomes the lens capsule." },
  { id: 19, question: "How do secondary lens fibers and lens sutures form?", answer: "Cells in the equatorial (pre-equatorial) zone retain mitotic activity throughout life, producing secondary lens fibers that elongate and meet at lens sutures. The first sutures (marking the fetal nucleus) form a Y anteriorly and an inverted Y posteriorly, transforming the lens from spherical to ellipsoid. Lens growth continues throughout life." },
  { id: 20, question: "What causes primary versus secondary congenital aphakia?", answer: "Primary congenital aphakia: failure of lens placode formation in week 4 (failure of lens induction by the optic vesicle) — associated with FOXE3 mutation. Secondary congenital aphakia: a lens that formed but was later absorbed or destroyed (e.g., in-utero rubella infection)." },
  { id: 21, question: "What initiates corneal development, and what are the three neural crest waves?", answer: "Separation of the lens vesicle from the surface ectoderm initiates corneal development; the overlying surface ectoderm becomes the corneal epithelium. Neural crest cells then migrate in three waves: 1st → corneal endothelium; 2nd → keratocytes of the corneal stroma; 3rd (with mesoderm) → iris stroma and pupillary membrane." },
  { id: 22, question: "Give the embryonic origin of each corneal layer.", answer: "Corneal epithelium — surface ectoderm. Bowman membrane — condensation of anterior stroma (acellular; neural crest, 2nd wave). Stroma (substantia propria) — keratocytes (neural crest, 2nd wave) secreting type I collagen and fibronectin. Descemet membrane and endothelium — neural crest (1st wave), structurally/functionally present by the 6th month when active ion transport clears water and the cornea becomes transparent." },
  { id: 23, question: "What is anterior segment dysgenesis, and which genes are implicated?", answer: "A group of developmental disorders of the cornea, iris, ciliary body, lens, and anterior chamber angle caused by disruption of the three waves of neural crest migration — including Axenfeld-Rieger syndrome and Peter's anomaly. Implicated genes: FOXC1, FOXE3, PITX2/3, and PAX6." },
  { id: 24, question: "Describe Axenfeld-Rieger syndrome.", answer: "An autosomal dominant spectrum disorder (chromosome 4, PITX2) featuring posterior embryotoxon (a thickened, anteriorly displaced Schwalbe line) with attached iris strands, iris stromal abnormalities (hypoplasia is most common, with corectopia/ectropion uveae), and a ~50% risk of glaucoma. Systemic features include dental anomalies, distinct facies, redundant periumbilical skin, hypospadias, cardiac valvular defects, and pituitary abnormalities." },
  { id: 25, question: "Describe Peter's anomaly and its two types.", answer: "A central posterior corneal defect with overlying stromal opacity. Type I has an adherent iris strand; Type II has adherence of the lens to the cornea with cataract and central corneal opacity." },
  { id: 26, question: "What is the origin and general sequence of neural retina differentiation?", answer: "The neural retina is neuroectodermal, differentiating within the 1st month starting at the center of the optic cup and extending peripherally toward the rim. It gives rise to five neurons (photoreceptors, bipolar, ganglion, amacrine, horizontal cells) and one glial cell (Müller cell)." },
  { id: 27, question: "Describe the inner and outer neuroblastic layers and the transient layer of Chievitz.", answer: "By the end of the 6th week the neural retina forms two nuclear layers: the inner neuroblastic layer (→ ganglion cells, amacrine cells, Müller cells) and the outer neuroblastic layer (→ bipolar neurons, horizontal cells, photoreceptors). They are separated by the transient layer of Chievitz, which becomes the inner plexiform layer by the 9th–12th week." },
  { id: 28, question: "Which retinal cells differentiate first, and what is their proliferation pattern?", answer: "Ganglion cells are the first retinal cell type to differentiate. They proliferate rapidly during the 15th–17th week (early second trimester), then decrease during the 18th–30th week due to apoptosis." },
  { id: 29, question: "When do photoreceptor inner and outer segments form?", answer: "Photoreceptors form from the cilia of the outermost cells of the optic cup's inner layer: the inner segment appears in the 5th month and the outer segment in the 7th month." },
  { id: 30, question: "Describe foveal development, including the key postnatal milestones.", answer: "Macular differentiation begins ~6th month (ganglion and cone precursors thicken the macula); central thinning ~7th month creates the primitive foveal pit; by the 8th month two ganglion cell layers remain. After birth, relocation of the inner layers to the foveal slope by ~4 months of age uncovers the cone nuclei, and foveal remodeling continues until nearly 4 years of age." },
  { id: 31, question: "What is notable about RPE melanogenesis and its inductive role?", answer: "By the 6th week the RPE forms tight junctions and begins melanogenesis — the RPE is the first tissue in the body to produce melanin. It is a single columnar epithelium fully functional by the 3rd–4th month, and it exerts an inductive influence on the neural retina, choroid, and sclera (where RPE fails to form, these underlying tissues are hypoplastic)." },
  { id: 32, question: "How do the iris and ciliary body form, and what are the origins of the iris tissues?", answer: "At the end of the 3rd month, mesoderm at the anterior optic cup proliferates and pushes the anterior rim inward. The iris forms as a thin ring between the corneal endothelium and lens (3rd neural crest wave + mesoderm → iris stroma; the outer and inner layers of the anterior rim → the neuroectodermal anterior and posterior iris epithelia). The ciliary body forms as folds just posterior to the iris. The dilator pupillae is a direct extension of the anterior iris epithelium (neuroectoderm)." },
  { id: 33, question: "What determines eye color developmentally?", answer: "The concentration and distribution of pigment: pigment confined to the posterior iris epithelium yields a blue iris, whereas pigment distributed throughout the stroma yields a brown iris (definitive color is acquired after birth)." },
  { id: 34, question: "What causes aniridia versus a coloboma of the iris?", answer: "Iris hypoplasia that is focal produces a coloboma iridis, whereas diffuse hypoplasia produces aniridia (near-total absence of the iris) — from arrest of development at the rim of the optic cup around the 8th week, associated with PAX6 mutation." },
  { id: 35, question: "How do the anterior and posterior chambers and the pupillary membrane form?", answer: "At the 9th week, mesenchyme over the lens (after the 1st and 2nd neural crest waves) splits into a slit that enlarges into the anterior chamber. The deep layers of its posterior wall break down by vacuolization to form the posterior chamber; the thin remaining layer becomes the pupillary membrane, which regresses during the 6th–8th month to open the pupil." },
  { id: 36, question: "How does the anterior chamber angle and its trabecular meshwork develop?", answer: "At the 7th week, neural crest cells occupy the angle recess between the corneal endothelium and iris, forming the trabecular meshwork. The angle is demarcated by the 15th week and its recess deepens progressively from the 3rd gestational month until about 4 years after birth." },
  { id: 37, question: "What is the origin of the Schlemm canal, and what causes congenital glaucoma?", answer: "The Schlemm canal develops in the 3rd month from a small venous plexus (canaliculi), is mesodermal in origin, and initially functions as a blood vessel (aqueous sinus draining to episcleral veins). Congenital glaucoma results from disrupted development of the trabecular meshwork and Schlemm canal impairing aqueous drainage — the CYP1B1 mutation accounts for ~85% of cases." },
  { id: 38, question: "Describe the embryonic origins and layers of the choroid.", answer: "The choroid condenses from mesenchyme around the optic cup (inner vascular layer, homologous with pia/arachnoid). It is mostly neural crest–derived (stromal cells, melanocytes, pericytes) with some mesoderm (vascular endothelium). The choriocapillaris appears in the 4th–5th week, outer larger vessels form in the 3rd–5th month, and melanocyte differentiation occurs in the 7th month." },
  { id: 39, question: "Describe the embryonic origins of the sclera.", answer: "The sclera condenses from mesenchyme around the optic cup (outer fibrous layer, homologous with dura mater), mostly neural crest–derived, though the temporal sclera arises from mesoderm. It first joins the developing cornea near the equator and later expands to surround the optic cup." },
  { id: 40, question: "Describe the development of the optic nerve from the optic stalk.", answer: "The optic nerve develops from the optic stalk (neuroectoderm surrounded by neural crest). At the 6th week the inner layer cells vacuolate to create space for ganglion cell axons; outer layer cells form the lamina cribrosa, inner cells become glia (oligodendrocytes), and neural crest/mesoderm form the meninges. By the 8th week the hollow stalk lumen obliterates and the nerve becomes solid." },
  { id: 41, question: "Describe optic nerve myelination and the changing fetal axon count.", answer: "Myelination begins in the 7th month at the optic chiasm, proceeds toward the eye, and ceases at the lamina cribrosa (continuing 1–2 months after birth). Axon counts rise then fall with ganglion cell apoptosis: ~1.9 million (10th–12th wk), 3.7 million (16th wk), and 1.1 million (33rd wk, the adult number)." },
  { id: 42, question: "Describe the primary, secondary, and tertiary vitreous.", answer: "Primary vitreous (weeks 4–5, maximal by 2nd month): fibers, mesenchymal cells, and hyaloid vessels (ectoderm + mesoderm origins). Secondary vitreous: avascular, with hyalocytes and type II collagen, enveloping the regressing primary vitreous (remnant → Cloquet canal). Tertiary vitreous (3rd–4th month): condensed collagen attaching to the ILM and lens equator, forming the zonular fibers (4th–6th month)." },
  { id: 43, question: "Describe the hyaloid vascular system and its regression.", answer: "The hyaloid artery (a branch of the ophthalmic artery lying in the embryonic fissure, maximal at the 10th week) supplies the developing retina and lens; the tunica vasculosa lentis (with the anterior pupillary membrane) envelops the lens. The system regresses in the 3rd trimester when the lens matures — leaving Cloquet (hyaloid) canal, with the proximal hyaloid becoming the central retinal artery." },
  { id: 44, question: "When and how does retinal vascularization occur, and how does it relate to ROP?", answer: "Retinal vascularization begins at the 16th week and spreads concentrically from the optic nerve head toward the ora serrata, completing nasally ~36th week and temporally ~40th week — the basis for the zone I–III designations in retinopathy of prematurity." },
  { id: 45, question: "What is persistent fetal vasculature (PFV), including Mittendorf dot and Bergmeister papilla?", answer: "PFV (formerly persistent hyperplastic primary vitreous) is failure of the hyaloid artery and primary vitreous to regress, often with unilateral cataract, microphthalmia, ± retinal detachment. A Mittendorf dot is a small benign opacity on the (classically nasal) posterior lens capsule — the anterior hyaloid termination. A Bergmeister papilla is a benign linear gray structure anterior to the optic disc — the posterior hyaloid remnant." },
  { id: 46, question: "What is persistent pupillary membrane?", answer: "The most common developmental abnormality of the iris — remnant web-like strands of connective tissue or vascular arcades from the pupillary membrane crossing the pupil, occasionally interfering with vision." },
  { id: 47, question: "Summarize the pathogenesis and risk factors of retinopathy of prematurity.", answer: "A vasoproliferative disorder unique to premature infants (≤30 weeks, birth weight ≤1500 g). Phase 1: relative hyperoxia and delayed normal vessel growth from low VEGF; Phase 2: hypoxia and abnormal neovascular growth from a rebound VEGF surge. Environmental factors: oxygen, oxidative stress, light; endogenous: VEGF and IGF-1. Classified by ICROP3 (zone, stage, extent, vessel characteristics)." },
  { id: 48, question: "Give the embryonic origins of the eyelid tissues and the timeline of lid folding, fusion, and separation.", answer: "Eyelid skin — surface ectoderm; soft tissue — neural crest mesenchyme; eyelid muscle — mesoderm; conjunctiva — surface ectoderm. Eyelid folds appear at weeks 4–5 (upper from the frontonasal process, lower from the maxillary process), fuse in the 2nd month, and separate in the 5th–7th month." },
  { id: 49, question: "Describe development of the lacrimal gland and nasolacrimal apparatus, including relevant anomalies.", answer: "The lacrimal gland develops at weeks 6–7 from the basal conjunctiva at the temporal fornix (a solid surface-ectoderm cord canalized by the 3rd month); it matures and produces reflex tears about 3 weeks (up to ~6 weeks) after birth, so newborns initially cry without tears. The nasolacrimal apparatus forms from the nasolacrimal groove (weeks 5–7) with canalization at the 10th week; failure causes congenital nasolacrimal duct obstruction (dacryostenosis)." },
  { id: 50, question: "Summarize globe realignment and the key postnatal ocular changes.", answer: "The optic cup axes start at 180°, decreasing to 105° by the 3rd month, 71° at birth, and 68° (adult) by 3 years. Postnatally: corneal diameter grows from 9.5–10.5 mm to ~12 mm; axial length from 17 mm to ~24 mm; the AC angle deepens until ~4 years; lens fibers are added throughout life; foveal development and optic-nerve myelination (ceasing at the lamina cribrosa ~1 month after birth) continue after birth." },
];

const BASIC_EMBRYO_MCQ: MCQ[] = [
  { id: 1, question: "The three primary germ layers formed at gastrulation are:", options: ["Epiblast, hypoblast, trophoblast", "Ectoderm, mesoderm, endoderm", "Neuroectoderm, neural crest, surface ectoderm", "Optic cup, optic stalk, optic vesicle"], correct: 1, explanation: "Gastrulation (week 3) forms the trilaminar disc: ectoderm, mesoderm, and endoderm." },
  { id: 2, question: "During gastrulation, epiblast cells that invaginate through the primitive streak become:", options: ["Endoderm", "Mesoderm", "Ectoderm", "Trophoblast"], correct: 1, explanation: "Invaginating epiblast → mesoderm; cells replacing the hypoblast → endoderm; remaining epiblast → ectoderm." },
  { id: 3, question: "The eye and orbital tissues develop from all of the following EXCEPT:", options: ["Neuroectoderm", "Neural crest cells", "Surface ectoderm", "Endoderm"], correct: 3, explanation: "Endoderm does not contribute to the eye; neuroectoderm, neural crest, surface ectoderm, and mesoderm do." },
  { id: 4, question: "Which structure is derived from NEUROECTODERM?", options: ["Corneal epithelium", "Lens", "Retina and optic nerve", "Extraocular muscles"], correct: 2, explanation: "The retina and optic nerve (optic cup/stalk derivatives) are neuroectodermal; lens and corneal epithelium are surface ectoderm; EOMs are mesoderm." },
  { id: 5, question: "The lens is derived from:", options: ["Neural crest", "Surface ectoderm", "Mesoderm", "Neuroectoderm"], correct: 1, explanation: "The lens is surface ectoderm (LEVL: Lens, Epithelium, Vitreous, Lacrimal apparatus)." },
  { id: 6, question: "Which ocular structure is derived from MESODERM?", options: ["Iris stroma", "Corneal endothelium", "Endothelium of ocular blood vessels", "Ciliary epithelium"], correct: 2, explanation: "Mesoderm (MESV) gives the vascular endothelium and muscles (except pupillary muscles); corneal endothelium and iris stroma are neural crest." },
  { id: 7, question: "The pupillary (iris sphincter and dilator) muscles are unusual among ocular muscles because they arise from:", options: ["Mesoderm", "Neuroectoderm", "Surface ectoderm", "Endoderm"], correct: 1, explanation: "The iris muscles arise from neuroectoderm; all other ocular muscles are mesodermal." },
  { id: 8, question: "The master control gene for eye development is:", options: ["Nrl", "Pax6", "Mitf", "CYP1B1"], correct: 1, explanation: "Pax6 is the master switch for eye development; Nrl regulates rod photoreceptor development." },
  { id: 9, question: "The eye primordium arises from which part of the developing brain?", options: ["Rhombencephalon", "Mesencephalon", "Prosencephalon (future diencephalon)", "Metencephalon"], correct: 2, explanation: "The prosencephalon (future diencephalon) gives rise to the eye via the optic vesicle." },
  { id: 10, question: "The optic vesicle appears at approximately:", options: ["Day 10", "Day 25", "Day 44", "Day 60"], correct: 1, explanation: "The optic pit/groove evaginates to form the optic vesicle by about day 25 (week 4)." },
  { id: 11, question: "The optic vesicle induces the overlying surface ectoderm to form the lens placode at about:", options: ["Day 18", "Day 28", "Day 40", "Day 56"], correct: 1, explanation: "The optic vesicle induces the lens placode by about day 28." },
  { id: 12, question: "The OUTER layer of the optic cup becomes the:", options: ["Neural retina", "Retinal pigment epithelium", "Lens", "Choroid"], correct: 1, explanation: "The outer optic cup layer becomes the RPE; the inner layer becomes the neural retina." },
  { id: 13, question: "The embryonic (choroidal) fissure is important because it:", options: ["Forms the eyelids", "Allows entry of the hyaloid vasculature and neural crest/mesoderm into the eye", "Becomes the trabecular meshwork", "Gives rise to the lens"], correct: 1, explanation: "The embryonic fissure admits the hyaloid vessels and mesoderm/neural crest; its lips fuse to complete the globe." },
  { id: 14, question: "The embryonic fissure is located in which quadrant of the globe?", options: ["Superotemporal", "Inferonasal", "Superonasal", "Inferotemporal"], correct: 1, explanation: "The embryonic fissure lies in the inferonasal quadrant — the classic location of colobomas." },
  { id: 15, question: "Failure of the embryonic fissure to close causes:", options: ["Aniridia", "Coloboma", "Cataract", "Anophthalmia"], correct: 1, explanation: "Failure of embryonic fissure closure (~week 5) causes coloboma, which may extend from iris to optic nerve, typically inferonasally." },
  { id: 16, question: "The subretinal space is a persistent potential space because the RPE and neural retina:", options: ["Are fused by desmosomes", "Are joined only by non-covalent forces (hydrogen bonds, osmotic/electrostatic) and never fuse firmly", "Share a common basement membrane", "Are connected by tight junctions"], correct: 1, explanation: "Only weak non-covalent forces hold the RPE to the neural retina, so they never fuse firmly — predisposing to retinal detachment." },
  { id: 17, question: "Primary anophthalmia results from:", options: ["Failure of optic vesicle formation in week 4", "Failure of lens placode formation", "Failure of embryonic fissure closure", "A forebrain defect"], correct: 0, explanation: "Primary anophthalmia is failure of optic vesicle formation; secondary anophthalmia is due to a forebrain developmental defect." },
  { id: 18, question: "Microphthalmia is characterized by:", options: ["Total absence of the eyeball", "A small, underdeveloped eye from arrest after the optic vesicle formed", "Absence of the lens", "Absence of the iris"], correct: 1, explanation: "Microphthalmia is a small eye (reduced axial length) from arrested development after optic vesicle formation." },
  { id: 19, question: "The single-layer anterior epithelium of the lens vesicle:", options: ["Elongates to form primary lens fibers", "Persists throughout life as the lens epithelium", "Becomes the lens capsule", "Degenerates by the 3rd month"], correct: 1, explanation: "The anterior cuboidal epithelium persists for life; the posterior cells elongate into primary lens fibers (embryonic nucleus)." },
  { id: 20, question: "The primary lens fibers (from the posterior lens vesicle) form the:", options: ["Lens capsule", "Embryonic nucleus", "Lens epithelium", "Zonular fibers"], correct: 1, explanation: "Posterior cells elongate and obliterate the vesicle cavity, forming the compact embryonic nucleus." },
  { id: 21, question: "The lens capsule is derived from the:", options: ["Basal lamina of the lens epithelium", "Neural crest", "Primary vitreous", "Tunica vasculosa lentis"], correct: 0, explanation: "The lens capsule is the basal lamina of the lens epithelial cells." },
  { id: 22, question: "The first (fetal-nucleus) lens sutures are shaped like:", options: ["A star anteriorly and posteriorly", "An upright Y anteriorly and an inverted Y posteriorly", "An X anteriorly and posteriorly", "A single vertical line"], correct: 1, explanation: "The first sutures form an upright Y anteriorly and an inverted Y posteriorly, marking the fetal nucleus." },
  { id: 23, question: "Primary congenital aphakia is associated with which gene and mechanism?", options: ["PAX6; arrest at the optic cup rim", "FOXE3; failure of lens placode formation/induction", "CYP1B1; trabecular meshwork defect", "PITX2; posterior embryotoxon"], correct: 1, explanation: "Primary congenital aphakia results from failure of lens placode formation/induction and is associated with FOXE3." },
  { id: 24, question: "Corneal development is initiated by:", options: ["Neural crest migration alone", "Separation of the lens vesicle from the surface ectoderm", "Formation of the optic stalk", "Closure of the embryonic fissure"], correct: 1, explanation: "Separation of the lens vesicle from surface ectoderm initiates corneal development; the surface ectoderm becomes corneal epithelium." },
  { id: 25, question: "The FIRST wave of neural crest migration in corneal development forms the:", options: ["Keratocytes (stroma)", "Corneal endothelium", "Iris stroma", "Corneal epithelium"], correct: 1, explanation: "1st wave → corneal endothelium; 2nd wave → keratocytes; 3rd wave (+ mesoderm) → iris stroma and pupillary membrane." },
  { id: 26, question: "The corneal epithelium is derived from:", options: ["Neural crest, 1st wave", "Neural crest, 2nd wave", "Surface ectoderm", "Mesoderm"], correct: 2, explanation: "The corneal epithelium is surface ectoderm; the endothelium and stroma are neural crest." },
  { id: 27, question: "Descemet membrane and the corneal endothelium arise from:", options: ["Surface ectoderm", "Neural crest (1st wave)", "Mesoderm", "Neuroectoderm"], correct: 1, explanation: "Descemet membrane and endothelium are neural crest (1st wave), functional by the 6th month when the cornea becomes transparent." },
  { id: 28, question: "The cornea becomes transparent in the 6th month primarily because the endothelium:", options: ["Deposits melanin", "Actively transports ions to remove water (deturgescence)", "Produces type I collagen", "Forms tight junctions with the epithelium"], correct: 1, explanation: "Active ion transport by the endothelium removes water from the stroma, producing transparency." },
  { id: 29, question: "Anterior segment dysgenesis results from disruption of:", options: ["Lens fiber elongation", "The three waves of neural crest migration", "Retinal lamination", "Hyaloid regression"], correct: 1, explanation: "ASD (Axenfeld-Rieger, Peter's anomaly) arises from disrupted neural crest migration; genes include FOXC1, PITX2/3, PAX6, FOXE3." },
  { id: 30, question: "Axenfeld-Rieger syndrome is most often caused by a mutation in:", options: ["PAX6", "PITX2 (chromosome 4)", "CYP1B1", "SOX2"], correct: 1, explanation: "Axenfeld-Rieger is autosomal dominant, classically PITX2 on chromosome 4, with ~50% glaucoma risk." },
  { id: 31, question: "'Posterior embryotoxon' in Axenfeld-Rieger syndrome refers to:", options: ["A thickened, anteriorly displaced Schwalbe line", "A congenital cataract", "A retinal coloboma", "Persistent hyaloid artery"], correct: 0, explanation: "Posterior embryotoxon is a prominent, anteriorly displaced Schwalbe line, often with attached iris strands." },
  { id: 32, question: "Peter's anomaly is characterized by:", options: ["Absence of the iris", "A central posterior corneal defect with overlying stromal opacity", "A small nasal lens opacity", "Failure of eyelid separation"], correct: 1, explanation: "Peter's anomaly features a posterior corneal defect with stromal opacity; type I has an iris strand, type II lens-cornea adhesion with cataract." },
  { id: 33, question: "The retinal cell type that differentiates FIRST is the:", options: ["Photoreceptor", "Ganglion cell", "Bipolar cell", "Horizontal cell"], correct: 1, explanation: "Ganglion cells differentiate first, proliferating in weeks 15–17 then declining by apoptosis (weeks 18–30)." },
  { id: 34, question: "The transient layer of Chievitz becomes the:", options: ["Nerve fiber layer", "Inner plexiform layer (by weeks 9–12)", "Outer nuclear layer", "External limiting membrane"], correct: 1, explanation: "The transient layer of Chievitz (between neuroblastic layers) becomes the inner plexiform layer by weeks 9–12." },
  { id: 35, question: "The outer neuroblastic layer of the retina gives rise to all of the following EXCEPT:", options: ["Bipolar neurons", "Horizontal cells", "Photoreceptors", "Ganglion cells"], correct: 3, explanation: "The outer neuroblastic layer → bipolar, horizontal, and photoreceptor cells; ganglion (and amacrine/Müller) cells come from the inner layer." },
  { id: 36, question: "Photoreceptor outer segments form at approximately the:", options: ["3rd month", "5th month", "7th month", "9th month"], correct: 2, explanation: "The inner segment forms in the 5th month and the outer segment in the 7th month." },
  { id: 37, question: "Which tissue is the FIRST in the body to produce melanin?", options: ["Iris stroma", "Choroidal melanocytes", "Retinal pigment epithelium", "Skin melanocytes"], correct: 2, explanation: "The RPE is the first tissue in the body to produce melanin (by ~6 weeks)." },
  { id: 38, question: "Where RPE fails to form (e.g., at the embryonic fissure), the underlying neural retina, choroid, and sclera are:", options: ["Hyperplastic", "Hypoplastic", "Normal", "Calcified"], correct: 1, explanation: "The RPE exerts an inductive influence, so where it is absent the underlying tissues are hypoplastic." },
  { id: 39, question: "A blue iris results from pigment that is:", options: ["Distributed throughout the stroma", "Confined to the posterior iris epithelium", "Absent entirely", "Located only in the anterior border layer"], correct: 1, explanation: "Pigment confined to the posterior epithelium gives a blue iris; pigment throughout the stroma gives brown." },
  { id: 40, question: "The iris dilator pupillae muscle is a direct extension of the:", options: ["Corneal endothelium", "Anterior iris epithelium (neuroectoderm)", "Ciliary muscle", "Iris stroma"], correct: 1, explanation: "The dilator pupillae extends directly from the anterior iris epithelium (neuroectoderm)." },
  { id: 41, question: "Aniridia results from arrest of development at the optic cup rim (~week 8) and is associated with:", options: ["PITX2", "PAX6 mutation", "CYP1B1", "FOXE3"], correct: 1, explanation: "Diffuse iris hypoplasia (aniridia) is associated with PAX6 mutation." },
  { id: 42, question: "The pupillary membrane normally regresses during the:", options: ["4th–5th week", "6th–8th month", "1st month after birth", "3rd year of life"], correct: 1, explanation: "The pupillary membrane regresses in the 6th–8th month to open the pupil; failure → persistent pupillary membrane." },
  { id: 43, question: "The trabecular meshwork forms from neural crest cells occupying the angle recess at about the:", options: ["4th week", "7th week", "15th week", "3rd month after birth"], correct: 1, explanation: "Neural crest cells form the trabecular meshwork at ~7 weeks; the angle is demarcated by 15 weeks and deepens until ~4 years after birth." },
  { id: 44, question: "The Schlemm canal is:", options: ["Neuroectodermal, forming from the optic stalk", "Mesodermal, developing from a small venous plexus", "Neural crest, forming in the 3rd wave", "Surface ectodermal"], correct: 1, explanation: "The Schlemm canal is mesodermal, developing from venous plexus canaliculi and initially functioning as a blood vessel." },
  { id: 45, question: "The gene mutation responsible for ~85% of primary congenital glaucoma is:", options: ["PAX6", "PITX2", "CYP1B1", "SOX2"], correct: 2, explanation: "CYP1B1 mutation accounts for about 85% of congenital glaucoma (from disrupted TM/Schlemm canal development)." },
  { id: 46, question: "The choroid and sclera are homologous, respectively, with the:", options: ["Dura mater and pia/arachnoid", "Pia/arachnoid mater and dura mater", "Ependyma and choroid plexus", "Neural tube and notochord"], correct: 1, explanation: "The vascular choroid is homologous with the pia/arachnoid; the fibrous sclera is homologous with the dura mater." },
  { id: 47, question: "The choroid and sclera are derived MOSTLY from:", options: ["Mesoderm", "Neural crest cells", "Surface ectoderm", "Neuroectoderm"], correct: 1, explanation: "Both are mostly neural crest–derived (with some mesoderm — vascular endothelium of choroid, temporal sclera)." },
  { id: 48, question: "The lamina cribrosa's outer-layer cells of the optic stalk are formed by the 6th week, while the optic nerve becomes solid (lumen obliterates) by the:", options: ["4th week", "8th week", "7th month", "Birth"], correct: 1, explanation: "The hollow optic stalk lumen obliterates and the nerve becomes solid by the 8th week." },
  { id: 49, question: "Optic nerve myelination begins at the chiasm in the 7th month and normally ceases at the:", options: ["Optic chiasm", "Lateral geniculate body", "Lamina cribrosa", "Ora serrata"], correct: 2, explanation: "Myelination proceeds toward the eye and stops at the lamina cribrosa (~1–2 months after birth)." },
  { id: 50, question: "The adult number of optic nerve axons (~1.1 million) is reached at about:", options: ["10th–12th week", "16th week", "33rd week", "3 years after birth"], correct: 2, explanation: "Axons peak at ~3.7 million (16 wk) then fall to ~1.1 million by 33 weeks (adult), paralleling ganglion cell apoptosis." },
  { id: 51, question: "The remnant of the regressed primary vitreous/hyaloid system is the:", options: ["Cloquet canal", "Bergmeister papilla only", "Zonule of Zinn", "Bruch membrane"], correct: 0, explanation: "Regression of the hyaloid system and primary vitreous leaves Cloquet (hyaloid) canal." },
  { id: 52, question: "The secondary vitreous is characterized by:", options: ["Hyaloid vessels and mesenchyme", "Avascular hyalocytes and type II collagen", "Zonular fibers", "Type I collagen and fibronectin"], correct: 1, explanation: "The secondary vitreous is avascular with hyalocytes and type II collagen, enveloping the regressing primary vitreous." },
  { id: 53, question: "The zonular fibers of the lens develop from the:", options: ["Primary vitreous", "Secondary vitreous", "Tertiary vitreous", "Tunica vasculosa lentis"], correct: 2, explanation: "Tertiary vitreous condensation extending to the lens equator forms the zonular fibers (4th–6th month)." },
  { id: 54, question: "The proximal portion of the hyaloid artery persists as the:", options: ["Central retinal artery", "Long posterior ciliary artery", "Ophthalmic artery", "Cilioretinal artery"], correct: 0, explanation: "The proximal hyaloid artery becomes the central retinal artery; the distal portion regresses (Cloquet canal)." },
  { id: 55, question: "A Mittendorf dot is:", options: ["A gray structure anterior to the optic disc", "A small opacity on the (nasal) posterior lens capsule — anterior hyaloid remnant", "A remnant on the trabecular meshwork", "A macular pigment deposit"], correct: 1, explanation: "A Mittendorf dot is the benign anterior hyaloid termination on the posterior lens capsule (classically nasal)." },
  { id: 56, question: "A Bergmeister papilla is:", options: ["A posterior hyaloid remnant (glial tissue) anterior to the optic disc", "An opacity on the lens capsule", "A persistent pupillary strand", "A choroidal coloboma"], correct: 0, explanation: "A Bergmeister papilla is the benign remnant of the posterior hyaloid artery's fibrous sheath at the optic disc." },
  { id: 57, question: "Retinal vascularization begins at the optic nerve head at about the ___ and reaches the temporal periphery at about ___.", options: ["8th week; 24th week", "16th week; 40th week", "24th week; 32nd week", "Birth; 3 months of age"], correct: 1, explanation: "Retinal vessels begin at ~16 weeks and reach the periphery nasally ~36 weeks and temporally ~40 weeks (basis for ROP zones)." },
  { id: 58, question: "In retinopathy of prematurity, Phase 1 is characterized by:", options: ["Hypoxia with a VEGF surge and neovascularization", "Relative hyperoxia with delayed vessel growth from low VEGF", "Complete retinal vascularization", "Choroidal neovascularization"], correct: 1, explanation: "Phase 1 ROP: relative hyperoxia and delayed normal vessel growth (low VEGF); Phase 2: hypoxia and abnormal vessel growth (VEGF surge)." },
  { id: 59, question: "Endogenous factors central to ROP pathogenesis include:", options: ["VEGF and IGF-1", "Melanin and rhodopsin", "Collagen I and fibronectin", "PITX2 and FOXC1"], correct: 0, explanation: "VEGF and IGF-1 are the key endogenous factors (with oxygen, oxidative stress, and light as environmental factors)." },
  { id: 60, question: "The most common developmental abnormality of the iris is:", options: ["Aniridia", "Coloboma", "Persistent pupillary membrane", "Ectropion uveae"], correct: 2, explanation: "Persistent pupillary membrane (remnant strands over the pupil) is the most common iris developmental anomaly." },
  { id: 61, question: "Persistent fetal vasculature (formerly PHPV) results from:", options: ["Failure of the embryonic fissure to close", "Failure of the hyaloid artery/primary vitreous to regress", "Arrest at the optic cup rim", "Failure of lens induction"], correct: 1, explanation: "PFV is failure of hyaloid artery and primary vitreous regression, often with unilateral cataract, microphthalmia, ± RD." },
  { id: 62, question: "The eyelid soft tissue (other than skin and muscle) is derived from:", options: ["Surface ectoderm", "Neural crest mesenchyme", "Mesoderm", "Endoderm"], correct: 1, explanation: "Eyelid skin is surface ectoderm, muscle is mesoderm, and the soft tissue is neural crest mesenchyme." },
  { id: 63, question: "The eyelids fuse and later separate at approximately:", options: ["Fuse 2nd month, separate 5th–7th month", "Fuse 5th month, separate at birth", "Fuse at birth, separate 3 months later", "They never fuse"], correct: 0, explanation: "Eyelid folds appear at weeks 4–5, fuse in the 2nd month, and separate in the 5th–7th month." },
  { id: 64, question: "A newborn typically cries without tears because:", options: ["The puncta are congenitally absent", "The lacrimal gland does not produce reflex tears until ~3 weeks after birth", "The cornea is not yet innervated", "Tears drain too rapidly"], correct: 1, explanation: "The lacrimal gland matures and produces reflex tears about 3 weeks after birth, so newborns initially cry without tears." },
  { id: 65, question: "Failure of canalization of the nasolacrimal cord (~10th week) causes:", options: ["Dacryostenosis (congenital nasolacrimal duct obstruction)", "Cryptophthalmos", "Ankyloblepharon", "Dermoid cyst"], correct: 0, explanation: "Failed canalization of the nasolacrimal apparatus causes congenital nasolacrimal duct obstruction (dacryostenosis)." },
  { id: 66, question: "Extraocular muscles are derived from:", options: ["Neural crest only", "Paraxial and prechordal mesoderm", "Surface ectoderm", "Neuroectoderm"], correct: 1, explanation: "EOMs arise from paraxial and prechordal mesoderm, induced by the optic cup and surrounding neural crest mesenchyme." },
  { id: 67, question: "Cryptophthalmos is:", options: ["Fusion of the eyelid margins", "Congenital absence of the eyelids with skin covering the eye and no palpebral fissure", "A notch of the upper eyelid", "Absence of the lens"], correct: 1, explanation: "Cryptophthalmos is congenital absence of eyelids — skin passes over the eye with no palpebral fissure (failed lid differentiation)." },
  { id: 68, question: "By 3 years of age the angle between the optic cup axes reaches the adult value of approximately:", options: ["180°", "105°", "71°", "68°"], correct: 3, explanation: "The axes decrease from 180° to 105° (3rd month) to 71° (birth) to 68° (adult) by 3 years of age." },
];

// Ocular pharmacology & drug delivery lecture, merged into Part 1
// (Pharmacokinetics). ids continue from BASIC_PK arrays (recall from 43,
// MCQ from 51) so the concatenated Part 1 pool keeps unique ids.
const BASIC_PHARM2_RECALL: RecallQ[] = [
  { id: 43, question: "Distinguish pharmacokinetics from pharmacodynamics.", answer: "Pharmacokinetics is the movement of a drug through the body — absorption, distribution, metabolism, and excretion (ADME). Pharmacodynamics is the biochemical and physiological effect of a drug on the body — its mechanism of action, adverse effects, and drug interactions. (Simply: PK is what the body does to the drug; PD is what the drug does to the body.)" },
  { id: 44, question: "What is bioavailability, and why does it matter?", answer: "The amount and concentration of active drug reaching the therapeutic site. To achieve a therapeutic effect a drug must reach its site of action in sufficient concentration — a drug that cannot reach the target in adequate amount is ineffective regardless of its potency." },
  { id: 45, question: "What does Paracelsus's 'only the dose makes the poison' mean for drug therapy?", answer: "Every drug has a therapeutic window: below it there is no therapeutic benefit, and above it there are harmful (toxic) effects. Dosing aims to keep the drug within that window." },
  { id: 46, question: "List the factors that determine a drug's bioavailability.", answer: "Route of administration, amount administered, extent and rate of absorption at the administration site, distribution and tissue binding, movement by bulk flow in circulating fluids, transport between body compartments, biotransformation/metabolism (mainly liver), and excretion (kidney and liver)." },
  { id: 47, question: "How does aging affect drug handling?", answer: "Elderly patients have altered pharmacokinetics and pharmacodynamics (independently of each other), so therapeutic effect and toxicity can change independently of the dose (e.g., ~30% reduction in GFR). Agent selection should be guided by general health, age, and concomitant medications." },
  { id: 48, question: "What are the main routes of ocular drug administration?", answer: "Topical (eyedrops, ointments), local (periocular injections and intraocular injections), and systemic (intravenous, intramuscular, oral)." },
  { id: 49, question: "Why are eyedrops the mainstay of ocular therapy, and what drives their absorption?", answer: "They maximize anterior segment drug concentration while minimizing systemic toxicity. The concentration gradient between the drug-rich tear reservoir and the corneal/conjunctival epithelia forces a passive route of absorption." },
  { id: 50, question: "What formulation properties of an eyedrop affect ocular drug delivery?", answer: "Drug concentration, solubility (suspension vs emulsion), viscosity, lipid solubility, pH and ionic charge, and surfactants (preservatives)." },
  { id: 51, question: "How is eyedrop concentration expressed, and what does a high concentration provoke?", answer: "A 1% concentration = 1 g/100 mL = 10 mg/mL (and 1:1000 = 1 g in 1000 mL). A high drug concentration can elicit reflex tearing, which washes drug away." },
  { id: 52, question: "Why must a topical drug have balanced (biphasic) lipid solubility to penetrate the cornea?", answer: "The corneal epithelial cell membranes are hydrophobic/lipophilic, so a drug needs lipid solubility to cross them — but if lipid solubility is too high, the drug is poorly soluble in the aqueous tear film. Good corneal penetration requires both lipid and water solubility (an amphipathic/biphasic drug)." },
  { id: 53, question: "What is the normal tear pH, and what triggers reflex tearing?", answer: "Tear pH is about 7.4. Reflex tearing is provoked by excessively high viscosity or a large difference in pH between the drug and the tears." },
  { id: 54, question: "To what does topical medication bind, reducing free drug?", answer: "Tear and ocular surface proteins and melanin (pigment), which sequester drug and reduce the free fraction available for absorption." },
  { id: 55, question: "Describe the volumes of the tear reservoir relative to an eyedrop.", answer: "One drop from a dispenser is ~50 µL, but the tear lake holds only ~7 µL (rising to ~10 µL just after a drop). So the maximum drug retained in the tear reservoir is ~10 µL — only about 20% of a dispensed drop; the rest overflows or drains." },
  { id: 56, question: "How quickly does an instilled drug wash out of the tear reservoir?", answer: "Rapidly: about 50% of the initial reservoir (5 µL, ~10% of a drop) remains 4 minutes after instillation, and only ~17% (1.7 µL, ~3.4% of a drop) remains at 10 minutes. Residence time depends on formulation, timing of subsequent drops, and tear production/drainage." },
  { id: 57, question: "How can drug retention (residence time) and absorption be improved with eyedrops?", answer: "Allow ~5 minutes between successive drops; prevent tear-lake emptying by compressing the nasolacrimal duct (digital pressure at the medial canthus) or closing the eyes for ~5 minutes after instillation; and increase drug viscosity or use delivery devices." },
  { id: 58, question: "Besides improving absorption, what is the benefit of nasolacrimal occlusion or eyelid closure after instilling drops?", answer: "It decreases systemic absorption and potential toxicity. Blinking activates the nasolacrimal pump, which empties the tear lake into the nose, where drug absorbed by the nasal mucosa can reach significant blood levels (e.g., atropine). Occlusion blunts this systemic uptake." },
  { id: 59, question: "How do ointments work as an ocular vehicle?", answer: "They increase contact time. Petrolatum and mineral oil melt at body temperature and are effective lipid solvents; water-soluble drugs are suspended as microcrystals trapped until the ointment melts, giving slow release. Examples include fluorometholone, chloramphenicol, and tetracycline." },
  { id: 60, question: "What are the advantages and disadvantages of ophthalmic ointments?", answer: "Advantages: less irritation/stinging, increased contact time, and not being diluted by tears. Disadvantages: blurred vision and a greasy sensation/discomfort. Apply a thin strip in the conjunctival sac (inner to outer canthus), avoid touching the tube tip to the eye, and have the patient roll the eye behind closed lids to distribute it." },
  { id: 61, question: "Why are periocular injections used, and how do subconjunctival and subtenon injections differ?", answer: "They bypass the conjunctival and corneal epithelial barriers to deliver drug closer to its site of action. A subconjunctival injection sits behind a hydrophilic barrier and creates a reservoir that slowly releases into the tear film. A subtenon injection sits behind a lipophilic barrier — a hydrophilic drug placed in the sub-Tenon space can penetrate intraocular tissue faster than topical application can. Retrobulbar injection is a deeper periocular route." },
  { id: 62, question: "What are the benefits, ocular risks, and aseptic requirements of intraocular injections?", answer: "They deliver drug effectively to the target while decreasing systemic adverse effects; ocular risks include transient ocular hypertension and inflammation/infection (endophthalmitis). Use only non-preserved medications, control the intraocular concentration to avoid toxicity, and use standard aseptic technique — 5% povidone-iodine applied for 30–90 seconds." },
  { id: 63, question: "For systemic (IV) drug delivery to the eye, what governs penetration across the blood–ocular barrier?", answer: "Only the unbound (free) fraction of drug crosses the blood–ocular barrier, and higher lipid solubility increases penetration. A bolus injection can transiently exceed the plasma-protein binding capacity, producing higher peak intraocular drug levels than a slow drip. An inflamed eye has a disrupted barrier and allows more drug in." },
  { id: 64, question: "What sustained-release and drug-design strategies are used in ophthalmology?", answer: "Prodrugs; sustained-release delivery via inserts (placed in the fornix), implants (e.g., dexamethasone, and the ranibizumab Port Delivery System), and drug-eluting intraocular lenses; and collagen corneal shields." },
  { id: 65, question: "Describe the dexamethasone intravitreal implant (Ozurdex).", answer: "A 0.7-mg dexamethasone biodegradable polymer-matrix implant injected into the vitreous cavity, approved for macular edema secondary to retinal vein occlusion, noninfectious posterior uveitis, and diabetic macular edema. The polymer dissolves, releasing dexamethasone for up to 6 months, with clinical efficacy lasting at least 3 months." },
  { id: 66, question: "Name emerging ocular drug-delivery technologies.", answer: "Drug-eluting contact lenses, punctal plug–mediated delivery, encapsulated cell technology, nanotechnology, and microelectromechanical systems (MEMS) — along with the refillable Port Delivery System." },
  { id: 67, question: "What is fluorescein used for, and what is its most common adverse effect?", answer: "Sodium fluorescein is used to study the retinal circulation (fluorescein angiography). Adverse effects range from localized skin reactions to hypersensitivity/allergic reactions; the most common adverse effect is nausea (up to 10% of patients)." },
  { id: 68, question: "What is indocyanine green (ICG) used for, and what are its risks?", answer: "ICG studies the choroidal vasculature and helps identify and delineate poorly defined choroidal neovascular membranes in AMD and polypoidal choroidal vasculopathy (PCV); it can also evaluate anterior scleritis. It is mildly toxic (localized skin reactions, sore throat, hot flushes), with rare severe reactions (anaphylactic shock, hypotension, tachycardia, dyspnea, urticaria)." },
  { id: 69, question: "Which vital dyes are used to stain the anterior lens capsule, the internal limiting membrane, and the vitreous in surgery?", answer: "Anterior lens capsule: trypan blue (FDA-approved) and ICG. Internal limiting membrane: brilliant blue G (BBG; TissueBlue 0.025%, FDA-approved), trypan blue, and ICG. Vitreous: triamcinolone acetonide (FDA-approved)." },
  { id: 70, question: "What is the rationale for anti-VEGF therapy, and what are its main indications?", answer: "Breakdown of the blood–ocular barrier disrupts the balance among growth factors — an imbalance among TGF-βs, basic FGF, VEGF, and IGFs drives ocular neovascularization. Anti-VEGF agents are used for diabetic macular edema, wet AMD/PCV, macular edema secondary to RVO, proliferative diabetic retinopathy, and retinopathy of prematurity." },
  { id: 71, question: "Distinguish pharmacogenetics from pharmacogenomics.", answer: "Pharmacogenetics studies how variation in a single gene affects an individual's response to a drug. Pharmacogenomics is broader — how the whole genome (many genes) influences drug response. Both aim to individualize therapy based on genetic makeup." },
  { id: 72, question: "Which single property most reduces the corneal bioavailability of a topical drug — and why?", answer: "Low lipid solubility: because the corneal epithelium is lipophilic, a poorly lipid-soluble drug cannot cross it well, reducing bioavailability. (High viscosity actually helps by increasing residence time, and isotonicity/near-neutral pH minimize reflex tearing.)" },
];

const BASIC_PHARM2_MCQ: MCQ[] = [
  { id: 51, question: "Pharmacokinetics is best defined as:", options: ["The effect of the drug on the body", "The movement of the drug through the body (absorption, distribution, metabolism, excretion)", "The genetic basis of drug response", "The study of drug receptors"], correct: 1, explanation: "PK = what the body does to the drug (ADME); pharmacodynamics = what the drug does to the body." },
  { id: 52, question: "The 'ADME' of pharmacokinetics stands for:", options: ["Action, Dose, Metabolism, Effect", "Absorption, Distribution, Metabolism, Excretion", "Affinity, Delivery, Mechanism, Elimination", "Adverse, Drug, Metabolic, Events"], correct: 1, explanation: "ADME = Absorption, Distribution, Metabolism, Excretion." },
  { id: 53, question: "Bioavailability refers to:", options: ["The total dose administered", "The amount/concentration of active drug reaching its therapeutic site", "The half-life of the drug", "The receptor-binding affinity"], correct: 1, explanation: "Bioavailability is the amount and concentration of active drug that reaches the site of action." },
  { id: 54, question: "'Only the dose makes the poison' (Paracelsus) captures the concept of:", options: ["First-pass metabolism", "The therapeutic window (benefit below toxicity, no benefit below a threshold)", "Zero-order kinetics", "Receptor desensitization"], correct: 1, explanation: "It describes the therapeutic window: too little gives no benefit, too much is toxic." },
  { id: 55, question: "Drug metabolism (biotransformation) occurs primarily in the:", options: ["Kidney", "Liver", "Lung", "Spleen"], correct: 1, explanation: "Biotransformation is mainly hepatic; excretion is via the kidney and liver." },
  { id: 56, question: "In elderly patients, drug therapeutic effect and toxicity may change:", options: ["Only if the dose is increased", "Independently of the drug dosage, due to altered PK and PD", "Never — aging does not affect drug handling", "Only for topical drugs"], correct: 1, explanation: "Aging alters PK and PD independently, so effect/toxicity can change independent of dose (e.g., ~30% lower GFR)." },
  { id: 57, question: "Absorption of a topical eyedrop across the cornea is driven mainly by:", options: ["Active transport", "A passive concentration gradient between the tear reservoir and the epithelia", "Pinocytosis", "The nasolacrimal pump"], correct: 1, explanation: "The concentrated tear reservoir versus the epithelia forces a passive route of absorption." },
  { id: 58, question: "A 1% ophthalmic solution contains:", options: ["1 mg/mL", "10 mg/mL", "100 mg/mL", "0.1 mg/mL"], correct: 1, explanation: "1% = 1 g/100 mL = 1000 mg/100 mL = 10 mg/mL." },
  { id: 59, question: "A very high topical drug concentration can reduce delivery by:", options: ["Increasing corneal thickness", "Eliciting reflex tearing that washes drug away", "Blocking the puncta", "Raising IOP"], correct: 1, explanation: "High concentration (and high viscosity or large pH mismatch) elicits reflex tearing, diluting/washing out the drug." },
  { id: 60, question: "For good corneal penetration, a topical drug ideally has:", options: ["Very high lipid solubility only", "Balanced (biphasic) lipid and water solubility", "Very high water solubility only", "No charge and no solubility"], correct: 1, explanation: "The lipophilic epithelium needs a lipid-soluble drug, but the aqueous tear film needs water solubility — so a biphasic drug penetrates best." },
  { id: 61, question: "Normal tear film pH is approximately:", options: ["5.5", "6.5", "7.4", "8.5"], correct: 2, explanation: "Tear pH is about 7.4; a large drug–tear pH difference provokes reflex tearing." },
  { id: 62, question: "Topical drug is bound (and thus partly inactivated) by:", options: ["Corneal collagen", "Tear/ocular surface proteins and melanin", "Aqueous humor bicarbonate", "The lens capsule"], correct: 1, explanation: "Tear/surface proteins and melanin bind drug, reducing the free fraction available for absorption." },
  { id: 63, question: "One drop from a dispenser is ~50 µL, but the tear lake can hold a maximum of only about:", options: ["1 µL", "10 µL", "25 µL", "50 µL"], correct: 1, explanation: "The tear lake holds ~7 µL (up to ~10 µL after a drop) — only ~20% of a dispensed drop is retained." },
  { id: 64, question: "Approximately what fraction of the initial tear reservoir remains 4 minutes after instillation?", options: ["90%", "50%", "17%", "5%"], correct: 1, explanation: "About 50% (5 µL) remains at 4 minutes, falling to ~17% (1.7 µL) by 10 minutes — rapid turnover." },
  { id: 65, question: "Which technique improves eyedrop retention AND reduces systemic absorption?", options: ["Blinking rapidly after instillation", "Nasolacrimal occlusion or eyelid closure for ~5 minutes", "Instilling several drops in quick succession", "Warming the drop"], correct: 1, explanation: "Punctal (nasolacrimal) occlusion or lid closure keeps drug on the eye longer and limits nasal/systemic uptake." },
  { id: 66, question: "Blinking promotes drug loss from the eye because it:", options: ["Increases corneal permeability", "Activates the nasolacrimal pump, emptying the tear lake", "Dilutes the drug with aqueous", "Raises tear pH"], correct: 1, explanation: "Blinking drives the nasolacrimal pump, emptying the tear lake into the nose." },
  { id: 67, question: "Systemic toxicity from a topical drug absorbed via the nasal mucosa is classically illustrated by:", options: ["Timolol only", "Atropine", "Artificial tears", "Fluorescein"], correct: 1, explanation: "Atropine is the classic example of significant systemic effect from nasal-mucosal absorption of a topical drug." },
  { id: 68, question: "Ophthalmic ointment vehicles (petrolatum and mineral oil):", options: ["Are water-soluble gels", "Melt at body temperature and act as lipid solvents, giving slow release", "Dissolve immediately in tears", "Increase reflex tearing"], correct: 1, explanation: "They melt at body temperature; water-soluble drugs sit as microcrystals trapped until the ointment melts (slow release)." },
  { id: 69, question: "A recognized disadvantage of ophthalmic ointments is:", options: ["Stinging on instillation", "Dilution by tears", "Transient blurred vision and a greasy sensation", "Very short contact time"], correct: 2, explanation: "Ointments blur vision and feel greasy, though they increase contact time and cause less stinging than drops." },
  { id: 70, question: "A subconjunctival injection lowers a barrier that is:", options: ["Lipophilic", "Hydrophilic, and creates a reservoir releasing into the tear film", "Impermeable to all drugs", "The blood–brain barrier"], correct: 1, explanation: "The subconjunctival route has a hydrophilic barrier and forms a slow-release reservoir into the tear film." },
  { id: 71, question: "A hydrophilic drug injected into the sub-Tenon space, compared with topical application, can:", options: ["Never reach intraocular tissue", "Penetrate intraocular tissue more quickly", "Only reach the eyelid", "Cause immediate cataract"], correct: 1, explanation: "The sub-Tenon (lipophilic-barrier) route lets a hydrophilic drug penetrate intraocular tissue faster than topical dosing." },
  { id: 72, question: "The primary purpose of periocular injections is to:", options: ["Increase systemic drug levels", "Bypass the conjunctival/corneal epithelial barrier and deliver drug closer to its target", "Replace all topical therapy", "Lower intraocular pressure directly"], correct: 1, explanation: "Periocular injections bypass the epithelial barriers to deliver drug nearer the site of action." },
  { id: 73, question: "Standard aseptic prophylaxis before an intravitreal injection uses:", options: ["Topical antibiotic for 3 days", "5% povidone-iodine for 30–90 seconds", "Oral antibiotics", "Chlorhexidine irrigation of the retina"], correct: 1, explanation: "5% povidone-iodine applied for 30–90 seconds is the key evidence-based prophylaxis against endophthalmitis." },
  { id: 74, question: "For intraocular injection, medications should be:", options: ["Highly preserved for sterility", "Non-preserved, with controlled concentration to prevent toxicity", "Diluted in tap water", "Warmed to 45°C"], correct: 1, explanation: "Preservatives are toxic intraocularly, so non-preserved, concentration-controlled formulations are used." },
  { id: 75, question: "A recognized OCULAR adverse effect of intraocular injection is:", options: ["Hepatotoxicity", "Transient ocular hypertension and inflammation/infection", "Bronchospasm", "Nephrotoxicity"], correct: 1, explanation: "Intraocular injection risks transient IOP elevation and inflammation/infection (endophthalmitis); systemic effects are reduced." },
  { id: 76, question: "For a systemically administered drug, the fraction that crosses the blood–ocular barrier is the:", options: ["Protein-bound fraction", "Unbound (free) fraction", "Ionized fraction only", "Metabolized fraction"], correct: 1, explanation: "Only unbound (free) drug crosses the blood–ocular barrier; higher lipid solubility also increases penetration." },
  { id: 77, question: "Compared with a slow IV drip, a bolus IV injection tends to produce:", options: ["Lower intraocular drug levels", "Higher peak intraocular levels by exceeding plasma-protein binding capacity", "No intraocular drug", "Only systemic effects"], correct: 1, explanation: "A bolus can transiently saturate plasma-protein binding, raising the free fraction and peak intraocular levels." },
  { id: 78, question: "An inflamed eye typically shows ___ intraocular penetration of a systemic drug.", options: ["Decreased", "Increased (disrupted blood–ocular barrier)", "Unchanged", "Zero"], correct: 1, explanation: "Inflammation disrupts the blood–ocular barrier, increasing intraocular drug penetration." },
  { id: 79, question: "The dexamethasone intravitreal implant (Ozurdex) contains:", options: ["0.7 mg dexamethasone in a biodegradable polymer", "0.1 mg dexamethasone in a permanent reservoir", "7 mg dexamethasone as a suspension", "0.05 mg fluocinolone"], correct: 0, explanation: "Ozurdex is a 0.7-mg dexamethasone biodegradable polymer-matrix implant." },
  { id: 80, question: "Approved indications for the dexamethasone intravitreal implant include all of the following EXCEPT:", options: ["Macular edema from retinal vein occlusion", "Diabetic macular edema", "Noninfectious posterior uveitis", "Infectious endophthalmitis"], correct: 3, explanation: "It is approved for RVO macular edema, DME, and noninfectious posterior uveitis — not infectious endophthalmitis." },
  { id: 81, question: "Dexamethasone is released from the Ozurdex implant for up to:", options: ["1 week", "1 month", "6 months", "2 years"], correct: 2, explanation: "The polymer releases dexamethasone for up to 6 months, with clinical efficacy lasting at least 3 months." },
  { id: 82, question: "The most common adverse effect of intravenous fluorescein is:", options: ["Anaphylaxis", "Nausea (up to ~10%)", "Retinal toxicity", "Permanent skin staining"], correct: 1, explanation: "Nausea (up to 10%) is the most common adverse effect; serious hypersensitivity is rare." },
  { id: 83, question: "Indocyanine green (ICG) is especially useful for imaging:", options: ["The retinal nerve fiber layer", "The choroidal vasculature and poorly defined CNV in AMD/PCV", "The corneal endothelium", "The trabecular meshwork"], correct: 1, explanation: "ICG images the choroidal circulation and helps delineate occult/poorly defined CNV in AMD and PCV." },
  { id: 84, question: "Which FDA-approved vital dye is used to stain the internal limiting membrane?", options: ["Rose bengal", "Brilliant blue G (TissueBlue)", "Methylene blue", "Fluorescein"], correct: 1, explanation: "Brilliant blue G (BBG; TissueBlue 0.025%) is FDA-approved for ILM staining; trypan blue and ICG are also used." },
  { id: 85, question: "Trypan blue is an FDA-approved dye classically used to stain the:", options: ["Vitreous", "Anterior lens capsule", "Optic nerve", "Sclera"], correct: 1, explanation: "Trypan blue (FDA-approved) stains the anterior lens capsule (and is also used for the ILM)." },
  { id: 86, question: "The vital stain used to visualize residual vitreous during surgery is:", options: ["Triamcinolone acetonide", "Fluorescein", "Rose bengal", "Lissamine green"], correct: 0, explanation: "Triamcinolone acetonide (FDA-approved) is used to make residual vitreous visible during vitrectomy." },
  { id: 87, question: "Which is NOT a typical indication for intravitreal anti-VEGF therapy?", options: ["Wet AMD/PCV", "Diabetic macular edema", "Macular edema from retinal vein occlusion", "Primary open-angle glaucoma"], correct: 3, explanation: "Anti-VEGF is used for wet AMD/PCV, DME, RVO macular edema, PDR, and ROP — not open-angle glaucoma." },
  { id: 88, question: "A study testing whether variants in a single specific gene alter a patient's response to a drug falls within:", options: ["Pharmacokinetics", "Pharmacodynamics", "Pharmacogenetics", "Pharmacoeconomics"], correct: 2, explanation: "Pharmacogenetics examines how variation in a single gene affects drug response; pharmacogenomics is the broader, genome-wide counterpart." },
];

const BASIC_VITRETINA_RECALL: RecallQ[] = [
  { id: 1, question: "Summarize the basic anatomy of the vitreous (volume, water content, viscosity).", answer: "The vitreous occupies about four-fifths of the globe's volume (~4 mL), is a gel-like structure that is ~99% water, and has a viscosity about twice that of water owing to the mucopolysaccharide hyaluronic acid (hyaluronan). It is important for the metabolism of the lens, ciliary body, and retina." },
  { id: 2, question: "What are the ultrastructural components of the vitreous and what are hyalocytes?", answer: "Fine type II collagen fibrils and cells. The cells are hyalocytes — modified histiocytes (macrophage-like cells), glial cells, or fibroblasts of uncertain origin and function." },
  { id: 3, question: "Define the vitreous base and its attachment.", answer: "The vitreous base straddles the ora serrata, extending ~2 mm anterior and ~4 mm posterior to it. There the vitreous fibrils merge firmly with the basal lamina of the nonpigmented epithelium of the pars plana and the internal limiting membrane — the strongest vitreoretinal attachment." },
  { id: 4, question: "List the sites of vitreous attachment.", answer: "The vitreous base, the optic disc margin, the perimacular region, along the retinal vessels, and the periphery of the posterior lens capsule." },
  { id: 5, question: "What is Cloquet's canal and its embryologic remnants?", answer: "Cloquet's canal is the remnant of the regressed hyaloid vascular system, running from behind the lens (its termination is Mittendorf's dot, nasal to the posterior pole of the lens) to the margin of the optic nerve head. Remnants on the nerve head include vascular loops and Bergmeister's papilla." },
  { id: 6, question: "Describe the embryologic development of the primary, secondary, and tertiary vitreous.", answer: "Primary vitreous (weeks 4–5 to month 2): ectodermal fibrils, mostly mesodermal mesenchymal cells (with some neural crest), and the hyaloid vascular channels. Secondary vitreous: avascular, composed of type II collagen fibrils and hyalocytes. By month 3 the hyaloid system regresses and the primary vitreous retracts into Cloquet's canal; in months 3–4 secondary collagen condenses onto the ILM at the optic cup rim and extends to the lens equator as the tertiary vitreous, with the zonule developing anterior to it." },
  { id: 7, question: "What are the two basic functions of the vitreous?", answer: "To be a transparent gel occupying the major volume of the globe, and to serve as a route of nutrition to the lens." },
  { id: 8, question: "Give the composition of the vitreous.", answer: "About 98% water; ~0.15% macromolecules (collagen, hyaluronan, and soluble proteins); and solid matter (ions and low-molecular-weight solutes). It also contains hyaluronidase and at least one matrix metalloproteinase (MMP-2, or gelatinase)." },
  { id: 9, question: "Which collagen types make up the vitreous fibrils?", answer: "Type II (the major component of the fibrils), type IX (located on the fibril surface), and type V/XI (whose amino terminus projects from the fibril surface). Vitreous collagen is closely related to the collagen of hyaline cartilage." },
  { id: 10, question: "Describe the properties and distribution of hyaluronan in the vitreous.", answer: "Hyaluronan is a glycosaminoglycan that occupies an extremely large volume relative to its weight and is nontoxic, noninflammatory, and nonimmunogenic. Its concentration is highest near the retina and lowest behind the lens. It resists compression and confers viscoelastic properties, while stabilizing the collagen network." },
  { id: 11, question: "Contrast the mechanical roles of vitreous collagen and hyaluronan.", answer: "Collagen fibrils form the gel framework, supplying resistance to tensile forces and giving the vitreous plasticity (~286 µg/mL in humans). Hyaluronan resists compression and confers viscoelastic properties." },
  { id: 12, question: "What are the major soluble proteins of the vitreous and their origins?", answer: "Albumin is the major soluble vitreous protein (originating from plasma), followed by transferrin (originating from the ciliary body). Poorly defined glycoproteins make up ~20% (from surrounding tissues, not blood). Soluble protein is ~1.0 mg/mL and rises when the blood–retina barrier is compromised." },
  { id: 13, question: "Name two functionally notable vitreous proteins besides albumin/transferrin.", answer: "A neutrophil elastase inhibitor (which helps resist neovascularization) and tissue plasminogen activator (tPA), which has a fibrinolytic role in vitreous hemorrhage." },
  { id: 14, question: "How is the vitreous altered in high myopia?", answer: "In eyes with an axial length > 26 mm, collagen and hyaluronan concentrations are ~20–30% lower than in emmetropic eyes, suggesting a correlation between macromolecular composition and the physical (more liquefied) state of the vitreous." },
  { id: 15, question: "How does the vitreous act as an inhibitor of angiogenesis?", answer: "It contains angiogenesis inhibitors such as thrombospondin-1 and pigment epithelium–derived factor (PEDF), which inhibit angiogenesis in normal eyes. In proliferative diabetic retinopathy these inhibitors decrease while VEGF rises markedly, tipping the balance toward neovascularization." },
  { id: 16, question: "What physiologic changes follow pars plana vitrectomy (PPV)?", answer: "The vitreous cavity remains ~99% water but viscosity falls 300–2000-fold, so drugs/growth factors move between the anterior and posterior segments easily and clear quickly. Oxygen also moves more rapidly anterior-to-posterior, raising oxygen tension at the retina and at the posterior lens — the latter promoting cataract." },
  { id: 17, question: "What happens when hemorrhage enters the vitreous, and what glaucomas can result?", answer: "When blood penetrates the vitreous cortex, platelets contact vitreous collagen, aggregate, and initiate clot formation; the clot triggers a phagocytic inflammatory reaction and the vitreous liquefies. In severe cases, hemoglobin-laden macrophages can block the trabecular meshwork (secondary glaucoma), and rigid degenerated red cells cause ghost cell glaucoma. In already-liquefied vitreous (myopic, aphakic, senile eyes) the clot is loosely aggregated and resolves early." },
  { id: 18, question: "How do macular holes relate to the vitreous?", answer: "A macular hole can originate from traction by vitreous attachment at the fovea, with subsequent tangential tractional force along the internal limiting membrane causing the hole to enlarge." },
  { id: 19, question: "What is the genetic basis of Stickler (and Marshall) syndrome and its vitreous consequence?", answer: "Mutations in the alpha-1(II) and alpha-1(XI) collagen chains. The vitreous collapses prematurely, predisposing to retinal detachment." },
  { id: 20, question: "What is enzymatic vitreolysis, and which agent successfully induces PVD?", answer: "The use of enzymes to clear vitreous blood, perform noninvasive vitrectomy, or induce a posterior vitreous detachment (PVD) in young eyes. Proposed enzymes include hyaluronidase, plasmin, dispase, and chondroitinase. Ocriplasmin induces PVD (by cleaving fibronectin and laminin), whereas hyaluronidase and collagenase failed to induce PVD." },
  { id: 21, question: "List the 10 layers of the neurosensory retina from outer to inner (plus the RPE).", answer: "Outermost is the RPE, then the neurosensory layers: (1) photoreceptor layer, (2) external limiting membrane, (3) outer nuclear layer, (4) outer plexiform layer, (5) middle limiting membrane, (6) inner nuclear layer, (7) inner plexiform layer, (8) ganglion cell layer, (9) nerve fiber layer, and (10) internal limiting membrane." },
  { id: 22, question: "Describe the structure of the RPE and what forms the outer blood–retina barrier.", answer: "The RPE is a monolayer of hexagonal cells extending from the optic disc to the ora serrata, where it merges with the pigmented epithelium of the ciliary body. Its zonula occludens (tight junctions) and zonula adherens form the outer blood–retina barrier." },
  { id: 23, question: "List the major functions of the RPE.", answer: "Vitamin A metabolism; the outer blood–retina barrier; phagocytosis of shed photoreceptor outer segments; absorption of stray light (reducing scatter); heat exchange; forming the basal lamina of Bruch membrane; producing the mucopolysaccharide matrix around outer segments; and active transport of materials into and out of the RPE." },
  { id: 24, question: "How do RPE cells differ topographically between the fovea and the periphery?", answer: "At the fovea, RPE cells are taller and thinner with more and larger melanosomes; in the periphery they are shorter, broader, and less pigmented." },
  { id: 25, question: "What are the three element types of the neurosensory retina?", answer: "Neuronal elements, glial elements, and vascular elements." },
  { id: 26, question: "How many rods and cones are in the retina, and what do the connecting neurons do?", answer: "About 120 million rods and 6 million cones. Horizontal cells synapse between rods and cones and run horizontally in the outer plexiform layer; bipolar cells are oriented vertically and synapse between photoreceptors and ganglion/amacrine cells in the inner plexiform layer." },
  { id: 27, question: "What is the role of Müller cells, and which membranes do they form?", answer: "Müller cells are the principal glial cells, spanning from the external limiting membrane to the internal limiting membrane, providing structural support and nutrition. Their footplates form the internal limiting membrane, and junctional complexes with the photoreceptors form the external limiting membrane." },
  { id: 28, question: "Describe the retinal vascular supply and the inner blood–retina barrier.", answer: "The central retinal artery supplies the inner retina; a cilioretinal artery (from the ciliary circulation) supplies part of the macula in 18–32% of eyes. The retinal capillary endothelium forms the inner blood–retina barrier, and retinal capillaries do not extend deeper than the middle limiting membrane (inner nuclear layer)." },
  { id: 29, question: "What is the external limiting membrane, really?", answer: "Not a true membrane — it is a row of junctional (attachment) complexes between the photoreceptors and the Müller cells, and it is highly fenestrated." },
  { id: 30, question: "What is contained in the outer plexiform layer, and what is the Henle fiber layer?", answer: "The OPL contains the interconnections between photoreceptors and horizontal/bipolar cells. In the macula, the obliquely oriented photoreceptor axons form the Henle fiber layer." },
  { id: 31, question: "What cell bodies/processes occupy the inner nuclear, inner plexiform, and ganglion cell layers?", answer: "Inner nuclear layer: nuclei of bipolar, Müller, horizontal, and amacrine cells. Inner plexiform layer: axons of bipolar and amacrine cells and dendrites of ganglion cells. Ganglion cell layer: the ganglion cell bodies." },
  { id: 32, question: "What is the internal limiting membrane?", answer: "Not a true membrane — it is formed by the footplates of the Müller cells and forms the innermost boundary of the retina, apposed to the vitreous." },
  { id: 33, question: "Give the clinical and histologic definitions of the macula.", answer: "Clinically, the macula is the area within the temporal vascular arcades. Histologically, it is the region with two or more (≥1 by some definitions) layers of ganglion cell nuclei." },
  { id: 34, question: "What gives the macula lutea its color, and how are the pigments distributed?", answer: "The carotenoid xanthophyll pigments lutein and zeaxanthin, concentrated chiefly in the Henle fiber layer. Centrally (0.25 mm) the lutein:zeaxanthin ratio is ~1:2.4, whereas in the periphery (2.2–8.7 mm) it is > 2:1 — corresponding to the rod:cone distribution (lutein predominates in rod-dense areas, zeaxanthin in cone-dense areas)." },
  { id: 35, question: "Contrast the fovea and foveola histologically.", answer: "The fovea (~1.5 mm) has no nerve fiber layer, ganglion cell layer, or inner plexiform layer centrally, with xanthophyll pigment in the inner retina; it provides high acuity and color vision. The foveola (~0.35 mm) contains only cones (with Müller/glial cells) and has no xanthophyll pigment. Foveal cones synapse 1:1 with bipolar and ganglion cells, maximizing acuity." },
  { id: 36, question: "Which retinal layers are thickest at the parafovea, and why do bipolar/ganglion cells form Henle's layer?", answer: "The ganglion cell layer, inner nuclear layer, and outer plexiform layer are thickest around the fovea. Because foveal cones are densely packed and synapse 1:1, the bipolar and ganglion cells are displaced laterally away from the photoreceptors, and the elongated obliquely running interconnecting fibers form Henle's fiber layer." },
  { id: 37, question: "How is the blood supply of the retina divided between the retinal and choroidal circulations?", answer: "The inner two-thirds of the retina is supplied by the retinal vasculature (ophthalmic artery → central retinal artery); the outer one-third plus the RPE is supplied by the choriocapillaris of the choroid (which handles most of the metabolic demand). This is why the outer retina becomes ischemic in a retinal detachment." },
  { id: 38, question: "Trace the neuronal visual pathway from light to cortex.", answer: "Light is converted to an electrical signal in the photoreceptor cell → bipolar cell → ganglion cell → lateral geniculate body → visual cortex in the occipital lobe." },
  { id: 39, question: "Give the distances from the limbus to the ora serrata by quadrant.", answer: "Nasal ~5.7 mm, superior ~6.1 mm, inferior ~6.2 mm, and temporal ~6.5 mm. The distance also varies with refractive error (myopic > emmetropic > hyperopic)." },
  { id: 40, question: "Which extraocular muscle's scleral insertion corresponds to the location of the macula?", answer: "The inferior oblique muscle — its insertion on the sclera overlies the region of the macula, a useful surgical landmark." },
];

const BASIC_VITRETINA_MCQ: MCQ[] = [
  { id: 1, question: "The vitreous occupies approximately what fraction of the globe's volume?", options: ["One-third", "One-half", "Four-fifths", "One-tenth"], correct: 2, explanation: "The vitreous occupies about four-fifths of the globe (~4 mL)." },
  { id: 2, question: "The vitreous viscosity is about twice that of water because of:", options: ["Type II collagen", "Hyaluronic acid (hyaluronan)", "Albumin", "Sodium chloride"], correct: 1, explanation: "The mucopolysaccharide hyaluronan gives the vitreous ~2× the viscosity of water." },
  { id: 3, question: "The cells of the vitreous, of uncertain origin, are called:", options: ["Hyalocytes", "Keratocytes", "Müller cells", "Pericytes"], correct: 0, explanation: "Hyalocytes (modified histiocytes/glial cells/fibroblasts) are the vitreous cells." },
  { id: 4, question: "The vitreous base extends approximately:", options: ["2 mm anterior and 4 mm posterior to the ora serrata", "4 mm anterior and 2 mm posterior to the ora serrata", "Entirely anterior to the ora serrata", "Only over the optic disc"], correct: 0, explanation: "The vitreous base straddles the ora serrata (~2 mm anterior, ~4 mm posterior) and is the strongest vitreoretinal attachment." },
  { id: 5, question: "Which is NOT a site of firm vitreous attachment?", options: ["Optic disc margin", "Perimacular region", "Central cornea", "Periphery of the posterior lens capsule"], correct: 2, explanation: "Attachment sites are the vitreous base, disc margin, perimacular region, along retinal vessels, and posterior lens capsule periphery — not the cornea." },
  { id: 6, question: "Cloquet's canal is the remnant of the:", options: ["Regressed hyaloid vascular system", "Primary lens fibers", "Bergmeister's papilla only", "Zonular apparatus"], correct: 0, explanation: "Cloquet's canal is the remnant of the regressed hyaloid vasculature, running from behind the lens to the optic nerve head." },
  { id: 7, question: "Mittendorf's dot marks the anterior termination of the hyaloid remnant and lies:", options: ["Temporal to the posterior lens pole", "Nasal to the posterior pole of the lens", "At the fovea", "On the optic disc"], correct: 1, explanation: "Mittendorf's dot is nasal to the posterior pole of the lens (the anterior end of Cloquet's canal)." },
  { id: 8, question: "The major collagen type forming the vitreous fibrils is:", options: ["Type I", "Type II", "Type IV", "Type VII"], correct: 1, explanation: "Type II collagen is the major fibril component; type IX is on the surface and type V/XI projects its amino terminus." },
  { id: 9, question: "Vitreous collagen is most closely related to the collagen of:", options: ["Dermis", "Cornea", "Hyaline cartilage", "Sclera"], correct: 2, explanation: "Vitreous collagen is closely related to that of hyaline cartilage, differing from types I/III/XII/XIV of dermis/cornea/sclera." },
  { id: 10, question: "Hyaluronan concentration in the vitreous is:", options: ["Highest behind the lens, lowest near the retina", "Highest near the retina, lowest behind the lens", "Uniform throughout", "Absent in adults"], correct: 1, explanation: "Hyaluronan is most concentrated near the retina and least concentrated behind the lens." },
  { id: 11, question: "Within the vitreous gel, which component chiefly resists COMPRESSION and confers viscoelasticity?", options: ["Type II collagen fibrils", "Hyaluronan", "Albumin", "Fibronectin"], correct: 1, explanation: "Hyaluronan resists compression and confers viscoelastic properties; collagen fibrils resist tensile forces." },
  { id: 12, question: "The major soluble protein of the vitreous is:", options: ["Transferrin", "Albumin", "Fibrinogen", "Immunoglobulin G"], correct: 1, explanation: "Albumin (from plasma) is the major soluble vitreous protein, followed by transferrin (from the ciliary body)." },
  { id: 13, question: "Vitreous transferrin originates from the:", options: ["Plasma", "Ciliary body", "Retina", "Cornea"], correct: 1, explanation: "Vitreous transferrin originates from the ciliary body, whereas albumin comes from plasma." },
  { id: 14, question: "In eyes with an axial length > 26 mm, vitreous collagen and hyaluronan concentrations are:", options: ["20–30% higher than emmetropic eyes", "20–30% lower than emmetropic eyes", "Unchanged", "Completely absent"], correct: 1, explanation: "High myopia (AXL > 26 mm) is associated with ~20–30% lower collagen and hyaluronan (more liquefied vitreous)." },
  { id: 15, question: "Which pair are endogenous angiogenesis INHIBITORS present in the vitreous?", options: ["VEGF and IGF-1", "Thrombospondin-1 and PEDF", "TGF-β and bFGF", "Albumin and transferrin"], correct: 1, explanation: "Thrombospondin-1 and pigment epithelium–derived factor (PEDF) inhibit angiogenesis; they fall in PDR as VEGF rises." },
  { id: 16, question: "After pars plana vitrectomy, oxygen moves more freely to the posterior lens, promoting:", options: ["Glaucoma", "Cataract", "Retinal detachment", "Macular hole"], correct: 1, explanation: "Higher oxygen tension at the posterior lens after PPV promotes (nuclear) cataract; viscosity also falls 300–2000×." },
  { id: 17, question: "Rigid, degenerated red blood cells in the vitreous can obstruct the trabecular meshwork and cause:", options: ["Ghost cell glaucoma", "Neovascular glaucoma", "Angle-closure from a plateau iris", "Pigmentary glaucoma"], correct: 0, explanation: "Degenerated (ghost) red cells cause ghost cell glaucoma; hemoglobin-laden macrophages cause hemolytic (secondary) glaucoma." },
  { id: 18, question: "Premature vitreous collapse leading to retinal detachment, from alpha-1(II)/alpha-1(XI) collagen mutations, characterizes:", options: ["Marfan syndrome", "Stickler syndrome", "Homocystinuria", "Ehlers-Danlos syndrome"], correct: 1, explanation: "Stickler (and Marshall) syndrome results from type II/XI collagen mutations with premature vitreous collapse and RD risk." },
  { id: 19, question: "Which agent successfully induces a posterior vitreous detachment via enzymatic vitreolysis?", options: ["Hyaluronidase", "Collagenase", "Ocriplasmin (cleaves fibronectin and laminin)", "Chondroitinase alone"], correct: 2, explanation: "Ocriplasmin induces PVD by cleaving fibronectin and laminin; hyaluronidase and collagenase failed to induce PVD." },
  { id: 20, question: "The 10 layers of the neurosensory retina are bounded internally by the ___ and externally by the ___.", options: ["Nerve fiber layer; ganglion cell layer", "Internal limiting membrane; photoreceptor layer", "RPE; choroid", "Outer plexiform layer; inner nuclear layer"], correct: 1, explanation: "The neurosensory retina runs from the internal limiting membrane (inner) to the photoreceptor layer (outer); the RPE lies external to it." },
  { id: 21, question: "The outer blood–retina barrier is formed by:", options: ["Retinal capillary endothelial tight junctions", "RPE zonula occludens (tight junctions) and zonula adherens", "Müller cell footplates", "Bruch membrane elastic layer"], correct: 1, explanation: "The RPE tight junctions (zonula occludens) and zonula adherens form the outer blood–retina barrier." },
  { id: 22, question: "Which is NOT a function of the RPE?", options: ["Phagocytosis of photoreceptor outer segments", "Vitamin A metabolism", "Generation of action potentials for vision", "Formation of the basal lamina of Bruch membrane"], correct: 2, explanation: "The RPE handles vitamin A metabolism, outer-segment phagocytosis, light absorption, and Bruch's basal lamina — not phototransduction/action potentials." },
  { id: 23, question: "At the fovea, compared with the periphery, RPE cells are:", options: ["Shorter, broader, and less pigmented", "Taller, thinner, with more and larger melanosomes", "Absent", "Multilayered"], correct: 1, explanation: "Foveal RPE cells are taller/thinner with more, larger melanosomes; peripheral RPE cells are shorter/broader/less pigmented." },
  { id: 24, question: "The retina contains approximately:", options: ["6 million rods and 120 million cones", "120 million rods and 6 million cones", "Equal numbers of rods and cones", "60 million rods and 60 million cones"], correct: 1, explanation: "There are ~120 million rods and ~6 million cones." },
  { id: 25, question: "Horizontal cells synapse between rods and cones and are oriented horizontally within the:", options: ["Inner plexiform layer", "Outer plexiform layer", "Ganglion cell layer", "Nerve fiber layer"], correct: 1, explanation: "Horizontal cells run horizontally in the outer plexiform layer, connecting photoreceptors." },
  { id: 26, question: "Bipolar cells synapse with photoreceptors and with ganglion/amacrine cells in the:", options: ["Outer plexiform layer only", "Inner plexiform layer", "Outer nuclear layer", "External limiting membrane"], correct: 1, explanation: "Vertically oriented bipolar cells connect photoreceptors to ganglion and amacrine cells in the inner plexiform layer." },
  { id: 27, question: "The principal glial cell of the retina, spanning the ELM to the ILM, is the:", options: ["Astrocyte", "Microglia", "Müller cell", "Oligodendrocyte"], correct: 2, explanation: "Müller cells span from the ELM to the ILM, providing structural support and nutrition." },
  { id: 28, question: "The internal limiting membrane is formed by the:", options: ["Footplates of Müller cells", "RPE basal lamina", "Photoreceptor outer segments", "Ganglion cell axons"], correct: 0, explanation: "The ILM (not a true membrane) is formed by Müller cell footplates." },
  { id: 29, question: "The external limiting membrane is best described as:", options: ["A true basement membrane", "A row of junctional complexes between photoreceptors and Müller cells", "The RPE tight junctions", "A layer of collagen"], correct: 1, explanation: "The ELM is not a true membrane but a highly fenestrated row of attachment complexes between photoreceptors and Müller cells." },
  { id: 30, question: "A cilioretinal artery (from the ciliary circulation) supplies part of the macula in approximately:", options: ["1–2% of eyes", "18–32% of eyes", "50% of eyes", "90% of eyes"], correct: 1, explanation: "A cilioretinal artery is present in about 18–32% of eyes and can spare central vision in CRAO." },
  { id: 31, question: "The inner blood–retina barrier is formed by:", options: ["RPE tight junctions", "Retinal capillary endothelial tight junctions", "Bruch membrane", "The choriocapillaris"], correct: 1, explanation: "The retinal vascular (capillary) endothelium with its tight junctions forms the inner blood–retina barrier." },
  { id: 32, question: "Retinal capillaries normally do not extend deeper than the:", options: ["Photoreceptor layer", "Middle limiting membrane (inner nuclear layer)", "RPE", "Choroid"], correct: 1, explanation: "The retinal capillary network does not extend deeper than the middle limiting membrane (the inner nuclear layer level)." },
  { id: 33, question: "The Henle fiber layer of the macula is a specialized part of the:", options: ["Nerve fiber layer", "Outer plexiform layer", "Inner nuclear layer", "Ganglion cell layer"], correct: 1, explanation: "The obliquely oriented photoreceptor axons of the macular outer plexiform layer form the Henle fiber layer." },
  { id: 34, question: "The macula is defined CLINICALLY as:", options: ["The region with ≥1 layer of ganglion cell nuclei", "The area within the temporal vascular arcades", "The optic disc and surrounding retina", "The foveal avascular zone only"], correct: 1, explanation: "Clinically the macula is the area within the temporal vascular arcades; histologically it is the region with multiple ganglion cell layers." },
  { id: 35, question: "The xanthophyll pigments of the macula lutea are:", options: ["Melanin and lipofuscin", "Lutein and zeaxanthin", "Rhodopsin and iodopsin", "Hemoglobin and bilirubin"], correct: 1, explanation: "Lutein and zeaxanthin (carotenoids) concentrate chiefly in the Henle fiber layer, giving the macula its yellow color." },
  { id: 36, question: "In the central macula (~0.25 mm), the lutein:zeaxanthin ratio is about:", options: ["1:2.4 (zeaxanthin predominant)", "2:1 (lutein predominant)", "1:1", "10:1"], correct: 0, explanation: "Centrally L:Z ≈ 1:2.4 (zeaxanthin predominant, cone-dense area); peripherally L:Z > 2:1 (lutein predominant, rod-dense)." },
  { id: 37, question: "The foveola is characterized by:", options: ["Only cones and no xanthophyll pigment", "A thick nerve fiber layer", "Only rods", "Abundant ganglion cells"], correct: 0, explanation: "The foveola (~0.35 mm) contains only cones (plus Müller/glial cells) and lacks xanthophyll pigment." },
  { id: 38, question: "High foveal visual acuity is explained partly by foveal cones synapsing with bipolar and ganglion cells in a ratio of:", options: ["1:1", "10:1", "100:1", "1000:1"], correct: 0, explanation: "Foveal cones synapse ~1:1 with bipolar and ganglion cells, maximizing spatial resolution." },
  { id: 39, question: "Which layers are ABSENT centrally in the fovea?", options: ["Photoreceptor and outer nuclear layers", "Nerve fiber, ganglion cell, and inner plexiform layers", "RPE and Bruch membrane", "External limiting membrane"], correct: 1, explanation: "The central fovea lacks the NFL, GCL, and IPL, which are displaced peripherally to allow light direct access to the cones." },
  { id: 40, question: "The outer one-third of the retina and the RPE derive their blood supply from the:", options: ["Central retinal artery", "Choriocapillaris of the choroid", "Cilioretinal artery", "Vortex veins"], correct: 1, explanation: "The choriocapillaris supplies the outer third of the retina and the RPE (handling most metabolic demand); the inner two-thirds is supplied by the central retinal artery." },
  { id: 41, question: "Why does the outer retina become ischemic in a retinal detachment?", options: ["The central retinal artery is occluded", "It is separated from its choroidal (choriocapillaris) blood supply", "The vitreous compresses it", "The RPE overproliferates"], correct: 1, explanation: "Detachment separates the outer retina from the choriocapillaris, its source of oxygen/metabolites, causing ischemia." },
  { id: 42, question: "The correct sequence of the neuronal visual pathway is:", options: ["Photoreceptor → ganglion cell → bipolar cell → LGB → cortex", "Photoreceptor → bipolar cell → ganglion cell → lateral geniculate body → occipital visual cortex", "Bipolar cell → photoreceptor → ganglion cell → cortex", "Ganglion cell → bipolar cell → photoreceptor → LGB"], correct: 1, explanation: "Light → photoreceptor → bipolar cell → ganglion cell → lateral geniculate body → occipital (visual) cortex." },
  { id: 43, question: "The distance from the limbus to the ora serrata is SHORTEST in which quadrant?", options: ["Temporal", "Nasal", "Superior", "Inferior"], correct: 1, explanation: "It is shortest nasally (~5.7 mm) and longest temporally (~6.5 mm)." },
  { id: 44, question: "The distance from the limbus to the ora serrata is LONGEST in which quadrant?", options: ["Nasal", "Superior", "Inferior", "Temporal"], correct: 3, explanation: "It is longest temporally (~6.5 mm); nasal ~5.7, superior ~6.1, inferior ~6.2 mm." },
  { id: 45, question: "The scleral insertion of which extraocular muscle marks the location of the macula?", options: ["Superior oblique", "Inferior oblique", "Medial rectus", "Superior rectus"], correct: 1, explanation: "The inferior oblique insertion on the sclera corresponds to the macula — a surgical landmark." },
  { id: 46, question: "The primary vitreous contains all of the following EXCEPT:", options: ["Hyaloid vascular channels", "Mesenchymal cells (mostly mesodermal)", "Ectodermal fibrils", "Type II collagen with hyalocytes (avascular)"], correct: 3, explanation: "Avascular type II collagen with hyalocytes describes the SECONDARY vitreous; the primary vitreous is vascular (hyaloid system)." },
  { id: 47, question: "The secondary vitreous is characterized by:", options: ["Hyaloid vessels", "Being avascular, with type II collagen fibrils and hyalocytes", "Zonular fibers", "Mesenchymal vascular channels"], correct: 1, explanation: "The secondary vitreous is avascular, composed of type II collagen and hyalocytes." },
  { id: 48, question: "The tertiary vitreous gives rise to the:", options: ["Cloquet's canal", "Zonular fibers of the lens", "Bruch membrane", "Bergmeister's papilla"], correct: 1, explanation: "Condensed secondary/tertiary vitreous collagen at the lens equator forms the zonule." },
  { id: 49, question: "One of the two basic functions of the vitreous is to serve as a:", options: ["Site of aqueous production", "Route of nutrition to the lens", "Source of melanin", "Barrier to light"], correct: 1, explanation: "The vitreous is a transparent structural gel and a route of nutrition to the lens." },
  { id: 50, question: "The matrix metalloproteinase found in the vitreous is:", options: ["MMP-9 (collagenase)", "MMP-2 (gelatinase)", "MMP-1", "MMP-3 (stromelysin)"], correct: 1, explanation: "The vitreous contains hyaluronidase and at least one MMP — MMP-2 (gelatinase)." },
  { id: 51, question: "Which vitreous protein has a fibrinolytic role in vitreous hemorrhage?", options: ["Neutrophil elastase inhibitor", "Tissue plasminogen activator (tPA)", "Albumin", "Transferrin"], correct: 1, explanation: "tPA provides fibrinolytic activity in vitreous hemorrhage; the neutrophil elastase inhibitor helps resist neovascularization." },
  { id: 52, question: "The RPE extends from the optic disc to the ora serrata, where it merges with the:", options: ["Neurosensory retina", "Pigmented epithelium of the ciliary body", "Iris stroma", "Choroid"], correct: 1, explanation: "At the ora serrata the RPE becomes continuous with the pigmented (outer) epithelium of the ciliary body." },
  { id: 53, question: "The inner nuclear layer contains the nuclei of all of the following EXCEPT:", options: ["Bipolar cells", "Horizontal cells", "Amacrine cells", "Ganglion cells"], correct: 3, explanation: "The INL holds bipolar, Müller, horizontal, and amacrine cell nuclei; ganglion cell bodies are in the ganglion cell layer." },
  { id: 54, question: "In the parafoveal region, which layers are thickest?", options: ["Photoreceptor and RPE", "Ganglion cell, inner nuclear, and outer plexiform layers", "Nerve fiber layer alone", "External limiting membrane"], correct: 1, explanation: "The GCL, INL, and OPL are thickest around the fovea, where neurons are displaced laterally (forming Henle's layer)." },
  { id: 55, question: "The macula lutea's yellow pigment is concentrated chiefly in which layer?", options: ["Nerve fiber layer", "Henle fiber layer (outer plexiform)", "RPE", "Ganglion cell layer"], correct: 1, explanation: "Lutein and zeaxanthin are concentrated chiefly in the Henle fiber layer of the macula." },
  { id: 56, question: "The inner two-thirds of the retina is supplied by the:", options: ["Choriocapillaris", "Central retinal artery (from the ophthalmic artery)", "Posterior ciliary arteries", "Vortex system"], correct: 1, explanation: "The inner two-thirds of the retina is supplied by the central retinal artery; the outer third and RPE by the choriocapillaris." },
];

// Vitreous/Retina lecture Part 3, merged into Basic Science Part 7.
// ids continue from BASIC_VITRETINA (recall from 41, MCQ from 57).
const BASIC_VR3_RECALL: RecallQ[] = [
  { id: 41, question: "What is the major structural protein of the zonular fibers?", answer: "Fibrillin — a large, linear protein with a high cysteine content. Most zonular fibers form the zonular apparatus connecting the lens to the ciliary body." },
  { id: 42, question: "Describe the lipid content and major fatty acids of the vitreous.", answer: "Lipids are about 7% by weight of centrifuged vitreous. The major fatty acids in humans are palmitate (~25%), oleate (~23%), stearate (~18%), and arachidonate (~17%)." },
  { id: 43, question: "Where do the ions and low-molecular-weight solutes of the vitreous originate?", answer: "From the adjacent ocular tissues and the blood plasma." },
  { id: 44, question: "What three barriers control the composition of the vitreous?", answer: "The vascular endothelium of the retinal vessels, the retinal pigment epithelium (RPE), and the inner (nonpigmented) layer of the ciliary epithelium." },
  { id: 45, question: "How does vitreous liquefaction change with age and with myopia?", answer: "Liquefaction is progressive with increasing age; in myopia it is rapidly progressive, leading to early posterior vitreous detachment (PVD)." },
  { id: 46, question: "What is the structural basis of vitreous liquefaction (fibrillar opacities)?", answer: "Aggregation of the thin vitreous fibrils into thick fibers called fibrillar opacities. The fibrils are condensed in the gel phase and absent/low in the liquid phase, whereas hyaluronan concentration shows no difference between the gel and liquid phases; the process weakens the adhesion between the cortical vitreous gel and the ILM." },
  { id: 47, question: "Describe the mechanism of a posterior vitreous detachment (PVD).", answer: "Liquefied vitreous from the center passes through a hole in the posterior vitreous cortex and dissects the residual gel away from the internal limiting membrane, extending anteriorly as far as the posterior border of the vitreous base." },
  { id: 48, question: "What complication can occur as residual gel collapses during a PVD, and what protective effect can a PVD have?", answer: "As the residual gel collapses anteriorly, retinal tears may occur (from vitreoretinal traction). A complete PVD can protect against proliferative diabetic retinopathy by denying a scaffold for fibrovascular proliferation emanating from the retina." },
  { id: 49, question: "Why is internal limiting membrane peeling now a goal after a surgically induced PVD?", answer: "After a PVD is created during pars plana vitrectomy, some collagen fibrils remain adherent to the ILM; removing the ILM is now a goal to eliminate this residual scaffold." },
  { id: 50, question: "Why does high myopia predispose to earlier PVD and retinal breaks?", answer: "The myopic vitreous undergoes rapidly progressive liquefaction with weakening of the cortical gel–ILM adhesion, so a PVD occurs earlier and the anterior collapse of residual gel can create retinal tears." },
];

const BASIC_VR3_MCQ: MCQ[] = [
  { id: 57, question: "The major structural protein of the zonular fibers is:", options: ["Type II collagen", "Fibrillin (high cysteine content)", "Elastin", "Laminin"], correct: 1, explanation: "Zonular fibers are built mainly of fibrillin, a large linear protein rich in cysteine." },
  { id: 58, question: "Lipids constitute approximately what percentage of centrifuged vitreous by weight?", options: ["0.1%", "7%", "25%", "50%"], correct: 1, explanation: "Lipids are ~7% w/w of centrifuged vitreous; palmitate (~25%) is the most abundant fatty acid." },
  { id: 59, question: "Which structure is NOT one of the barriers controlling vitreous composition?", options: ["Retinal vascular endothelium", "Retinal pigment epithelium", "Inner (nonpigmented) ciliary epithelium", "Corneal endothelium"], correct: 3, explanation: "The barriers are the retinal vascular endothelium, RPE, and inner ciliary epithelium — not the corneal endothelium." },
  { id: 60, question: "Vitreous liquefaction is accelerated (leading to early PVD) by:", options: ["Hypermetropia", "Myopia", "Emmetropia", "Reduced axial length"], correct: 1, explanation: "Myopia causes rapidly progressive liquefaction and early posterior vitreous detachment." },
  { id: 61, question: "'Fibrillar opacities' in vitreous liquefaction represent:", options: ["Aggregation of thin fibrils into thick fibers", "New hyaluronan synthesis", "Calcific deposits", "Hemorrhage"], correct: 0, explanation: "Liquefaction aggregates thin fibrils into thick fibers (fibrillar opacities), while hyaluronan concentration is unchanged between phases." },
  { id: 62, question: "During liquefaction, the hyaluronan concentration in the gel versus liquid phase:", options: ["Is much higher in the gel", "Shows no difference between phases", "Is absent in the liquid phase", "Doubles in the liquid phase"], correct: 1, explanation: "Hyaluronan shows no concentration difference between gel and liquid phases; it is the collagen fibrils that condense in the gel phase." },
  { id: 63, question: "A posterior vitreous detachment begins when liquefied vitreous:", options: ["Is absorbed by the RPE", "Passes through a hole in the posterior vitreous cortex and dissects gel off the ILM", "Calcifies at the vitreous base", "Enters the anterior chamber"], correct: 1, explanation: "Liquefied central vitreous passes through a dehiscence in the posterior cortex and separates residual gel from the ILM." },
  { id: 64, question: "In a PVD, the residual gel is dissected anteriorly as far as the:", options: ["Ora serrata only", "Posterior border of the vitreous base", "Lens equator", "Optic disc"], correct: 1, explanation: "The dissection extends anteriorly to the posterior border of the vitreous base." },
  { id: 65, question: "As residual gel collapses anteriorly during a PVD, the feared complication is:", options: ["Cataract", "Retinal tears", "Glaucoma", "Corneal edema"], correct: 1, explanation: "Anterior collapse produces vitreoretinal traction that can cause retinal tears." },
  { id: 66, question: "A complete PVD can be protective in proliferative diabetic retinopathy because it:", options: ["Increases VEGF", "Denies a scaffold for fibrovascular proliferation", "Raises intraocular pressure", "Thickens the ILM"], correct: 1, explanation: "Without the posterior cortical vitreous, there is no scaffold for fibrovascular proliferation to grow along." },
  { id: 67, question: "After a surgically induced PVD during vitrectomy, ILM peeling is done because:", options: ["The ILM is toxic", "Residual collagen fibrils remain adherent to the ILM", "It lowers IOP", "It prevents cataract"], correct: 1, explanation: "Some collagen remains on the ILM after surgical PVD, so ILM removal eliminates this residual scaffold." },
  { id: 68, question: "The ions and low-molecular-weight solutes of the vitreous originate from:", options: ["Synthesis by hyalocytes only", "Adjacent ocular tissues and blood plasma", "The cornea", "The lens capsule"], correct: 1, explanation: "These solutes come from adjacent ocular tissues and the plasma." },
  { id: 69, question: "The most abundant fatty acid of the human vitreous is:", options: ["Palmitate (~25%)", "Stearate (~18%)", "Oleate (~23%)", "Arachidonate (~17%)"], correct: 0, explanation: "Palmitate (~25%) is the most abundant, followed by oleate (~23%), stearate (~18%), and arachidonate (~17%)." },
  { id: 70, question: "Vitreous liquefaction structurally involves weakening of the adhesion between the:", options: ["RPE and Bruch membrane", "Cortical vitreous gel and the internal limiting membrane", "Lens and zonule", "Sclera and choroid"], correct: 1, explanation: "Liquefaction weakens the adhesion between the cortical vitreous gel and the ILM, permitting PVD." },
];

// 'Fundamentals of Cornea' lecture, merged into Basic Science Part 3
// (Cornea Essentials). ids continue from BASIC_ANATOMY (recall from 79, MCQ from 93).
const BASIC_CORNEA2_RECALL: RecallQ[] = [
  { id: 79, question: "What is the refractive index of the cornea, and roughly how much of the eye's refractive power does it provide?", answer: "The corneal refractive index is 1.376 (the value 1.3375 is used for keratometer calibration). The cornea provides about 74% of the eye's total refractive power — about +43.25 D of the total ~58.6 D." },
  { id: 80, question: "Break down the corneal refractive power by interface.", answer: "Air–tear film +43.6 D; tear–cornea +5.3 D; cornea–aqueous −5.8 D — summing to a net corneal power of about +43.25 D. Most refraction occurs at the air–tear interface." },
  { id: 81, question: "Give the key corneal dimensions (thickness, size, surface area, optical zone).", answer: "Thickness ~0.52 mm centrally and ~0.67 mm peripherally; horizontal diameter 11–12 mm, vertical 10–11 mm; surface area ~1.3 cm² (about one-sixth of the globe); the central optical zone is ~5.4 mm." },
  { id: 82, question: "What are the corneal radii of curvature, and what is meant by its asphericity?", answer: "Anterior surface radius ~7.8 mm, posterior ~6.5 mm. The cornea is aspheric, being steepest centrally (apical zone) and showing the greatest flattening and asphericity in the peripheral (transitional) zone." },
  { id: 83, question: "Give the composition of the human cornea by weight.", answer: "Water ~78%, collagen ~15%, other protein ~5%, salts ~1%, and glycosaminoglycans — keratan sulfate ~0.7%, chondroitin/dermatan sulfate ~0.3%, plus hyaluronic acid." },
  { id: 84, question: "Which collagen types and proteoglycans predominate in the corneal stroma?", answer: "Stromal collagen is types I, V, and VI (type I predominates overall). The proteoglycans are decorin (a dermatan sulfate proteoglycan) and lumican (a keratan sulfate proteoglycan). Descemet membrane, by contrast, is rich in type IV collagen." },
  { id: 85, question: "Describe corneal innervation.", answer: "The cornea has one of the body's highest densities of nerve endings — corneal sensation is about 100 times that of the conjunctiva. It is supplied by the long posterior ciliary nerves (70–80 branches, from the ophthalmic division of CN V) that lose their myelin sheath 1–2 mm from the limbus, forming stromal, subepithelial, and intraepithelial plexuses." },
  { id: 86, question: "What is the corneal blood supply?", answer: "The cornea is avascular. Small vascular loops from the anterior ciliary vessels invade only the peripheral ~1 mm, and these loops actually lie in the subconjunctival tissue overlapping the cornea, not within the cornea itself." },
  { id: 87, question: "Outline the cornea-specific developmental timeline.", answer: "Week 5: surface ectoderm forms the lens placode and then the corneal/conjunctival epithelium. Week 6: neural crest (1st wave) forms the corneal endothelium. Week 7: neural crest forms the corneal stroma and sclera. Month 3: all corneal components are present except Bowman layer. At birth the infant globe is ~80% of adult size." },
  { id: 88, question: "Give the embryonic origin of each corneal layer.", answer: "Corneal epithelium — surface ectoderm; Bowman layer — mesenchyme (condensed anterior stroma); stroma — mesenchyme and neural crest; Descemet membrane — synthesized by the endothelium; endothelium — neural crest." },
  { id: 89, question: "Name the five layers of the cornea (behind the tear film) and their chief roles.", answer: "(1) Corneal epithelium (barrier); (2) Bowman layer; (3) stroma (transparency and bulk); (4) Descemet membrane; (5) endothelium (barrier and metabolic pump)." },
  { id: 90, question: "Describe the corneal epithelium's general properties.", answer: "Stratified, squamous, non-keratinized epithelium continuous with the conjunctival epithelium at the limbus but lacking goblet cells; 5–6 cell layers; about 5% (~50 µm) of corneal thickness; turnover every 7–14 days; tight junctions between superficial cells prevent tear penetration into the stroma." },
  { id: 91, question: "What are the three cell types of the corneal epithelium?", answer: "Basal cells (deepest, columnar, the germinative layer resting on the basal lamina), wing/umbrella cells (1–2 polyhedral layers capping the basal cells), and surface cells (2–3 flattened polyhedral/hexagonal layers with surface microvilli)." },
  { id: 92, question: "How is epithelial adhesion achieved, and what is the basal lamina made of?", answer: "Surface cells are joined by tight junctions and desmosomes; wing/superficial cells by desmosomes; basal cells by desmosomes and hemidesmosomes to the basal lamina. The basal lamina (0.5–1 µm; lamina lucida + lamina densa) is secreted by basal cells and consists of type IV collagen and laminin; it thickens with age, diabetes, and some corneal disorders." },
  { id: 93, question: "Outline corneal epithelial wound healing after an abrasion.", answer: "Cells at the wound edge retract, thicken, and lose attachments, then migrate by amoeboid movement (sending out filopodia/lamellipodia) to cover the defect; migration halts by contact inhibition and the cells anchor; mitosis then resumes to restore thickness, tight junctions re-form, and adhesion to Bowman layer is re-established within ~7 days if the basal lamina is intact. The regenerative source is the limbal stem cells (palisades of Vogt)." },
  { id: 94, question: "Describe Bowman layer.", answer: "A modified, acellular, homogeneous zone of anterior stroma, 8–14 µm thick, with a smooth anterior surface; it delineates the anterior junction between cornea and limbus and is strong/resistant to trauma. It does not regenerate — when damaged it is replaced by scar tissue — and it is perforated in many places by nerves passing to the epithelium." },
  { id: 95, question: "Describe the corneal stroma's structure.", answer: "About 500 µm thick (~90% of corneal thickness), composed of regularly arranged lamellae of collagen bundles (~300 centrally, ~500 peripherally) lying parallel to each other and to the surface within a proteoglycan ground substance. Anterior lamellae are short/narrow; posterior lamellae are long, wide, and 'wetter' (more proteoglycan). Keratocytes (flattened fibroblasts with gap junctions) lie between the lamellae." },
  { id: 96, question: "What accounts for stromal transparency?", answer: "The regular lattice arrangement of collagen fibrils, with inter-fibril spacing smaller than the wavelength of visible light, causes destructive interference of scattered light and minimizes scatter; transparency also depends on maintaining stromal water content at ~78%. The cornea has little elasticity, stretching only ~0.25% at normal IOP." },
  { id: 97, question: "Describe Descemet membrane.", answer: "The basement membrane of the endothelium, whose thickness increases from ~3 µm at birth to ~10–12 µm in adults. Its anterior third is the fetal (banded) portion and its posterior two-thirds form after birth. Its major protein is type IV collagen; it thickens with age and in degenerations, is a strong resistant sheet, and regenerates (the endothelium synthesizes a structurally identical new basal lamina after injury)." },
  { id: 98, question: "What is Schwalbe line, and what is posterior embryotoxon?", answer: "Schwalbe line is the peripheral termination of Descemet membrane — the internal landmark of the corneal limbus and the anterior limit of the drainage angle. When Schwalbe line is hypertrophied and anteriorly displaced (a congenital anomaly) it is visible on gonioscopy as posterior embryotoxon." },
  { id: 99, question: "Describe the corneal endothelium's structure and its changes with age.", answer: "A single layer of interdigitated hexagonal cells in a mosaic; it does not undergo mitosis, so cell loss is covered by enlargement and spreading of neighbors. Cell density is highest peripherally. Cells are ~10 µm tall at birth, flattening to 3–5 µm (18–20 µm wide) with age; density declines ~0.5–1% per year; cells become polymegathic (variable area) and pleomorphic (variable shape) with age." },
  { id: 100, question: "How does the endothelium maintain corneal deturgescence (fluid regulation)?", answer: "It provides a barrier limiting the ingress of salt/metabolites into the stroma and actively pumps ions (chiefly bicarbonate) from stroma to aqueous, drawing water out. The two main pump systems are the Na⁺/K⁺-ATPase pump and the Na⁺/H⁺ exchanger. The endothelium is metabolically very active (rich in mitochondria) and gets its nutrition and oxygen from the aqueous." },
  { id: 101, question: "What are the corneal oxygen sources and the key oxygen tensions?", answer: "Oxygen comes mainly from the atmosphere through the tear film, with minor contributions from the aqueous and limbal vasculature. Tear-film PO₂ is ~155 mm Hg and aqueous PO₂ ~40 mm Hg; a minimum of ~25 mm Hg is needed to maintain transparency." },
  { id: 102, question: "What are the corneal nutrient sources?", answer: "Glucose, amino acids, and vitamins are supplied mainly by the aqueous humor, with lesser amounts from the tears and limbal vessels; glucose is also derived from glycogen stores in the corneal epithelium. The most metabolically active layers are the epithelium and endothelium." },
  { id: 103, question: "What are the three metabolic pathways of the cornea and their yields?", answer: "(1) Pentose (hexose monophosphate) shunt — active in both hypoxic and normoxic conditions, producing NADPH and pentose (ribose-5-phosphate) for nucleic acid synthesis; (2) glycolysis (Embden-Meyerhof) — anaerobic, converting glucose/glycogen to pyruvate for 2 ATP; (3) TCA (Krebs) cycle — aerobic, oxidizing pyruvate to yield ~36 ATP, water, and CO₂." },
  { id: 104, question: "List the factors responsible for corneal transparency.", answer: "(1) Regular arrangement of stromal lamellae (lattice); (2) the epithelium and tear film (non-keratinization, uniform cell arrangement, intercellular junctions/compactness, and a homogeneous refractive index); (3) corneal avascularity; (4) non-myelinated nerve fibers; and (5) proper corneal hydration." },
  { id: 105, question: "How is corneal hydration controlled?", answer: "By (1) the stromal swelling pressure exerted by GAGs; (2) the barrier function of the epithelium and endothelium; (3) active endothelial pump systems (Na⁺/K⁺-ATPase, bicarbonate-dependent ATPase, carbonic anhydrase, Na⁺/H⁺ pump); (4) evaporation of water from the surface; and (5) the balance of IOP and swelling pressure (normal SP ~50–60 mm Hg). If IOP exceeds swelling pressure, corneal edema results; markedly elevated IOP causes epithelial edema." },
  { id: 106, question: "What factors affect drug penetration across the cornea?", answer: "The drug's lipid and water solubility (biphasic solubility is ideal); its molecular size, weight, and concentration; its ionic form; the pH and tonicity of the solution; surface-active agents (surfactants); and whether it is formulated as a prodrug." },
  { id: 107, question: "List the slit-lamp illumination techniques used to examine the cornea.", answer: "Direct illumination — diffuse, slit (optical section), and specular reflection (to view the endothelial mosaic); and indirect illumination — proximal illumination, sclerotic scatter, and retroillumination." },
  { id: 108, question: "Compare fluorescein, rose bengal, and lissamine green as ocular surface dyes.", answer: "Fluorescein — a water-soluble xanthene dye viewed with a cobalt-blue filter that pools in epithelial defects (positive staining) and is used for applanation tonometry, tear break-up time, and the Seidel test. Rose bengal (1%) stains devitalized cells and areas of mucin disruption, is irritating, and inhibits viral growth. Lissamine green stains similarly to rose bengal but does not stain healthy conjunctiva, is less irritating, and does not inhibit viral growth." },
  { id: 109, question: "What does corneal pachymetry measure, and what thickness thresholds are important?", answer: "Pachymetry measures corneal thickness and is a sensitive indicator of endothelial physiology (the thinnest zone is ~1.5 mm temporal to center). Descemet folds first appear when thickness increases by ≥10%; epithelial edema occurs when thickness exceeds ~700 µm; and a central thickness > 650 µm suggests a risk of symptomatic corneal edema after intraocular surgery." },
  { id: 110, question: "What parameters does specular (endothelial) microscopy report, and what are normal/critical values?", answer: "Endothelial cell density (CD) — ~3000–4000 cells/mm² at birth, ~2400–2500 in middle age, ~2000 in old age; mean cell size ~150–350 µm²; coefficient of variation (CV, polymegathism) ≤ 0.30 in young adults; and percentage hexagonality (pleomorphism when reduced), ideally ~100%. Before anterior segment surgery a CD > 1000–1200, CV < 0.40, and hexagonality > 50% are desired; a good donor cornea should have a CD ≥ 2000." },
  { id: 111, question: "What is esthesiometry and how is it performed?", answer: "Measurement of corneal sensation (the ophthalmic division of CN V). Topical medication must not be applied beforehand, and the result is compared with the normal fellow eye. Methods include a rolled cotton wisp (graded normal/reduced/absent per quadrant), non-contact air-pulse esthesiometry (pressure in millibars), and the handheld Cochet-Bonnet esthesiometer — a retractable nylon filament (full length 6 cm) shortened in 0.5-cm steps, where a shorter filament required to elicit sensation indicates reduced corneal sensation." },
  { id: 112, question: "Summarize the anterior segment imaging modalities relevant to the cornea.", answer: "Ultrasound biomicroscopy (UBM) — high-frequency ultrasound (water-bath immersion, ~5 mm penetration) that images through opaque media to assess cornea, angle, iris, ciliary body, and lens. Scheimpflug imaging (Pentacam) — a rotating camera taking ~50 slit images in < 2 s to map anterior and posterior corneal curvature, thickness, and ACD. Scanning-slit topography (Orbscan) — assesses anterior/posterior surfaces (its posterior elevation is mathematically derived and may be overestimated after LASIK). Anterior segment OCT — light-based, non-contact, high-resolution corneal/angle scans. Confocal microscopy gives cellular-level in vivo imaging, useful for post-refractive haze, dystrophies, and infections (bacteria, fungus, Acanthamoeba)." },
];

const BASIC_CORNEA2_MCQ: MCQ[] = [
  { id: 93, question: "The refractive index of the cornea is:", options: ["1.336", "1.376", "1.406", "1.333"], correct: 1, explanation: "The corneal refractive index is 1.376 (1.3375 is used for keratometer calibration)." },
  { id: 94, question: "The cornea provides approximately what share of the eye's total refractive power?", options: ["25%", "50%", "74% (~43.25 D of ~58.6 D)", "95%"], correct: 2, explanation: "The cornea provides ~74% of the eye's power — about +43.25 D of the total ~58.6 D." },
  { id: 95, question: "Most of the eye's refraction occurs at which interface?", options: ["Cornea–aqueous", "Air–tear film", "Lens–vitreous", "Tear–cornea"], correct: 1, explanation: "The air–tear interface (+43.6 D) accounts for most refraction; cornea–aqueous is actually −5.8 D." },
  { id: 96, question: "Central corneal thickness is approximately:", options: ["0.22 mm", "0.52 mm", "0.92 mm", "1.2 mm"], correct: 1, explanation: "The cornea is ~0.52 mm centrally and ~0.67 mm peripherally." },
  { id: 97, question: "The corneal horizontal diameter is approximately:", options: ["7–8 mm", "9–10 mm", "11–12 mm", "13–14 mm"], correct: 2, explanation: "The cornea is ~11–12 mm horizontally and ~10–11 mm vertically." },
  { id: 98, question: "The anterior corneal radius of curvature is approximately:", options: ["5.5 mm", "6.5 mm", "7.8 mm", "9.0 mm"], correct: 2, explanation: "Anterior radius ~7.8 mm; posterior radius ~6.5 mm." },
  { id: 99, question: "The normal water content of the cornea is approximately:", options: ["50%", "78%", "90%", "99%"], correct: 1, explanation: "The cornea is ~78% water, maintained by the endothelial pump." },
  { id: 100, question: "The predominant collagen type of the corneal stroma is:", options: ["Type II", "Type I", "Type IV", "Type VII"], correct: 1, explanation: "Type I predominates in the stroma (with types V and VI); type IV is in Descemet membrane." },
  { id: 101, question: "The keratan sulfate proteoglycan of the corneal stroma is:", options: ["Decorin", "Lumican", "Aggrecan", "Versican"], correct: 1, explanation: "Lumican is the keratan sulfate proteoglycan; decorin is the dermatan sulfate proteoglycan." },
  { id: 102, question: "Corneal sensation compared with the conjunctiva is about:", options: ["Equal", "10 times greater", "100 times greater", "Half as sensitive"], correct: 2, explanation: "The cornea has ~100 times the sensitivity of the conjunctiva — one of the highest nerve-ending densities in the body." },
  { id: 103, question: "Corneal sensory nerves lose their myelin sheath approximately how far from the limbus?", options: ["At the limbus exactly", "1–2 mm from the limbus", "At the central cornea", "They remain myelinated throughout"], correct: 1, explanation: "The long ciliary nerves lose their myelin 1–2 mm from the limbus, keeping the cornea transparent." },
  { id: 104, question: "Regarding corneal vascularity, which is TRUE?", options: ["The central cornea is richly vascularized", "The cornea is avascular; peripheral loops lie in overlapping subconjunctival tissue", "The stroma contains lymphatics centrally", "It is supplied by the central retinal artery"], correct: 1, explanation: "The cornea is avascular; small anterior ciliary loops invade only ~1 mm peripherally and lie in the subconjunctival tissue." },
  { id: 105, question: "The corneal endothelium is derived from:", options: ["Surface ectoderm", "Neural crest", "Mesoderm", "Neuroectoderm"], correct: 1, explanation: "The endothelium (and stroma keratocytes) are neural crest–derived; the epithelium is surface ectoderm." },
  { id: 106, question: "Which corneal layer is the last to appear in development (absent until later)?", options: ["Epithelium", "Bowman layer", "Endothelium", "Descemet membrane"], correct: 1, explanation: "By month 3 all components are present except Bowman layer, which forms later." },
  { id: 107, question: "The corneal epithelium is:", options: ["Keratinized stratified squamous with goblet cells", "Non-keratinized stratified squamous without goblet cells", "Simple columnar", "Transitional epithelium"], correct: 1, explanation: "It is non-keratinized stratified squamous epithelium, continuous with conjunctiva at the limbus but lacking goblet cells." },
  { id: 108, question: "The corneal epithelium turns over approximately every:", options: ["1–2 days", "7–14 days", "1–2 months", "1 year"], correct: 1, explanation: "Epithelial turnover is ~7–14 days; it is ~5% (~50 µm) of corneal thickness." },
  { id: 109, question: "The germinative (mitotically active) layer of the corneal epithelium is the:", options: ["Surface cells", "Wing cells", "Basal cells", "Goblet cells"], correct: 2, explanation: "The basal cells are the germinative layer, resting on the basal lamina." },
  { id: 110, question: "Basal epithelial cells attach to the basal lamina via:", options: ["Tight junctions only", "Hemidesmosomes (and desmosomes)", "Gap junctions only", "Zonulae occludentes"], correct: 1, explanation: "Basal cells anchor to the basal lamina by hemidesmosomes (with desmosomes laterally)." },
  { id: 111, question: "The corneal epithelial basal lamina is composed chiefly of:", options: ["Type I collagen and decorin", "Type IV collagen and laminin", "Type II collagen and hyaluronan", "Elastin and fibrillin"], correct: 1, explanation: "The basal lamina (lamina lucida + densa) consists of type IV collagen and laminin, and thickens with age/diabetes." },
  { id: 112, question: "After a simple corneal abrasion with an intact basal lamina, epithelial adhesion to Bowman layer is re-established within about:", options: ["1 hour", "7 days", "1 month", "6 months"], correct: 1, explanation: "If the basal lamina is intact, firm adhesion re-forms within ~7 days after migration and mitosis." },
  { id: 113, question: "Which statement about Bowman layer is TRUE?", options: ["It is cellular and regenerates readily", "It is acellular and does not regenerate (heals with scar)", "It is the basement membrane of the endothelium", "It is the thickest corneal layer"], correct: 1, explanation: "Bowman layer is an acellular modified anterior stroma (8–14 µm) that does not regenerate and heals with scar." },
  { id: 114, question: "The corneal stroma constitutes approximately what fraction of corneal thickness?", options: ["5%", "50%", "90%", "99%"], correct: 2, explanation: "The stroma (~500 µm) is ~90% of corneal thickness." },
  { id: 115, question: "The flattened fibroblast-like cells between the stromal lamellae are:", options: ["Melanocytes", "Keratocytes", "Langerhans cells", "Goblet cells"], correct: 1, explanation: "Keratocytes are flattened fibroblasts between lamellae that maintain the stromal matrix (with gap junctions)." },
  { id: 116, question: "Stromal transparency depends primarily on:", options: ["A random dense collagen arrangement", "A regular lattice of collagen fibrils spaced closer than the wavelength of light", "Melanin pigment", "Rich vascular supply"], correct: 1, explanation: "The regular lattice with sub-wavelength fibril spacing causes destructive interference, minimizing light scatter; hydration at ~78% is also required." },
  { id: 117, question: "The major protein of Descemet membrane is:", options: ["Type I collagen", "Type IV collagen", "Laminin only", "Fibrillin"], correct: 1, explanation: "Descemet membrane (the endothelial basement membrane) is rich in type IV collagen." },
  { id: 118, question: "Descemet membrane thickness changes from birth to adulthood approximately from:", options: ["3 µm to 10–12 µm", "10 µm to 3 µm", "1 µm to 2 µm", "50 µm to 500 µm"], correct: 0, explanation: "Descemet grows from ~3 µm at birth to ~10–12 µm in adults; its anterior third is fetal, posterior two-thirds postnatal." },
  { id: 119, question: "Schwalbe line represents the:", options: ["Termination of Descemet membrane / anterior limit of the drainage angle", "Insertion of the ciliary muscle", "Posterior limit of the trabecular meshwork", "Edge of the pupil"], correct: 0, explanation: "Schwalbe line is the peripheral termination of Descemet membrane; when hypertrophied/displaced it is posterior embryotoxon." },
  { id: 120, question: "A key feature of the corneal endothelium is that it:", options: ["Regenerates rapidly by mitosis", "Does not divide, so lost cells are covered by enlargement/spread of neighbors", "Contains goblet cells", "Is multilayered"], correct: 1, explanation: "The endothelium does not undergo mitosis; cell loss is compensated by enlargement and migration of neighboring cells." },
  { id: 121, question: "The corneal endothelial cell density declines with age at approximately:", options: ["0.5–1% per year", "10% per year", "0.01% per year", "It increases with age"], correct: 0, explanation: "Endothelial cell density declines ~0.5–1% per year; cells flatten and become polymegathic/pleomorphic." },
  { id: 122, question: "The endothelial pump maintaining corneal deturgescence relies chiefly on:", options: ["Na⁺/K⁺-ATPase (with a Na⁺/H⁺ exchanger)", "Passive diffusion only", "Melanin", "Aquaporins in Bowman layer"], correct: 0, explanation: "The endothelium actively pumps ions (bicarbonate) from stroma to aqueous via Na⁺/K⁺-ATPase and a Na⁺/H⁺ exchanger, drawing water out." },
  { id: 123, question: "The corneal endothelium derives its oxygen and nutrition mainly from the:", options: ["Tear film", "Aqueous humor", "Limbal vessels", "Atmosphere"], correct: 1, explanation: "The endothelium gets its oxygen and nutrients from the aqueous humor." },
  { id: 124, question: "The cornea's oxygen is supplied MAINLY by the:", options: ["Aqueous humor", "Atmosphere through the tear film", "Central retinal artery", "Limbal vessels"], correct: 1, explanation: "Corneal oxygen comes mainly from the atmosphere via the tear film (tear PO₂ ~155 mm Hg; aqueous ~40 mm Hg)." },
  { id: 125, question: "The minimum oxygen tension needed to maintain corneal transparency is about:", options: ["5 mm Hg", "25 mm Hg", "100 mm Hg", "155 mm Hg"], correct: 1, explanation: "A minimum PO₂ of ~25 mm Hg is needed to maintain corneal transparency." },
  { id: 126, question: "Which corneal metabolic pathway produces NADPH and ribose-5-phosphate and operates in both hypoxic and normoxic conditions?", options: ["Glycolysis (Embden-Meyerhof)", "The pentose (hexose monophosphate) shunt", "The TCA (Krebs) cycle", "Beta-oxidation"], correct: 1, explanation: "The pentose/HMP shunt yields NADPH and ribose-5-phosphate (for nucleic acid synthesis) in both conditions." },
  { id: 127, question: "Aerobic oxidation of pyruvate in the corneal TCA cycle yields approximately:", options: ["2 ATP", "12 ATP", "36 ATP", "0 ATP"], correct: 2, explanation: "The aerobic TCA cycle yields ~36 ATP (plus water and CO₂); anaerobic glycolysis yields only 2 ATP." },
  { id: 128, question: "Which is NOT one of the classic factors responsible for corneal transparency?", options: ["Regular stromal lamellae", "Avascularity", "Non-myelinated nerve fibers", "Rich melanin content"], correct: 3, explanation: "Transparency depends on regular lamellae, the epithelium/tear film, avascularity, non-myelinated nerves, and proper hydration — not melanin." },
  { id: 129, question: "Corneal edema develops when:", options: ["IOP exceeds the stromal swelling pressure", "Swelling pressure exceeds IOP by a small margin", "The epithelium thins", "Tear osmolarity falls"], correct: 0, explanation: "When IOP exceeds the stromal swelling pressure (normally ~50–60 mm Hg), fluid is forced in and the cornea becomes edematous; very high IOP causes epithelial edema." },
  { id: 130, question: "On specular microscopy, 'polymegathism' refers to:", options: ["Increased variation in endothelial cell AREA", "Increased variation in endothelial cell SHAPE", "Loss of endothelial cells", "Corneal thinning"], correct: 0, explanation: "Polymegathism is increased variation in cell area (e.g., contact lens wear); pleomorphism is increased variation in cell shape." },
  { id: 131, question: "A good donor cornea should have an endothelial cell density of at least:", options: ["500 cells/mm²", "1000 cells/mm²", "2000 cells/mm²", "5000 cells/mm²"], correct: 2, explanation: "A good donor cornea should have a CD ≥ 2000 cells/mm²; a healthy adult typically has ~2400–2500." },
  { id: 132, question: "On pachymetry, epithelial edema is expected when corneal thickness exceeds approximately:", options: ["500 µm", "600 µm", "700 µm", "900 µm"], correct: 2, explanation: "Epithelial edema occurs when thickness exceeds ~700 µm; central thickness > 650 µm suggests risk of edema after intraocular surgery." },
  { id: 133, question: "The Cochet-Bonnet esthesiometer indicates reduced corneal sensation when:", options: ["A longer filament is needed to elicit sensation", "A shorter (retracted) filament is needed to elicit sensation", "No filament is used", "The cornea stains with fluorescein"], correct: 1, explanation: "With the Cochet-Bonnet device, a shorter filament required to elicit a response indicates reduced corneal sensation." },
  { id: 134, question: "Which slit-lamp technique is used specifically to view the corneal endothelial mosaic?", options: ["Sclerotic scatter", "Specular reflection", "Retroillumination", "Diffuse illumination"], correct: 1, explanation: "Specular reflection is used to visualize the endothelial mosaic; sclerotic scatter highlights subtle stromal opacities." },
  { id: 135, question: "Which vital dye does NOT stain healthy conjunctiva and does not inhibit viral growth?", options: ["Fluorescein", "Rose bengal", "Lissamine green", "Trypan blue"], correct: 2, explanation: "Lissamine green stains devitalized cells like rose bengal but spares healthy conjunctiva, is less irritating, and does not inhibit viral growth." },
  { id: 136, question: "Which anterior segment imaging modality uses reflected LIGHT (not sound) for non-contact, high-resolution corneal and angle scans?", options: ["Ultrasound biomicroscopy (UBM)", "Anterior segment OCT", "A-scan ultrasonography", "Immersion B-scan"], correct: 1, explanation: "Anterior segment OCT emits and detects light for non-contact, high-resolution imaging; UBM uses high-frequency ultrasound." },
];

